c $6C00 Game Entry Point
@ $6C00 label=Start
D $6C00 Starts a game. The clean object, location and save bytes are copied
. out, then a keypress builds a new game from those copies. Holding N
. while that key is pressed turns the pictures off.
  $6C00,$01 Disable interrupts.
N $6C01 Initial set-up; copies "clean" data to the "CopyOf..." store. This is then used to create a new game in #R$6C27.
  $6C01,$0B Copies #N($0615,$04) bytes from #R$C11B to #R$F400.
  $6C0C,$08 Copies #N($05D9,$04) bytes from #R$BA8A to #R$FA15.
  $6C14,$0B Copies #N$1D bytes from #R$B6EB to #R$5F00.
  $6C1F,$08 Copies #N$BF bytes from #R$CA84 to #R$5F1D.
@ $6C27 label=Restart
  $6C27,$01 Disable interrupts.
  $6C28,$03 Set the stack pointer to #N$5EFF.
N $6C2B Reset the border and paper colours for location #LOCATION$05 - "#LOCATIONNAME$05".
  $6C2B,$04 Point #REGix at the location graphics table.
  $6C2F,$05 Call #R$9DBD with location #LOCATION$05 - "#LOCATIONNAME$05".
  $6C34,$03 Read the low byte of the graphics address for #LOCATION($05) (#R$E142).
  $6C37,$03 Read the high byte of the graphics address for #LOCATION($05) (#R$E142).
  $6C3A,$05 Write #N$00 over the border and colour of Trolls Clearing, #R$E142 and the next byte.
N $6C3F Copy "clean" data to the game tables ready for a new game.
  $6C3F,$0B Copies #N($0615,$04) bytes from #R$F400 to #R$C11B.
  $6C4A,$08 Copies #N($05D9,$04) bytes from #R$FA15 to #R$BA8A.
  $6C52,$0B Copies #N$1D bytes from #R$5F00 to #R$B6EB.
  $6C5D,$08 Copies #N$BF bytes from #R$5F1D back to #R$CA84.
  $6C65,$03 Set the border colour to #INK$00.
  $6C68,$05 Write #COLOUR$38 to #SKOOLKID$5C48(BORDCR).
@ $6C6D label=Restart_Keypress
  $6C6D,$03 Read from the keyboard port.
  $6C70,$02,b$01 Keep bits 0-4, one for each key in the half-row. XOR A then IN A,(#N$FE) reads every half-row at once, and a pressed key reads as a reset bit.
  $6C72,$04 Loop back to #R$6C6D until any key has been pressed.
  $6C76,$04 Read from the keyboard port. #TABLE(default,centre,centre,centre,centre,centre,centre) { =h,r2 Port Number | =h,c5 Bit } { =h 0 | =h 1 | =h 2 | =h 3 | =h 4 } { #N$7F | SPACE | SYMBOL SHIFT | M | N | B } TABLE#
  $6C7A,$02,b$01 Keep bit 3 ("N").
  $6C7C,$03 Write it to #R$B707.
  $6C7F,$06 Write #N$50E0 to #R$85B4(the percentage buffer).
  $6C85,$05 Write #N$2B to #R$85B4(#N$85B6).
  $6C8A,$06 Write #N$5020 to #R$869C.
  $6C90,$05 Write #N$01 to #R$869E.
  $6C95,$05 Write #N$20 to #R$85B3.
  $6C9A,$05 Write #N$2A to #R$869B.
  $6C9F,$08 Write #N$00 to #N$C8 bytes, starting from #R$B738 with #R$70E2.
  $6CA7,$05 Store the refresh register in the random seed.
  $6CAC,$0A Clear the space count and the character flag and the matcher path flag.
  $6CB6,$06 Clear the S-types-an-F gate and the printer-on flag.
  $6CBC,$0B Write #N$01 to #LIST { #R$B702 } { #R$B6FA } { #R$B704 } LIST#
  $6CC7,$06 Write #N($0000,$04,$04) to #R$B6F7.

c $6CCD Squiggle Line
@ $6CCD label=SquiggleLine
D $6CCD This section draws the "Squiggle" line which separates the windows.
E $6CCD Continue on to #R$6CEC.
N $6CCD #PUSHS #UDGTABLE { #SIM(start=$6CCD,stop=$6CEC) #SCR$04,$00,$12,$20,$01(squiggle) } UDGTABLE# #POPS
  $6CCD,$03 Call #R$6FD3 to clear screen.
  $6CD0,$03 Point at the squiggle row on screen.
  $6CD3,$03 Point at the squiggle pattern.
  $6CD6,$02 Five rows of the squiggle are drawn.
@ $6CD8 label=SquiggleLine_OuterLoop
  $6CD8,$03 Set a counter of #N$10.
@ $6CDB label=SquiggleLine_InnerLoop
  $6CDB,$01 Read the next byte of the picture.
  $6CDC,$01 Store it at #N$5140.
  $6CDD,$02 Increase both pointers by one.
  $6CDF,$02 Store the squiggle pattern in the squiggle row.
  $6CE1,$01 Step #REGhl on.
  $6CE2,$01 Step back one byte through #R$6DCC.
  $6CE3,$02 Decrease counter by one and loop back to #R$6CDB until counter is zero.
  $6CE5,$02 Step on through #R$6DCC.
  $6CE7,$05 Move to the next screen row and repeat while squiggle rows remain.

c $6CEC Turn: Read And Tokenise The Command
@ $6CEC label=TurnReadAndTokeniseTheCommand
D $6CEC Reads the player's command or replays a stored one, tokenises the
. words and hands them to the Inglish matcher.
  $6CEC,$05 Write #N$11 to #R$B716.
  $6CF1,$03 Read the replay path.
  $6CF4,$01 Count on from the replay path.
  $6CF5,$02 If #REGa is not zero then jump to #R$6D13.
  $6CF7,$03 Call #R$97AD to reset the turn to the player.
  $6CFA,$03 Point at the input buffer.
@ $6CFD label=TurnReadAndTokeniseTheCommand_Loop
  $6CFD,$01 Read the next byte of the input buffer.
N $6CFE Echo this character of the stored line.
  $6CFE,$03 Call #R$85B8 to print one character.
  $6D01,$05 Jump back to #R$6CFD if the front of the line is not #N$0D.
  $6D06,$0B Copies #N$05 bytes from #R$6FF4 to #R$6FF9.
  $6D11,$02 Jump to #R$6D22 to clear the token buffer.
@ $6D13 label=TurnReadAndTokeniseTheCommand_SetTheLineReadFlag
  $6D13,$05 Write #N$01 to #R$B705.
  $6D18,$05 Write #N$09 to #R$B716.
  $6D1D,$03 Call #R$6DD6 to read the input line.
  $6D20,$02 Jump to #R$6D8A if #R$6DD6 returned with the zero flag set.
@ $6D22 label=TurnReadAndTokeniseTheCommand_ClearTheTokenBuffer
  $6D22,$08 Write #N$00 to #N$40 bytes, starting from #R$709C with #R$70E2.
  $6D2A,$03 Point at the input buffer.
  $6D2D,$04 Point #REGiy at the token buffer.
@ $6D31 label=TurnReadAndTokeniseTheCommand_TakeTheNextWord
  $6D31,$03 Call #R$6E97 to take the next word.
  $6D34,$04 Jump to #R$6DA2 if the byte from #R$6E97 is #N$D0.
  $6D38,$04 Jump to #R$6D6C if that byte is not #N$90.
  $6D3C,$06 Jump to #R$6D6C if the low nibble of #REGb or any bit of #REGc is set.
N $6D42 Test the conjunction count.
  $6D42,$06 Jump to #R$6D4E if *#R$B71B is not zero.
  $6D48,$01 Count on from the conjunction count.
  $6D49,$03 Update *#R$B71B.
  $6D4C,$02 Jump to #R$6D6C to skip updating the conjunction count.
@ $6D4E label=TurnReadAndTokeniseTheCommand_CountConjunctions
  $6D4E,$01 Step the conjunction count down by one.
  $6D4F,$03 Update *#R$B71B.
  $6D52,$03 Read the previous token byte from #REGiy.
  $6D55,$06 Jump to #R$6D6C if the high nibble of the clause byte at offset -2 is #N$B0.
  $6D5B,$04 Jump to #R$6D6C if the high nibble of the clause byte at offset -2 is #N$A0.
  $6D5F,$02 Mark this token as a full stop.
  $6D61,$03 Store the token byte in the buffer at #REGiy.
  $6D64,$01 Take zero for the next token byte.
  $6D65,$03 Store that zero in the token buffer at #REGiy.
  $6D68,$04 Step to the next byte of the token buffer.
@ $6D6C label=TurnReadAndTokeniseTheCommand_StoreTheToken
  $6D6C,$03 Store the token byte in the buffer at #REGiy.
  $6D6F,$03 Store the token's second byte in the buffer at #REGiy.
  $6D72,$09 Jump back to #R$6D31 unless the class in #REGd is #N$C0.
N $6D7B Test the conjunction count.
  $6D7B,$06 Jump to #R$6D8A if *#R$B71B is zero.
  $6D81,$04 Clear *#R$B71B.
N $6D85 Deal with the conjunction before the next line.
  $6D85,$03 Call #R$792F to ask "what ?" and set the action countdown.
  $6D88,$02 Jump back to #R$6D13 to update the line-read flag.
@ $6D8A label=TurnReadAndTokeniseTheCommand_PointAtTheTokens
  $6D8A,$06 Point *#R$B6DC at #R$709C.
@ $6D90 label=TurnReadAndTokeniseTheCommand_PrepareTheClause
N $6D90 Match the tokens against the grammar.
  $6D90,$03 Call #R$7585 to prepare the clause.
  $6D93,$03 Jump back to #R$6D13 if #R$7585 returned with the zero flag clear.
N $6D96 Carry out the matched command.
  $6D96,$03 Call #R$7960 to apply A match.
N $6D99 Test the line-read flag.
  $6D99,$06 Jump to #R$6D90 if *#R$B705 is not zero.
  $6D9F,$03 Jump back to #R$6D13 to update the line-read flag.
@ $6DA2 label=TurnReadAndTokeniseTheCommand_ShowTheUnknownWord
  $6DA2,$03 Point at the message "i do not know the word " (#R$AD93).
  $6DA5,$05 Write #N$01 to #R$B701.
  $6DAA,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $6DAD,$03 Read *#R$B6DA.
@ $6DB0 label=TurnReadAndTokeniseTheCommand_TestForEnter
  $6DB0,$05 Jump to #R$6DC1 if the unknown word is #N$0D.
  $6DB5,$04 Jump to #R$6DC1 if the unknown word is #N$22.
N $6DB9 Print the character in #REGa.
  $6DB9,$03 Call #R$858B to print one character.
  $6DBC,$05 Jump back to #R$6DB0 if the unknown word is not #N$20.
@ $6DC1 label=TurnReadAndTokeniseTheCommand_PrintAQuote
N $6DC1 Print a quote.
  $6DC1,$05 Print a quote.
N $6DC6 Print a newline.
  $6DC6,$03 Call #R$8583 to print a newline.
  $6DC9,$03 Jump back to #R$6D13 to update the line-read flag.

b $6DCC Squiggle Graphics
@ $6DCC label=Graphics_Squiggle
D $6DCC Read by #R$6CCD.
  $6DCC,$0A,$05

c $6DD6 Read The Input Line
@ $6DD6 label=ReadTheInputLine
E $6DD6 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D8AD }
. LIST#
D $6DD6 Reads one line into the input buffer. Cursor keys become the compass
. letters, and a quote or full stop ends a word.
  $6DD6,$06 Store #N($0BB8,$04) in *#R$B714.
N $6DDC Update the action countdown.
  $6DDC,$08 Write #N$01 to *#R$B701 and *#R$B6FA.
N $6DE4 Print the ">" prompt.
  $6DE4,$05 Print '>'.
N $6DE9 Print a space.
  $6DE9,$05 Print a space.
  $6DEE,$03 Point at the input buffer.
  $6DF1,$02 Start the free-space count at #N$80.
@ $6DF3 label=ReadTheInputLine_Loop
  $6DF3,$02 No characters have been rubbed out yet.
@ $6DF5 label=ReadTheInputLine_WaitForAKey
N $6DF5 Read the next key.
  $6DF5,$03 Call #R$7249 to wait for a key.
  $6DF8,$04 Jump to #R$6E01 if bit 7 of the free-space count in #REGb is clear.
  $6DFC,$05 Jump to #R$6E7A when the character is #N$40.
@ $6E01 label=ReadTheInputLine_TestTheRubOutKey
  $6E01,$05 Call #R$6E4F if bit 0 of #REGc (set once a character has been rubbed out) is clear.
  $6E06,$06 Set the rubbed-out flag and jump to #R$6E11 unless the key is #N$18.
N $6E0C Rub out the whole line.
  $6E0C,$03 Call #R$6E8B to rub out characters.
  $6E0F,$02 Jump back to #R$6DF3 to read the next character.
@ $6E11 label=ReadTheInputLine_TestForABackspace
  $6E11,$04 Jump to #R$6E22 unless the key is #N$08.
  $6E15,$04 Jump back to #R$6DF5 if bit 7 of the free-space count in #REGb is set.
N $6E19 Print character #N$08.
  $6E19,$05 Print a backspace.
  $6E1E,$01 One more byte of the line is free.
  $6E1F,$01 Step back one byte through #R$6FF9.
  $6E20,$02 Jump back to #R$6DF5 to read the next key.
@ $6E22 label=ReadTheInputLine_TestForALetter
  $6E22,$04 Jump to #R$6E3A if the key is not below #N$40.
  $6E26,$04 Jump to #R$6E3A if the key is a quote.
  $6E2A,$04 Jump to #R$6E3A if the key is a space.
  $6E2E,$04 Jump to #R$6E3A if the key is ENTER.
  $6E32,$04 Jump to #R$6E3A if the key is a full stop.
  $6E36,$04 Jump back to #R$6DF5 if the key is not a comma.
@ $6E3A label=ReadTheInputLine_RememberTheAcceptedKey
  $6E3A,$03 Remember this key at #R$B704 so the next letter is capitalised.
  $6E3D,$04 Jump to #R$6E47 if the free-space count in #REGb is zero.
N $6E41 Print the character in #REGa.
  $6E41,$03 Call #R$858B to print one character.
  $6E44,$02 Store it at #R$6FF9 and step on.
  $6E46,$01 One fewer byte of the line is free.
@ $6E47 label=ReadTheInputLine_FinishWhenTheKeyIsEnter
  $6E47,$05 Jump back to #R$6DF5 if the key is not ENTER.
  $6E4C,$02 Clear the zero flag so ENTER ends the line.
  $6E4E,$01 Return.

c $6E4F Input: Map A Cursor Key
@ $6E4F label=Input_MapACursorKey
E $6E4F View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D978 }
. LIST#
D $6E4F Turns a cursor key into the matching direction letter and plants ENTER after it.
  $6E4F,$04 Jump to #R$6E72 if the key is right.
  $6E53,$04 Jump to #R$6E76 if the key is left.
  $6E57,$04 Jump to #R$6E6E if the key is down.
  $6E5B,$03 Return if the key is not up. The up cursor is #N$5B.
  $6E5E,$02 Turn the cursor key into the letter N.
@ $6E60 label=Input_MapACursorKey_StoreTheDirectionLetter
  $6E60,$02 Store it at #R$6FF9 and step on.
N $6E62 Print the character in #REGa.
  $6E62,$03 Call #R$858B to print one character.
  $6E65,$01 One fewer byte of the line is free.
  $6E66,$03 Set the line being typed to ENTER.
  $6E69,$01 Step to the next byte of the input buffer.
  $6E6A,$01 One fewer byte of the line is free.
  $6E6B,$02 Clear the zero flag. The direction letter is followed by ENTER.
  $6E6D,$01 Return.
@ $6E6E label=Input_MapACursorKey_TurnTheDownKeyIntoS
  $6E6E,$02 Turn the cursor key into the letter S.
  $6E70,$02 Jump back to #R$6E60 to store the letter S.
@ $6E72 label=Input_MapACursorKey_TurnTheRightKeyIntoE
  $6E72,$02 Turn the cursor key into the letter E.
  $6E74,$02 Jump back to #R$6E60 to store the letter E.
@ $6E76 label=Input_MapACursorKey_TurnTheLeftKeyIntoW
  $6E76,$02 Turn the cursor key into the letter W.
  $6E78,$02 Jump back to #R$6E60 to store the letter W.
@ $6E7A label=Input_MapACursorKey_ReadAnotherKeyWhenTheFlagIsSet
N $6E7A Test the fresh-game flag.
  $6E7A,$07 Jump to #R$6DF5 if *#R$B71A is not zero.
N $6E81 Print character #N$08.
  $6E81,$05 Print a backspace.
N $6E86 Print the character in #REGa.
  $6E86,$03 Call #R$858B to print one character.
  $6E89,$01 Return zero.
  $6E8A,$01 Return.

c $6E8B Input: Rub Out Characters
@ $6E8B label=Input_RubOutCharacters
E $6E8B View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D9B7 }
. LIST#
D $6E8B Prints backspaces and steps back through the input buffer until the count runs out.
  $6E8B,$03 Return if bit 7 of the free-space count in #REGb is set.
N $6E8E Print character #N$08.
  $6E8E,$05 Print a backspace.
  $6E93,$01 One more byte of the line is free.
  $6E94,$01 Step back one byte through #R$6FF9.
  $6E95,$02 Jump back to #R$6E8B to rub out another character.

c $6E97 Input: Take The Next Word
@ $6E97 label=Input_TakeTheNextWord
D $6E97 Skips spaces, stores the start of the next word, and classifies it.
  $6E97,$07 Jump back to #R$6E98 if the line being typed is a space.
@ $6E98 label=Input_TakeTheNextWord_SkipSpaces
  $6E9E,$01 Step back one byte through #R$6FF9.
  $6E9F,$03 Update *#R$B6DA.
  $6EA2,$04 Jump to #R$6EDE if the line being typed is ENTER.
  $6EA6,$03 Call #R$6F30 to classify punctuation.
  $6EA9,$02 Jump to #R$6EE3 if #R$6F30 classified this character as punctuation.
  $6EAB,$03 Call #R$6F47 to copy a dictionary word.
  $6EAE,$03 Jump to #R$6EBC if #R$6F47 found no dictionary word starting with the typed word's first letter.
@ $6EB1 label=Input_TakeTheNextWord_CompareTheDictionaryWord
  $6EB1,$03 Call #R$6FBA to compare the dictionary word.
  $6EB4,$02 Jump to #R$6EC0 if #R$6FBA found the words match.
@ $6EB6 label=Input_TakeTheNextWord_CopyADictionaryWord
  $6EB6,$03 Call #R$6F72 to copy a dictionary word.
  $6EB9,$03 Jump back to #R$6EB1 while #R$6F72 finds another dictionary word with the same first letter.
@ $6EBC label=Input_TakeTheNextWord_MarkThisTokenAsAnUnknownWord
  $6EBC,$02 Mark this token as an unknown word.
  $6EBE,$02 Jump to #R$6EE0 to finish this unknown-word token.
@ $6EC0 label=Input_TakeTheNextWord_CompareTheWordLengths
  $6EC0,$03 Read *#R$708A.
  $6EC3,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $6EC4,$03 Read *#R$709B.
  $6EC7,$03 Jump to #R$6EEB if the length of the dictionary word is not below the length of the typed word.
  $6ECA,$04 Jump back to #R$6EB6 if the length of the dictionary word is below #N$04.
  $6ECE,$05 Call #R$6F76 to unpack the dictionary word when its length matches the typed word.
  $6ED3,$02 Jump to #R$6EE9 if #R$6F76 found a mismatch.
  $6ED5,$03 Call #R$6FBA to compare the dictionary word.
  $6ED8,$02 Jump to #R$6EE9 if #R$6FBA found the words differ.
  $6EDA,$04 Jump back to #R$6EB6 to read the input line.
@ $6EDE label=Input_TakeTheNextWord_MarkTheEndOfTheCommand
  $6EDE,$02 Mark this token as the end of a command.
@ $6EE0 label=Input_TakeTheNextWord_FinishTheUnknownWord
  $6EE0,$03 Start from a clear pointer.
@ $6EE3 label=Input_TakeTheNextWord_ReturnTheClass
  $6EE3,$02 Keep the low byte of the saved pointer and put the class in #REGd.
  $6EE5,$01 Add the length of the typed word to the class in #REGa.
  $6EE6,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $6EE7,$01 Restore the end-of-command class #N$C0.
  $6EE8,$01 Return.
@ $6EE9 label=Input_TakeTheNextWord_PopTheSavedPointer
  $6EE9,$06 Read *#R$B717.
@ $6EEB label=Input_TakeTheNextWord_LoadTheDictionaryPointer
  $6EEF,$03 Save the dictionary pointer and start the length at zero.
@ $6EF2 label=Input_TakeTheNextWord_StepThroughTheDictionaryWord
  $6EF2,$09 Step to the next dictionary byte and loop back to #R$6EF2 while bit 7 of that byte is clear.
  $6EFB,$04 Jump back to #R$6EF2 if the dictionary byte count is #N$02.
  $6EFF,$04 Jump to #R$6F09 unless the byte is #N$03.
  $6F03,$06 Jump back to #R$6EF2 if bit 7 of the earlier dictionary byte is set.
@ $6F09 label=Input_TakeTheNextWord_TestForAnOffset
  $6F09,$06 Jump to #R$6F1A if bit 6 of this dictionary byte is clear.
  $6F0F,$03 Read the low byte of this dictionary offset.
  $6F12,$03 Read the high byte of this dictionary offset.
  $6F15,$04 Add the dictionary base at #R$6000.
  $6F19,$01 Exchange the dictionary address with the pointer saved on the stack.
@ $6F1A label=Input_TakeTheNextWord_PackTheClassBits
  $6F1A,$06 Pack the top two bits of this letter into the class.
  $6F20,$01 Step to the next byte of the input buffer.
  $6F21,$04 Pack the next two bits of the following letter into the class.
  $6F25,$01 Combine the two pairs of class bits.
  $6F26,$01 Step back one byte through #R$6FF9.
  $6F27,$03 Add the #N$A000 token base onto the token pointer.
  $6F2A,$01 Add the token base to #R$6FF9.
  $6F2B,$05 Keep the token address in #REGbc and jump to #R$6EE3 to return the class.

c $6F30 Input: Classify Punctuation
@ $6F30 label=Input_ClassifyPunctuation
E $6F30 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$DAC6 }
. LIST#
D $6F30 Turns a full stop, comma or quote into the class being matched the parser expects.
  $6F30,$06 Take class #N($B0), and jump to #R$6F41 if this character is a full stop.
  $6F36,$06 Take class #N($A0), and jump to #R$6F41 if this character is a comma.
  $6F3C,$03 Return unless this character is a quote.
  $6F3F,$02 A quote uses class #N$90.
@ $6F41 label=Input_ClassifyPunctuation_StepAlongTheInputBuffer
  $6F41,$01 Step to the next byte of the input buffer.
  $6F42,$01 Take the punctuation class into #REGa.
  $6F43,$03 Start from a clear pointer.
  $6F46,$01 Return.

c $6F47 Input: Copy A Dictionary Word
@ $6F47 label=Input_CopyADictionaryWord
D $6F47 Copies one typed word into the dictionary buffer and looks it up.
  $6F47,$03 Point #REGde at the typed word.
  $6F4A,$02 Start the pass count at zero.
@ $6F4C label=Input_CopyADictionaryWord_CopyTheNextLetter
  $6F4C,$05 Jump to #R$6F59 if this character is below #N$40.
  $6F51,$04 Store the low five bits of this character in the typed word and step on.
  $6F55,$01 Step to the next byte of the input buffer.
  $6F56,$01 Count one more letter of the typed word.
  $6F57,$02 Jump back to #R$6F4C to copy the next letter.
@ $6F59 label=Input_CopyADictionaryWord_CopyTheCount
  $6F59,$01 Copy the count in #REGb into #REGa.
  $6F5A,$03 Update *#R$708A.
  $6F5D,$04 Read *#R$707A.
  $6F61,$02 The dictionary offset's high byte is zero before the index is doubled.
  $6F63,$04 Add the token index to the pointer.
  $6F67,$01 Add the token index to the typed word.
  $6F68,$01 Read the low byte of the typed word.
  $6F69,$01 Step to the next byte of the typed word.
  $6F6A,$01 Read the high byte of the typed word.
  $6F6B,$07 Point #REGix at the dictionary word and restore the input pointer from the stack.
  $6F72,$04 Update *#R$B717.
  $6F76,$03 Read the first byte of the dictionary word.
  $6F79,$03 Keep the first letter (bits 0-4).
  $6F7C,$03 Read the typed word's first letter at #R$707A.
  $6F7F,$02 Return when this dictionary word does not start with the typed word's first letter.
  $6F81,$04 Point #REGhl at the buffer that receives the dictionary word.
  $6F85,$03 Start from a clear pointer.
@ $6F88 label=Input_CopyADictionaryWord_TestTheLetter
  $6F88,$07 Jump to #R$6F92 if the low five bits of this dictionary byte are zero.
  $6F8F,$02 Store it in the dictionary word and step on.
  $6F91,$01 Count one more letter of the dictionary word.
@ $6F92 label=Input_CopyADictionaryWord_StepTheDictionary
  $6F92,$09 Step to the next dictionary byte and loop back to #R$6F88 while bit 7 of that byte is clear.
  $6F9B,$05 Jump back to #R$6F88 if the letter count in #REGc is #N$02.
  $6FA0,$04 Jump to #R$6FAA if the letter count is not #N$03.
  $6FA4,$06 Jump back to #R$6F88 if bit 7 of the earlier dictionary byte is set.
@ $6FAA label=Input_CopyADictionaryWord_RestoreThePointer
  $6FAA,$02 Restore the saved pointer from the stack and keep the letter count.
  $6FAC,$03 Update *#R$709B.
  $6FAF,$05 Return if bit 6 of this dictionary byte is clear.
  $6FB4,$04 Step #REGix past the two flag bytes of this dictionary entry.
  $6FB8,$01 Return zero.
  $6FB9,$01 Return.

c $6FBA Input: Compare The Dictionary Word
@ $6FBA label=Input_CompareTheDictionaryWord
D $6FBA Compares the typed word with the dictionary word of the same length.
  $6FBA,$03 Read *#R$708A.
  $6FBD,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $6FBE,$03 Read *#R$709B.
  $6FC1,$03 Jump to #R$6FC5 if the length of the dictionary word is not below the length of the typed word.
  $6FC4,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
@ $6FC5 label=Input_CompareTheDictionaryWord_PointAtTheTypedWord
  $6FC5,$03 Point #REGhl at the typed word.
  $6FC8,$03 Point #REGde at the dictionary word.
@ $6FCB label=Input_CompareTheDictionaryWord_CompareTheLetters
  $6FCB,$03 Return if the dictionary word is not the typed word.
  $6FCE,$01 Step to the next byte of the dictionary word.
  $6FCF,$01 Step to the next byte of the typed word.
  $6FD0,$02 Loop back to #R$6FCB until the length of the dictionary word runs out.
  $6FD2,$01 Return.

c $6FD3 Clear Screen
@ $6FD3 label=ClearScreen
E $6FD3 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$C10F }
. LIST#
D $6FD3 Clears the picture and paints the attributes for a fresh screen.
  $6FD3,$07 Set the border colour to #INK$07.
  $6FDA,$0D Write #N$00 to #N$1800 memory locations (clearing the screen buffer).
  $6FE7,$07 Write #COLOUR$38 to the following #N($0300,$04) memory locations (the attribute buffer).
  $6FEE,$03 Restore #REGbc, #REGde and #REGhl from the stack.
  $6FF1,$01 Return.

b $6FF2 Input Buffer
@ $6FF2 label=InputBuffer
D $6FF2 Read by #R$6CEC, #R$6DD6, #R$6E97, #R$6F47 and #R$6FBA.
  $6FF2,$02 Two bytes at the front of the line. #R$6CEC scans from here up to the carriage return.
  $6FF4,$05 Five bytes copied onto the line at #R$6FF9 by #R$6D06.
  $6FF9,$81,$10 The line being typed. #R$6DD6 reads and writes it.
  $707A,$10,$0A
  $708A,$01
  $708B,$10,$0A
  $709B,$01
  $709C,$40,$10 Token buffer. #R$6D22 clears #N$40 bytes here and #R$6D2D points #REGiy at the start.

g $70DC Stored Registers For PrintMessage
@ $70DC label=PrintMessage_SavedA
D $70DC Six bytes. #R$72DD saves #REGa, #REGde and #REGix here, and #R$735B puts them back.
  $70DC,$01 #REGa saved by #R$72DD and restored by #R$7363.
@ $70DD label=PrintMessage_SavedDE
W $70DD,$02 #REGde saved by #R$72DD and restored by #R$735B.
  $70DF,$01 Byte tested by #R$745C while a class token is printed.
@ $70E0 label=PrintMessage_SavedIX
W $70E0,$02 #REGix saved by #R$72DD and restored by #R$735F.

c $70E2 Blanks "n" memory locations
@ $70E2 label=Blanker
E $70E2 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$A2AB }
. LIST#
D $70E2 Fills a run of bytes with one value. The turn loop uses it to clear
. the token buffer and the workspace.
R $70E2 B Counter
R $70E2 HL Target
  $70E2,$01 Fill with zero.
@ $70E3 label=Blanker_Loop
  $70E3,$02 Store it and step on.
  $70E5,$02 Decrease counter by one and loop back to #R$70E3 until counter is zero.
  $70E7,$01 Return.

c $70E8 Index Action
@ $70E8 label=IndexAction
E $70E8 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$A2B1 }
. LIST#
D $70E8 Turns an action id into the address of its #N$08-byte entry in #R$AB53.
R $70E8 A The ID of the Action; #N$01-#N$3B
  $70E8,$0A Turn the id into an address in the action table, #N$08 bytes per entry.
  $70F2,$01 Return.

c $70F3 Clause: Pack The Action Scores
@ $70F3 label=ClausePackTheActionScores
E $70F3 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$A2BD }
. LIST#
D $70F3 Packs two scores from the action entry into the clause. The high nibbles of bytes 5
. and 7 are stored at #R$B71E. The high nibbles of bytes 1 and 3 are stored at #R$B71D.
  $70F3,$03 Read byte 5 of this action entry.
  $70F6,$07 Take the high nibble of byte 5.
  $70FD,$03 Read byte 7 of this action entry.
  $7100,$03 Add byte 5's high nibble, now the low nibble, onto the high nibble of byte 7.
  $7103,$03 Update *#R$B71E.
  $7106,$03 Read byte 1 of this action entry.
  $7109,$07 Take the high nibble of byte 1.
  $7110,$03 Read byte 3 of this action entry.
  $7113,$03 Add the high nibble of byte 1 onto the high nibble of byte 3.
  $7116,$03 Update *#R$B71D.
  $7119,$01 Return.

c $711A Inglish: Arm The Message Gate
@ $711A label=Inglish_ArmTheMessageGate
D $711A Clears the message-suppressed flag, sets the message gate, and describes the action when the
. command had no conjunction. Returns zero.
  $711A,$04 Clear the message-suppressed flag.
  $711E,$04 Set the message gate, so #R$72DD skips its setup.
  $7122,$07 Describe the action when the conjunction count is zero.
  $7129,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $712A,$01 Return.

c $712B Inglish: Describe The Action
@ $712B label=Inglish_DescribeTheAction
D $712B Prints the sentence for the current action: who, the verb, and the objects. Bit 4 of the
. packed token classes (#R$B71D) suppresses the whole sentence. What the other bits select is only
. partly confirmed.
N $712B Decide whether a sentence is due, and which noun it names.
  $712B,$05 Mark a class token as due.
  $7130,$04 Clear which noun is being named.
  $7134,$0C The action countdown is #N$01 when the message-suppressed flag is clear, and #N$00 when a message was held back.
  $7140,$01 Clear the countdown when the message-suppressed flag was set.
@ $7141 label=Inglish_DescribeTheAction_Store
  $7141,$03 Store the action countdown.
  $7144,$0A Look up the record for the current action.
  $714E,$03 Use that action record from here on.
  $7151,$04 Jump to #R$715C to skip the newline if the message-suppressed flag was zero.
  $7155,$07 Print a newline if it is the player's turn.
@ $715C label=Inglish_DescribeTheAction_Pack
  $715C,$03 Call #R$70F3 to pack this action entry's token classes into the clause.
  $715F,$06 Jump to #R$71C9 if bit 4 of the packed token classes (#R$B71D) is set. That bit suppresses the sentence.
@ $7165 label=Inglish_DescribeTheAction_Who
  $7165,$03 Print the current character.
  $7168,$08 Print the token #TOKEN($00EE) when the message-suppressed flag is zero.
  $7170,$05 Keep the pointer those prints returned, and step #N$06 bytes, the length of an object name.
  $7175,$03 Print the action's name.
  $7178,$03 Test whether the location is lit.
  $717B,$03 Jump to #R$718F if the location is lit.
  $717E,$07 Jump to #R$718F if the action is strike (#N$0B) or later.
  $7185,$03 Take the token #TOKEN($0AEA).
  $7188,$02 Step two bytes to the next word token.
  $718A,$03 Print that token.
  $718D,$02 Jump to #R$7192, skipping a second copy of the action name.
@ $718F label=Inglish_DescribeTheAction_Verb
  $718F,$03 Print the action's name.
@ $7192 label=Inglish_DescribeTheAction_Object
  $7192,$04 Jump to #R$71A6 if bit 3 of the scores #R$70F3 left in #REGc is clear.
  $7196,$05 Print the action's name again if bit 5 of the packed scores is set.
  $719B,$08 Print the action's name again if bit 7 of the clause byte is set.
  $71A3,$03 Print the current object.
@ $71A6 label=Inglish_DescribeTheAction_Second
  $71A6,$07 Jump to #R$71C1 if there is no second object.
  $71AD,$04 Jump to #R$71C1 if bit 2 of the packed token classes (#R$B71D) is clear.
  $71B1,$05 Print the action's name if bit 5 of the packed scores is clear.
  $71B6,$08 Print the action's name if bit 7 of the clause byte is clear.
  $71BE,$03 Print the second noun.
@ $71C1 label=Inglish_DescribeTheAction_Stop
  $71C1,$05 Print a full stop.
  $71C6,$03 Print a newline.
@ $71C9 label=Inglish_DescribeTheAction_Done
  $71C9,$04 Clear the class-token flag.
  $71CD,$07 Restore #REGde, #REGhl, #REGix, #REGbc and #REGiy from the stack.
  $71D4,$01 Return.

c $71D5 Table: Address Past The Location
@ $71D5 label=Table_AddressPastTheLocation
E $71D5 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$A4AB }
. LIST#
D $71D5 Finds the location record and returns the address of its name. The name token starts two bytes in.
  $71D5,$05 Find the location record.
  $71DA,$03 Take that record's address.
  $71DD,$02 Step to the location's name, two bytes into the record.
  $71DF,$02 Restore #REGix from the stack.
  $71E1,$01 Return.

c $71E2 Table: Address The Object Name
@ $71E2 label=Table_AddressTheObjectName
E $71E2 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$A4BC }
. LIST#
D $71E2 Finds the object record and returns the address of its name, eight bytes in.
  $71E2,$06 Find the object's record.
  $71E8,$03 Take that record's address.
  $71EB,$04 Step #N$08 bytes to the object's name.
  $71EF,$03 Restore #REGix and #REGde from the stack.
  $71F2,$01 Return.

c $71F3 Inglish: Match Clause Pairs
@ $71F3 label=Inglish_MatchClausePairs
E $71F3 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$A4E0 }
. LIST#
D $71F3 Tries several pairs of clause bytes. One pattern returns #N$01 and another returns
. #N$00. Which dictionary words those pairs stand for is unconfirmed.
  $71F3,$07 Compare the first pair.
  $71FA,$02 Jump to #R$7229 if that pair did not match.
  $71FC,$05 Mark the low byte of the class being matched.
  $7201,$03 Compare the next pair.
  $7204,$02 Jump to #R$720D if that pair did not match.
  $7206,$03 Compare a third pair.
  $7209,$04 Jump to #R$7229 with zero when the third pair matches.
@ $720D label=Inglish_MatchClausePairs_Shift
  $720D,$0B Move the parser pointer on #N$04 bytes.
  $7218,$02 Move the text pointer on two bytes.
  $721A,$03 Compare the shifted pair.
  $721D,$02 Jump to #R$7229 if that pair did not match.
  $721F,$05 Move the parser pointer back #N$04 bytes.
  $7224,$03 Compare the pair at the restored window.
  $7227,$02 Return #N$01 for this pattern.
@ $7229 label=Inglish_MatchClausePairs_Done
  $7229,$04 Restore #REGiy, #REGhl and #REGde from the stack.
  $722D,$01 Return.

c $722E Inglish: Compare One Clause Pair
@ $722E label=Inglish_CompareOneClausePair
E $722E View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$A51B }
. LIST#
D $722E Compares one pair of clause bytes with the pair the caller addressed. An empty pair
. matches. Otherwise the low nibbles of the second bytes must agree and the first bytes must be
. equal. Both pointers then move on by two.
  $722E,$06 Jump to #R$7241 if this pair is empty.
  $7234,$03 Read the second byte of the pair being tested.
  $7237,$05 Jump to #R$7241 if its low nibble differs from the clause byte.
  $723C,$01 Step back to the first byte of the pair.
  $723D,$01 Read that first byte.
  $723E,$03 Compare it with the first byte of the pair being tested.
@ $7241 label=Inglish_CompareOneClausePair_Advance
  $7241,$03 Move on to the next pair in the text.
  $7244,$04 Move on to the next pair being tested.
  $7248,$01 Return.

c $7249 Input: Wait For A Key
@ $7249 label=Input_WaitForAKey
D $7249 Counts down the input delay while polling the keyboard. A key raises the next delay by
. #N($01F4), up to #N($0BB8), and is returned. If the delay runs out, #R$7291 is printed into the
. buffer, ENTER is returned, and the next delay is cleared.
  $7249,$04 Read the input delay.
@ $724D label=Input_WaitForAKey_Poll
  $724D,$03 Poll the keyboard.
  $7250,$03 Jump to #R$7273 if a key is waiting.
  $7253,$05 Loop back to #R$724D to keep polling until the delay runs out.
  $7258,$05 Rub out the characters typed so far.
  $725D,$03 Point at the letters W, A, I, T.
  $7260,$02 Four letters.
@ $7262 label=Input_WaitForAKey_Wait
  $7262,$04 Store the next letter of #R$7291 in the input buffer.
  $7266,$03 Print that letter.
  $7269,$02 Loop back to #R$7262 until #R$7291 is done.
  $726B,$08 Remember the pointer after #R$7291, and prepare ENTER. The next delay is #N$FE0C plus #N($01F4), which is zero.
@ $7273 label=Input_WaitForAKey_AddToTheDelay
  $7273,$07 Add #N($01F4,$04) to the delay. A key raises the current delay; the timeout path adds it to zero.
  $727A,$03 The cap is #N($0BB8,$04).
  $727D,$02 Jump to #R$7284 if the sum overflowed, and use the cap.
  $727F,$03 Call #R$728B to compare the delay with the cap.
  $7282,$02 Jump to #R$7285 if the delay is still under the cap.
@ $7284 label=Input_WaitForAKey_Cap
  $7284,$01 Use the cap instead.
@ $7285 label=Input_WaitForAKey_Store
  $7285,$03 Store the next input delay.
  $7288,$02 Restore #REGaf and #REGhl from the stack.
  $728A,$01 Return.

c $728B Input: Compare Two Totals
@ $728B label=Input_CompareTwoTotals
D $728B Compares two 16-bit totals. Carry is set when the first is below the second. The input
. delay uses it to cap the next wait at #N($0BB8,$04).
  $728B,$03 Return at once when the high bytes differ. Carry is set when the first total is below.
  $728E,$02 Compare the low bytes.
  $7290,$01 Return.

b $7291 Print Table
D $7291 Read by #R$7249 and #R$72DD.
B $7291,$04 The letters W, A, I, T. #R$7249 types them when the input delay runs out.
@ $7295 label=PrintTable
W $7295,$2E

c $72C3 Message: Print One Character
@ $72C3 label=MessagePrintOneCharacter
E $72C3 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$A63A }
. LIST#
D $72C3 Prints one character, after the message gate and the day/night
. filter.
. Reached through the control-code table at #R$7295, dispatched by #R$72DD.
  $72C3,$03 Call #R$858B to print one character.
  $72C6,$03 Return unless the character just printed was #N$0D.
  $72C9,$04 Clear the capitalise-the-next-letter flag at #R$B704.
  $72CD,$01 Return.

c $72CE Response: "I Cannot Do That"
@ $72CE label=Response_ICannotDoThat
D $72CE Prints "I cannot do that" and returns to the command loop.
  $72CE,$03 Point at the message "i cannot do that" (#R$AFBF).
  $72D1,$02 Jump to #R$72DD to print "i cannot do that".

c $72D3 Message: Open The Gate For A Conjunction
@ $72D3 label=MessageOpenTheGateForAConjunction
D $72D3 Opens the message gate when the command contained a conjunction, so
. the next sentence is printed.
N $72D3 Test the conjunction count.
  $72D3,$06 Jump to #R$72DD if *#R$B71B is zero.
  $72D9,$04 Clear *#R$B6FA.

c $72DD Print Message
@ $72DD label=PrintMessage
E $72DD View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$A59F }
. LIST#
D $72DD Prints a message. Bytes under #N$20 are control codes from the table
. at #R$7295. Dictionary tokens are expanded in line.
R $72DD HL Text message address
N $72DD Store #REGa, #REGde and #REGix for later (see #R$735B).
  $72DD,$04 Store #REGde at #R$70DD.
  $72E1,$04 Store #REGix at #R$70E0.
  $72E5,$03 Save #REGa for #R$735B.
  $72E8,$06 If #R$B6FA is not zero, jump to #R$72F1
  $72EE,$03 Clear the message-suppressed flag, because this message is being printed.
@ $72F1 label=PrintMessage_Body
  $72F1,$03 Use the text pointer as the message pointer.
@ $72F4 label=PrintMessage_Loop
  $72F4,$03 Fetch the next character of the text message.
  $72F7,$04 Jump to #R$7318 if bit 7 of the character is clear, so it is plain text rather than a dictionary token.
  $72FB,$0E Jump to #R$7348 if the high nibble of this token byte, with bit 7 clear, is #N$30.
  $7309,$04 Jump to #R$7348 if the high nibble of this token byte is #N$20.
  $730D,$04 Jump to #R$7348 if the high nibble of this token byte is #N$60.
@ $7311 label=PrintMessage_Token
  $7311,$03 Call #R$74C1 to print a dictionary token.
@ $7314 label=PrintMessage_StepToTheNextCharacter
  $7314,$02 Step to the next character of the message.
  $7316,$02 Jump back to #R$72F4.
@ $7318 label=PrintMessage_PlainText
  $7318,$04 If the character is less than #N$20 jump to #R$7326.
  $731C,$05 Jump to #R$7493 if the character is #N$60 or above, as that is a common word.
  $7321,$03 Else, call #R$72C3.
  $7324,$02 Jump back to #R$7314 to move onto the next character.
@ $7326 label=PrintMessage_PrintTable
  $7326,$02 Save #REGde and keep #REGa in #REGe.
  $7328,$02 The control-code offset's high byte is zero.
  $732A,$04 Point #REGhl at entry #REGde of #R$7295.
  $732E,$01 Add the control code again, so this selects the handler word in #R$7295.
  $732F,$01 Read the low byte of the handler.
  $7330,$01 Step to the high byte of the handler word.
  $7331,$01 Read the high byte of the handler.
  $7332,$01 Point #REGhl at the control-code handler.
  $7333,$05 Restore #REGde from the stack, and jump to #R$733F if the character is not below #N$14.
  $7338,$03 Call #R$733F to run the control-code handler.
  $733B,$02 Jump back to #R$7314 if the control-code handler returned zero.
  $733D,$02 Jump back to #R$7311 to print a dictionary token.

c $733F Message: Dispatch The Control Code
@ $733F label=PrintMessage_Indirect_Jump
D $733F Jumps to the control-code handler the message printer looked up.
  $733F,$01 Jump to the address the message left behind.

c $7340 Message: Print A Stop
@ $7340 label=MessagePrintAStop
E $7340 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
