c $743F Message: Choose A Class Token
@ $743F label=Message_ChooseAClassToken
E $743F View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$A7D0 }
. LIST#
D $743F Turns a class token into a dictionary word and prints it. Bit 7 of the token selects the
. class table; a zero neighbour ends the line.
  $743F,$04 Jump to #R$7455 if the token is not a class token.
  $7443,$07 Jump to #R$744F if the low byte is not #TOKEN($07A8).
  $744A,$05 Return if the high nibble matches #TOKEN($07A8).
@ $744F label=Message_ChooseAClassToken_SetTheCapitaliseFlag
  $744F,$05 Set the capitalise-the-next-letter flag.
  $7454,$01 Return.
@ $7455 label=Message_ChooseAClassToken_ChooseTheTokenTable
  $7455,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$AD2D.
  $7458,$03 Read *#R$B701.
  $745B,$07 Jump to #R$7465 if *#R$B701 and *#R$70DF are both zero.
  $7462,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$AD35.
@ $7465 label=Message_ChooseAClassToken_ShiftTheClassToken
  $7465,$06 Shift the class token two places.
  $746B,$02 Save #REGde and keep #REGa in #REGe.
  $746D,$02 The class-token offset's high byte is zero.
  $746F,$01 Add the class index to the token table.
  $7470,$01 Read the low byte of #R$AD35.
  $7471,$01 Step to the high byte of the token entry.
  $7472,$01 Read the high byte of #R$AD35.
  $7473,$03 Call #R$74C1 to print a dictionary token.
  $7476,$01 Restore #REGde from the stack.
  $7477,$01 Return.

c $7478 Message: Print The Class Token
@ $7478 label=Message_PrintTheClassToken
E $7478 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$A80E }
. LIST#
D $7478 Prints the class token for the noun being named. When no noun is selected it returns the
. token at #R$AD35.
  $7478,$04 Read which noun is being named.
  $747C,$04 Call #R$743F if #REGa is non-zero.
  $7480,$02 Take the high byte of the saved word.
  $7482,$02,b$01 Keep the low four bits of the class token.
  $7484,$01 Store that class token.
  $7485,$03 Jump to #R$74C1 to print a dictionary token.

c $7488 Message: Print The Fallback Token
@ $7488 label=Message_PrintTheFallbackToken
E $7488 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$A82F }
. LIST#
D $7488 Prints #TOKEN($0AE3) when the class token at #R$AD35 is #N$FF. Any other token goes back to
. naming the object.
  $7488,$05 Jump back to #R$73D6 while the class token is not #N$FF.
  $748D,$03 Use the token #TOKEN($0AE3).
  $7490,$03 Jump to #R$74C1 to print a dictionary token.

c $7493 Get Common Word
@ $7493 label=GetCommonWord
E $7493 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$A840 }
. LIST#
D $7493 Looks up one of the common words used by the message printer.
R $7493 A Letter reference
R $7493 O:DE Common word address
  $7493,$02 Treat a letter at #N$60 or above as a common word.
  $7495,$03 Create offset for the dictionary look-up.
  $7498,$03 Point #REGhl at common Words.
  $749B,$02 Add to base address at #R$AD3D so that pointer is at the referenced word.
  $749D,$01 Read the low byte of #R$AD3D.
  $749E,$01 Step to the next letter.
  $749F,$01 Read the next byte of the text.
  $74A0,$02 Add #N$50 to #REGa and store the result in #REGd.
  $74A2,$01 Keep the class token plus #N$50.
  $74A3,$03 Jump back to #R$7311 to print a dictionary token.

g $74A6 Output Buffer
@ $74A6 label=OutputBuffer
D $74A6 Read by #R$74C1.
  $74A6,$14

c $74BA Print Action
@ $74BA label=PrintAction
E $74BA Continues on to #R$74C1 to print.
E $74BA View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$A880 }
. LIST#
D $74BA Prints the name of an action from the action table.
R $74BA HL Pointer to an action in the #R$AB53(actions table)
R $74BA O:HL Pointer is incremented twice
R $74BA O:DE A word token
M $74BA,$07 Store the token in #REGde, keeping only bits 0-3 of the high byte.
  $74BA,$04
  $74BE,$02,b$01
  $74C0,$01

c $74C1 Print Token
@ $74C1 label=PrintToken
D $74C1 Prints a dictionary token, expanding letters until the dictionary word ends.
R $74C1 DE The token.
  $74C1,$01 Take the token's high byte.
  $74C2,$02,b$01 Keep its low nibble (bits 8-11 of the token).
  $74C4,$01 Merge in the low byte.
  $74C5,$01 Return if bits 0-11 of the token are all zero.
  $74C6,$04 Save the text pointer, #REGbc and the token, and keep the class in #REGc.
  $74CA,$01 Take the token's high byte again.
  $74CB,$03 Keep bits 8-11 of the token in #REGd.
  $74CE,$04 Add the token index to the pointer.
  $74D2,$03 Point #REGde at output buffer.
  $74D5,$03 Save the dictionary address and start the letter count at zero.
@ $74D8 label=PrintToken_Loop
  $74D8,$01 Read the next byte of the dictionary word.
  $74D9,$04 Jump to #R$74F9 if the low five bits of this dictionary byte are zero.
  $74DD,$01 Count one more character of the message.
  $74DE,$02 Add #N$60 to #REGa to convert to ASCII.
  $74E0,$01 Store the result in the #R$74A6(output buffer).
  $74E1,$01 Increase the #R$74A6(output buffer) pointer by one.
  $74E2,$02 Check if this is the last character in it.
  $74E4,$01 Increase the character pointer reference by one.
  $74E5,$02 If this is not the last character in it, loop back to #R$74D8 to continue processing.
N $74E7 Check minimum length.
  $74E7,$01 Copy the count in #REGb into #REGa.
  $74E8,$04 If the number of characters is only #N$02 then jump back to #R$74D8 to continue processing.
  $74EC,$04 If it is not #N($03), then jump to #R$74F9.
  $74F0,$07 Otherwise fetch the second character again and test its bit 7.
  $74F7,$02 Loop back to #R$74D8 to continue processing if bit 7 of the second character is set.
@ $74F9 label=PrintToken_ChooseTheSubject
  $74F9,$08 Jump to #R$7530 if the high nibble of the result is #N$50.
  $7501,$04 Jump to #R$7512 if the high nibble of the result is #N$40.
  $7505,$07 Take the current object, and jump to #R$750F if the high nibble of the result is #N$10.
  $750C,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
@ $750F label=PrintToken_TestForThePlayer
  $750F,$03 Jump to #R$7530 if the subject in #REGa is the player (id #N$00).
@ $7512 label=PrintToken_ExpandTheVerbEnding
  $7512,$05 Jump to #R$7530 if the token index is not a class token.
  $7517,$01 Step to the next byte of the token index.
  $7518,$01 Read the token index.
  $7519,$05 Shift the top three bits down by three places. That index, times four, selects an ending in #R$B71F.
  $751E,$01 The shifted index is the low byte of the pointer.
  $751F,$02 The pointer's high byte is zero.
  $7521,$04 Add the verb-ending table at #R$B71F.
  $7525,$02 Copy at most #N$04 bytes of the ending.
@ $7527 label=PrintToken_CopyTheEnding
  $7527,$04 Jump to #R$7530 if the token index is zero.
  $752B,$01 Step to the next byte of the token index.
  $752C,$02 Store it at #R$74A6 and step on.
  $752E,$02 Loop back to #R$7527 until #N$04 runs out.
@ $7530 label=PrintToken_MeasureTheOutput
  $7530,$03 Point #REGhl at output buffer.
  $7533,$01 Swap the print pointer with the start of the output buffer.
  $7534,$01 Clear carry before measuring how far the print pointer has moved.
  $7535,$02 See how far the pointer is past #R$74A6.
  $7537,$04 Keep the length just measured, and read the action countdown.
  $753B,$01 See whether the action countdown is zero.
  $753C,$03 Jump to #R$7543 if the action countdown is not zero.
  $753F,$03 Read the character flag.
  $7542,$01 See whether the character flag is zero.
@ $7543 label=PrintToken_PrintASpace
  $7543,$05 Take #N($20), and call #R$858B to print it if the action countdown or the character flag is not zero.
  $7548,$06 Take the characters left on the line from #R$85B3, and jump to #R$7551 if the action countdown is not zero.
  $754E,$03 Read the room left on the line at #R$869B.
@ $7551 label=PrintToken_FitOnTheLine
  $7551,$06 Jump to #R$755C if #REGa is not below the character count. #REGa is *#R$85B3 when the action countdown is not zero, and the room left on the line otherwise.
  $7557,$01 Save the capitalise-the-next-letter flag.
N $7558 Print a newline.
  $7558,$03 Call #R$8583 to print a newline.
  $755B,$01 Put the capitalise-the-next-letter flag back as it was before the newline.
@ $755C label=PrintToken_TestTheClassNibble
  $755C,$0A Take #N$01 and jump to #R$7567 unless the high nibble of #REGd is #N$70.
  $7566,$01 Set the capitalise-the-next-letter flag.
@ $7567 label=PrintToken_Print
  $7567,$03 Point #REGhl at output buffer.
@ $756A label=PrintToken_Print_Loop
  $756A,$01 Fetch the next character from the output buffer.
  $756B,$03 Call #R$858B to print one character.
  $756E,$01 Increase the output buffer pointer by one.
  $756F,$02 Decrease counter by one and loop back to #R$756A until counter is zero.
  $7571,$02 Restore #REGbc and #REGhl from the stack.
  $7573,$01 Return.

c $7574 Inglish: Prepare The Clause
@ $7574 label=InglishPrepareTheClause
D $7574 Prepares a clause in the parser workspace before the grammar is
. tried.
B $7574,$06
B $757A,$01
B $757B,$0A
  $7585,$04 Point #REGiy at the parser workspace.
  $7589,$01 Clear the conjunction count.
  $758A,$03 Write #REGa to #R$B71B.
  $758D,$03 Call #R$785F to clear the matcher phase.
  $7590,$03 Write #N$00 to #R$B706.
  $7593,$02 The class marker starts at #N$FF.
  $7595,$03 Read the matcher path flag.
  $7598,$01 See whether the matcher path flag is zero.
  $7599,$02 Class #N$A0 is used when a game is already in progress.
  $759B,$03 If #REGa is not zero jump to #R$7614.
  $759E,$02 Class #N$C0 is used for a fresh game.
  $75A0,$04 Write #N$00 to #R$757A.
  $75A4,$01 Take the class marker #N$FF into #REGa.
  $75A5,$02 Merge in #N$C7.
  $75A7,$01 Keep the merged class flags in #REGe.
N $75A8 Test the matcher phase.
  $75A8,$07 Jump to #R$75B1 if *#R$B719 is not #N$02.
  $75AF,$02 Clear bit 1.
@ $75B1 label=InglishPrepareTheClause_BlankAParserSlot
  $75B1,$03 Call #R$7864 to blank a parser slot.
  $75B4,$08 Write #N$00 to #N$0A bytes, starting from #R$757B with #R$70E2.
  $75BC,$02 Set bit 4.
  $75BE,$03 Call #R$7873 to read the next grammar byte.
  $75C1,$01 Save #REGde.
  $75C2,$03 Turn the grammar byte into a jump-table offset.
  $75C5,$01 Keep the jump-table offset in #REGe.
  $75C6,$02 The grammar-table offset's high byte is zero.
  $75C8,$04 Point #REGhl at entry #REGde of #R$75D2.
  $75CC,$01 Read the low byte of the handler.
  $75CD,$01 Step to the high byte of the handler.
  $75CE,$01 Read the high byte of the handler.
  $75CF,$01 Point #REGhl at the handler.
  $75D0,$02 Jump to the action handler the caller addressed.
@ $75D2 label=JumpTable
W $75D2,$1A,$02

c $75EC Inglish: Count A Conjunction
@ $75EC label=InglishCountAConjunction
D $75EC Counts a conjunction in the clause so a later sentence can be
. printed.
  $75EC,$03 Read *#R$B71B.
  $75EF,$01 Step the conjunction count down by one.
  $75F0,$01 Return.

c $75F1 Inglish: Test The Conjunction Count
@ $75F1 label=Inglish_TestTheConjunctionCount
D $75F1 Reads the conjunction count at #R$B71B and returns with the zero flag set when the
. clause has no conjunction left.
  $75F1,$03 Read *#R$B71B.
  $75F4,$01 See whether the conjunction count is zero.
  $75F5,$01 Return.

c $75F6 Inglish: Clear The Repeat Flag
@ $75F6 label=InglishClearTheRepeatFlag
D $75F6 Clears the flag that would make the matcher repeat a word.
. Reached through the jump table at #R$75D2, dispatched from #R$75D0.
  $75F6,$04 Clear *#R$B705.

c $75FA Inglish: Match One Word
@ $75FA label=InglishMatchOneWord
E $75FA View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$AD57 }
. LIST#
D $75FA Matches one word of the clause against the dictionary.
. Reached through the jump table at #R$75D2, dispatched from #R$75D0.
  $75FA,$04 Jump to #R$7614 if bit 1 of the grammar flag is clear.
N $75FE Test the replay path.
  $75FE,$06 Jump to #R$7614 if *#R$B706 is not zero.
N $7604 Test the class being matched.
  $7604,$07 Jump to #R$760E if *#R$B6DE is not #N$C0.
  $760B,$03 Jump to #R$75EC when this letter matches the dictionary.
@ $760E label=InglishMatchOneWord_TestTheConjunction
  $760E,$03 Call #R$75F1 to test the conjunction count.
  $7611,$03 Jump to #R$792F if #R$75F1 found no conjunction left.
@ $7614 label=InglishMatchOneWord_MarkTheClauseByte
  $7614,$03 Read *#R$B719.
  $7617,$03 Jump to #R$761E unless the matcher phase is #N$01.
  $761A,$04 Mark the clause byte at offset 1 so bit 7 is on.
@ $761E label=InglishMatchOneWord_PointAtReplayPath
  $761E,$07 Point #REGhl at #R$B706 and jump to #R$7629 unless the matcher phase was #N$02.
  $7625,$04 Jump to #R$762A if the class in #REGd is #N$A0.
@ $7629 label=InglishMatchOneWord_CountOneMoreInTheReplayPath
  $7629,$01 Count one more in the replay path.
@ $762A label=InglishMatchOneWord_ExchangeTheTwoRecords
  $762A,$03 Call #R$7893 to exchange the two records.
  $762D,$05 Jump back to #R$75A0 if the class is #N$A0.
N $7632 Test the fresh-game flag.
  $7632,$06 Jump to #R$7682 if *#R$B71A is zero.
  $7638,$02 Save #REGde and keep #REGa in #REGe.
  $763A,$02 The word-class offset's high byte is zero.
  $763C,$04 Index the parser workspace at #R$B9C8.
  $7640,$06 Point #REGiy at the first parser slot.
  $7646,$03 Read the name token from #REGiy.
  $7649,$08 Jump to #R$7668 if the name token at #REGiy is zero.
  $7651,$03 Read the name token from #REGiy.
  $7654,$03 Jump to #R$765F if the name token at #REGiy+8 does not match.
  $7657,$03 Read the name token's high byte from #REGiy.
  $765A,$05 Jump to #R$7668 if the high byte of that word matches too.
@ $765F label=InglishMatchOneWord_TestTheNameWord
  $765F,$03 Read byte +#N$12 from #REGiy.
  $7662,$03 Test whether the word at offset #N$12 is zero.
  $7665,$03 Point #REGde at the third parser slot.
@ $7668 label=InglishMatchOneWord_TestTheNameToken
  $7668,$04 Jump to #R$7681 if the name token just tested is not zero.
  $766C,$01 Swap the word pointer with the parser slot.
  $766D,$02 Count #N$03 passes.
@ $766F label=InglishMatchOneWord_Loop
  $766F,$06 Jump to #R$7677 unless this grammar pair is empty.
  $7675,$02 Copy the workspace byte into the empty slot pair.
@ $7677 label=InglishMatchOneWord_StepThePairPointers
  $7677,$04 Step #REGhl and #REGde on, and jump to #R$767D if the pair was not empty.
  $767B,$02 Copy the second workspace byte into the slot.
@ $767D label=InglishMatchOneWord_StepToTheNextParserByte
  $767D,$01 Step to the next byte of the parser slot.
  $767E,$01 Step to the next byte of the parser workspace.
  $767F,$02 Loop back to #R$766F until #N$03 runs out.
@ $7681 label=InglishMatchOneWord_PopTheMatchPointer
  $7681,$07 Point at the location table.
@ $7682 label=InglishMatchOneWord_PointAtTheLocationTable
  $7688,$03 Read *#R$B706.
  $768B,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $768C,$04 Call #R$7892 to drop one from the character count and swap the two records.
@ $7690 label=InglishMatchOneWord_ExchangeForTheReplay
  $7690,$04 Jump to #R$76A4 if the replay path is zero.
  $7694,$03 Call #R$7892 to exchange the two records.
  $7697,$03 Read the clause byte at offset 1.
  $769A,$08 Keep bits 0-6 of this clause byte, and call #R$78B7 if it and the byte at #REGiy are both zero.
  $76A2,$02 Jump back to #R$7690 to read the next word.
@ $76A4 label=InglishMatchOneWord_SkipToThePreviousParserSlot
  $76A4,$08 Call #R$788C to drop one from the character count and skip to the previous parser slot.
@ $76AC label=InglishMatchOneWord_TestWhetherTheReplayPathIsZero
  $76AC,$05 Jump to #R$76DE if the replay path is zero.
  $76B1,$03 Call #R$788C to step to the previous slot.
  $76B4,$03 Read byte +#N$12 from #REGiy.
  $76B7,$05 Jump back to #R$76AC if the name token at offset #N$12 is not zero.
  $76BC,$03 Read the clause byte at offset 0.
  $76BF,$05 Jump back to #R$76AC if the clause byte at offset 0 is not byte 0 of this record.
  $76C4,$03 Read the clause byte at offset 1.
  $76C7,$05 Jump back to #R$76AC if the clause byte at offset 1 is not byte 1 of this record.
  $76CC,$03 Read clause byte #N$0E of the other record.
  $76CF,$05 Jump back to #R$76AC if bytes #N$0E and #N$0F of this record are both zero.
  $76D4,$01 Switch to the saved registers for this phrase.
  $76D5,$03 The copy starts #N$0E bytes into the record.
  $76D8,$03 Call #R$78EA to copy the grammar bytes into the clause.
  $76DB,$03 Jump back to #R$76AC to read the next word.
@ $76DE label=InglishMatchOneWord_ReadTheLineReadFlag
  $76DE,$05 Read *#R$B705.
  $76E3,$02 Return if the line-read flag is zero.
  $76E5,$03 Call #R$75F1 to test the conjunction count.
  $76E8,$01 Return if no conjunction is left.
  $76E9,$03 Jump to #R$75A0 to reset the parser flags and blank the parser slot.

c $76EC Inglish: Reject A Class
@ $76EC label=InglishRejectAClass
D $76EC Rejects a word class that does not fit the grammar.
. Reached through the jump table at #R$75D2, dispatched from #R$75D0.
  $76EC,$04 Jump to #R$7731 if bit 1 of the grammar flag is set.
  $76F0,$02 Clear #REGd before the class bits in #REGe are tested.

c $76F2 Inglish: Accept The Clause
@ $76F2 label=InglishAcceptTheClause
D $76F2 Accepts the clause once the grammar matches.
. Reached through the jump table at #R$75D2, dispatched from #R$75D0.
  $76F2,$05 Jump to #R$7929 if bit 2 of the grammar flag is clear.
N $76F7 Test the matcher phase.
  $76F7,$07 Jump to #R$7701 if *#R$B719 is not #N$02.
  $76FE,$03 Call #R$7858 to clear the matcher phase.
@ $7701 label=InglishAcceptTheClause_StoreThePair
  $7701,$02 Clear the flags so bit 2 is off.
  $7703,$02 Two bytes of the accepted pair are stored.
  $7705,$03 Call #R$7918 to store the pair of clause bytes.
  $7708,$03 Jump back to #R$75B4 to clear the matcher scratch.

c $770B Inglish: Save The Match
@ $770B label=InglishSaveTheMatch
E $770B View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$ACCC }
. LIST#
D $770B Saves the bytes of a match so it can be restored if a later try
. fails.
. Reached through the jump table at #R$75D2, dispatched from #R$75D0.
  $770B,$03 Clear the flags so bit 3 is off.
@ $770E label=InglishSaveTheMatch_Loop
  $770E,$03 Call #R$7873 to read the next grammar byte.
  $7711,$04 Jump back to #R$770E if the grammar byte is #N$A0.
  $7715,$01 Step back one byte through the text.
  $7716,$01 Step back one byte through the text.
  $7717,$03 Remember the text pointer at #R$7574.
  $771A,$03 Update *#R$B6DC.
  $771D,$02 Take the low byte of the saved word.
  $771F,$03 Store the result at #N$7576.
  $7722,$04 Store #REGiy at #N$7577.
  $7726,$03 Read *#R$B706.
  $7729,$03 Store the result at #N$7579.
  $772C,$03 Jump to #R$75FA to match one word.

c $772F Inglish: Clear The Match Bit
@ $772F label=InglishClearTheMatchBit
D $772F Clears the bit that says a class matched.
. Reached through the jump table at #R$75D2, dispatched from #R$75D0.
  $772F,$02 Clear the flags so bit 0 is off.
  $7731,$02 Class #N$30 selects the match-bit test.

c $7733 Inglish: Restore The Match
@ $7733 label=InglishRestoreTheMatch
D $7733 Restores a saved match and tries the clause again.
. Reached through the jump table at #R$75D2, dispatched from #R$75D0.
  $7733,$04 Jump to #R$7755 if bit 3 of the grammar flag is set.
  $7737,$03 Call #R$75F1 to test the conjunction count.
  $773A,$02 Jump to #R$7755 if a conjunction is still left.
  $773C,$03 Read the pointer stored at #R$7574.
  $773F,$03 Update *#R$B6DC.
  $7742,$02 The restored class is #N$B0.
  $7744,$03 Read the saved grammar flag.
  $7747,$01 Keep the saved grammar flag.
  $7748,$03 Read the saved replay path.
  $774B,$03 Update *#R$B706.
  $774E,$04 Read the saved matcher pointer.
  $7752,$03 Jump to #R$75FA to match one word.
@ $7755 label=InglishRestoreTheMatch_TestTheGrammarFlag
  $7755,$03 Call #R$785F to clear the matcher phase.
  $7758,$05 Jump to #R$7929 if bit 1 of the saved grammar flag is clear.
  $775D,$04 Jump to #R$7767 if the match bit of the saved grammar flag is clear.
@ $7761 label=InglishRestoreTheMatch_StoreThePairOfClauseBytes
  $7761,$03 Call #R$7914 to store the pair of clause bytes.
  $7764,$03 Jump back to #R$75B4 to clear the matcher scratch.
@ $7767 label=InglishRestoreTheMatch_SaveTheTokenPointer
  $7767,$06 Save #REGde, #REGbc and the token pointer at #R$B6DC on the stack.
@ $776D label=InglishRestoreTheMatch_Loop
  $776D,$03 Call #R$7873 to read the next grammar byte.
  $7770,$04 Jump back to #R$776D if the grammar byte is zero.
  $7774,$06 Set bit 0 of #REGe, and jump to #R$7782 if the grammar byte is #N$20.
  $777A,$04 Update *#R$B6DC.
  $777E,$04 Jump back to #R$7761 to store the next pair of clause bytes.
@ $7782 label=InglishRestoreTheMatch_StoreTheNextPair
  $7782,$03 Call #R$7914 to store the pair of clause bytes.
  $7785,$05 Drop one saved value, restore #REGbc and #REGhl, and force #REGl to #N$02.
  $778A,$03 Call #R$7918 to store the pair of clause bytes.
  $778D,$03 Jump back to #R$75B4 to clear the matcher scratch.

c $7790 Inglish: Try The Next Class
@ $7790 label=InglishTryTheNextClass
D $7790 Tries the next word class when this one did not match.
. Reached through the jump table at #R$75D2, dispatched from #R$75D0.
  $7790,$02 Clear the flags so bit 4 is off.
  $7792,$03 Jump back to #R$75BE to follow the handler addressed here.

c $7795 Inglish: Check The Word Class
@ $7795 label=InglishCheckTheWordClass
D $7795 Checks that the typed word's class is one the grammar allows here.
. Reached through the jump table at #R$75D2, dispatched from #R$75D0.
  $7795,$04 Jump to #R$77A0 if bit 1 of the grammar flag is clear.
N $7799 Test the class being matched.
  $7799,$07 Jump to #R$7731 if *#R$B6DE is #N$A0.
@ $77A0 label=InglishCheckTheWordClass_SetTheClassToCheck
  $77A0,$02 The class being checked is #N$70.

c $77A2 Inglish: Walk The Grammar
@ $77A2 label=InglishWalkTheGrammar
D $77A2 Walks one step of the grammar, including groups in brackets.
. Reached through the jump table at #R$75D2, dispatched from #R$75D0.
  $77A2,$03 Call #R$781C to count another grammar try.
@ $77A5 label=InglishWalkTheGrammar_Loop
  $77A5,$03 Call #R$7873 to read the next grammar byte.
  $77A8,$04 Jump back to #R$77A2 if the grammar byte is #N$70.
  $77AC,$04 Jump to #R$77B9 unless the grammar byte is #N$80.
  $77B0,$05 Call #R$7924 if bit 4 of the grammar flag is clear.
  $77B5,$02 Clear the flags so bit 4 is off.
  $77B7,$02 Jump back to #R$77A5 to run the grammar.

c $77B9 Inglish: Keep The Unknown Word
@ $77B9 label=Inglish_KeepTheUnknownWord
D $77B9 Keeps the unknown word at #R$B6DA as the token pointer while the matcher counts at #N$60
. and #N$50 are still live.
  $77B9,$04 Jump to #R$77C9 if the grammar byte is #N$60.
  $77BD,$04 Jump to #R$77D1 if the grammar byte is #N$50.
  $77C1,$03 Read *#R$B6DA.
  $77C4,$03 Update *#R$B6DC.
  $77C7,$02 Jump to #R$77D7 to keep a count of #N$50.

c $77C9 Inglish: Step Over A Group
@ $77C9 label=InglishStepOverAGroup
D $77C9 Skips a grammar group that does not apply to this clause.
. Reached through the jump table at #R$75D2, dispatched from #R$75D0.
  $77C9,$03 Call #R$7809 to patch the matcher word.
  $77CC,$03 Call #R$7873 to read the next grammar byte.
  $77CF,$02 Jump back to #R$77B9 to read the next word.

c $77D1 Inglish: Run The Grammar
@ $77D1 label=InglishRunTheGrammar
D $77D1 Runs the grammar over the tokens and, when they match, performs the
. action.
. Reached through the jump table at #R$75D2, dispatched from #R$75D0.
  $77D1,$03 Point #REGhl at the matcher patch at #N$757F.
  $77D4,$01 Store the saved byte in the matcher patch.
  $77D5,$01 Step to the next byte of the matcher patch.
  $77D6,$01 Store the saved byte in the matcher patch.
  $77D7,$02 The class being run is #N$50.
N $77D9 Test the matcher phase.
  $77D9,$07 Jump to #R$7802 if *#R$B719 is not #N$02.
  $77E0,$07 Jump to #R$77FF if the word at #R$757B is not zero.
  $77E7,$06 Jump to #R$77F5 if bit 6 of the clause byte at offset #N$19 is set.
  $77ED,$05 Step back one parser record.
  $77F2,$03 Call #R$7864 to blank a parser slot.
@ $77F5 label=InglishRunTheGrammar_CopyFourGrammarBytes
  $77F5,$03 Call #R$7850 to copy four grammar bytes.
  $77F8,$04 Set the clause byte at offset 1 to #N$40.
  $77FC,$03 Jump back to #R$75B4 to clear the matcher scratch.
@ $77FF label=InglishRunTheGrammar_ClearTheMatcherPhase
  $77FF,$03 Call #R$7858 to clear the matcher phase.
@ $7802 label=InglishRunTheGrammar_FollowTheGrammarFlag
  $7802,$03 Call #R$782B to follow the grammar flag.
  $7805,$03 Jump back to #R$75B4. #R$782B always returns zero, so this jump is always taken.
  $7808,$01 Return.

c $7809 Inglish: Patch The Matcher Word
@ $7809 label=Inglish_PatchTheMatcherWord
E $7809 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$AC94 }
. LIST#
D $7809 Patches the matcher word at #N$7581 with the saved bytes. When those bytes do not match,
. the player is asked "what?".
  $7809,$03 Point #REGhl at the patched matcher word at #N$7581.
@ $780C label=Inglish_PatchTheMatcherWord_Loop
  $780C,$05 Jump to #R$7818 if this grammar pair is empty.
  $7811,$07 Jump to #R$7924 unless the next grammar pair is empty.
@ $7818 label=Inglish_PatchTheMatcherWord_StoreTheSavedBytes
  $7818,$01 Store the saved byte in the patched matcher word.
  $7819,$01 Step back one byte through the patched matcher word.
  $781A,$01 Store the saved byte in the patched matcher word.
  $781B,$01 Return.

c $781C Inglish: Count Another Grammar Try
@ $781C label=Inglish_CountAnotherGrammarTry
D $781C Counts another grammar try at #R$757A, up to three, then returns to reading the next
. word.
  $781C,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$757A.
  $781F,$01 Count one more in the grammar-try count.
  $7820,$06 Jump to #R$7924 if the grammar-try count is not below #N$03.
  $7826,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$757B.
  $7829,$02 Jump back to #R$780C to read the next word.

c $782B Inglish: Follow The Grammar Flag
@ $782B label=Inglish_FollowTheGrammarFlag
E $782B View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$ACA6 }
. LIST#
D $782B Follows the grammar flag in #REGe. Bit 6 copies four grammar bytes, bit 7 copies ten,
. and neither asks "what?" and reports no match.
  $782B,$04 Jump to #R$7850 if bit 6 of the grammar flag is set.
  $782F,$04 Jump to #R$7838 if bit 7 of the grammar flag is set.
  $7833,$03 Call #R$7924 to ask "what ?" when a conjunction is still open.
  $7836,$01 Return zero.
  $7837,$01 Return.

c $7838 Inglish: Copy Ten Grammar Bytes
@ $7838 label=Inglish_CopyTenGrammarBytes
D $7838 Clears bit 7 of the grammar flag and copies ten bytes from #N$0E into the clause, then
. reports no match.
  $7838,$02 Clear the flags so bit 7 is off.
  $783A,$04 The ten grammar bytes start #N$0E bytes into the record.
@ $783E label=Inglish_CopyTenGrammarBytes_PointAtTheClause
  $783E,$05 Save #REGbc and point #REGhl at the clause plus the offset in #REGde.
  $7843,$01 Point #REGde at those bytes.
  $7844,$08 Copy the prepared bytes.
  $784C,$03 Restore the saved registers from the stack and clear #REGa.
  $784F,$01 Return.

c $7850 Inglish: Copy Four Grammar Bytes
@ $7850 label=Inglish_CopyFourGrammarBytes
D $7850 Clears bit 6 of the grammar flag and copies four bytes into the record, then joins the
. ten-byte copy.
  $7850,$02 Clear the flags so bit 6 is off.
  $7852,$04 The copy starts #N$04 bytes into the record.
  $7856,$02 Jump back to #R$783E to copy the ten grammar bytes.

c $7858 Inglish: Clear The Matcher Phase
@ $7858 label=Inglish_ClearTheMatcherPhase
D $7858 Skips a location marked in the clause and clears the matcher phase at #R$B719.
  $7858,$03 Call #R$78A5 to step past a clause whose location is marked.
  $785B,$04 Use the saved address as the parser pointer.
  $785F,$04 Clear *#R$B719.
  $7863,$01 Return.

c $7864 Inglish: Blank A Parser Slot
@ $7864 label=Inglish_BlankAParserSlot
D $7864 Blanks one #N$18-byte parser slot and stores the clause bytes at offsets -24 and -23.
  $7864,$05 Count #N$18 passes.
N $7869 Fill the buffer at #REGhl.
  $7869,$03 Call #R$70E2 to fill a run of bytes.
  $786C,$03 Clear byte 0 of the previous clause record.
  $786F,$03 Clear byte 1 of the previous clause record.
  $7872,$01 Return.

c $7873 Inglish: Read The Next Grammar Byte
@ $7873 label=Inglish_ReadTheNextGrammarByte
E $7873 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$AE7F }
. LIST#
D $7873 Reads the next grammar byte from the token pointer, splits it into nibbles, and stores
. the token pointer, the unknown word and the class.
  $7873,$03 Read *#R$B6DC.
  $7876,$03 Update *#R$B6DA.
  $7879,$01 Take the class into #REGa.
  $787A,$03 Update *#R$B6DE.
  $787D,$01 Read the token.
  $787E,$03 Keep the low nibble of the token.
  $7881,$01 Read the token.
  $7882,$03 Keep the high nibble of the token.
  $7885,$01 Step to the next byte of the token pointer.
  $7886,$01 Read a byte from the pointer into #REGc.
  $7887,$01 Step to the next byte of the token pointer.
  $7888,$03 Update *#R$B6DC.
  $788B,$01 Return.

c $788C Inglish: Step To The Previous Slot
@ $788C label=Inglish_StepToThePreviousSlot
D $788C Drops the character count and skips to the previous parser slot.
  $788C,$01 Drop one from the character count.
  $788D,$03 Call #R$78A5 to step past a clause whose location is marked.
  $7890,$02 Jump to #R$7896 to exchange the clause records.

c $7892 Inglish: Exchange The Two Records
@ $7892 label=Inglish_ExchangeTheTwoRecords
D $7892 Drops the character count and exchanges the two clause records.
  $7892,$01 Drop one from the character count.
  $7893,$03 Call #R$789F to address the previous slot.
@ $7896 label=Inglish_ExchangeTheTwoRecords_SwapRecordPointers
  $7896,$08 Swap #REGix and #REGiy.
  $789E,$01 Return.

c $789F Inglish: Address The Previous Slot
@ $789F label=Inglish_AddressThePreviousSlot
D $789F Addresses the previous #N$18-byte parser slot.
  $789F,$04 Step back one #N$18-byte parser record.
  $78A3,$02 Jump to #R$78A9 to walk the previous parser slot.

c $78A5 Inglish: Skip A Marked Location
@ $78A5 label=Inglish_SkipAMarkedLocation
D $78A5 Steps through clause records of #N$18 bytes, skipping any whose location has bit 6 set,
. and returns the caller's pointer.
  $78A5,$04 Each clause record is #N$18 bytes.
@ $78A9 label=Inglish_SkipAMarkedLocation_CopyTheParserPointer
  $78A9,$04 Copy the parser pointer into #REGix.
@ $78AD label=Inglish_SkipAMarkedLocation_SkipAMarkedSlot
  $78AD,$08 Jump back to #R$78AD if bit 6 of the clause byte at offset 1 is set.
  $78B5,$01 Restore #REGde from the stack.
  $78B6,$01 Return.

c $78B7 Inglish: Copy Two Bytes From The Other Clause
@ $78B7 label=Inglish_CopyTwoBytesFromTheOtherClause
E $78B7 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$AEF1 }
. LIST#
D $78B7 Copies byte 0 and byte 1 from the other clause record into this clause. When bit 7 of the
. grammar flag is set, the name bytes are copied too.
  $78B7,$01 Switch to the saved registers for this phrase.
  $78B8,$03 Read byte 1 of the other clause record.
  $78BB,$05 OR it, with bit 7 clear, into the clause byte at offset 1.
  $78C0,$03 Update the clause byte at offset 1.
  $78C3,$03 Read byte 0 of the other clause record.
  $78C6,$03 Update the clause byte at offset 0.
  $78C9,$01 Switch to the saved registers for this phrase.
  $78CA,$03 Read byte +#N$1C from #REGiy.
  $78CD,$04 Return if bytes #N$1C and #N$1D are both zero.
  $78D1,$03 Return if bit 7 of the grammar flag is clear.
  $78D4,$01 Switch to the saved registers for this phrase.
  $78D5,$07 Step the pointer on by #N$12 bytes.
  $78DC,$04 Step the copy source back #N$0A bytes.
  $78E0,$01 Add #N$FFF6 to the token pointer.
  $78E1,$06 Copy the prepared bytes.
  $78E7,$03 The copy starts #N$04 bytes into the clause.
  $78EA,$03 Call #R$78FF to copy the ten grammar bytes.
  $78ED,$03 Read the clause byte at offset 2.
  $78F0,$09 Call #R$7903 to copy two clause bytes when the next clause byte is zero.
  $78F9,$01 Switch to the saved registers for this phrase.
  $78FA,$01 Return.
  $78FB,$02 Copy #N$06 bytes of the clause.
  $78FD,$02 Jump to #R$7905 to copy them.

c $78FF Inglish: Copy With A Count Of Ten
@ $78FF label=Inglish_CopyWithACountOfTen
E $78FF View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$AF52 }
. LIST#
D $78FF Sets the copy length to ten and joins the two-byte copy.
  $78FF,$02 Copy #N$0A bytes of the clause.
  $7901,$02 Jump to #R$7905 to copy them.

c $7903 Inglish: Copy With A Count Of Two
@ $7903 label=Inglish_CopyWithACountOfTwo
E $7903 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$AF56 }
. LIST#
D $7903 Copies two bytes, then the prepared bytes, from the token pointer into the clause.
  $7903,$02 Copy the two clause bytes.
@ $7905 label=Inglish_CopyWithACountOfTwo_PointAtTheClause
  $7905,$04 Point #REGhl at #REGiy plus the offset in #REGde.
  $7909,$05 Save that address and point #REGhl at #REGix plus the offset in #REGde.
  $790E,$05 Copy the prepared bytes.
  $7913,$01 Return.

c $7914 Inglish: Store The Byte Pair
@ $7914 label=Inglish_StoreTheBytePair
D $7914 Stores a pair of saved bytes at the token pointer and clears bit 1 of the flags.
  $7914,$02 Clear the flags so bit 1 is off.
  $7916,$02 The stored pair starts at zero.
  $7918,$06 Save the text pointer and take the parser pointer in #REGde.
  $791E,$01 Add the parser pointer onto the offset in #REGl.
  $791F,$01 Store the saved byte in the token pointer.
  $7920,$01 Step to the next byte of the token pointer.
  $7921,$01 Store the saved byte in the token pointer.
  $7922,$01 Restore #REGde from the stack.
  $7923,$01 Return.

c $7924 Inglish: Ask What
@ $7924 label=Inglish_AskWhat
D $7924 Prints "what ?" and sets the action countdown when the clause still has a conjunction.
. It returns at once when that count is already zero.
  $7924,$03 Call #R$75F1 to test the conjunction count.
  $7927,$02 Return if a conjunction is still left, and otherwise drop the return address.
  $7929,$03 Call #R$75F1 to test the conjunction count.
  $792C,$03 Jump to #R$75B4 if a conjunction is still left.
  $792F,$03 Point at the message "what ?[0x14]" (#R$AD9F).
  $7932,$05 Write #N$01 (the countdown running) to *#R$B701.
  $7937,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $793A,$02 Clear the zero flag.
  $793C,$01 Return.

b $793D Grammar Workspace
D $793D Slots the grammar fills while a clause is matched. #R$79A9 clears the first #N$11 bytes. Nothing executes these bytes. Read by #R$79A9, #R$79B6, #R$7A14, #R$7AA1, #R$7ABA, #R$7AD8, #R$7AF5, #R$7B9E, #R$7C23, #R$7CCB, #R$7CFC, #R$7D17, #R$7D54, #R$7D74 and #R$7E7C.
. Read by #R$7C23, #R$7CFC and #R$7D74.
  $793D,$01 First byte of the match buffer. #R$79A9 blanks this and the next #N$10 bytes.
  $793E,$01 Second byte of the match buffer.
  $793F,$01 Third byte of the match buffer. The matcher tests it for zero.
  $7940,$01 Grammar marker. Compared with #N$01 while a clause is scored.
  $7941,$01 Second grammar marker, tested beside #R$7940.
  $7942,$06 First copy window. The front word is read with #R$7D86, and #R$7B19 points #REGde here.
  $7948,$06 Second copy window. #R$7B26 points #REGde here and #R$7DB7 reads the first byte.
  $794E,$02 Object pointer saved from #REGix.
  $7950,$02 Other object pointer saved from #REGix.
  $7952,$01 Phrase byte copied into #R$B71C.
  $7953,$01 Flag derived from the phrase byte.
  $7954,$01 Grammar count. #R$7D83 reads it.
  $7955,$01 Second grammar count. #R$7DB4 reads it.
  $7956,$01 Copy of the grammar marker.
  $7957,$01 Copy of the second grammar marker.
  $7958,$02 Text pointer saved from #REGhl.
  $795A,$02 Text pointer swapped with #R$795C.
  $795C,$02 The other pointer swapped with #R$795A.
  $795E,$02 Action entry saved from #REGix at the end of a match.

c $7960 Inglish: Apply A Match
@ $7960 label=InglishApplyAMatch
D $7960 Starts matching the current clause. Clears the phrase scratch byte and uses the parser workspace at #R$B9C8. A different replay path skips ahead to #R$7994.
  $7960,$04 Clear *#R$B71C.
  $7964,$0C Jump to #R$7994 if the matcher path flag is not zero.
@ $7970 label=InglishApplyAMatch_ScoreTheCurrentAction
  $7970,$03 Call #R$79B6 to score the current action.
  $7973,$02 Jump to #R$797A if #R$79B6 returned with the zero flag clear (an action was scored).
  $7975,$04 Clear *#R$B705.
  $7979,$01 Return.
@ $797A label=InglishApplyAMatch_RunTheAction
  $797A,$03 Call #R$7AED to run the action and see whether its message was held back.
  $797D,$03 Jump to #R$7DF5 if #R$7AED did not hold the message back.
  $7980,$05 Write #N$01 (the gate shut) to *#R$B6FA.
  $7985,$03 Call #R$712B to describe the action.
  $7988,$03 Call #R$950F to run the handler when one is present.
@ $798B label=InglishApplyAMatch_GiveEveryOtherCharacterATurn
  $798B,$03 Call #R$96B3 to give every other character a turn.
@ $798E label=InglishApplyAMatch_TestThePhraseScratchByte
N $798E Test the phrase scratch byte.
  $798E,$06 Jump to #R$7970 if *#R$B71C is not zero.
@ $7994 label=InglishApplyAMatch_UpdateTheReplayPath
  $7994,$03 Read *#R$B706.
  $7997,$01 Step the replay path down by one.
  $7998,$03 Update *#R$B706.
  $799B,$01 Return if the replay path reached zero.
  $799C,$03 Step back one clause record. Each record is #N$18 bytes.
@ $799F label=InglishApplyAMatch_SkipMarkedClause
  $799F,$08 Jump back to #R$799F if bit 6 of the clause byte at offset 1 is set.
  $79A7,$02 Jump back to #R$7970 to run the grammar.

c $79A9 Inglish: Clear The Match Buffer
@ $79A9 label=Inglish_ClearTheMatchBuffer
D $79A9 Clears the action countdown and blanks #N$11 bytes of the match buffer.
  $79A9,$04 Clear *#R$B701.
  $79AD,$03 Point #REGhl at the match buffer.
  $79B0,$02 Count #N$11 passes.
N $79B2 Fill the buffer at #REGhl.
  $79B2,$03 Call #R$70E2 to fill a run of bytes.
  $79B5,$01 Return.

c $79B6 Inglish: Score The Current Action
@ $79B6 label=Inglish_ScoreTheCurrentAction
D $79B6 Scores the current action against the action table, stores the winning action and
. object, and clears the message gate.
N $79B6 Update the second object.
  $79B6,$08 Write #N$FF to *#R$B6E9 and *#R$B6E8.
  $79BE,$03 Call #R$79A9 to clear the match buffer.
  $79C1,$03 Call #R$7B9E to save the clause window.
  $79C4,$01 Return if #R$7B9E found no match.
  $79C5,$02 The first action id is #N$01.
  $79C7,$08 Turn the action-table pointer into an offset from #R$AB53.
  $79CF,$02 Jump to #R$79D9 if the pointer is the action table.
  $79D1,$03 Each action record is #N$08 bytes.
@ $79D4 label=Inglish_ScoreTheCurrentAction_CountOnFromOne
  $79D4,$01 Count on from #N$01.
  $79D5,$02 Take #N$08 off the offset.
  $79D7,$02 Jump back to #R$79D4 until the offset is used up (each #N$08 bytes is one more action id).
@ $79D9 label=Inglish_ScoreTheCurrentAction_UpdateCurrentAction
  $79D9,$03 Update *#R$B6E7.
  $79DC,$03 Update *#R$B6E6.
  $79DF,$04 Remember this record in the grammar object pointer.
  $79E3,$03 Call #R$7B78 to split the clause flags.
  $79E6,$03 Call #R$7AA1 to note the first noun.
  $79E9,$04 Clear *#R$B6FA.
  $79ED,$03 Read *#R$B71D.
  $79F0,$04 Jump to #R$7A11 when bits 2 and 3 of the phrase scratch are clear.
  $79F4,$03 Read the saved phrase byte.
  $79F7,$03 Update *#R$B71C.
  $79FA,$01 Expose the high bit of the saved phrase byte.
  $79FB,$02 Keep only bit 0.
  $79FD,$03 Update the phrase-byte flag.
@ $7A00 label=Inglish_ScoreTheCurrentAction_ResolveTheFirstNoun
  $7A00,$03 Call #R$7A14 to resolve the first noun.
  $7A03,$03 Jump to #R$7DBC if #R$7A14 returned with the zero flag clear (a grammar marker was not #N$01).
N $7A06 Test the phrase scratch byte.
  $7A06,$06 Jump to #R$7A11 if *#R$B71C is zero.
  $7A0C,$03 Call #R$7A73 to search the previous slot.
  $7A0F,$02 Jump back to #R$7A00 if #R$7A73 found the current object in the previous parser record.
@ $7A11 label=Inglish_ScoreTheCurrentAction_ClearTheZeroFlag
  $7A11,$02 Clear the zero flag.
  $7A13,$01 Return.

c $7A14 Inglish: Resolve The First Noun
@ $7A14 label=Inglish_ResolveTheFirstNoun
D $7A14 Resolves the first noun of the clause into the current object, or counts another try in
. the third match buffer.
  $7A14,$03 Call #R$7CCB to find the first noun.
  $7A17,$02 Jump to #R$7A2E if #R$7CCB returned an object.
  $7A19,$06 Return if the grammar marker is not #N$01.
  $7A1F,$03 Read the copied grammar marker.
  $7A22,$03 Update *#R$B6E8.
  $7A25,$03 Call #R$7ABA to note the second noun.
  $7A28,$03 Call #R$7AD8 to test the scratch bits.
  $7A2B,$02 Jump to #R$7A55 if #R$7AD8 returned with the zero flag clear.
  $7A2D,$01 Return.
@ $7A2E label=Inglish_ResolveTheFirstNoun_CountTheThirdMatch
  $7A2E,$03 Point #REGhl at the third match buffer.
  $7A31,$01 Count one more in the third match buffer.
  $7A32,$03 Call #R$7AD8 to test the scratch bits.
  $7A35,$02 Jump to #R$7A50 if #R$7AD8 returned zero.
  $7A37,$03 Call #R$7ABA to note the second noun.
  $7A3A,$03 Call #R$7D17 to find the second noun.
@ $7A3D label=Inglish_ResolveTheFirstNoun_TestForASecondNoun
  $7A3D,$02 Jump back to #R$7A14 if #R$7D17 found no second noun (#N$FF).
  $7A3F,$05 Return if the phrase-byte flag reached zero.
  $7A44,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $7A47,$03 Update the copied grammar marker.
  $7A4A,$03 Point #REGhl at the grammar marker.
  $7A4D,$01 Count one more in the grammar marker.
  $7A4E,$02 Jump back to #R$7A14 to run the grammar.
@ $7A50 label=Inglish_ResolveTheFirstNoun_RunTheAction
  $7A50,$03 Call #R$7AED to run the action and see whether its message was held back.
  $7A53,$02 Jump back to #R$7A3D to read the next word.
@ $7A55 label=Inglish_ResolveTheFirstNoun_FindTheSecondNoun
  $7A55,$03 Call #R$7D17 to find the second noun.
  $7A58,$02 Jump to #R$7A67 if #R$7D17 found a second noun.
  $7A5A,$06 Return if the second grammar marker is not #N$01.
  $7A60,$03 Read the copied second marker.
  $7A63,$03 Update *#R$B6E9.
  $7A66,$01 Return.
@ $7A67 label=Inglish_ResolveTheFirstNoun_ReadTheSecondObject
  $7A67,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $7A6A,$03 Update the copied second marker.
  $7A6D,$03 Point #REGhl at the second grammar marker.
  $7A70,$01 Count one more in the second grammar marker.
  $7A71,$02 Jump back to #R$7A55 to run the grammar.

c $7A73 Inglish: Search The Previous Slot
@ $7A73 label=Inglish_SearchThePreviousSlot
E $7A73 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$B517 }
. LIST#
D $7A73 Searches the previous parser slot for an object in the same place as the player whose id matches the current
. object, and sets that object when bit 6 of the clause byte is set.
  $7A73,$07 Step back one #N$18-byte parser record.
@ $7A77 label=Inglish_SearchThePreviousSlot_StepBackOneRecord
  $7A7A,$08 Jump to #R$7A9C if bit 6 of the clause byte at offset 1 is clear.
  $7A82,$04 Start the object search one slot before #R$C063.
@ $7A86 label=Inglish_SearchThePreviousSlot_TestTheNextObject
  $7A86,$03 Use the parser workspace from here on.
  $7A89,$04 Step the pointer on by #N$08 bytes.
  $7A8D,$03 Call #R$9DD9 to test whether the object is in the same place as the player.
  $7A90,$04 Jump back to #R$7A77 if #R$9DD9 returned #N$FF.
  $7A94,$06 Jump back to #R$7A86 unless the object #R$9DD9 found is the current object.
  $7A9A,$02 Clear the zero flag.
@ $7A9C label=Inglish_SearchThePreviousSlot_RestoreAndReturn
  $7A9C,$04 Restore #REGhl, #REGde and #REGiy from the stack.
  $7AA0,$01 Return.

c $7AA1 Inglish: Note The First Noun
@ $7AA1 label=Inglish_NoteTheFirstNoun
D $7AA1 Notes the first noun in the saved object pointer when the phrase scratch byte is clear
. and the noun-print flag is set.
  $7AA1,$03 Read *#R$B71C.
  $7AA4,$02 Return if the phrase scratch byte is not zero.
  $7AA6,$03 Read *#R$B71E.
  $7AA9,$01 Expose the low bit of the clause being built.
  $7AAA,$01 Expose the low bit of the clause being built.
  $7AAB,$03 Call #R$7ACC to choose the object table.
  $7AAE,$03 Read *#R$B6FE.
  $7AB1,$04 Call #R$9E95 if the noun-print flag is not zero.
  $7AB5,$04 Remember this record in the saved object pointer.
  $7AB9,$01 Return.

c $7ABA Inglish: Note The Second Noun
@ $7ABA label=Inglish_NoteTheSecondNoun
D $7ABA Notes the second noun in the other saved object pointer when the second-noun flag is
. set.
  $7ABA,$03 Read *#R$B71E.
  $7ABD,$03 Call #R$7ACC to choose the object table.
  $7AC0,$03 Read *#R$B6FF.
  $7AC3,$04 Call #R$9E95 if the second-noun flag is not zero.
  $7AC7,$04 Remember this record in the other saved object pointer.
  $7ACB,$01 Return.

c $7ACC Inglish: Choose The Object Table
@ $7ACC label=Inglish_ChooseTheObjectTable
D $7ACC Points the object search at the slot before the object table when the second-noun flag
. is set. Otherwise it returns at once.
  $7ACC,$07 Use the record at #N$C063 and return when the low two bits of #REGa are clear.
  $7AD3,$04 Start the object search one slot before #R$C063.
  $7AD7,$01 Return.

c $7AD8 Inglish: Test The Scratch Bits
@ $7AD8 label=Inglish_TestTheScratchBits
E $7AD8 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$B387 }
. LIST#
D $7AD8 Tests bits 0, 1 and 2 of the phrase scratch byte and the second match buffer, and
. reports no match when bit 1 is set.
  $7AD8,$03 Read *#R$B71D.
  $7ADB,$03 Return if bit 2 of the phrase scratch byte is clear.
  $7ADE,$06 Return if bit 0 of the second match buffer is set.
  $7AE4,$04 Jump to #R$7AEB if bit 1 of the phrase scratch byte is set.
  $7AE8,$02 Clear the zero flag.
  $7AEA,$01 Return.
@ $7AEB label=Inglish_TestTheScratchBits_ReturnZero
  $7AEB,$01 Return zero from the scratch-bit test.
  $7AEC,$01 Return.

c $7AED Inglish: Ask Whether To Suppress
@ $7AED label=Inglish_AskWhetherToSuppress
D $7AED Runs the action handler if one is present and returns whether the message-suppressed flag is
. zero, which suppresses the sentence.
  $7AED,$03 Call #R$950F to run the handler when one is present.
  $7AF0,$03 Read the message-suppressed flag.
  $7AF3,$01 Return with the zero flag set when no message was held back.
  $7AF4,$01 Return.

c $7AF5 Inglish: Build The Action Sentence
@ $7AF5 label=Inglish_BuildTheActionSentence
D $7AF5 Builds the action sentence from the saved object pointer, the current action and both
. nouns, copying each name into the clause.
  $7AF5,$0A Save #REGhl, #REGiy, #REGix, #REGde and #REGbc, then read the object pointer at #R$794E.
  $7AFF,$04 Read *#R$B6E7.
  $7B03,$03 Call #R$70E8 to index action.
  $7B06,$03 Point #REGix at the action entry #R$70E8 returned.
  $7B09,$03 Call #R$79A9 to clear the match buffer.
  $7B0C,$03 Call #R$70F3 to pack this action entry's token classes into the clause.
  $7B0F,$03 Call #R$7B78 to split the clause flags.
  $7B12,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $7B15,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $7B16,$03 Read *#R$B6FE.
  $7B19,$03 Point #REGde at the first copy window.
  $7B1C,$03 Call #R$7B63 to copy a name into the clause.
  $7B1F,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $7B22,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $7B23,$03 Read *#R$B6FF.
  $7B26,$03 Point #REGde at the second copy window.
  $7B29,$03 Call #R$7B63 to copy a name into the clause.
  $7B2C,$03 Call #R$7AA6 to note the first noun.
  $7B2F,$04 Clear *#R$B6FA.
  $7B33,$03 Read *#R$B71D.
  $7B36,$04 Jump to #R$7B43 if either bit 2 or bit 3 of the phrase scratch is set.
  $7B3A,$03 Call #R$950F to run the handler when one is present.
  $7B3D,$03 Read the message-suppressed flag.
  $7B40,$01 See whether the message-suppressed flag is zero.
  $7B41,$02 Jump to #R$7B52 to update the message gate.
@ $7B43 label=Inglish_BuildTheActionSentence_SetThePhraseFlag
  $7B43,$05 Set the phrase flag at #R$7953.
  $7B48,$03 Call #R$7A14 to resolve the first noun.
  $7B4B,$02 Jump to #R$7B50 if #R$7A14 returned with the zero flag set.
  $7B4D,$01 Take the zero path, which still opens the message gate.
  $7B4E,$02 Jump to #R$7B52 to update the message gate.
@ $7B50 label=Inglish_BuildTheActionSentence_ClearTheZeroFlag
  $7B50,$02 Clear the zero flag.
@ $7B52 label=Inglish_BuildTheActionSentence_ShutTheMessageGate
  $7B52,$05 Write #N$01 (the gate shut) to *#R$B6FA.
  $7B57,$04 Remember the pointer in the saved object pointer.
  $7B5B,$07 Restore #REGbc, #REGde, #REGix, #REGiy and #REGhl from the stack.
  $7B62,$01 Return.

c $7B63 Inglish: Copy A Name Into The Clause
@ $7B63 label=Inglish_CopyANameIntoTheClause
D $7B63 Copies an object's name, or a location's name, into the clause. An id of #N$FF returns
. at once.
  $7B63,$02 Return if there is no object (#REGb is #N$FF).
  $7B65,$05 Jump to #R$7B6F to name an object when the noun-type flag in #REGa is zero; otherwise name a location.
  $7B6A,$03 Call #R$71D5 to address past the location.
  $7B6D,$02 Jump to #R$7B72 to copy the six name bytes.
@ $7B6F label=Inglish_CopyANameIntoTheClause_AddressTheName
  $7B6F,$03 Call #R$71E2 to address the object name.
@ $7B72 label=Inglish_CopyANameIntoTheClause_CopyTheName
  $7B72,$05 Copy the prepared bytes.
  $7B77,$01 Return.

c $7B78 Inglish: Split The Clause Flags
@ $7B78 label=Inglish_SplitTheClauseFlags
D $7B78 Splits the clause flags into the noun-print flag, the second-noun flag and the bytes
. kept for the match.
  $7B78,$03 Read *#R$B71E.
  $7B7B,$02 Keep only bit 6.
  $7B7D,$03 Store the result at #R$B711.
  $7B80,$03 Read *#R$B71D.
  $7B83,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $7B84,$02 Keep only bit 0.
  $7B86,$03 Store the result at #R$B70F.
  $7B89,$01 Use the phrase scratch byte again.
  $7B8A,$02 Keep only bit 7.
  $7B8C,$02 Jump to #R$7B90 if bit 7 of the phrase scratch byte is clear.
  $7B8E,$02 Set the noun-print flag.
@ $7B90 label=Inglish_SplitTheClauseFlags_StoreTheNounPrintFlag
  $7B90,$03 Update *#R$B6FE.
  $7B93,$01 Use the phrase scratch byte again.
  $7B94,$02 Keep only bit 6.
  $7B96,$02 Jump to #R$7B9A if bit 6 of the phrase scratch byte is clear.
  $7B98,$02 Set the second-noun flag.
@ $7B9A label=Inglish_SplitTheClauseFlags_StoreTheSecondNounFlag
  $7B9A,$03 Update *#R$B6FF.
  $7B9D,$01 Return.

c $7B9E Inglish: Save The Clause Window
@ $7B9E label=Inglish_SaveTheClauseWindow
E $7B9E View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$B39D }
. LIST#
D $7B9E Saves the clause window: the object's id and location, then copies the clause pairs into
. the match buffers.
  $7B9E,$05 Read the low byte of the pointer in the clause.
  $7BA3,$03 Read the high byte of the pointer in the clause.
  $7BA6,$01 Use the clause byte at offset 1 again.
  $7BA7,$02 Keep only bit 7.
  $7BA9,$03 Update the saved phrase byte.
  $7BAC,$02 Clear bit 7 of the pointer's high byte.
  $7BAE,$03 Remember the pointer in the saved text pointer.
  $7BB1,$03 Point #REGhl at the swapped text pointer.
  $7BB4,$03 Count #N$04 passes.
N $7BB7 Fill the buffer at #REGhl.
  $7BB7,$03 Call #R$70E2 to fill a run of bytes.
  $7BBA,$03 Count #N$02 passes.
  $7BBD,$02 Save the clause bytes at offset #N$04.
  $7BBF,$03 Call #R$7CAC to copy one clause pair.
  $7BC2,$02 Save the clause bytes at offset #N$0E.
  $7BC4,$03 Call #R$7CAC to copy one clause pair.
  $7BC7,$02 Save the clause bytes at offset #N$06.
  $7BC9,$03 Call #R$7CAC to copy one clause pair.
  $7BCC,$02 Save the clause bytes at offset #N$10.
  $7BCE,$03 Call #R$7CAC to copy one clause pair.
  $7BD1,$04 Clear *#R$B6DF.
  $7BD5,$03 Point #REGhl at the saved text pointer.
  $7BD8,$03 Each action record is #N$08 bytes.
  $7BDB,$04 Point #REGix at the action table.
@ $7BDF label=Inglish_SaveTheClauseWindow_UseTheActionTable
  $7BDF,$04 Use the action table as the parser pointer.
  $7BE3,$03 Call #R$71F3 to match clause pairs.
  $7BE6,$02 Jump to #R$7C1B if #R$71F3 returned zero.
  $7BE8,$02 Add #N($0008,$04,$04) onto the action table.
  $7BEA,$03 Read the high byte of this action's token.
  $7BED,$05 Jump back to #R$7BDF if this action's token is not zero.
  $7BF2,$02 Restore the clause pointer from the stack.
N $7BF4 Test the low half of the class being matched.
  $7BF4,$07 Jump to #R$7EA8 if *#R$B6DF is not zero.
  $7BFB,$03 Read the pointer stored in the saved text pointer.
  $7BFE,$04 Point #REGhl at "[0x0d] You[0x01].[0x0d] Time passes.".
  $7C02,$04 Clear *#R$B701.
  $7C06,$03 Update *#R$B71C.
  $7C09,$05 Write #N$01 (the gate shut) to *#R$B6FA.
  $7C0E,$03 Call #R$72D3 to open the message gate for a conjunction.
  $7C11,$03 Read *#R$B71B.
  $7C14,$02 Return if the conjunction count reached zero.
  $7C16,$05 Jump back to #R$798B to advance the other characters.
@ $7C1B label=Inglish_SaveTheClauseWindow_ScoreTheSwappedClause
  $7C1B,$05 Restore the clause pointer from the stack and call #R$7C23 to score the swapped clause.
  $7C20,$02 Clear the zero flag.
  $7C22,$01 Return.

c $7C23 Inglish: Score The Swapped Clause
@ $7C23 label=Inglish_ScoreTheSwappedClause
E $7C23 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$B405 }
. LIST#
D $7C23 Swaps the two clause pointers when the conjunction count is odd, then scores that clause
. against the action table.
  $7C23,$03 Jump to #R$7C34 if the match result is zero.
  $7C26,$03 Read the pointer stored in the swapped text pointer.
  $7C29,$04 Read the pointer stored in the other swapped pointer.
  $7C2D,$04 Remember the pointer in the swapped text pointer.
  $7C31,$03 Remember the pointer in the other swapped pointer.
@ $7C34 label=Inglish_ScoreTheSwappedClause_PackTheTokenClasses
  $7C34,$03 Call #R$70F3 to pack this action entry's token classes into the clause.
  $7C37,$08 Jump to #R$7C44 unless the phrase slot is empty.
  $7C3F,$03 Read *#R$B71E.
  $7C42,$02 Jump to #R$7C63 because the phrase slot is empty.
@ $7C44 label=Inglish_ScoreTheSwappedClause_CompareClauseByte
  $7C44,$07 Jump to #R$7C53 if the swapped clause byte is not the clause byte at offset #N$0E.
  $7C4B,$07 Jump to #R$7C5E if the swapped clause byte is the clause byte at offset #N$0F.
  $7C52,$01 Step back one byte through the swapped text pointer.
@ $7C53 label=Inglish_ScoreTheSwappedClause_CompareTheNextByte
  $7C53,$06 Jump to #R$7C5E if the swapped clause byte is not the clause byte at offset #N$10.
  $7C59,$01 Step to the next byte of the swapped text pointer.
  $7C5A,$01 Read the swapped clause byte.
  $7C5B,$03 Compare the swapped clause byte with the clause byte at offset #N$11.
@ $7C5E label=Inglish_ScoreTheSwappedClause_ReadTheScoreByte
  $7C5E,$03 Read *#R$B71D.
  $7C61,$02 Jump to #R$7C65 unless the swapped clause matched the clause bytes at offsets #N$0E and #N$0F or at offsets #N$10 and #N$11.
@ $7C63 label=Inglish_ScoreTheSwappedClause_FlipBitFive
  $7C63,$02 Flip the bits selected by #N$20.
@ $7C65 label=Inglish_ScoreTheSwappedClause_TestTheScoreBit
  $7C65,$07 Jump to #R$7C6F if bit 5 of the phrase scratch byte is clear.
  $7C6C,$03 Store the swapped score bytes #N$08 and #N$12.
@ $7C6F label=Inglish_ScoreTheSwappedClause_StoreGrammarCount
  $7C6F,$03 Point #REGhl at the grammar count.
  $7C72,$01 Store the saved byte in the grammar count.
  $7C73,$01 Step to the next byte of the grammar count.
  $7C74,$01 Store the saved byte in the grammar count.
  $7C75,$01 Take the first score byte into #REGa.
  $7C76,$03 Point #REGde at the first copy window.
  $7C79,$03 Point #REGhl at the match buffer.
  $7C7C,$03 Call #R$7C91 to score the swapped clause.
  $7C7F,$0C Copy #N$06 bytes from the first copy window to #R$B6E0.
  $7C8B,$03 Point #REGde at the second copy window.
  $7C8E,$03 Point #REGhl at the second match buffer.
  $7C91,$02 Save #REGbc and keep #REGa in #REGc.
  $7C93,$02 Start the character count at zero.
  $7C95,$05 Add #N($0006,$04,$04) to the second match buffer.
  $7C9A,$05 Copy the prepared bytes.
  $7C9F,$01 Start the six-byte zero test with A clear.
  $7CA0,$02 Count #N$06 passes.
@ $7CA2 label=Inglish_ScoreTheSwappedClause_TestTheMatchBytes
  $7CA2,$01 Step back one byte through the second match buffer.
  $7CA3,$01 Fold this byte into the test. All six bytes are zero when Z stays set.
  $7CA4,$02 Loop back to #R$7CA2 until #N$06 runs out.
  $7CA6,$03 Return if the six copied bytes are all zero.
  $7CA9,$02 Mark the second match buffer so bit 0 is on.
  $7CAB,$01 Return.

c $7CAC Inglish: Copy One Clause Pair
@ $7CAC label=Inglish_CopyOneClausePair
E $7CAC View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$B483 }
. LIST#
D $7CAC Copies one id-and-location pair from the object into the second match buffer, and stops
. when #N$06 or the clause bits say so.
  $7CAC,$03 Return when the count in #REGb is zero.
  $7CAF,$02 The clause offset's high byte is zero.
  $7CB1,$04 Add the second copy window onto the parser workspace.
  $7CB5,$03 Read the clause byte at this offset.
  $7CB8,$02 Store it in the second match buffer and step on.
  $7CBA,$03 Read the next clause byte.
  $7CBD,$01 Store it in the second match buffer.
  $7CBE,$01 Step back one byte through the second match buffer.
  $7CBF,$03 OR it with the clause byte just read.
  $7CC2,$03 Return if both clause bytes are zero.
  $7CC5,$01 Drop one from the character count.
  $7CC6,$02 Step on through the second match buffer.
  $7CC8,$01 Return.

c $7CC9 Inglish: Jump To The Clause Handler
@ $7CC9 label=Inglish_JumpToTheClauseHandler
E $7CC9 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$B4A0 }
. LIST#
D $7CC9 Jumps to the handler the clause selected.
  $7CC9,$02 Jump to the handler this clause selected.

c $7CCB Inglish: Find The First Noun
@ $7CCB label=Inglish_FindTheFirstNoun
E $7CCB View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$B4C1 }
. LIST#
D $7CCB Finds the first noun. When the noun-print flag at #R$B6FE is #N$01 it uses the place-by-exit test at
. #R$9EA0; otherwise it uses the object test at #R$9DD9, which looks in the same place as the player. The record
. found is kept in the saved object pointer at #R$794E.
  $7CCB,$06 Use the saved object pointer as the record being walked.
  $7CD1,$03 Read *#R$B6FE.
  $7CD4,$03 Jump to #R$7CF1 if the noun-print flag is #N$01.
  $7CD7,$04 Point #REGiy at #R$9DD9 to use as the noun test.
  $7CDB,$03 Read *#R$B71E.
  $7CDE,$04 Keep the low two bits of the clause marker.
  $7CE2,$03 Update *#R$B710.
  $7CE5,$03 Call #R$7CFC to run the first noun test.
  $7CE8,$02 Compare the result of the noun test with #N$FF.
@ $7CEA label=Inglish_FindTheFirstNoun_SaveTheObjectRecord
  $7CEA,$04 Remember this record in the saved object pointer.
  $7CEE,$02 Restore #REGiy from the stack.
  $7CF0,$01 Return.
@ $7CF1 label=Inglish_FindTheFirstNoun_PointAtTheNounTest
  $7CF1,$04 Point #REGiy at #R$9EA0 to use as the noun test.
  $7CF5,$03 Call #R$7CFC to run the first noun test.
  $7CF8,$02 Compare the clause marker with #N$FF.
  $7CFA,$02 Jump back to #R$7CEA to save this object record.

c $7CFC Inglish: Run The First Noun Test
@ $7CFC label=Inglish_RunTheFirstNounTest
E $7CFC View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$B4FD }
. LIST#
D $7CFC Runs the first-noun test through the clause handler, stores the object, and runs the
. action handler until the message-suppressed flag is set.
  $7CFC,$03 Point #REGhl at the first copy window.
  $7CFF,$03 Call #R$7CC9 to jump to the clause handler.
  $7D02,$03 Return when the clause marker is #N$FF.
  $7D05,$03 Update *#R$B6E8.
  $7D08,$03 Point #REGhl at the match buffer.
  $7D0B,$02 Mark the match buffer so bit 1 is on.
  $7D0D,$03 Call #R$94D6 to run the action handler.
N $7D10 Test the message-suppressed flag.
  $7D10,$06 Loop back to #R$7CFC to keep running the action while the message-suppressed flag is clear.
  $7D16,$01 Return.

c $7D17 Inglish: Find The Second Noun
@ $7D17 label=Inglish_FindTheSecondNoun
E $7D17 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$B568 }
. LIST#
D $7D17 Finds the second noun the same way, storing it as the second object. When the second-noun flag at
. #R$B6FF is #N$01 it uses the place-by-exit test at #R$9EA0; otherwise it uses the object test at #R$9DD9.
. The record found is kept at #R$7950, and bit 0 of #R$B71D is copied to #R$B70F.
  $7D17,$04 Clear *#R$B70F.
  $7D1B,$06 Use the other saved object pointer as the record being walked.
  $7D21,$03 Read *#R$B6FF.
  $7D24,$03 Jump to #R$7D49 if the second-noun flag is #N$01.
  $7D27,$04 Point #REGiy at #R$9DD9 to use as the noun test.
  $7D2B,$03 Read *#R$B71E.
  $7D2E,$05 Store the low two bits of the clause being built in *#R$B710.
  $7D33,$03 Call #R$7D54 to run the second noun test.
  $7D36,$02 Compare the result of the noun test with #N$FF.
@ $7D38 label=Inglish_FindTheSecondNoun_SaveTheSecondRecord
  $7D38,$04 Remember this record in the other saved object pointer.
  $7D3C,$06 Restore the parser pointer from the stack and read *#R$B71D.
  $7D42,$02 Keep only bit 0.
  $7D44,$03 Store the result at #R$B70F.
  $7D47,$01 Restore #REGaf from the stack.
  $7D48,$01 Return.
@ $7D49 label=Inglish_FindTheSecondNoun_PointAtTheOtherNounTest
  $7D49,$04 Point #REGiy at #R$9EA0 to use as the noun test.
  $7D4D,$03 Call #R$7D54 to run the second noun test.
  $7D50,$02 Compare the result of the noun test with #N$FF.
  $7D52,$02 Jump back to #R$7D38 to save the second object record.

c $7D54 Inglish: Run The Second Noun Test
@ $7D54 label=Inglish_RunTheSecondNounTest
E $7D54 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$B5A4 }
. LIST#
D $7D54 Runs the second-noun test, stores the second object, and asks whether the sentence
. should be suppressed.
  $7D54,$03 Point #REGhl at the second copy window.
  $7D57,$03 Call #R$7CC9 to jump to the clause handler.
  $7D5A,$03 Return if the noun test returned #N$FF.
  $7D5D,$03 Update *#R$B6E9.
  $7D60,$03 Point #REGhl at the second match buffer.
  $7D63,$02 Mark the second match buffer so bit 1 is on.
  $7D65,$03 Call #R$7AED to run the action and see whether its message was held back.
  $7D68,$02 Jump back to #R$7D54 if no message was held back.
  $7D6A,$01 Return.

c $7D6B Inglish: Open The Message Gate
@ $7D6B label=Inglish_OpenTheMessageGate
D $7D6B Opens the message gate and starts the action countdown, both set to #N$01.
N $7D6B Update the message gate.
  $7D6B,$08 Write #N$01 to *#R$B6FA and *#R$B701.
  $7D73,$01 Return.

c $7D74 Inglish: Copy The Parser Slot
@ $7D74 label=Inglish_CopyTheParserSlot
D $7D74 Copies the parser slot into the clause. If the grammar count still needs a noun, it
. prints "which?" and reports no match.
  $7D74,$03 Update *#R$B71A.
  $7D77,$0B Copy the parser slot at #REGiy into #R$B9C8.
  $7D82,$01 Return.
@ $7D83 label=Inglish_CopyTheParserSlot_ReadTheGrammarCount
  $7D83,$03 Read the grammar count.
  $7D86,$03 Read the pointer stored in the first copy window.
@ $7D89 label=Inglish_CopyTheParserSlot_CopyIntoTheClause
  $7D89,$04 Call #R$7D74 to copy the parser slot into the clause.
  $7D8D,$03 Point #REGhl at "which[0x01] ?".
@ $7D90 label=Inglish_CopyTheParserSlot_OpenTheMessageGate
  $7D90,$03 Call #R$7D6B to open the message gate.
  $7D93,$03 Call #R$72D3 to open the message gate for a conjunction.
  $7D96,$01 Return zero from the parser-slot copy.
  $7D97,$01 Return.
@ $7D98 label=Inglish_CopyTheParserSlot_TestTheSecondMatch
  $7D98,$08 Jump to #R$7E4D if bit 0 of the second match buffer is clear.
  $7DA0,$02 See whether bit 1 of the second match buffer is set.
  $7DA2,$0B Jump to #R$7DFE if bit 1 of the second match buffer is clear.
N $7DAD Test this flag.
  $7DAD,$07 Jump to #R$7DF5 if *#R$7941 is zero.
  $7DB4,$03 Read the second grammar count.
  $7DB7,$03 Read the second copy window.
  $7DBA,$02 Jump to #R$7D89 to copy the slot and print the question.
@ $7DBC label=Inglish_CopyTheParserSlot_ReadTheConjunctionCount
  $7DBC,$03 Read *#R$B71B.
  $7DBF,$02 Return if the conjunction count reached zero.
N $7DC1 Test the phrase scratch byte.
  $7DC1,$06 Jump to #R$7DCB if *#R$B71C is zero.
  $7DC7,$04 Jump back to #R$7994 to update the replay path.
@ $7DCB label=Inglish_CopyTheParserSlot_TestTheGrammarMarker
N $7DCB Test the flag.
  $7DCB,$07 Jump to #R$7D98 if *#R$7940 is #N$01.
  $7DD2,$08 Jump to #R$7E30 if bit 0 of the match buffer is clear.
  $7DDA,$02 See whether bit 1 of the match buffer is set.
  $7DDC,$0B Jump to #R$7DFE if bit 1 of the match buffer is clear.
N $7DE7 Test this flag.
  $7DE7,$06 Jump to #R$7DF5 if *#R$793F is zero.
  $7DED,$03 Jump back to #R$7D83 if the third match buffer has not reached zero.
  $7DF0,$03 Call #R$7AD8 to test the scratch bits.
  $7DF3,$02 Jump back to #R$7D98 unless #R$7AD8 returned with the zero flag set.
@ $7DF5 label=Inglish_CopyTheParserSlot_DispatchTheAction
  $7DF5,$03 Call #R$7D6B to open the message gate.
  $7DF8,$03 Call #R$950F to run the handler when one is present.
  $7DFB,$03 Jump back to #R$798E to test the phrase scratch byte.
@ $7DFE label=Inglish_CopyTheParserSlot_PointAtTheExitList
  $7DFE,$04 Call #R$9E95 to point #REGix at the exit list.
  $7E02,$03 Write #N$01 to the flag at #REGbc: the noun-print flag at #R$B6FE, or the second-noun flag at #R$B6FF when reached from #R$7DA2.
  $7E05,$03 Call #R$9EA0 to find the exit to the place named.
  $7E08,$04 Jump to #R$7E20 if #R$9EA0 did not return #N$FF.
  $7E0C,$06 Write #N$02 to *#R$B710.
  $7E12,$04 Start the object search one slot before #R$C063.
  $7E16,$01 Put zero in #REGa.
  $7E17,$01 Store it in the noun-print flag.
  $7E18,$04 Save #REGhl and call #R$9DD9 to test whether the object at #R$B70C is here.
  $7E1C,$04 Jump to #R$7E25 if #R$9DD9 returned #N$FF.
@ $7E20 label=Inglish_CopyTheParserSlot_StoreTheObject
  $7E20,$02 Store it in the current object.
  $7E22,$03 Jump back to #R$711A to arm the message gate.
@ $7E25 label=Inglish_CopyTheParserSlot_SayItIsNotHere
  $7E25,$03 Call #R$7D6B to open the message gate.
  $7E28,$03 Point #REGhl at "i do not see[0x00] here[0x14]".
  $7E2B,$03 Call #R$72D3 to open the message gate for a conjunction.
  $7E2E,$01 Return zero.
  $7E2F,$01 Return.
@ $7E30 label=Inglish_CopyTheParserSlot_PlantJumpIfNotZero
  $7E30,$03 Call #R$7E7C to plant JR NZ and read the two fields.
  $7E33,$03 Read the pointer stored in the saved text pointer.
  $7E36,$04 Point #REGhl at "[0x0b] D[0x01][0x01][0x14]".
N $7E3A Test this flag.
  $7E3A,$07 Jump to #R$7D90 if *#R$7940 is zero.
  $7E41,$03 Read the grammar count.
  $7E44,$03 Call #R$7D74 to copy the parser slot.
  $7E47,$03 Point #REGhl at the "what ?" prompt in #R$ADC0.
  $7E4A,$03 Jump to #R$7D90 to open the message gate and print the prompt.
@ $7E4D label=Inglish_CopyTheParserSlot_PlantJumpIfZero
  $7E4D,$03 Call #R$7E78 to plant JR Z and read the two fields.
  $7E50,$03 Start from a clear pointer.
  $7E53,$04 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $7E57,$03 Call #R$71E2 to address the object name.
  $7E5A,$04 Call #R$7E7C to plant JR NZ and read the two fields.
  $7E5E,$03 Read the pointer stored in the saved text pointer.
  $7E61,$04 Point #REGhl at "[0x0b] Eat[0x01][0x01][0x00][0x01][0x01][0x01][0x14]".
N $7E65 Test this flag.
  $7E65,$07 Jump to #R$7D90 if *#R$7941 is zero.
  $7E6C,$03 Read the second grammar count.
  $7E6F,$03 Call #R$7D74 to copy the parser slot.
  $7E72,$03 Point #REGhl at the "what ?" prompt in #R$ADC0.
  $7E75,$03 Jump to #R$7D90 to open the message gate and print the prompt.

c $7E78 Inglish: Plant JR Z
@ $7E78 label=Inglish_PlantJumpRelativeIfZero
E $7E78 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$B62F }
. LIST#
D $7E78 Plants the JR Z opcode at #R$7E92 and #R$7EA1, then reads two words from the action entry. Each word is kept when its bit is clear.
  $7E78,$02 Take the JR Z opcode.
  $7E7A,$02 Jump to #R$7E7E to plant that opcode.

c $7E7C Inglish: Plant JR NZ
@ $7E7C label=Inglish_PlantJumpRelativeIfNonZero
E $7E7C View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$B633 }
. LIST#
D $7E7C Plants the JR NZ opcode at #R$7E92 and #R$7EA1, then reads two words from the action entry. Each word is kept when its bit is set.
  $7E7C,$02 Take the JR NZ opcode.
@ $7E7E label=Inglish_PlantJumpRelativeIfNonZero_PlantTheOpcode
  $7E7E,$03 Plant the opcode at #R$7E92.
  $7E81,$03 Plant the same opcode at #R$7EA1.
  $7E84,$04 Point #REGix at the action entry saved at #N$795E.
  $7E88,$03 Read the low byte of the word at offset 4 of this action.
  $7E8B,$03 Read the high byte of the word at offset 4 of this action.
  $7E8E,$06 Test bit 7 of byte 7 of this action; the jump planted at #R$7E92 by #R$7E78 as JR Z or #R$7E7C as JR NZ goes to #R$7E97.
  $7E94,$03 Start from a clear pointer.
@ $7E97 label=Inglish_PlantJumpRelativeIfNonZero_ReadActionWord
  $7E97,$01 Swap #REGhl with the address on the stack.
  $7E98,$04 Save the word just read, and read the low byte at offset 2 of this action.
  $7E9C,$03 Read the high byte at offset 2 of this action.
  $7E9F,$04 Test bit 5 of that high byte; the jump planted at #R$7EA1 by #R$7E78 as JR Z or #R$7E7C as JR NZ goes to #R$7EA6.
  $7EA3,$03 Start from a clear pointer.
@ $7EA6 label=Inglish_PlantJumpRelativeIfNonZero_CallTheHandler
  $7EA6,$01 Swap #REGhl with the address on the stack.
  $7EA7,$01 Jump to the action handler the caller addressed.
@ $7EA8 label=Inglish_PlantJumpRelativeIfNonZero_ReadThePointers
  $7EA8,$03 Read the pointer stored in the other swapped pointer.
  $7EAB,$04 Read the pointer stored in the swapped text pointer.
  $7EAF,$04 Read the pointer stored in the saved text pointer.
  $7EB3,$04 Point #REGhl at "i do not know the verb".
  $7EB7,$03 Jump to #R$7D90 to open the message gate and print the prompt.

c $7EBA Inglish: Fill The Claimed Slots
@ $7EBA label=Inglish_FillTheClaimedSlots
D $7EBA Gives the current object up to #REGa of the claimed character slots at #R$B738: it writes the
. object id into the first #REGa slots marked #N$FF (claimed by #R$834B), capping #REGa at the
. claimed-slot count in #R$B737, then frees the rest of the claimed slots by writing #N$00 to them.
  $7EBA,$05 Save #REGbc, #REGix and #REGde, and keep #REGa in #REGb.
  $7EBF,$03 Read the slot count.
  $7EC2,$04 Jump to #R$7EC7 if the slot count is not below the count in #REGb.
  $7EC6,$01 Cap the count at the slot count.
@ $7EC7 label=Inglish_FillTheClaimedSlots_UseTheSlotCountAgain
  $7EC7,$01 Use the slot count again.
  $7EC8,$01 Take the count in #REGb off the slot count, leaving how many slots remain.
  $7EC9,$01 Copy the result into #REGc.
  $7ECA,$04 Point #REGix one #N$19-byte slot before the first character slot at #R$B738.
  $7ECE,$03 Each slot is #N$19 bytes.
  $7ED1,$04 Jump to #R$7EE6 if there are no slots to fill.
@ $7ED5 label=Inglish_FillTheClaimedSlots_FindAClaimedSlot
  $7ED5,$02 Step #REGix on to the next #N$19-byte slot.
  $7ED7,$03 Read the object id in this slot.
  $7EDA,$04 Jump back to #R$7ED5 while this slot is not claimed (not #N$FF).
  $7EDE,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $7EE1,$03 Store the current object id in this slot.
  $7EE4,$02 Loop back to #R$7ED5 until #REGb slots are filled.
@ $7EE6 label=Inglish_FillTheClaimedSlots_TestTheSlotCount
  $7EE6,$05 Jump to #R$7EFA if no slots remain.
@ $7EEB label=Inglish_FillTheClaimedSlots_FreeTheRest
  $7EEB,$02 Step #REGix on to the next #N$19-byte slot.
  $7EED,$03 Read the object id in this slot.
  $7EF0,$04 Jump back to #R$7EEB while this slot is not claimed (not #N$FF).
  $7EF4,$04 Mark this slot free with #N$00.
  $7EF8,$02 Loop back to #R$7EEB until the slot count runs out.
@ $7EFA label=Inglish_FillTheClaimedSlots_RestoreAndReturn
  $7EFA,$04 Restore #REGde, #REGix and #REGbc from the stack.
  $7EFE,$01 Return.

c $7EFF Inglish: Find This Character's Slot
@ $7EFF label=Inglish_FindThisCharacterSlot
D $7EFF Finds this character's slot among the eight slots at #R$B738. Each slot is #N$19 bytes,
. and the slot id is whose turn it is.
  $7EFF,$03 Point at the first character slot at #R$B738.
  $7F02,$03 Each character slot at #R$B738 is #N$19 bytes.
  $7F05,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $7F08,$02 Count #N$08 passes.
@ $7F0A label=Inglish_FindThisCharacterSlot_TestThisSlot
  $7F0A,$02 Return if the character whose turn it is is this character slot.
  $7F0C,$01 Step to the next #N$19-byte character slot.
  $7F0D,$02 Loop back to #R$7F0A until #N$08 runs out.
  $7F0F,$01 Return.

c $7F10 Inglish: Keep The Character Slot
@ $7F10 label=Inglish_KeepTheCharacterSlot
D $7F10 Finds this character's slot and returns.
. size.
  $7F10,$06 Call #R$7EFF to find this character's slot.
  $7F16,$03 Restore #REGbc, #REGde and #REGhl from the stack.
  $7F19,$01 Return.

c $7F1A Inglish: Save The Clause And Act
@ $7F1A label=Inglish_SaveTheClauseAndAct
D $7F1A Saves the clause and scores the action for this character. When it is not the player's
. turn the saved registers are returned.
  $7F1A,$08 Save #REGix, #REGiy, #REGbc, #REGde and #REGhl, and keep #REGa in #REGc.
  $7F22,$03 Call #R$7EFF to find this character's slot.
  $7F25,$02 Clear it.
  $7F27,$05 Jump to #R$7F37 if the character whose turn it is is not zero.
  $7F2C,$02 Clear the zero flag.
  $7F2E,$01 Swap #REGhl with the address on the stack.
@ $7F2F label=Inglish_SaveTheClauseAndAct_RestoreAndReturn
  $7F2F,$07 Restore #REGhl, #REGde, #REGbc, #REGiy and #REGix from the stack.
  $7F36,$01 Return.
@ $7F37 label=Inglish_SaveTheClauseAndAct_PointAtTheSlot
  $7F37,$03 Point #REGiy at byte #N$01 of this character's slot.
  $7F3A,$05 Write #N$01 to *#R$B71B.
  $7F3F,$03 Read *#R$B71C.
  $7F42,$04 Call #R$79B6 to score the current action.
  $7F46,$01 Switch #REGaf with its shadow.
  $7F47,$04 Clear *#R$B71B.
  $7F4B,$04 Update *#R$B71C.
  $7F4F,$01 Switch #REGaf with its shadow.
  $7F50,$02 Jump to #R$7F57 if #R$79B6 found no match.
  $7F52,$03 Call #R$7AED to run the action and see whether its message was held back.
  $7F55,$02 Jump back to #R$7F2F if #R$7AED held the message back.
@ $7F57 label=Inglish_SaveTheClauseAndAct_ClearThisCharacter
  $7F57,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $7F5A,$03 Call #R$7F60 to walk the character slots.
  $7F5D,$01 Take the zero path back into the clause save.
  $7F5E,$02 Jump back to #R$7F2F to return.

c $7F60 Inglish: Walk The Character Slots
@ $7F60 label=Inglish_WalkTheCharacterSlots
D $7F60 Walks the eight character slots at #R$B738 and frees (#N$00) any slot held by the character in #REGa.
  $7F60,$06 Save #REGhl, #REGde and #REGbc, and point #REGhl at the first character slot at #R$B738.
  $7F66,$03 Each character slot at #R$B738 is #N$19 bytes.
  $7F69,$02 Count #N$08 passes.
@ $7F6B label=Inglish_WalkTheCharacterSlots_ClearThisSlot
  $7F6B,$03 Jump to #R$7F70 unless this slot holds the character in #REGa.
  $7F6E,$02 Clear it.
@ $7F70 label=Inglish_WalkTheCharacterSlots_StepToTheNextSlot
  $7F70,$01 Step to the next #N$19-byte character slot.
  $7F71,$02 Loop back to #R$7F6B until #N$08 runs out.
  $7F73,$03 Restore #REGbc, #REGde and #REGhl from the stack.
  $7F76,$01 Return.

g $7F77 Location ID.
@ $7F77 label=LocationID
D $7F77 Contains #N$FF when the location has no graphics data, else the location ID. Read by #R$7F78 and #R$965B.
  $7F77,$01

c $7F78 Drawing Routine
@ $7F78 label=Drawing
D $7F78 Draws a location picture from its line, fill and attribute commands.
. Nothing is drawn when the picture switch is off, and a dark place is
. forced black. See #HTML(<a rel="noopener" href="https://www.pobtastic.co.uk/post/skoolkit/hobbit/graphics/">)Location Graphics Decoding#HTML(</a>).
  $7F78,$07 Save #REGaf and jump to #R$7F86 if the picture switch at #R$B707 is not zero.
  $7F7F,$05 Write #N$FF to #R$7F77.
  $7F84,$01 Restore #REGaf from the stack.
  $7F85,$01 Return.
N $7F86 Drawing Check
@ $7F86 label=Drawing_Check
  $7F86,$0B Keep the saved flags, save the registers, and point #REGix at the location graphics table.
  $7F91,$03 Call #R$9DBD to look this id up. The zero flag is set when nothing matches.
  $7F94,$03 Store the location ID at #R$7F77.
  $7F97,$03 Read the low byte of this location's graphics address.
  $7F9A,$03 Read the high byte of this location's graphics address.
  $7F9D,$03 If there is graphics data for this location then call #R$7FA7.
  $7FA0,$06 Restore #REGix, #REGde, #REGbc, #REGhl and #REGaf from the stack.
  $7FA6,$01 Return.

c $7FA7 Drawing: Start A Picture Command
@ $7FA7 label=DrawingSetup
E $7FA7 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$F3C2 }
. LIST#
D $7FA7 Reads picture commands. #N$00 ends the picture, #N$08 moves the pen, bit 7 draws a line,
. and the other bytes fill or paint.
N $7FA7 Drawing routines.
  $7FA7,$0B Save the picture registers and clear the window when the place is dark.
  $7FB2,$02 Start the high coordinate at #N$7F.
  $7FB4,$02 Start the low coordinate at #N$3F.
  $7FB6,$02 Start the step count at #N$01.
  $7FB8,$02 Start the direction at #N$01.
  $7FBA,$02 Start the length at #N$01.
@ $7FBC label=Drawing_Loop
  $7FBC,$03 Fetch the next drawing instruction.
  $7FBF,$04 If the drawing instruction is zero, jump to #R$8069.
  $7FC3,$02 Increment the graphics data pointer by one.
  $7FC5,$04 If the graphics data value which was fetched is not #N$08 (move to X/Y) jump to #R$7FD5.
N $7FC9 Moves the "pen" held in #REGde to a screen location.
  $7FC9,$0A Populate #REGde with two values from the graphics data and increment the pointer by two.
  $7FD3,$02 Jump back to #R$7FBC for the next drawing instruction.
@ $7FD5 label=DrawingSetup_TestForALine
  $7FD5,$04 If bit 7 is clear, jump to #R$7FFA. A set bit 7 is a line.
  $7FD9,$01 Keep the line command.
  $7FDA,$02,b$01 The low three bits are the direction.
  $7FDC,$01 Store the direction in #REGc.
  $7FDD,$04 Bring the line command back.
  $7FE1,$01 Store this in #REGb.
  $7FE2,$03 Read the length byte of the line.
  $7FE5,$02,b$01 The low six bits are the length.
  $7FE7,$01 Store that length.
  $7FE8,$01 A stored length of zero draws one pixel.
  $7FE9,$03 Read the length byte again for the step spacing.
  $7FEC,$02 Increment the graphics data pointer by one.
  $7FEE,$04 Move bits 6-7 down to bits 0-1.
  $7FF2,$01 Combine the step spacing from both command bytes.
  $7FF3,$01 Store the step spacing.
  $7FF4,$01 A stored spacing of zero steps every pixel.
  $7FF5,$03 Call #R$8151 to plot the line.
  $7FF8,$02 Jump back to #R$7FBC for the next drawing instruction.
@ $7FFA label=DrawingSetup_TestForAFill
N $7FFA A set bit 6 is a fill.
  $7FFA,$04 Jump to #R$8012 if bit 6 is clear.
  $7FFE,$02,b$01 The low three bits are the fill ink.
  $8000,$04 Read the fill's X coordinate.
  $8004,$02 Advance the graphics pointer.
  $8006,$03 Read the fill's other coordinate.
  $8009,$02 Advance the graphics pointer.
N $800B Flood-fill from the point in #REGde.
  $800B,$03 Call #R$8071 to flood-fill from that point.
  $800E,$04 Jump back to #R$7FBC to fetch the next drawing instruction.
@ $8012 label=DrawingSetup_RepeatWhileBitFiveIsClear
  $8012,$05 Jump back to #R$7FBC if bit 5 of this picture command is clear.
  $8017,$02,b$01 The low three bits are the ink to paint.
  $8019,$01 Shift the ink towards the paper bits.
  $801A,$01 Shift the ink towards the paper bits.
  $801B,$01 Shift the ink into the paper bits.
  $801C,$04 Save #REGhl, #REGde and #REGbc, and keep the scaled bits in #REGc.
  $8020,$03 Read the attribute address high byte into #REGh.
  $8023,$02 Advance the graphics pointer.
  $8025,$03 Read the attribute address low byte into #REGl.
  $8028,$02 Advance the graphics pointer.
@ $802A label=DrawingSetup_ReadTheNextAttributeByte
  $802A,$03 Read the next attribute byte.
  $802D,$02 Advance the graphics pointer.
  $802F,$04 Jump to #R$8063 at the #N$FF terminator.
  $8033,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $8034,$02,b$01 The low two bits choose which way this attribute run steps.
  $8036,$01 Store that step.
  $8037,$05 Shift the length down two bits.
  $803C,$01 A stored length of zero paints one cell.
  $803D,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
@ $803E label=DrawingSetup_Loop
  $803E,$01 Read the next byte.
  $803F,$08,b$02,$06 Jump to #R$8049 when this cell's ink is not the ink being replaced.
  $8047,$02 Flip the bits selected by #N$38.
@ $8049 label=DrawingSetup_StoreTheAttribute
  $8049,$0A Shift the ink back into bits 0-2, merge it with #REGc and store the attribute; then call #R$80F5 if the direction in #REGe is #N$00.
  $8053,$04 Call #R$8121 if the direction is #N$01.
  $8057,$04 Call #R$8106 if the direction is #N$02.
  $805B,$04 Call #R$8117 if the direction is #N$03.
  $805F,$02 Loop back to #R$803E until the count runs out.
  $8061,$02 Jump back to #R$802A for the next attribute byte.
@ $8063 label=DrawingSetup_FetchTheNextDrawingInstruction
N $8063 Restore the registers saved for this shape.
  $8063,$06 Restore #REGbc, #REGde and #REGhl from the stack, then jump to #R$7FBC for the next drawing instruction.
@ $8069 label=Drawing_End
  $8069,$05 Restore #REGbc, #REGde, #REGhl and #REGiy from the stack.
  $806E,$01 Return.
B $806F,$02
  $8071,$03 Store the result at #R$824E.
  $8074,$06 Allow #N$80 steps of this flood fill.
@ $807A label=DrawingSetup_MaskOneScreenByte
  $807A,$03 Call #R$80EE to mask one screen byte.
  $807D,$02 Jump to #R$8086 if #R$80EE found the masked screen byte set.
  $807F,$03 Call #R$8148 to retreat the high coordinate.
  $8082,$02 Jump back to #R$807A if #R$8148 did not wrap the high coordinate.
  $8084,$02 Jump to #R$808C to clear the saved coordinate, because the high coordinate wrapped.
@ $8086 label=DrawingSetup_AddressThePixel
  $8086,$03 Call #R$81B5 to address the pixel.
  $8089,$03 Call #R$8141 to advance the high coordinate.
@ $808C label=DrawingSetup_ClearTheFloodFillCount
  $808C,$06 Store #N($0000,$04,$04) in *#R$806F.
@ $8092 label=DrawingSetup_AdvanceTheLowCoordinate
  $8092,$03 Call #R$812B to advance the low coordinate.
  $8095,$04 Take zero and jump to #R$80AE if #R$812B wrapped the low coordinate.
  $8099,$03 Call #R$80EE to mask one screen byte.
  $809C,$04 Take zero and jump to #R$80A9 if #R$80EE found the masked screen byte set.
N $80A0 Test this flag.
  $80A0,$06 Jump to #R$80A9 if *#R$806F is not zero.
  $80A6,$03 Use #N$01 when the coordinate was zero.
@ $80A9 label=DrawingSetup_RetreatTheLowCoordinate
  $80A9,$05 Call #R$813A to retreat the low coordinate, then restore #REGaf from the stack.
@ $80AE label=DrawingSetup_StoreTheResultAtFloodFillCount
  $80AE,$03 Store the result at #R$806F.
  $80B1,$03 Call #R$813A to retreat the low coordinate.
  $80B4,$04 Take zero, and jump to #R$80CD if #R$813A returned with the zero flag set.
  $80B8,$03 Call #R$80EE to mask one screen byte.
  $80BB,$04 Take zero, and jump to #R$80C8 if #R$80EE found the masked screen byte set.
N $80BF Test this flag.
  $80BF,$06 Jump to #R$80C8 if the drawing scratch is not zero.
  $80C5,$03 Use #N$01 when the coordinate was zero.
@ $80C8 label=DrawingSetup_AdvanceAndRestore
  $80C8,$05 Call #R$812B to advance the low coordinate, then restore #REGaf from the stack.
@ $80CD label=DrawingSetup_StoreTheHighCoordinate
  $80CD,$03 Store the result at #N$8070.
  $80D0,$03 Call #R$81B5 to address the pixel.
  $80D3,$03 Call #R$8141 to advance the high coordinate.
  $80D6,$02 Jump to #R$80E0 if #R$8141 wrapped the high coordinate.
  $80D8,$03 Call #R$80EE to mask one screen byte.
  $80DB,$02 Jump back to #R$8092 if #R$80EE found the masked screen byte clear.
  $80DD,$03 Call #R$81B5 to address the pixel.
@ $80E0 label=DrawingSetup_TestTheFloodFillCount
  $80E0,$06 Jump back to #R$807A unless the flood-fill count is #N$80.
  $80E6,$05 Write #N$00 to *#R$824E.
  $80EB,$02 Restore #REGhl and #REGde from the stack.
  $80ED,$01 Return.

c $80EE Drawing: Mask One Screen Byte
@ $80EE label=Drawing_MaskOneScreenByte
E $80EE View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$F50D }
. LIST#
D $80EE Masks one screen byte. It turns the row into a screen address and keeps the picture
. pointer.
  $80EE,$04 Call #R$81DE to turn the pen into a screen address.
  $80F2,$02 Apply the mask in #REGa and restore #REGhl from the stack.
  $80F4,$01 Return.

c $80F5 Drawing: Step Up One Row
@ $80F5 label=Drawing_StepUpOneRow
E $80F5 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$F514 }
. LIST#
D $80F5 Moves the screen address up one character row.
  $80F5,$05 One screen row is #N$20 bytes.
  $80FA,$01 Clear carry before stepping the screen address back one row.
  $80FB,$02 See how far the pointer is past #N($0020,$04,$04).
  $80FD,$05 Jump to #R$8103 if the screen address is not in row #N$57.
  $8102,$01 Step down one attribute row.
@ $8103 label=Drawing_StepUpOneRow_RestoreTheRowStep
  $8103,$02 Restore #REGde and #REGaf from the stack.
  $8105,$01 Return.

c $8106 Drawing: Step Down One Row
@ $8106 label=Drawing_StepDownOneRow
E $8106 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$F525 }
. LIST#
D $8106 Moves the screen address down one character row, and undoes that step if it would leave
. the screen.
  $8106,$0B Step down one attribute row and jump to #N$8113 if the screen address is not #N$5A.
  $8111,$01 Clear the carry flag.
  $8112,$02 Undo the step.
  $8114,$02 Restore #REGde and #REGaf from the stack.
  $8116,$01 Return.

c $8117 Drawing: Step Left One Pixel
@ $8117 label=Drawing_StepLeftOnePixel
E $8117 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$F536 }
. LIST#
D $8117 Moves the screen address left one pixel, wrapping inside the line.
  $8117,$07 Step the screen address back and jump to #R$811F if it is not in row #N$57.
  $811E,$01 Step #REGhl on.
@ $811F label=Drawing_StepLeftOnePixel_RestoreTheLeftStep
  $811F,$01 Restore #REGaf from the stack.
  $8120,$01 Return.

c $8121 Drawing: Step Right One Pixel
@ $8121 label=Drawing_StepRightOnePixel
E $8121 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$F540 }
. LIST#
D $8121 Moves the screen address right one pixel, wrapping inside the line.
  $8121,$07 Step the screen address on and jump to #R$8129 if it has not reached row #N$5A.
  $8128,$01 Step the screen address back one byte.
@ $8129 label=Drawing_StepRightOnePixel_RestoreTheRightStep
  $8129,$01 Restore #REGaf from the stack.
  $812A,$01 Return.

c $812B Drawing: Advance The Low Coordinate
@ $812B label=Drawing_AdvanceTheLowCoordinate
E $812B View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$F54A }
. LIST#
D $812B Advances the low coordinate. If that sets bit 7, the coordinate is put back and the routine returns zero.
  $812B,$05 Step the low coordinate on and jump to #R$8135 if bit 7 is clear.
  $8130,$01 Put the low coordinate back.
  $8131,$01 Keep #REGa in #REGh.
  $8132,$01 Set the zero flag.
  $8133,$01 Bring #REGa back from #REGh.
  $8134,$01 Return.
@ $8135 label=Drawing_AdvanceTheLowCoordinate_StoreTheCoordinate
  $8135,$01 Keep #REGa in #REGh.
  $8136,$02 Clear the zero flag.
  $8138,$01 Bring #REGa back from #REGh.
  $8139,$01 Return.

c $813A Drawing: Retreat The Low Coordinate
@ $813A label=Drawing_RetreatTheLowCoordinate
E $813A View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$F555 }
. LIST#
D $813A Retreats the low coordinate. If that sets bit 7, the coordinate is put back and one step is counted.
  $813A,$05 Step the low coordinate back and jump to #R$8135 if bit 7 is clear.
  $813F,$01 Put the low coordinate back.
  $8140,$01 Return.

c $8141 Drawing: Advance The High Coordinate
@ $8141 label=Drawing_AdvanceTheHighCoordinate
E $8141 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$F55E }
. LIST#
D $8141 Advances the high coordinate. When it wraps from #N($FF), it is put back and the routine returns zero.
  $8141,$02 Step the high coordinate on and return unless it wrapped to zero.
  $8143,$01 Put the high coordinate back to #N$FF.
  $8144,$01 Keep #REGa in #REGh.
  $8145,$01 Set the zero flag.
  $8146,$01 Bring #REGa back from #REGh.
  $8147,$01 Return.

c $8148 Drawing: Retreat The High Coordinate
@ $8148 label=Drawing_RetreatTheHighCoordinate
E $8148 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$F565 }
. LIST#
D $8148 Retreats the high coordinate. When it was already zero, it is put back and one step is counted.
  $8148,$07 Step the high coordinate back and return unless it wrapped to #N$FF.
  $814F,$01 Put the high coordinate back to #N$00.
  $8150,$01 Return.

c $8151 Drawing: Plot The Selected Pixel
@ $8151 label=Drawing_PlotTheSelectedPixel
E $8151 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$F56E }
. LIST#
D $8151 Plots one line. Bit 0 of the direction chooses which coordinate steps first, and #REGl is how many pixels are drawn.
  $8151,$06 Jump to #R$8185 when bit 0 of the direction is set.
@ $8157 label=Drawing_PlotTheSelectedPixel_AddressThePixel
  $8157,$03 Call #R$81B5 to address the pixel.
  $815A,$04 Jump to #R$8165 if bit 2 of the direction is clear.
  $815E,$03 Call #R$8148 to retreat the high coordinate.
  $8161,$02 Jump to #R$8182 if #R$8148 wrapped the high coordinate.
  $8163,$02 Jump to #R$816A to count this pixel.
@ $8165 label=Drawing_PlotTheSelectedPixel_StepHigh
  $8165,$03 Call #R$8141 to advance the high coordinate.
  $8168,$02 Jump to #R$8182 if #R$8141 wrapped the high coordinate.
@ $816A label=Drawing_PlotTheSelectedPixel_CountTheLength
  $816A,$01 Drop one from the remaining length.
  $816B,$02 Jump to #R$817F if it is not zero.
  $816D,$04 Jump to #R$8178 if bit 1 of the direction is clear.
  $8171,$03 Call #R$813A to retreat the low coordinate.
  $8174,$02 Jump to #R$8182 if #R$813A wrapped the low coordinate.
  $8176,$02 Jump to #R$817D to bring the count back.
@ $8178 label=Drawing_PlotTheSelectedPixel_StepLow
  $8178,$03 Call #R$812B to advance the low coordinate.
  $817B,$02 Jump to #R$8182 if #R$812B wrapped the low coordinate.
@ $817D label=Drawing_PlotTheSelectedPixel_HoldThePixelCount
  $817D,$02 Copy the count, leaving it on the stack.
@ $817F label=Drawing_PlotTheSelectedPixel_CountDownThePixels
  $817F,$03 Loop back to #R$8157 until the pixel count reaches zero.
@ $8182 label=Drawing_PlotTheSelectedPixel_FinishTheFirstPass
  $8182,$02 Restore #REGbc and #REGhl from the stack.
  $8184,$01 Return.
@ $8185 label=Drawing_PlotTheSelectedPixel_OtherDirection
  $8185,$05 The other direction steps the low coordinate first.
@ $8187 label=Drawing_PlotTheSelectedPixel_StepTheLowCoordinate
  $818A,$04 Jump to #R$8195 if bit 1 of the direction is clear.
  $818E,$03 Call #R$813A to retreat the low coordinate.
  $8191,$02 Jump to #R$81B2 if #R$813A wrapped the low coordinate.
  $8193,$02 Jump to #R$819A to skip that picture step.
@ $8195 label=Drawing_PlotTheSelectedPixel_StepLowOtherWay
  $8195,$03 Call #R$812B to advance the low coordinate.
  $8198,$02 Jump to #R$81B2 if #R$812B wrapped the low coordinate.
@ $819A label=Drawing_PlotTheSelectedPixel_CountDownTheLength
  $819A,$03 Drop one from the remaining length, and jump to #R$81AF to skip the high-coordinate step unless it is zero.
  $819D,$04 Jump to #R$81A8 if bit 2 of the direction is clear.
  $81A1,$03 Call #R$8148 to retreat the high coordinate.
  $81A4,$02 Jump to #R$81B2 if #R$8148 wrapped the high coordinate.
  $81A6,$02 Jump to #R$81AD to bring the count back.
@ $81A8 label=Drawing_PlotTheSelectedPixel_StepHighOtherWay
  $81A8,$03 Call #R$8141 to advance the high coordinate.
  $81AB,$02 Jump to #R$81B2 if #R$8141 wrapped the high coordinate.
@ $81AD label=Drawing_PlotTheSelectedPixel_RefreshThePixelCount
  $81AD,$02 Copy the count, leaving it on the stack.
@ $81AF label=Drawing_PlotTheSelectedPixel_RepeatForEachPixel
  $81AF,$03 Loop back to #R$8187 until the pixel count reaches zero.
@ $81B2 label=Drawing_PlotTheSelectedPixel_FinishTheOtherPass
  $81B2,$02 Restore #REGbc and #REGhl from the stack.
  $81B4,$01 Return.

c $81B5 Drawing: Address The Pixel
@ $81B5 label=Drawing_AddressThePixel
E $81B5 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$F5D2 }
. LIST#
D $81B5 Turns the pixel coordinate into a screen address and sets the bit in that screen byte.
  $81B5,$04 Call #R$81DE to turn the pen into a screen address.
  $81B9,$03 Keep the screen address and split out its row bits.
  $81BC,$02,b$01 Move the row bits of the screen address into the attribute address.
  $81BE,$01 Rotate right.
  $81BF,$01 Rotate right.
  $81C0,$01 Rotate right.
  $81C1,$02 Add it on.
  $81C3,$01 Point #REGh at that attribute.
  $81C4,$01 Read the attribute byte.
  $81C5,$0C,b$02,$0A Keep only the paper bits (3-5) of this attribute, and jump to #R$81D3 unless they match the colour in #R$824E shifted into bits 3-5.
  $81D1,$02 Flip the bits selected by #N$38.
@ $81D3 label=Drawing_AddressThePixel_RestoreTheInkBits
  $81D3,$03 Shift the replacement ink back into the ink bits.
  $81D6,$01 Combine it with the attribute on screen.
  $81D7,$01 Store the attribute.
  $81D8,$03 Plot the pixel into the screen byte.
  $81DB,$01 Store the screen byte.
  $81DC,$01 Restore #REGhl from the stack.
  $81DD,$01 Return.

c $81DE Drawing: Screen Address From The Row
@ $81DE label=Drawing_ScreenAddressFromTheRow
D $81DE Builds a Spectrum screen address from the pen in #REGd and #REGe.
  $81DE,$02 The top screen row is #N$7F.
  $81E0,$01 The pixel row is #N$7F minus the low coordinate.
  $81E1,$01 Keep #N$7F.
  $81E2,$02,b$01 Keep the low three bits of the row and select the screen base.
  $81E4,$02 Merge in #N$40.
  $81E6,$01 Store the screen address high byte so far.
  $81E7,$01 Take the pixel row back into #REGa.
  $81E8,$02,b$01 Take the top two bits of the row.
  $81EA,$01 Move the screen-third bits down one place.
  $81EB,$01 Move the screen-third bits down another place.
  $81EC,$01 Move the screen-third bits down to bits 3-4.
  $81ED,$01 Fold them into the screen address.
  $81EE,$01 Store the screen address high byte.
  $81EF,$01 Take the pixel row back into #REGa.
  $81F0,$02,b$01 Take the middle three bits of the row.
  $81F2,$01 Move the middle row bits up one place.
  $81F3,$01 Move them up to bits 5-7.
  $81F4,$01 Those bits start the screen address low byte.
  $81F5,$06 Take the high coordinate.
  $81FB,$01 Fold the character column into the screen address.
  $81FC,$01 Store the screen address low byte.
  $81FD,$01 Take the high coordinate again.
  $81FE,$02,b$01 The low three bits choose the pixel inside the byte.
  $8200,$02 Save #REGbc and keep #REGa in #REGb.
  $8202,$01 Make the rotate count one more than the bit number.
  $8203,$02 Start the pixel mask at #N$01.
@ $8205 label=Drawing_ScreenAddressFromTheRow_ShiftPixelMask
  $8205,$02 Rotate the pixel mask one place right.
  $8207,$02 Decrease counter by one and loop back to #R$8205 until counter is zero.
  $8209,$01 Restore #REGbc from the stack.
  $820A,$01 Return.

c $820B Drawing: Read The Next Picture Byte
@ $820B label=DrawingClear
D $820B Reads the next picture byte. A dark location forces the border and paper to black, then
. the screen and the attributes are cleared.
  $820B,$03 Call #R$95ED to test whether the location is lit.
  $820E,$04 Save the lighting flags and read the next picture byte.
  $8212,$02 Increment the graphic data pointer to the next byte.
  $8214,$01 Switch back to the "normal" #REGaf register to check the result of the call to #R$95ED.
  $8215,$02 Jump to #R$821A if #R$95ED found the location lit (carry clear).
  $8217,$02 Overwrite the graphic data in the shadow #REGaf register to #N$00 (black).
  $8219,$01 Switch back to the "normal" #REGaf register simply to accommodate the jump from #R$8215.
@ $821A label=Drawing_Border
  $821A,$01 Switch to the shadow #REGaf register containing the first byte of the graphic data (or #N$00 if it's dark).
  $821B,$02 Set the border colour from #REGa.
  $821D,$06 Point at the screen buffer at #N$4000.
  $8223,$0A Fill the screen buffer at #N$4000 with zero.
  $822D,$09 Point #REGhl at the attributes at #N($5800), #REGde at the next byte, and set the length to #N($01FF,$04).
  $8236,$03 Read the attribute byte for this picture.
  $8239,$02 Increment the graphic data pointer to the next byte.
  $823B,$01 Use the flags saved for this picture.
  $823C,$02 Jump to #R$8241 if the location is lit (carry clear).
  $823E,$02 Overwrite the graphic data in the shadow #REGaf register to #N$00 (black).
  $8240,$01 Switch back to the "normal" #REGaf register simply to accommodate the jump from #R$823C.
@ $8241 label=Drawing_Paper
  $8241,$01 Switch to the shadow #REGaf register containing the second byte of the graphic data (or #N$00 if it's dark).
  $8242,$03 Write this to the attribute buffer using the target/ length specified previously.
  $8245,$04 Restore the pen from the stack. A dark place ends the picture.
  $8249,$01 Return if it is LIGHT.
  $824A,$04 Jump back to #R$8069 to return.
B $824E,$03

c $8251 Command: Match A Special Key
@ $8251 label=Command_MatchASpecialKey
E $8251 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$AFB3 }
. LIST#
D $8251 Matches a special key such as save, load or inventory.
. Reached through the jump table at #R$75D2, dispatched from #R$75D0.
  $8251,$03 Point at the special-key table.
  $8254,$03 Thirteen entries.
@ $8257 label=Command_MatchASpecialKey_Loop
  $8257,$05 Jump to #R$8260 if this entry's first byte is not the key.
  $825C,$04 Jump to #R$8267 if the second byte matches as well.
@ $8260 label=Command_MatchASpecialKey_Next
  $8260,$04 Step to the next pair, and loop back to #R$8257 until all #N$0D entries have been tried.
  $8264,$03 Jump to #R$75A0 when no special key matched.
@ $8267 label=Command_MatchASpecialKey_JumpToTheHandler
  $8267,$04 The handler word sits #N$19 bytes after the matched pair.
  $826B,$01 Read the low byte of that handler.
  $826C,$01 Step on to its high byte.
  $826D,$01 Read the high byte of the handler.
  $826E,$01 Take the handler address.
  $826F,$02 Jump to the handler this key selected.

b $8271 Table: Special Keys
D $8271 Thirteen token pairs, then the handler word for each pair. #R$8251 scans the pairs and jumps #N$1A bytes on to the handler. The first pair is empty. The rest are #TOKEN($004E), #TOKEN($0245), #TOKEN($039E), #TOKEN($04AD), #TOKEN($04FB), #TOKEN($0479), #TOKEN($03EE), #TOKEN($0580), #TOKEN($0518), #TOKEN($031E), #TOKEN($058A) and #TOKEN($04D8).
  $8271,$02 Empty pair.
  $8273,$02 #WORDPTR
L $8273,$02,$0C
W $828B,$02 Handler for the pair #N$1A bytes back.
L $828B,$02,$0D

c $82A5 Command: #TOKEN($04FB)
@ $82A5 label=Command_PRINT
D $82A5 Turns the printer on when bit 6 of port #N$FB is clear. #R$82AF is #TOKEN($0479), which turns it off.
. Reached through the special-key table at #R$8271, dispatched from #R$8251.
  $82A5,$02 Read the printer port #N$FB.
  $82A7,$02 Test bit 6.
  $82A9,$02 Jump to #R$82B3 if bit 6 is set.
  $82AB,$02 Take #N$01.
  $82AD,$02 Jump to #R$82B0 to store the flag.
@ $82AF label=Command_PRINT_NOPRINT
N $82AF Reached through the special-key table at #R$8271, dispatched from #R$8251.
  $82AF,$01 Clear the accumulator.
  $82B0,$03 Write it to the printer-on flag at #R$B6F2.

c $82B3 Command: Dispatch A Control
@ $82B3 label=CommandDispatchAControl
D $82B3 Dispatches a control key to the routine that handles it.
  $82B3,$03 Read *#R$B6DE.
  $82B6,$01 Keep the class being matched.
  $82B7,$03 Jump back to #R$75B4 to clear the matcher scratch.
N $82BA Test the matcher phase. Reached through the special-key table at #R$8271, dispatched from #R$8251.
  $82BA,$08 Jump to #R$7929 if *#R$B719 is not #N$01.
  $82C2,$05 Write #N$02 to *#R$B719.
  $82C7,$03 Set bit 7 of the next byte of this record.
  $82CA,$02 Merge in #N$80.
  $82CC,$03 Store that byte.
  $82CF,$03 Jump back to #R$75B4 to clear the matcher scratch.
N $82D2 Test the matcher phase. Reached through the special-key table at #R$8271, dispatched from #R$8251.
  $82D2,$08 Jump to #R$75B4 if *#R$B719 is #N$02.
  $82DA,$05 Write #N$01 to *#R$B719.
  $82DF,$03 Jump back to #R$75B4 to clear the matcher scratch.
N $82E2 Reached through the special-key table at #R$8271, dispatched from #R$8251.
  $82E2,$04 Point #REGhl at the six match bytes.
  $82E6,$03 Name the matcher patch at #N$757F as the copy destination.
  $82E9,$05 Copy the prepared bytes.
  $82EE,$03 Restore the caller's pointer from the stack and test its top bit.
  $82F1,$03 Call #R$82F7 to follow the grammar flag.
  $82F4,$03 Jump back to #R$75B4 to clear the matcher scratch.

c $82F7 Command: Follow The Grammar Flag
@ $82F7 label=Command_FollowTheGrammarFlag
D $82F7 Follows the grammar flag for a special key. A clear bit 7 copies ten grammar bytes,
. otherwise four, and the replay path is then scored.
  $82F7,$03 Jump back to #R$7838 if bit 7 of the grammar flag is clear.
  $82FA,$03 Jump to #R$7850 to clear bit 6 and copy the grammar bytes.
@ $82FD label=Command_FollowTheGrammarFlag_Loop
  $82FD,$01 Use the class being matched again.
  $82FE,$03 Store the result at #N$824F.
  $8301,$07 Save #REGde, #REGbc and #REGiy, and read *#R$B706.
  $8308,$03 Remember the replay flag from #R$B706 at #N$8250.
  $830B,$03 Step back one #N$18-byte parser record.
  $830E,$02 Add #N$FFE8 onto this record.
  $8310,$02 Pass #N$01 on to the grammar scorer at #R$758A.
  $8312,$03 Jump back to #R$758A to update the conjunction count.
N $8315 Test the conjunction count. Reached through the special-key table at #R$8271, dispatched from #R$8251.
  $8315,$06 Jump to #R$82FD if *#R$B71B is zero.
  $831B,$01 Step the conjunction count down by one.
  $831C,$03 Update *#R$B71B.
  $831F,$03 Read the saved class.
  $8322,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $8323,$03 Read *#R$B706.
  $8326,$06 Clear #REGc and jump to #R$8378 if the replay flag equals the saved class.
  $832C,$04 Restore the parser pointer from the stack, keeping a copy there.
  $8330,$03 Step back one #N$18-byte parser record.
  $8333,$02 Add #N$FFE8 onto this record.
  $8335,$01 Use the difference as the record count in #REGb.
@ $8336 label=Command_FollowTheGrammarFlag_PointAtTheSlots
  $8336,$04 Point at the first character slot at #R$B738.
  $833A,$05 Each character slot at #R$B738 is #N$19 bytes.
  $833F,$02 Count #N$08 passes.
@ $8341 label=Command_FollowTheGrammarFlag_SkipAUsedSlot
  $8341,$06 Jump to #R$834B if this character slot is empty.
  $8347,$02 Step to the next #N$19-byte character slot.
  $8349,$02 Loop back to #R$8341 until #N$08 runs out.
@ $834B label=Command_FollowTheGrammarFlag_ClaimTheSlot
  $834B,$04 Claim this free slot with #N$FF.
  $834F,$02 Step to the next byte of this character slot.
  $8351,$04 Jump to #R$8378 if every character slot is in use.
  $8355,$06 Jump to #R$8361 if bit 6 of byte 1 of this record is clear.
  $835B,$04 Clear the previous byte of this entry.
  $835F,$02 Jump to #R$8373 to update the matcher phase.
@ $8361 label=Command_FollowTheGrammarFlag_CopyTheRecord
  $8361,$03 Count #N$18 passes.
@ $8364 label=Command_FollowTheGrammarFlag_CopyTheNextByte
  $8364,$06 Copy the next byte into this entry.
  $836A,$02 Step to the next byte of this record.
  $836C,$02 Step to the next byte of this character slot.
  $836E,$02 Loop back to #R$8364 until #N$18 runs out.
  $8370,$03 Restore the record count, and step #REGiy back #N$18 bytes to the start of this record.
@ $8373 label=Command_FollowTheGrammarFlag_StepBackOneRecord
  $8373,$02 Step #REGiy back #N$18 bytes to the previous record.
  $8375,$01 Count one more character slot.
  $8376,$02 Decrease the record count in #REGb and loop back to #R$8336 until it is zero.
@ $8378 label=Command_FollowTheGrammarFlag_ClearTheMatcherPhase
  $8378,$04 Clear *#R$B719.
  $837C,$01 Copy the count in #REGc into #REGa.
  $837D,$03 Store the number of claimed slots at #R$B737.
  $8380,$05 Restore the parser pointer from the stack and restore the replay flag from #N$8250.
  $8385,$03 Update *#R$B706.
  $8388,$05 Restore the saved pair from the stack and read the class from #N$824F.
  $838D,$01 Keep the saved class.
  $838E,$03 Jump back to #R$75B4 to clear the matcher scratch.
N $8391 Reached through the special-key table at #R$8271, dispatched from #R$8251.
  $8391,$03 Call #R$83F5 to print the score.
@ $8394 label=Command_FollowTheGrammarFlag_WaitForAKey
  $8394,$01 Select every keyboard row (#REGa=#N$00).
  $8395,$02 Read port #N$FE into #REGa.
  $8397,$06,b$04,$02 Jump back to #R$8394 while the low five bits are all set.
  $839D,$03 Jump back to #R$6C27 to restart the game.

c $83A0 Display Locate Help Message
@ $83A0 label=DisplayLocateHelpMessage
D $83A0 Prints the hint for the player's current location.
. Reached through the special-key table at #R$8271, dispatched from #R$8251.
N $83A0 Test whose turn it is.
  $83A0,$07 Jump to #R$75B4 if *#R$B6EA is not zero.
  $83A7,$06 Save #REGhl and #REGix, and point #REGhl at #R$B467.
N $83AA Default message response should there be no match for the current location.
N $83AD Use the current location ID to look-up if there is a specific help message.
  $83AD,$0A Using the #R$C12B(current location number), see if there is a help message available for display. This is achieved by passing #R$83CD to the #R$9DBD(Index ID Table) routine.
  $83B7,$02 If there is no message, jump directly to #R$83BF to display the default message.
N $83B9 Point to the specific help for the current location.
  $83B9,$03 Read the low byte of this location's hint message address.
  $83BC,$03 Read the high byte of this location's hint message address.
@ $83BF label=DisplayLocateHelpMessage_Print
N $83BF Print the message.
  $83BF,$05 Write #N$01 to #R$B701.
  $83C4,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $83C7,$06 Jump back to #R$82B3 to dispatch a control.

b $83CD Locate Help Message
@ $83CD label=LocateHelpMessage
D $83CD Read by #R$83A0.
  $83CD,$01 Location #LOCATION(#PEEK(#PC)) - "#LOCATIONNAME(#PEEK(#PC))".
W $83CE,$02 #TEXTMESSAGE(#PC)
L $83CD,$03,$0B
  $83EE,$01 #TERMINATOR

c $83EF Game Over
@ $83EF label=GameOver
D $83EF Ends the game and prints the percentage complete.
. Reached through the special-key table at #R$8271, dispatched from #R$8251.
  $83EF,$03 Call #R$83F5 to print the score.
  $83F2,$03 Jump back to #R$82B3 to dispatch a control.

c $83F5 Game Over: Print The Score
@ $83F5 label=GameOver_Start
D $83F5 Prints the score as a percentage of the adventure, taken from the percentage complete,
. and finishes the game-over text.
N $83F5 Actions "game over".
  $83F5,$06 Write #N$00 to #R$B701.
  $83FB,$03 Point at the message "you have mastered [0x16]" (#R$B454).
  $83FE,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $8401,$03 Read the percentage complete from #R$B6F7.
  $8404,$03 Divide by #N($0064,$04,$04) (100).
  $8407,$03 Call #R$842E to make the hundreds digit.
  $840A,$03 Call #R$858B if #R$842E made a hundreds digit other than zero.
  $840D,$03 Divide by #N($000A,$04,$04) (10).
  $8410,$03 Call #R$842E to make the tens digit.
  $8413,$03 Call #R$858B to print one character.
  $8416,$02 Use the character '.'.
  $8418,$03 Call #R$858B to print one character.
  $841B,$01 Copy the remainder into #REGa as the percentage digit.
  $841C,$02 Add #N$30 to make the ASCII digit.
  $841E,$03 Call #R$858B to print one character.
  $8421,$04 Clear the capitalise-the-next-letter flag.
  $8425,$03 Point at the message "% of this adventure" (#R$B462).
  $8428,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $842B,$02 Restore #REGde and #REGhl from the stack.
  $842D,$01 Return.

c $842E Print: Percentage Digit
@ $842E label=PercentageAscii
D $842E Turns one decimal digit of the score into ASCII, counting up from '0'.
N $842E Converts e.g. "0075" into "7.5" in ASCII for display purposes.
  $842E,$02 Start the quotient digit one below '0'.
@ $8430 label=PercentageAscii_Loop
  $8430,$01 Count the digit up by one.
  $8431,$01 Clear carry for the subtraction.
  $8432,$02 Subtract the power of ten in #REGde from #REGhl.
  $8434,$02 Jump back to #R$8430 to count another digit unless the subtraction went below zero.
  $8436,$01 Add the power of ten back to leave the remainder.
  $8437,$02 Compare against #N$30 (ASCII "0") to set flags for the return.
  $8439,$01 Return.
N $843A Reached through the special-key table at #R$8271, dispatched from #R$8251.
  $843A,$04 Set the border to #N$04 (green).
  $843E,$03 Call #R$84B9 to debounce keyboard.
@ $8441 label=Wait_ForKey
  $8441,$03 Read from the keyboard port.
  $8444,$02,b$01 Keep bits 0-4, one for each key in the half-row. XOR A then IN A,(#N$FE) reads every half-row at once, and a pressed key reads as a reset bit.
  $8446,$04 Loop back to #R$8441 while a key is being pressed.
  $844A,$04 Set the border to #N$07 (white).
  $844E,$03 Jump back to #R$82B3 to dispatch a control.

c $8451 Load Game
@ $8451 label=LoadGame
D $8451 Loads a saved game back into the live state.
. Reached through the special-key table at #R$8271, dispatched from #R$8251.
  $8451,$05 Set the load as a "data block".
N $8454 Load "flags".
  $8456,$01 Set carry so the ROM loads the saved-game header.
  $8457,$04 Set the start address to #R$B6EB.
  $845B,$03 Set the block length to #N$1D bytes.
  $845E,$03 Call #R$8498 to read the save block.
N $8461 Load "objects".
  $8461,$02 Set the load as a "data block".
  $8463,$01 Set carry so the ROM loads the object table.
  $8464,$04 Set the start address to #R$C11B.
  $8468,$03 Set the block length to #N($0615,$04) bytes.
  $846B,$03 Call #R$8498 to read the save block.
N $846E Load...
  $846E,$02 Set the load as a "data block".
  $8470,$01 Set carry so the ROM loads the object handlers.
  $8471,$04 Set the start address to #R$CA84.
  $8475,$03 Set the block length to #N$BF bytes.
  $8478,$03 Call #R$8498 to read the save block.
N $847B Load "locations".
  $847B,$02 Set the load as a "data block".
  $847D,$01 Set carry so the ROM loads the location table.
  $847E,$04 Set the start address to #R$BA8A.
  $8482,$03 Set the block length to #N($05D9,$04) bytes.
  $8485,$03 Call #R$8498 to read the save block.
N $8488 Success!
  $8488,$01 Disable interrupts.
  $8489,$09 Call #R$84B3 with #REGhl pointing at #R$B6EB and #REGde pointing at #N$C9E2.
  $8492,$06 Jump back to #R$82B3 to dispatch a control.

c $8498 Load: Read The Save Block
@ $8498 label=LoadBlock
D $8498 Reads a save block from tape, prints the prompt at #R$B357, and waits for a key before
. returning to the loader.
N $8498 Load Block
  $8498,$03 Call #SKOOLKID$0556(LD_BYTES).
  $849B,$01 Return if the tape block loaded (carry set).
  $849C,$05 Write #N$01 to #R$B701.
  $84A1,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$B357.
  $84A4,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
@ $84A7 label=LoadBlock_Loop
  $84A7,$03 Read from the keyboard port.
  $84AA,$02,b$01 Keep bits 0-4, one for each key in the half-row. XOR A then IN A,(#N$FE) reads every half-row at once, and a pressed key reads as a reset bit.
  $84AC,$04 Loop back to #R$84A7 until any key has been pressed.
  $84B0,$03 Jump back to #R$6C27 to restart the game.

c $84B3 Copy Three Bytes
@ $84B3 label=ThreeByteCopy
D $84B3 Copies three bytes from a source record to a destination.
R $84B3 DE Source
R $84B3 HL Target
  $84B3,$03 The copy length is #N$03.
  $84B6,$02 Copy three bytes of data from #REGde to #REGhl.
  $84B8,$01 Return.

c $84B9 Debounce keyboard
@ $84B9 label=DebounceAnyKey
D $84B9 Waits until the current key has been released.
  $84B9,$03 Read from the keyboard port.
  $84BC,$02,b$01 Keep bits 0-4, one for each key in the half-row. XOR A then IN A,(#N$FE) reads every half-row at once, and a pressed key reads as a reset bit.
  $84BE,$04 Loop back to #R$84B9 if a key is being pressed.
@ $84C2 label=Normal_AnyKey
  $84C2,$03 Read from the keyboard port.
  $84C5,$02,b$01 Keep bits 0-4, one for each key in the half-row. XOR A then IN A,(#N$FE) reads every half-row at once, and a pressed key reads as a reset bit.
  $84C7,$04 Loop back to #R$84C2 until any key has been pressed.
  $84CB,$01 Return.

c $84CC Save Game
@ $84CC label=SaveGame
D $84CC Saves the live game to tape.
. Reached through the special-key table at #R$8271, dispatched from #R$8251.
  $84CC,$0C Save #REGix and #REGde, then call #R$84B3 with #REGde at #R$B6EB and #REGhl at #N$C9E2.
  $84D8,$05 Write #N$01 to #R$B701.
  $84DD,$03 Point at the message "start tape then press any key" (#R$B342).
  $84E0,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $84E3,$03 Call #R$84B9 to debounce keyboard.
N $84E6 Save "flags".
  $84E6,$02 Set the save as a "data block".
  $84E8,$04 Set the start address to #R$B6EB.
  $84EC,$03 Set the block length to #N$1D bytes.
  $84EF,$03 Call #SKOOLKID$04C2(SA_BYTES).
N $84F2 Save "objects".
  $84F2,$02 Set the save as a "data block".
  $84F4,$04 Set the start address to #R$C11B.
  $84F8,$03 Set the block length to #N($0615,$04) bytes.
  $84FB,$03 Call #SKOOLKID$04C2(SA_BYTES).
N $84FE Save...
  $84FE,$02 Set the save as a "data block".
  $8500,$04 Set the start address to #R$CA84.
  $8504,$03 Set the block length to #N$BF bytes.
  $8507,$03 Call #SKOOLKID$04C2(SA_BYTES).
N $850A Save "locations".
  $850A,$02 Set the save as a "data block".
  $850C,$04 Set the start address to #R$BA8A.
  $8510,$03 Set the block length to #N($05D9,$04) bytes.
  $8513,$03 Call #SKOOLKID$04C2(SA_BYTES).
N $8516 Begin verification.
  $8516,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$B3A4.
  $8519,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $851C,$03 Call #R$84B9 to debounce keyboard.
N $851F Verify "flags".
  $851F,$02 Set the load as a "data block".
  $8521,$01 Clear carry so the ROM verifies the flags.
  $8522,$04 Set the start address to #R$B6EB.
  $8526,$03 Set the block length to #N$1D bytes.
  $8529,$03 Call #R$855A to verify the block.
N $852C Verify "objects".
  $852C,$02 Set the load as a "data block".
  $852E,$01 Clear carry so the ROM verifies the object table.
  $852F,$04 Set the start address to #R$C11B.
  $8533,$03 Set the block length to #N($0615,$04) bytes.
  $8536,$03 Call #R$855A to verify the block.
N $8539 Verify the object handlers.
  $8539,$02 Set the load as a "data block".
  $853B,$01 Clear carry so the ROM verifies the object handlers.
  $853C,$04 Set the start address to #R$CA84.
  $8540,$03 Set the block length to #N$BF bytes.
  $8543,$03 Call #R$855A to verify the block.
N $8546 Verify "locations".
  $8546,$02 Set the load as a "data block".
  $8548,$01 Clear carry so the ROM verifies the location table.
  $8549,$04 Set the start address to #R$BA8A.
  $854D,$03 Set the block length to #N($05D9,$04) bytes.
  $8550,$03 Call #R$855A to verify the block.
N $8553 Success!
@ $8553 label=SaveGame_Done
  $8553,$01 Disable interrupts.
  $8554,$06 Jump back to #R$82B3 to dispatch a control.

c $855A Save: Verify A Block
@ $855A label=VerifyBlock
D $855A Verifies a block just saved, using LD_BYTES with carry clear. A tape error prints "tape error - hit any key to continue"
. and waits for a key.
N $855A Verify block.
  $855A,$03 Call #SKOOLKID$0556(LD_BYTES).
  $855D,$01 Return if the block verified (carry set).
  $855E,$05 Write #N$01 to #R$B701.
  $8563,$03 Point at the message "tape error - hit any key to continue[0x15]" (#R$B381).
  $8566,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
N $8569 This is the same key wait as #R$84C2.
@ $8569 label=VerifyBlock_WaitForKey
  $8569,$03 Read from the keyboard port.
  $856C,$02,b$01 Keep bits 0-4, one for each key in the half-row. XOR A then IN A,(#N$FE) reads every half-row at once, and a pressed key reads as a reset bit.
  $856E,$04 Loop back to #R$8569 until any key has been pressed.
  $8572,$04 Jump back to #R$8553 to dispatch a control.

c $8576 Print: Gate On The Time Of Day
@ $8576 label=PrintGateOnTheTimeOfDay
D $8576 Gates printing on the time of day, and prints a character once the
. gate allows it.
  $8576,$05 Read the message gate.
  $857B,$01 Keep the message gate.
  $857C,$03 Read the day flag.
  $857F,$02 See whether the day flag meets the message gate, and bring the character back.
  $8581,$01 Restore #REGhl from the stack.
  $8582,$01 Return.

c $8583 Print: A Newline
@ $8583 label=Print_LineBreak
E $8583 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$A2A2 }
. LIST#
D $8583 Prints a carriage return.
  $8583,$03 Use ENTER as the character.
  $8586,$03 Call #R$858B to print one character.
  $8589,$01 Restore #REGaf from the stack.
  $858A,$01 Return.

c $858B Print: One Character
@ $858B label=Print_Character
D $858B Prints the character in the accumulator, after the day and night filter.
  $858B,$03 Call #R$8576 to gate printing on the time of day.
  $858E,$01 Return if #R$8576 found the day flag does not meet the message gate.
  $858F,$07 If #R$B701 is not zero then jump to #R$85B7.
  $8596,$04 Restore the character from the stack and call #R$86A1 to draw it.
  $859A,$07 If #R$B700 is not zero then jump to #R$85A3.
  $85A1,$01 Restore the character from the stack.
  $85A2,$01 Return.
@ $85A3 label=Print_Character_TestForAnS
  $85A3,$05 If this is ASCII "S" then jump to #R$85AB.
  $85A8,$03 If this is not ASCII "s" then return.
@ $85AB label=Print_Character_PrintAnH
  $85AB,$03 Use the character 'H'.
  $85AE,$03 Call #R$86A1 to draw the character on the screen.
  $85B1,$01 Restore #REGaf from the stack.
  $85B2,$01 Return.
  $85B3,$01 Characters left on the current line (#N$20 at the start of a line).
B $85B4,$03 Percentage buffer.
@ $85B4 label=PercentageBuffer
@ $85B7 label=Print_Character_TestForAReturn
  $85B7,$0A Save the character and the text pointer, and jump to #R$85C8 if the character is not a carriage return (#N$0D).
N $85C1 Print a space.
  $85C1,$05 Print a space.
  $85C6,$02 Jump to #R$85DF to start a new line.
@ $85C8 label=Print_Character_TestForBackspace
  $85C8,$04 Jump to #R$85F5 if the character is #N$08.
  $85CC,$04 Jump to #R$85D6 if the character is below #N$61.
  $85D0,$04 Jump to #R$85D6 if the character is not below #N$7B.
  $85D4,$02 Keep bits 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 6.
@ $85D6 label=Print_Character_DrawThisCharacterWithTheROMFont
N $85D6 Draw this character with the ROM font.
  $85D6,$03 Call #R$867A to draw this character with the ROM font.
  $85D9,$06 Count down the characters left on the line, and jump to #R$85E6 unless the line is now full.
@ $85DF label=Print_Character_StartANewLine
  $85DF,$02 Move the print position back to the start of the line (#N$E0).
  $85E1,$03 Call #R$860D to scroll the line buffer up.
  $85E4,$02 Reset the count to #N$20 for the new line.
@ $85E6 label=Print_Character_StoreTheCharactersLeft
  $85E6,$03 Store the result at #R$85B3.
  $85E9,$03 Read the spare grammar byte.
  $85EC,$03 Store #REGhl at #R$85B4.
N $85EF Draw this character with the ROM font.
  $85EF,$03 Call #R$867A to draw this character with the ROM font.
  $85F2,$02 Restore #REGaf and #REGhl from the stack.
  $85F4,$01 Return.
@ $85F5 label=Print_Character_RubOut
N $85F5 Print a space.
  $85F5,$05 Print a space over the character being rubbed out.
  $85FA,$0A Step the print position back, add one to the characters left, and jump back to #R$85E6 unless that goes past the start of the line (#N$21).
  $8604,$02 The previous pattern byte is at offset #N$FF.
  $8606,$03 Call #R$864A to scroll the attribute line up.
  $8609,$02 Continue with #N$01 now that the character is drawn.
  $860B,$02 Jump back to #R$85E6 to draw this character.

c $860D Print: Scroll The Line Buffer
@ $860D label=Print_ScrollTheLineBuffer
D $860D Scrolls the line buffer up by four rows of #N$20 bytes and fills the vacated row with
. spaces.
  $860D,$07 Save #REGhl, #REGde, #REGbc and #REGaf, and point #REGhl at the screen line #N$5080.
  $8614,$03 Point #REGde at the line buffer.
  $8617,$02 Load #N$04 before the day and night test.
  $8619,$02 Start the token count at zero.
@ $861B label=Print_ScrollTheLineBuffer_ScrollOneCharacterRow
  $861B,$04 Save the screen pointers and count #N$08 pixel rows.
@ $861F label=Print_ScrollTheLineBuffer_Loop
  $861F,$07 Copy the prepared bytes.
  $8626,$08 Restore the screen pointers from the stack, step to the next pixel row, and jump back to #R$861F until the count in #REGc reaches zero.
  $862E,$04 Restore the saved screen pointers from the stack and set #REGbc to #N($20), one screen row.
  $8632,$01 Step one screen row, #N$20 bytes.
  $8633,$01 Swap the two screen pointers.
  $8634,$01 Step the other pointer one screen row, #N$20 bytes.
  $8635,$01 Swap the two screen pointers.
  $8636,$03 Jump back to #R$861B until the row count in #REGa reaches zero.
  $8639,$02 Count #N$20 passes.
  $863B,$03 Point #REGhl at the attribute line.
  $863E,$02 Use the character ' '.
@ $8640 label=Print_ScrollTheLineBuffer_BlankTheNewLine
N $8640 Blank the new line with spaces.
  $8640,$03 Call #R$867A to draw this character with the ROM font.
  $8643,$02 Loop back to #R$8640 until #N$20 runs out.
  $8645,$04 Restore #REGaf, #REGbc, #REGde and #REGhl from the stack.
  $8649,$01 Return.

c $864A Print: Scroll The Attribute Line
@ $864A label=Print_ScrollTheAttributeLine
D $864A Scrolls the attribute line up by five rows and restores the pointers.
  $864A,$07 Point at #N$50C0.
  $8651,$03 Point #REGde at the attribute line.
  $8654,$02 Load #N$05 before the day and night test.
@ $8656 label=Print_ScrollTheAttributeLine_ScrollOneRow
  $8656,$04 Count #N$08 passes.
@ $865A label=Print_ScrollTheAttributeLine_CopyThePreparedBytes
  $865A,$08 Copy the prepared bytes.
  $8662,$04 Restore #REGbc, #REGde and #REGhl from the stack, and step #REGhl down one pixel row.
  $8666,$01 Step #REGde down one pixel row.
  $8667,$02 Loop back to #R$865A until all #N$08 pixel rows are done.
  $8669,$06 Restore the screen pointers and step #REGhl back one row.
  $866F,$01 Swap the two screen pointers.
  $8670,$01 Step the other pointer up one screen row.
  $8671,$01 Swap the two screen pointers.
  $8672,$03 Jump back to #R$8656 until the row count in #REGa reaches zero.
  $8675,$04 Restore #REGaf, #REGbc, #REGde and #REGhl from the stack.
  $8679,$01 Return.

c $867A Print: ROM Font
@ $867A label=Print_RomFont
D $867A Draws one character with the Spectrum ROM font.
R $867A A ASCII value of character to print
N $867A Prints using the standard ZX Spectrum #SKOOLKID$3D00(CHARSET).
  $867A,$0C Save #REGaf, #REGbc, #REGde and #REGhl, subtract #N($20), and multiply the offset by eight.
  $8686,$04 Calculate font graphic data address using #SKOOLKID$3D00(CHARSET) + offset.
  $868A,$03 Store the result in #REGde, restore the screen location to #REGhl.
@ $868F label=Print_RomFont_Loop
  $868D,$02 Set a counter for #N$08 lines.
  $868F,$02 Fetch the font byte and copy it to the screen.
  $8691,$01 Move onto the next font data byte.
  $8692,$01 Move the screen pointer down one row.
  $8693,$02 Decrease counter by one and loop back to #R$868F until counter is zero.
  $8695,$05 Move the screen pointer across one column, ready for printing the next character.
  $869A,$01 Return.

c $869B Print: Write A Character To The Screen
@ $869B label=PrintWriteACharacterToTheScreen
D $869B Writes one character into the text window, scrolling when the line
. is full.
B $869B,$01
W $869C,$02
B $869E,$01
B $869F,$01
B $86A0,$01
  $86A1,$06 Save #REGhl, #REGbc and #REGaf, and bring #REGhl back from #R$869C.
  $86A7,$03 Read the print column.
  $86AA,$01 Copy the result into #REGc.
N $86AB Test this flag.
  $86AB,$06 Jump to #R$86C6 if *#R$86A0 is not zero.
N $86B1 Test this flag.
  $86B1,$06 Jump to #R$86C6 if *#R$869F is zero.
  $86B7,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
@ $86B8 label=PrintWriteACharacterToTheScreen_Loop
  $86B8,$02 Use the character ' '.
N $86BA Draw this character with the game font.
  $86BA,$03 Call #R$87C9 to draw this character with the game's font.
  $86BD,$03 Read the room left on the line at #R$869B.
  $86C0,$01 Drop one from the room left on the line.
  $86C1,$03 Update the room left on the line.
  $86C4,$02 Loop back to #R$86B8 until the space count runs out.
@ $86C6 label=PrintWriteACharacterToTheScreen_TestForAReturn
  $86C6,$02 Restore the character from the stack, keeping a copy there.
  $86C8,$04 Jump to #R$870D unless the character is #N$0D.
  $86CC,$05 Set the capitalise-the-next-letter flag after a carriage return.
@ $86D1 label=PrintWriteACharacterToTheScreen_ClearTheFlag
  $86D1,$04 Clear *#R$86A0.
  $86D5,$03 Call #R$8B22 to copy this text line to the ZX printer.
  $86D8,$01 Save #REGbc.
N $86D9 Test how many words will be accepted.
  $86D9,$06 Jump to #R$86F3 if *#R$B716 is not zero.
  $86DF,$03 Point #REGbc at #R$8000.
@ $86E2 label=PrintWriteACharacterToTheScreen_PollKeys
  $86E2,$01 Select every keyboard row.
  $86E3,$02 Read port #N$FE into #REGa.
  $86E5,$06 Jump to #R$86F7 unless the low five bits are all set.
  $86EB,$05 Loop back to #R$86E2 until the keyboard timeout reaches zero.
  $86F0,$03 Jump to #R$8701 to scroll line.
@ $86F3 label=PrintWriteACharacterToTheScreen_CountDownTheWords
  $86F3,$01 Count down how many words will be accepted.
  $86F4,$03 Update *#R$B716.
@ $86F7 label=PrintWriteACharacterToTheScreen_DropTheTimeout
  $86F7,$02 Drop the timeout and select every keyboard row.
@ $86F8 label=PrintWriteACharacterToTheScreen_WaitForRelease
  $86F9,$02 Read port #N$FE into #REGa.
  $86FB,$06 Jump back to #R$86F8 unless the low five bits are all set.
@ $8701 label=PrintWriteACharacterToTheScreen_PointAtTheTextLine
  $8701,$03 Point #REGhl at the text line.
  $8704,$02 One pixel row of the character.
  $8706,$03 Call #R$876B to scroll line.
  $8709,$02 Reset the room left on the line to #N$2A.
  $870B,$02 Jump to #R$8753 to store the room left on the line.
@ $870D label=PrintWriteACharacterToTheScreen_TestBackspace
  $870D,$04 Jump to #R$8722 if the character is not #N$08.
  $8711,$03 Call #R$8761 to wrap the print column.
  $8714,$02 Use the character ' '.
N $8716 Draw this character with the game font.
  $8716,$03 Call #R$87C9 to draw this character with the game's font.
  $8719,$03 Call #R$8761 to wrap the print column.
  $871C,$03 Read the room left on the line at #R$869B.
  $871F,$01 Count one more character on the line.
  $8720,$02 Jump to #R$8753 to store the room left on the line.
@ $8722 label=PrintWriteACharacterToTheScreen_TestForALetter
  $8722,$04 Jump to #R$872C if the character is below #N$41.
  $8726,$04 Jump to #R$872C if the character is #N$5B or above.
  $872A,$02 Merge in #N$20.
@ $872C label=PrintWriteACharacterToTheScreen_TestCapitalise
  $872C,$04 Point #REGhl at the capitalise-the-next-letter flag.
  $8730,$02 Test the capitalise-the-next-letter flag without changing it.
  $8732,$02 Jump to #R$8740 if the next letter should not be capitalised.
  $8734,$04 Jump to #R$8740 if the character is below #N$61.
  $8738,$04 Jump to #R$8740 if it is #N$7B or above.
  $873C,$02 Clear bit 5.
  $873E,$02 Clear the capitalise-the-next-letter flag once the letter is upper case.
@ $8740 label=PrintWriteACharacterToTheScreen_TestForAFullStop
  $8740,$04 Jump to #R$8745 unless it is #N$2E.
  $8744,$01 A full stop bumps the capitalise-the-next-letter flag.
@ $8745 label=PrintWriteACharacterToTheScreen_DrawTheGlyph
  $8745,$04 Pop the screen address and call #R$87C9 to draw the glyph.
N $8746 Draw this character with the game font.
  $8749,$0A Store the column and jump back to #R$86D1 when the room left on the line reaches zero.
@ $8753 label=PrintWriteACharacterToTheScreen_StoreTheRoomLeft
  $8753,$03 Update the room left on the line.
  $8756,$03 Store #REGhl at #R$869C.
  $8759,$04 Store the print column.
  $875D,$03 Restore #REGaf, #REGbc and #REGhl from the stack.
  $8760,$01 Return.

c $8761 Print: Wrap The Column
@ $8761 label=Print_WrapTheColumn
E $8761 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$C267 }
. LIST#
D $8761 Wraps the print column. A column below #N$06 is moved on by #N($08), and the count drops
. by one.
  $8761,$05 Take #N$06 off the column and return if it did not go below zero.
  $8766,$02 Otherwise add #N$08 to it.
  $8768,$01 Copy the result into #REGc.
  $8769,$01 Drop one from the count.
  $876A,$01 Return.

c $876B Scroll Line
@ $876B label=ScrollLine
D $876B Scrolls the text window up by one line.
  $876B,$07 Save #REGaf, #REGbc, #REGhl and #REGde, and point #REGhl at screen line #N$4020.
N $876F Set up the initial values.
  $8772,$03 Point #REGde at the display file.
  $8775,$02 Load #N$11 for the scroll.
  $8777,$02 Start the pass count at zero.
@ $8779 label=ScrollLine_Loop
  $8779,$04 Save the screen pointers and count #N$08 pixel rows.
@ $877D label=ScrollLine_Line
  $877D,$05 Save the row addresses and the pixel count, and set the byte count to #N$20.
  $8782,$02 Copy the row up one.
  $8784,$05 Increment the MSB of #REGhl and #REGde by one to point to the next line down.
  $8789,$01 Drop one pixel row.
  $878A,$02 Jump back to #R$877D unless the whole line is moved (i.e. #REGc is now zero).
  $878C,$04 Restore the saved screen pointers from the stack and set #REGbc to #N($20), one screen row.
  $8790,$01 Step one screen row, #N$20 bytes.
  $8791,$01 Swap the two screen pointers.
  $8792,$01 Step the other pointer one screen row, #N$20 bytes.
  $8793,$01 Swap the two screen pointers.
  $8794,$02 Save #REGaf and use #REGd as the next byte.
  $8796,$02,b$01 Keep the selected bits.
  $8798,$02 Skip to #R$879E if zero.
N $879A Handle changing the target between each screen buffer area.
  $879A,$01 Take the high byte of #REGde.
  $879B,$02 Add #N$07 onto it.
  $879D,$01 Store the row address past the screen-third gap.
@ $879E label=ScrollLine_SkipDE
  $879E,$01 Take the high byte of the screen address.
  $879F,$02,b$01 Keep the selected bits.
  $87A1,$02 Skip to #R$87A7 if zero.
N $87A3 Handle changing the source between each screen buffer area.
  $87A3,$01 Take the high byte of the screen address.
  $87A4,$02 Add #N$07 onto it.
  $87A6,$01 Store the other row address past the screen-third gap.
@ $87A7 label=ScrollLine_SkipHL
  $87A7,$02 Restore the line counter and drop one line.
  $87A9,$02 Loop back to #R$8779 until the line counter is zero.
N $87AB Handle the attributes.
  $87AB,$0B Move the attributes up one character block.
N $87B6 This creates the indent.
  $87B6,$02 Set a counter for #N($2A,$02,$03,$01,$01)($) spaces.
  $87B8,$03 Set the target screen location to #N($5020,$02,$03,$01,$01)($).
  $87BB,$02 One column of the scrolled line.
  $87BD,$02 Use the character ' '.
@ $87BF label=ScrollLine_Spacing
  $87BF,$03 Call #R$87C9 to draw this character with the game's font.
  $87C2,$02 Decrease counter by one and loop back to #R$87BF until counter is zero.
N $87C4 Housekeeping and return.
  $87C4,$04 Restore #REGde, #REGhl, #REGbc and #REGaf from the stack.
  $87C8,$01 Return.

c $87C9 Print: Proportional Character
@ $87C9 label=Print_ProportionalCharacter
E $87C9 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$C388 }
. LIST#
D $87C9 Draws one character with the game's own font.
R $87C9 A ASCII value
R $87C9 C Bit offset
R $87C9 HL Target screen location
N $87C9 Prints using the custom #R$8822(font).
  $87C9,$07 Save #REGaf, #REGbc, #REGde and #REGhl, and put #REGa in #REGhl.
N $87CD Calculate font graphic address, some examples would be;
. #TABLE(default,centre,centre,centre,centre,centre)
. { =h Letter | =h ASCII | =h * 8 | =h Sum | =h Font Graphic Address }
. { #LET(id=$21) "#CHR({id})" | #N({id},$02,$03,$01,$01)($) | #N(#EVAL({id}*$08),$02,$03,$01,$01)($) | (#N({id},$02,$03,$01,$01)($)*#N$08)+#N$8722 | #R(#EVAL($8722+({id}*$08))) }
. { #LET(id=$41) "#CHR({id})" | #N({id},$02,$03,$01,$01)($) | #N(#EVAL({id}*$08),$02,$03,$01,$01)($) | (#N({id},$02,$03,$01,$01)($)*#N$08)+#N$8722 | #R(#EVAL($8722+({id}*$08))) }
. { #LET(id=$61) "#CHR({id})" | #N({id},$02,$03,$01,$01)($) | #N(#EVAL({id}*$08),$02,$03,$01,$01)($) | (#N({id},$02,$03,$01,$01)($)*#N$08)+#N$8722 | #R(#EVAL($8722+({id}*$08))) }
. TABLE#
N $87D0 Calculate font graphic data address.
  $87D0,$07 Multiply the offset by #N$08 and add the table base.
  $87D7,$03 Store the result in #REGde, restore the screen location to #REGhl.
  $87DA,$02 Set a counter for #N$08 lines.
@ $87DC label=Print_ProportionalCharacter_Loop
  $87DC,$01 Read the next byte of the font graphic data.
  $87DD,$07 Jump to #R$87EB if the count in #REGc is zero.
@ $87E4 label=Print_ProportionalCharacter_Shift
  $87E4,$02 Shift the font graphic data right.
  $87E6,$02 Shift the mask data right.
  $87E8,$01 Drop one from the count.
  $87E9,$02 Loop back to #R$87E4 until the count in #REGc runs out.
@ $87EB label=Print_ProportionalCharacter_SkipShift
  $87EB,$01 Stash the font graphic data in #REGc.
  $87EC,$01 Take the mask that was built in #REGb.
  $87ED,$01 Invert the mask so the new pixels replace the old ones.
  $87EE,$01 Merge the mask with the existing data in the screen buffer.
  $87EF,$01 OR the font graphic data.
  $87F0,$01 Copy the processed font graphic data to the screen.
  $87F1,$05 Jump to #R$880E if the count in #REGc is zero.
  $87F6,$03 Eight pixel bits in a character row. Subtract how many have already been shifted.
  $87F9,$01 Take the shift count off #N$08.
  $87FA,$01 Copy the result into #REGc.
  $87FB,$01 Read the font byte.
  $87FC,$02 Start the mask with every bit set.
@ $87FE label=Print_ProportionalCharacter_ShiftTheMask
  $87FE,$07 Shift the character byte and the mask, and loop until #REGc is zero.
  $8805,$01 Copy the result into #REGc.
  $8806,$01 Take the mask into #REGa.
  $8807,$01 Complement #REGa.
  $8808,$01 Step to the next screen byte.
  $8809,$02 Mask the screen byte and merge the character bits.
  $880B,$01 Store the merged byte on screen.
  $880C,$02 Step back one byte through the text.
@ $880E label=Print_ProportionalCharacter_StepDownARow
  $880E,$01 Step to the next byte beside it.
  $880F,$01 Step the screen address down one pixel row.
  $8810,$02 Decrease counter by one and loop back to #R$87DC until counter is zero.
  $8812,$0A Jump to #R$881F if the column plus #N$06 is below #N$08.
  $881C,$02 Take #N$08 off the column plus #N$06.
  $881E,$01 Step to the next character cell on this line.
@ $881F label=Print_ProportionalCharacter_StoreTheColumn
  $881F,$01 Copy the result into #REGc.
  $8820,$01 Restore #REGaf from the stack.
  $8821,$01 Return.

b $8822 Main Font
@ $8822 label=MainFont
D $8822 #N$60 characters of #N$08 bytes each, from #N$20 to #N$7F. Read by #R$87C9, which adds #N$08 times the character code to #N($8722), so character #N$20 is the first.
N $8822 #LET(id=#EVAL($20+(#PC-$8822)/$08))CHARACTER: "#MAP({id})(#CHR({id}),$20:SPACE,$60:£,$7F:CLARET)".
  $8822,$08,b$01 #UDG(#PC)
L $8822,$08,$60

c $8B22 Print: ZX Printer
@ $8B22 label=ZXPrinter
D $8B22 Copies eight pixel rows of the text line at #N$5020 to the ZX printer
. through port #N$FB when the printer-on flag at #R$B6F2 is set.
  $8B22,$03 Read the printer-on flag at #R$B6F2.
  $8B25,$02 Return if the printer is off.
  $8B27,$05 Save the text pointer. The printer row count starts at #N$01.
  $8B2C,$03 Point #REGhl at the text line.
  $8B2F,$01 Take zero for the first printer byte.
  $8B30,$01 Clear the marker's low byte.
@ $8B31 label=ZXPrinter_WriteAByteToThePrinter
  $8B31,$02 Write a byte to the printer.
@ $8B33 label=ZXPrinter_Loop
  $8B33,$02 Read the printer port #N$FB.
  $8B35,$01 Double the printer status.
  $8B36,$03 Jump to #R$8B74 if bit 6 of the printer port is set (no printer attached).
  $8B39,$02 Jump back to #R$8B33 while bit 7 of the printer port is clear.
  $8B3B,$03 Save the line address and the marker, and take the row count into #REGa.
  $8B3E,$02 Compare the printer row count with #N$02.
  $8B40,$01 Make #REGa #N$FF when that row count was below #N($02), and zero otherwise.
  $8B41,$04 Build the marker bit from #REGe.
@ $8B45 label=ZXPrinter_ReadAByteFromThePointer
  $8B45,$01 Read a byte from the pointer into #REGc.
  $8B46,$03 Count #N$08 passes.
@ $8B49 label=ZXPrinter_TakeThePrinterByte
  $8B49,$01 Take the byte being sent to the printer.
  $8B4A,$02 Rotate the next source bit into carry.
  $8B4C,$01 Shift that bit into the byte for the printer.
  $8B4D,$01 Remember the byte built so far.
@ $8B4E label=ZXPrinter_WaitWhileThePrinterIsBusy
  $8B4E,$02 Read the printer port #N$FB.
  $8B50,$03 Jump back to #R$8B4E while the printer is busy.
  $8B53,$01 Take the byte built for the printer.
  $8B54,$02 Write a byte to the printer.
  $8B56,$02 Loop back to #R$8B49 until #N$08 runs out.
  $8B58,$07 Step along the text line at #N$5020 and continue while a character remains.
@ $8B5F label=ZXPrinter_WaitUntilThePrinterIsReady
  $8B5F,$02 Read the printer port #N$FB.
  $8B61,$03 Wait until the printer is ready (bit 0 of port #N$FB).
  $8B64,$01 Take the byte being sent to the printer.
  $8B65,$01 Rotate the next bit out towards the printer.
  $8B66,$02 Write a byte to the printer.
  $8B68,$08 Restore #REGde and #REGhl from the stack, step to the next row, and jump back to #R$8B31 if bit 3 of #REGe is clear.
  $8B70,$02 Send #N$04 to the printer port.
  $8B72,$02 Write a byte to the printer.
@ $8B74 label=ZXPrinter_RestoreAndReturn
N $8B74 Restore the registers saved for the printer.
  $8B74,$03 Restore #REGbc, #REGde and #REGhl from the stack.
  $8B77,$01 Return.

c $8B78 Pause
@ $8B78 label=Pause
D $8B78 Waits a short while.
  $8B78,$03 Count down #N($03E8,$04) before returning.
@ $8B7B label=Pause_Loop
  $8B7B,$01 Decrease the counter by one.
  $8B7C,$04 Loop back to #R$8B7B until the counter is zero.
  $8B80,$01 Return.

b $8B81 Keyboard Row Masks
@ $8B81 label=KeyboardRowMasks
D $8B81 One mask byte per keyboard half-row, the half-row and key bits of the last newly pressed key in the word at
. #R$8B89, and the previous scan at #R$8B8B. #R$8B93 compares each new row with that scan.
B $8B81,$08
W $8B89,$02
B $8B8B,$08

c $8B93 Get Key
@ $8B93 label=GetKey
D $8B93 Reads a newly pressed key. Returns #N$00 if there isn't one.
  $8B93,$07 Save #REGhl, #REGix and #REGbc and call #R$8B78 to pause.
  $8B9A,$04 Write #N($0000,$04,$04) to #R$8B89.
  $8B9E,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$8B8B.
  $8BA1,$04 Point #REGix at #R$8B81.
  $8BA5,$03 Read the keyboard from port #N$FEFE.
@ $8BA8 label=GetKey_Loop
  $8BA8,$02 Read from the keyboard port.
  $8BAA,$02,b$01 Keep the selected bits.
  $8BAC,$03 OR against what #REGix is pointing to.
  $8BAF,$02 Save the row and complement it.
  $8BB1,$02 Keep the keys that are pressed now but were up in the previous scan at #REGhl. The second CPL leaves the flags alone.
  $8BB3,$02 Jump to #R$8BBC if no key in this half-row has been newly pressed.
  $8BB5,$04 Store #REGbc at #R$8B89.
  $8BB9,$03 Store the result at #R$8B89.
@ $8BBC label=GetKey_StoreTheRow
  $8BBC,$03 Store the result, then step #REGhl on.
  $8BBF,$02 Step #REGix to the next byte of #R$8B81.
  $8BC1,$02 Rotate the next bit of #REGb into carry.
  $8BC3,$02 Jump back to #R$8BA8 until all eight half-rows have been read.
  $8BC5,$04 Bring #REGbc back from #R$8B89.
N $8BC9 No newly pressed key was stored.
  $8BC9,$04 If #REGbc is #N($0000,$04,$04), return #N$00 through #R$8BF6.
  $8BCD,$02 Start the key index at #N$FB (that is -5).
@ $8BCF label=GetKey_CountTheRows
  $8BCF,$02 Add #N$05 for each keyboard row passed.
  $8BD1,$02 Shift the next row bit of #REGb into the carry flag.
  $8BD3,$02 If there is any carry, jump to #R$8BCF.
  $8BD5,$01 Step back one so the next loop starts in the same place.
@ $8BD6 label=GetKey_CountTheKeys
  $8BD6,$01 Count one more key along the row.
  $8BD7,$02 Shift the next key bit of #REGc into the carry flag.
  $8BD9,$02 If there is any carry, jump to #R$8BD6.
  $8BDB,$01 Copy the key code into #REGc.
  $8BDC,$02 Start the character count at zero.
  $8BDE,$03 Point #REGhl at the unshifted keyboard map at #R$8BFB.
  $8BE1,$04 Read from the keyboard port. #TABLE(default,centre,centre,centre,centre,centre,centre) { =h,r2 Port Number | =h,c5 Bit } { =h 0 | =h 1 | =h 2 | =h 3 | =h 4 } { #N$FE | SHIFT | Z | X | C | V } TABLE#
  $8BE5,$02,b$01 Keep only bit 0 (SHIFT).
  $8BE7,$02 If it is zero, jump to #R$8BF1.
  $8BE9,$04 Read from the keyboard port. #TABLE(default,centre,centre,centre,centre,centre,centre) { =h,r2 Port Number | =h,c5 Bit } { =h 0 | =h 1 | =h 2 | =h 3 | =h 4 } { #N$7F | SPACE | SYMBOL SHIFT | M | N | B } TABLE#
  $8BED,$02,b$01 Keep only bit 1 (SYMBOL SHIFT).
  $8BEF,$02 If it is not zero, jump to #R$8BF4.
@ $8BF1 label=GetKey_UseTheShiftedMap
  $8BF1,$03 Point #REGhl at the shifted keyboard map at #R$8C23.
@ $8BF4 label=GetKey_GetByte
  $8BF4,$01 Add the key code in #REGbc to the keyboard map pointer.
  $8BF5,$01 Read the character for this key from the keyboard map.
@ $8BF6 label=GetKey_Return
  $8BF6,$04 Restore #REGbc, #REGix and #REGhl from the stack.
  $8BFA,$01 Return.

b $8BFB Unshifted Keyboard Map
@ $8BFB label=KeyboardMapUnshifted
D $8BFB The characters #R$8B93 reads when neither shift is held.
B $8BFB,$05 #FOR$00,$04||n|#IF(#PEEK(#PC+n)>$00)(#CHR(#PEEK(#PC+n)),--)|, ||
L $8BFB,$05,$08

b $8C23 Shifted Keyboard Map
@ $8C23 label=KeyboardMapShifted
D $8C23 The characters #R$8B93 reads when shift is held.
B $8C23,$05 #FOR$00,$04||n|#IF(#PEEK(#PC+n)>$00)(#CHR(#PEEK(#PC+n)),--)|, ||
L $8C23,$05,$08

c $8C4B Action: Look
@ $8C4B label=Action_Look
D $8C4B Describes where the current character is. The player gets the place
. name; another character is reported instead.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
N $8C4B If the message is held back, the place is not described.
  $8C4B,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the place is not described.
  $8C4E,$04 Use the record of the character whose turn it is.
  $8C52,$03 Read where this character is.
  $8C55,$04 Jump to #R$8C95 if this character is not inside another object.
  $8C59,$06 Store #N($0080,$04,$04) in #N$AFFD.
  $8C5F,$03 Point at the message "You are in[0x16]" (#R$AFFC).
  $8C62,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $8C65,$05 Read where this character is.
N $8C6A Find the object's record.
  $8C6A,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
N $8C6D Describe the place.
  $8C6D,$03 Call #R$A09D to print the exits for this location.
  $8C70,$03 Read the name token from the object record.
  $8C73,$03 Read the name token's high byte from the object record.
  $8C76,$03 Keep the low nibble of byte 9 of this object.
N $8C79 Print the token in #REGde.
  $8C79,$03 Call #R$74C1 to print a dictionary token.
N $8C7C Print a full stop.
  $8C7C,$05 Print a full stop after the place name.
N $8C81 Print a newline.
  $8C81,$03 Call #R$8583 to print a newline.
  $8C84,$03 Point at the message "You see :[0x14]" (#R$B003).
  $8C87,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $8C8A,$05 Read where this object is from the object record.
  $8C8F,$03 Read the location from the object record.
  $8C92,$03 Jump to #R$9FAF to list what is in this place.
@ $8C95 label=Action_Look_DescribeTheLocation
  $8C95,$03 Read the location from the object record.
  $8C98,$03 Jump to #R$9630 to describe this location.

c $8C9B Action: Refuse If Not Carried
@ $8C9B label=Action_RefuseIfNotCarried
D $8C9B Refuses the verb when the object is not carried, and the player sees "You are not
. carrying it".
N $8C9B Test whether the object is being carried.
  $8C9B,$03 Call #R$9C78 to test whether the object is carried.
  $8C9E,$01 Return if the object is carried (carry set).
  $8C9F,$04 Point at the message "You are not carrying it[0x15]".
  $8CA3,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD to print "You are not carrying it".

c $8CA6 Action: Put Down
@ $8CA6 label=Action_Putdown
D $8CA6 Puts the current object down in this place. A fluid that is dropped
. evaporates.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
N $8CA6 Refuse the verb when the object is not carried.
  $8CA6,$03 Call #R$8C9B to refuse the verb when the object is not carried.
N $8CA9 If the message is held back, the object is not put down.
  $8CA9,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the object is not put down.
  $8CAC,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $8CB0,$03 Read where this object is from the object record.
  $8CB3,$04 Jump to #R$8CBB if where this object is is not #N$12.
  $8CB7,$04 Point #REGix at #OBJECT($12,$01,$01)($).
@ $8CBB label=Action_Putdown_DropTheObject
  $8CBB,$06 Read *#R$B70C.
  $8CC1,$03 Read where the character is.
  $8CC4,$0A Put the object where the character is, and return unless it is a fluid.
  $8CCE,$04 Clear the object's location.
  $8CD2,$03 Point at the message "[0x00] evaporate(s|d|ing|es)[0x15]" (#R$B142).
  $8CD5,$05 Step the pointer on by #N$08 bytes.
  $8CDA,$05 Save the name pointer and call #R$72DD to print the message.
  $8CDF,$01 Return.
N $8CE0 Reached through the action lists of #OBJECT($1F,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($21,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($25,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($29,$01,$01)($) for action #N($14), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $8CE0,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $8CE3,$03 Point #REGhl at the second object.
  $8CE6,$03 Call #R$9C7E to test whether the object is inside the second object.
  $8CE9,$06 Point at "[0x07] is not in[0x09]" (#R$AFB5) and jump back to #R$72DD if #R$9C7E found the object is not inside the second object.
  $8CEF,$02 Jump to #R$8D3C to locate object.

c $8CF1 Action: Add The Object's Mass
@ $8CF1 label=Action_AddTheObjectMass
D $8CF1 Adds the current object's mass to what the character is carrying. Too much and the player
. sees "You are carrying too much".
  $8CF1,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $8CF5,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $8CF8,$03 Call #R$9CED to total the mass inside the object.
  $8CFB,$03 Add this object's mass onto the count.
  $8CFE,$02 Jump to #R$8D02 if the sum did not overflow.
  $8D00,$02 Cap the total mass at #N$FF.
@ $8D02 label=Action_AddTheObjectMass_Putdown_CopyTheResult
  $8D02,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $8D03,$04 Read *#R$B70C.
  $8D07,$03 Read the mass from #REGiy.
  $8D0A,$06 Point #REGhl at #R$AE04 and jump to #R$8D20 if the mass of the object at #R$B70C is below the total in #REGb.
  $8D10,$04 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $8D14,$03 Call #R$9CED to total the mass inside the character.
  $8D17,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $8D18,$02 Subtract the count in #REGb from the value saved on the stack.
  $8D1A,$03 Jump to #R$8D25 if the result is not negative.
  $8D1D,$03 Point at the message "You are carrying too much" (#R$AE0C).
@ $8D20 label=Action_AddTheObjectMass_Putdown_PrintTheMessage
  $8D20,$01 Swap #REGhl with the address on the stack.
  $8D21,$04 Jump back to #R$72DD to print message.

c $8D25 Action: Stop If The Object Is Fixed
@ $8D25 label=Action_StopIfTheObjectIsFixed
D $8D25 Stops the verb when the object is fixed or a fluid, and says that will not work.
  $8D25,$03 Call #R$9246 to point at the current object and see whether its copy count is #N$01.
  $8D28,$02 Jump to #R$8D2F if the copy count is not #N$01.
  $8D2A,$05 Return if the object is not a fluid.
@ $8D2F label=Action_StopIfTheObjectIsFixed_SayThatWillNotWork
  $8D2F,$04 Jump to #R$9F76 to say that will not work.

c $8D33 Action: Pick Up
@ $8D33 label=Action_Pickup
D $8D33 Picks the current object up, when it is here and can be carried.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
N $8D33 Test whether the object is being carried.
  $8D33,$03 Call #R$9C78 to test whether the object is carried.
  $8D36,$03 Point at the message "You are already carrying[0x07][0x15]" (#R$AE11).
  $8D39,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD if #R$9C78 found the object carried, to print "You are already carrying[0x07]".
  $8D3C,$03 Call #R$8CF1 to add the object's mass.
  $8D3F,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
@ $8D42 label=Action_Pickup_Loop
N $8D42 Find the object's record.
  $8D42,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $8D45,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $8D48,$06 Jump to #R$9F76 if the character is inside the current object.
  $8D4E,$03 Read where this object is from the object record.
  $8D51,$04 Jump back to #R$8D42 if where this object is is not #N$FF.
  $8D55,$04 Read *#R$B708.
N $8D59 If the message is held back, the object is not picked up.
  $8D59,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the object is not picked up.
  $8D5C,$03 Read where this object is from the object record.
  $8D5F,$02 Compare where this object is with #N$12.
  $8D61,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $8D64,$02 Jump to #R$8D6A if where this object is is #N$12.
  $8D66,$03 Update where this object is.
  $8D69,$01 Return.
@ $8D6A label=Action_Pickup_TakeTheRopeInstead
  $8D6A,$03 The object is tied to the rope, so give the rope (#N$12) to the character instead.
  $8D6D,$01 Return.

b $8D6E Location Percentage Completion Table
@ $8D6E label=PercentageLocationTable
D $8D6E Read by #R$8D9D.
N $8D6E #LOCATION(#PEEK(#PC)) - "#LOCATIONNAME(#PEEK(#PC))".
B $8D6E,$01 Location ID.
W $8D6F,$02 Percentage to add.
L $8D6E,$03,$0E
N $8D98 End of table.
B $8D98,$01 #TERMINATOR

g $8D99 Action Location Buffer
@ $8D99 label=ActionLocationData
D $8D99 The location the current move is heading for, and the volume of that move. Read by #R$8D9D, #R$8E85, #R$9BDC and #R$C7DD.
W $8D99,$02 Pointer to the current location data from #R$BA8A.
@ $8D9B label=ActionLocationID
B $8D9B,$01 The current location ID.
@ $8D9C label=ActionMoveVolume
B $8D9C,$01 Move volume. #R$8DCC adds the object's volume to the character's volume and stores the sum here. #R$8E85 compares it before a move.

c $8D9D Action: Move
@ $8D9D label=Action_Direction
D $8D9D Moves the current character one step in the commanded direction, if
. that exit is open.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
  $8D9D,$05 Jump to #R$8DAB if #R$95ED reports that it is light.
  $8DA2,$02 Roll a random number below #N$09.
N $8DA4 Roll a random number.
  $8DA4,$03 Call #R$9C9F to roll a number from the limit in #REGa.
  $8DA7,$01 Add one, making the roll #N$01-#N$09.
  $8DA8,$03 Update *#R$B6E7.
@ $8DAB label=Action_Direction_TestIfInside
  $8DAB,$04 Read *#R$B70C.
  $8DAF,$03 Read where the character is.
  $8DB2,$04 Jump to #R$8DC3 if this character is not inside another object.
N $8DB6 Find the object's record.
  $8DB6,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $8DB9,$06 Jump to #R$8DD9 if the object is not an animal.
  $8DBF,$04 Mark where this object is as unset.
@ $8DC3 label=Action_Direction_TotalTheVolume
  $8DC3,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $8DC6,$03 Call #R$9CE8 to total the volume inside the character.
  $8DC9,$03 Add the character's volume onto the total from #R$9CE8.
  $8DCC,$03 Store the character's volume plus the object's volume as the move volume.
  $8DCF,$03 Read *#R$B6E7.
  $8DD2,$03 Call #R$9F08 to find an exit in the direction in #REGa.
  $8DD5,$04 Jump to #R$8DFA if #R$9F08 found an exit in that direction.
@ $8DD9 label=Action_Direction_TestForLight
  $8DD9,$06 Jump to #R$9F76 if #R$95ED reports that it is light.
  $8DDF,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the fall is not described.
  $8DE2,$04 Point #REGix at #OBJECT($00,$01,$01)($).
  $8DE6,$01 Clear carry so a zero is shifted into the strength.
  $8DE7,$03 Point at the message "but fall and hit your head[0x15]" (#R$AD7D).
  $8DEA,$04 Shift the player's strength right.
  $8DEE,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD unless halving the player's strength left it zero.
  $8DF1,$03 Point at the message "but fall and smash your skull" (#R$AD8A).
  $8DF4,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $8DF7,$03 Jump to #R$90D2 to end the game.
@ $8DFA label=Action_Direction_ReadThisExitDestination
  $8DFA,$03 Read this exit's destination.
  $8DFD,$03 Jump back to #R$8DD9 if this exit's destination is zero.
  $8E00,$03 Store the destination at #R$8D9B.
  $8E03,$03 Read the object this exit goes via.
  $8E06,$03 Call #R$8E85 to check whether this object can be entered.
  $8E09,$03 Jump back to #R$8DD9 when the check returns #N($01), and the character falls.
  $8E0C,$03 Jump to #R$8E6D when the check returns #N$02: the way is too small.
  $8E0F,$03 Jump to #R$8E72 when the check returns #N$03: the way is too full.
  $8E12,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the character does not move.
  $8E15,$03 Update the location.
  $8E18,$03 Read the character whose turn it is.
  $8E1B,$03 Call #R$9BDD to move what is inside the character to the new location.
  $8E1E,$03 Read the character whose turn it is.
  $8E21,$03 Return if the character is not #OBJECT($00,$01,$01)($).
  $8E24,$0A Call #R$9DBD using the #R$C78E(events table), looking for a match with #R$8D9B.
  $8E2E,$02 Jump to #R$8E39 if no event was found.
  $8E30,$06 Load the event from the #R$C78E(events table).
  $8E36,$03 Call #R$9B6C to execute it.
N $8E39 The game adds a one-time only value to the completion percentage once certain locations are reached (as long as
. it's not dark).
@ $8E39 label=PercentageLocation
  $8E39,$04 Return if #R$95ED reports that it is dark.
  $8E3D,$03 Read the character whose turn it is.
  $8E40,$01 See whether the character whose turn it is is zero.
  $8E41,$03 Read the current location.
  $8E44,$02 Jump to #R$8E6A if the character whose turn it is is not zero.
  $8E46,$03 Read the location pointer from #R$8D99.
  $8E49,$05 If bit 6 is already (percentage is already added) set jump to #R$96A8.
  $8E4E,$02 Mark the current location data so bit 6 is on.
  $8E50,$08 Call #R$9DBD with #R$8D6E.
  $8E58,$02 Jump to #R$8E69 if there was no match.
  $8E5A,$04 Read the low byte of this location's percentage.
  $8E5E,$03 Read the high byte of this location's percentage.
  $8E61,$08 Add the percentage value to #R$B6F7 and write it.
@ $8E69 label=PercentageLocation_Return
  $8E69,$04 Jump to #R$9630 to locate location.
@ $8E6A label=PercentageLocation_Skip
@ $8E6D label=Action_Direction_TooSmallToEnter
  $8E6D,$03 Point at the message "[0x04] is too small for you to enter" (#R$AE23).
  $8E70,$02 Jump to #R$8E79 to print "[0x04] is too small for you to enter".
@ $8E72 label=Action_Direction_TooFullToEnter
  $8E72,$04 Point #REGix at the location record stored at #R$8D99.
  $8E76,$03 Point at the message "[0x04] is too full for you to{5} enter[0x15]" (#R$AE2E).
@ $8E79 label=Action_Direction_ReadTheLocationLowByte
  $8E79,$04 Save the message address and read the low byte at offset 2 of the location.
  $8E7D,$03 Read the high byte at offset 2 of the location.
  $8E80,$01 Swap #REGhl with the address on the stack.
  $8E81,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $8E84,$01 Return.

c $8E85 Action: Check The Object Flags
@ $8E85 label=Action_CheckTheObjectFlags
D $8E85 Checks the object's attributes and volume against the move volume at #R$8D9C and the
. current location at #R$8D9B before a move is allowed.
  $8E85,$03 Jump to #R$8EA7 if #REGa is zero.
N $8E88 Find the object's record.
  $8E88,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $8E8B,$03 Read the attribute flags from the object record.
  $8E8E,$02 Keep bits 3, 5.
  $8E90,$02 Jump to #R$8ECE when the object is neither broken nor open.
N $8E92 Test whose turn it is.
  $8E92,$06 Jump to #R$8E9E if *#R$B6EA is not zero.
  $8E98,$06 Jump to #R$8ECE if bit 7 of the object's second flag is set.
@ $8E9E label=Action_CheckTheObjectFlags_ReadTheMoveVolume
  $8E9E,$03 Read the move volume.
  $8EA1,$03 Read the volume from the object record.
  $8EA4,$03 Jump to #R$8ECA if the move volume is not below the object's volume.
@ $8EA7 label=Action_CheckTheObjectFlags_ReadTheLocation
  $8EA7,$03 Read the current location.
  $8EAA,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
N $8EAB Find the location's record.
  $8EAB,$03 Call #R$9BB1 to locate location.
  $8EAE,$0B Save the location record pointer at #R$8D99, and jump to #R$8EC4 if this location's volume byte is #N$FF.
  $8EB9,$01 Use the current location again.
  $8EBA,$03 Call #R$9C41 to find an exit.
  $8EBD,$07 Jump to #R$8EC6 if the move volume is not below the current location.
@ $8EC4 label=Action_CheckTheObjectFlags_ReturnZero
  $8EC4,$01 Return zero.
  $8EC5,$01 Return.
@ $8EC6 label=Action_CheckTheObjectFlags_ReturnFlagValueThree
  $8EC6,$02 The flag value to return is #N$03.
  $8EC8,$01 Set the flags from #N$03. The zero flag is clear.
  $8EC9,$01 Return.
@ $8ECA label=Action_CheckTheObjectFlags_ReturnFlagValueTwo
  $8ECA,$02 The flag value to return is #N$02.
  $8ECC,$01 Set the flags from #N$02. The zero flag is clear.
  $8ECD,$01 Return.
@ $8ECE label=Action_CheckTheObjectFlags_ReturnFlagValueOne
  $8ECE,$02 The flag value to return is #N$01.
  $8ED0,$01 Set the flags from #N$01. The zero flag is clear.
  $8ED1,$01 Return.

c $8ED2 Action: Locate And Follow
@ $8ED2 label=Action_LocateAndFollow
D $8ED2 Finds where the object is. An open container is followed to the object inside, and that
. location is returned.
  $8ED2,$05 Call #R$9BCA to locate the object.
N $8ED4 Find the object's record.
@ $8ED7 label=Action_LocateAndFollow_Direction_Loop
  $8ED7,$03 Read where this object is from the object record.
  $8EDA,$04 Jump to #R$8EE9 if this object is not inside another object.
N $8EDE Find the object's record.
  $8EDE,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $8EE1,$06 Jump back to #R$8ED7 if the object is open.
  $8EE7,$02 Clear the zero flag.
@ $8EE9 label=Action_LocateAndFollow_PopTheRecord
  $8EE9,$02 Restore #REGix from the stack.
  $8EEB,$01 Return.

c $8EEC Action: Carry On With The Object
@ $8EEC label=Action_CarryOnWithTheObject
D $8EEC Carries on with the current object. An open object is followed, and a passage whose
. volume is zero is refused.
. Reached through the action lists of #OBJECT($01,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($05,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($06,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($08,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0A,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0B,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0C,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0D,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($11,$01,$01)($) for action #N($18), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $8EEC,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
N $8EEF Find the object's record.
  $8EEF,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $8EF2,$03 Call #R$A5CA to test whether the object is open.
  $8EF5,$03 Jump to #R$A16C if the object is not open.
N $8EF8 Reached through the action lists of #OBJECT($09,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($2A,$01,$01)($) for action #N($19), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $8EF8,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $8EFB,$03 Call #R$8ED2 to find where the object is.
  $8EFE,$01 Return if #R$8ED2 stopped inside a closed object.
N $8EFF Find the exit through the current object.
  $8EFF,$03 Call #R$9F25 to find the exit through the current object.
  $8F02,$05 Jump to #R$9F76 if #R$9F25 found no exit through the current object.
  $8F07,$03 Read this exit's destination.
  $8F0A,$05 Jump to #R$9F76 if this exit's destination is zero.
N $8F0F If the message is held back, the object is not followed.
  $8F0F,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the object is not followed.
  $8F12,$05 Call #R$9BB1 to find this location's record.
N $8F14 Find the location's record.
  $8F17,$04 Test bit 7 of the location attribute.
  $8F1B,$04 Restore the exit and jump to #R$8F35 if bit 7 of the location attribute is clear.
  $8F1F,$04 Read *#R$B70C.
  $8F23,$03 Read the location from #REGiy.
  $8F26,$04 Save the location and read this exit's destination.
  $8F2A,$03 Store the exit's destination in the character's location.
  $8F2D,$03 Call #R$962B to print that you see this place.
  $8F30,$04 Put the character back in its saved location.
  $8F34,$01 Return.
@ $8F35 label=Action_CarryOnWithTheObject_PrintItIsDark
  $8F35,$03 Point at the message "it is dark" (#R$AE1F).
  $8F38,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD to print "it is dark".

c $8F3B Action: Leave If The Way Is Open
@ $8F3B label=Action_LeaveIfTheWayIsOpen
D $8F3B Leaves through an exit when the way is open and the object's volume is not zero, then
. runs the move.
. Reached through the action lists of #OBJECT($01,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($05,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($06,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($07,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($08,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0A,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0B,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0C,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0D,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($11,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($27,$01,$01)($) for action #N($1E), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
N $8F3B Find the exit through the current object.
  $8F3B,$03 Call #R$9F25 to find the exit through the current object.
  $8F3E,$05 Jump to #R$9F76 if the exit search returned #N$FF.
  $8F43,$03 Read this exit's destination.
  $8F46,$05 Jump to #R$9F76 if this exit's destination is zero.
  $8F4B,$03 Read the object this exit goes via.
  $8F4E,$05 Call #R$8E85 to check whether the character can pass via the object this exit goes through.
  $8F53,$05 Jump to #R$9F76 if #R$8E85 did not return zero.
N $8F58 If the message is held back, the character does not leave.
  $8F58,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the character does not leave.
  $8F5B,$03 Read this exit's direction.
  $8F5E,$03 Update *#R$B6E7.
  $8F61,$05 Write #N$FF to *#R$B6E8.
  $8F66,$03 Jump back to #R$8DAB to carry on with the move.
N $8F69 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($13,$01,$01)($) for action #N($23), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $8F69,$04 Read *#R$B708.
N $8F6D Test the second object.
  $8F6D,$07 Jump to #R$8F92 if *#R$B6E9 is #N$17.
  $8F74,$04 Jump to #R$8F9A if the second object is #N$18.
@ $8F78 label=Action_LeaveIfTheWayIsOpen_TestTheFluid
  $8F78,$04 Read *#R$B70A.
  $8F7C,$07 Jump to #R$9F76 if the object is not a fluid.
  $8F83,$09 Take #N($82), and jump to #R$A16C if the object is full.
  $8F8C,$03 Point #REGhl at the fixed-object check at #R$924F.
  $8F8F,$03 Jump to #R$9F4A to hand an object over.
@ $8F92 label=Action_LeaveIfTheWayIsOpen_UseTheWater
  $8F92,$02 Pass object id #N$15.
  $8F94,$04 Point #REGiy at #OBJECT($15,$01,$01)($).
  $8F98,$02 Jump to #R$8FA0 to update the second object.
@ $8F9A label=Action_LeaveIfTheWayIsOpen_UseTheBlackWater
  $8F9A,$02 Pass object id #N$16.
  $8F9C,$04 Point #REGiy at #OBJECT($16,$01,$01)($).
@ $8FA0 label=Action_LeaveIfTheWayIsOpen_StoreTheSecondObject
  $8FA0,$03 Update *#R$B6E9.
  $8FA3,$04 Update *#R$B70A.
  $8FA7,$04 Mark where this object is as unset.
  $8FAB,$02 Jump back to #R$8F78 to test the second object.

c $8FAD Action: Run
@ $8FAD label=Action_Run
D $8FAD Runs in the commanded direction until the way is blocked.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
  $8FAD,$02 Roll a random number below #N$0A.
N $8FAF Roll a random number.
  $8FAF,$03 Call #R$9C9F to roll a number from the limit in #REGa.
  $8FB2,$04 Jump back to #R$8FAD when the roll is zero.
@ $8FB6 label=Action_Run_Loop
  $8FB6,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $8FB7,$03 Call #R$9F08 to find an exit in the direction in #REGa.
  $8FBA,$03 Jump to #R$8FC7 if this exit's direction is the one being sought.
  $8FBD,$06 Try the next direction and jump back to #R$8FB6 while it is below #N$0A.
  $8FC3,$02 Set the direction back to #N$01.
  $8FC5,$02 Jump back to #R$8FB6 to find an exit in the direction in #REGa.
@ $8FC7 label=Action_Run_StartTheMove
  $8FC7,$03 Update *#R$B6E7.
  $8FCA,$03 Jump to #R$8D9D to move.

c $8FCD Action: Enter
@ $8FCD label=Action_Enter
D $8FCD Enters the current object when it is a container that can be
. entered.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
  $8FCD,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $8FD0,$03 Call #R$9F2D to find an exit whose destination is this object id.
  $8FD3,$03 Jump to #R$8F3E to test the exit just found.

c $8FD6 Action: Follow
@ $8FD6 label=Action_Follow
D $8FD6 Follows another character through the exit they took.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
  $8FD6,$04 Read *#R$B70C.
  $8FDA,$03 Read the location from the object record.
  $8FDD,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $8FE1,$03 Read the location from the object record.
  $8FE4,$03 Jump to #R$8FEF if both objects are in the same location.
  $8FE7,$03 Call #R$9F2D to find an exit whose destination is that location.
  $8FEA,$05 Jump to #R$8F3E if an exit leads there.
@ $8FEF label=Action_Follow_PrintCannotFollow
  $8FEF,$03 Point at the message "i cannot follow[0x07] from here[0x15]" (#R$AFE9).
  $8FF2,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD to print "i cannot follow[0x07] from here".

c $8FF5 Action: Throw At
@ $8FF5 label=Action_ThrowAt
D $8FF5 Throws the current object at the second object.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
  $8FF5,$03 Call #R$8CF1 to add the object's mass.
  $8FF8,$03 Point #REGhl at the attack handler.
  $8FFB,$02 The second object is taken from #R$B70A, with #N$0F in #REGa.
  $8FFD,$04 Read *#R$B70A.
  $9001,$06 Jump to #R$900C if the object is an animal.
  $9007,$03 Point #REGhl at action: Use The Other Object.
  $900A,$02 Set the current action to #N$0B.
@ $900C label=Action_ThrowAt_HandTheObjectOver
  $900C,$03 Update *#R$B6E7.
  $900F,$03 Call #R$9F4A to hand the object from one character to another.
  $9012,$05 Write #N$2A (throw) to *#R$B6E7.
  $9017,$03 Read *#R$B6FA.
  $901A,$03 Return if the message gate is not #N$01.
  $901D,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $9021,$04 Mark where this object is as unset.
  $9025,$05 Write #N$0F (attack) to *#R$B6E7.
  $902A,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $902D,$03 Jump to #R$95DF to start the object's special-object script if it is an animal and not broken.
  $9030,$04 Mark where this object is as unset.

c $9034 Action: Talk To
@ $9034 label=Action_TalkTo
D $9034 Talks to the current character, when they still have something to
. say.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
N $9034 If the message is held back, the character says nothing.
  $9034,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the character says nothing.
  $9037,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $903A,$03 Call #R$9A85 to find the special-object record.
  $903D,$06 Take zero, and jump to #R$9058 if this object has no special-object record.
N $9043 Test the talk flag.
  $9043,$07 Jump to #R$9058 if *#R$B6F9 is not #N$00.
  $904A,$03 Read byte 6 of this special-object record.
  $904D,$04 Jump to #R$9058 if that byte is zero.
N $9051 Roll a random number.
  $9051,$03 Call #R$9C9F to roll a number from the limit in #REGa.
  $9054,$04 Jump to #R$905C if the roll is zero.
@ $9058 label=Action_TalkTo_FillTheClaimedSlots
  $9058,$03 Call #R$7EBA to give #REGa of the claimed slots to the current object.
  $905B,$01 Return.
@ $905C label=Action_TalkTo_PrintSays
  $905C,$03 Point at the message "[0x07] says " (#R$B1E3).
  $905F,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $9062,$01 Take zero so #R$7EBA marks the claimed slots free.
  $9063,$02 Jump back to #R$9058 to free every claimed slot and return.
N $9065 If the message is held back, the reply about the open object is skipped.
N $9065 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($1D,$01,$01)($) for action #N($39), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $9065,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the reply about the open object is skipped.
  $9068,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $906C,$07 Jump to #R$9117 if the object is not open.
  $9073,$03 Jump to #R$9145 to close the object.

c $9076 Action: Shoot
@ $9076 label=Action_Shoot
D $9076 Shoots an arrow at the current object.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
  $9076,$02 Pass object id #N$19 to the carried test.
  $9078,$03 Call #R$9C7B to test whether the character whose turn it is is carrying it.
  $907B,$03 Point at the message "You are not carrying the bow" (#R$B121).
  $907E,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD if #R$9C7B found the object not carried, to print "You are not carrying the bow".
  $9081,$03 Call #R$914A to test the shared flag bits.
N $9084 If the message is held back, no arrow is shot.
  $9084,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, no arrow is shot.
  $9087,$05 Write #N$0F (attack) to *#R$B6E7.
N $908C Test whose turn it is.
  $908C,$07 Jump to #R$90A8 if *#R$B6EA is #N$46.
  $9093,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $9096,$08 Jump back to #R$72DD if the current object is #N$3C.
  $909E,$02 Roll a random number below #N$08.
N $90A0 Roll a random number.
  $90A0,$03 Call #R$9C9F to roll a number from the limit in #REGa.
  $90A3,$05 Jump back to #R$72DD if the roll is below #N$03.
@ $90A8 label=Action_Shoot_PointAtTheArrow
  $90A8,$04 Point #REGix at #OBJECT($1A,$01,$01)($).
N $90AC Test the current object.
  $90AC,$07 Jump to #R$90B7 if *#R$B6E8 is #N$1A.
  $90B3,$04 Mark where this object is as unset.
@ $90B7 label=Action_Shoot_PrintTheHit
  $90B7,$03 Point at the message "the arrow{5} hits[0x07][0x15]" (#R$B136).
N $90BA Print the message at #REGhl.
  $90BA,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $90BD,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $90C1,$03 Call #R$A1C8 to test whether the object is an animal and not broken.
  $90C4,$03 Jump to #R$92ED unless #R$A1C8 found the object is an animal and not broken.
  $90C7,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $90CA,$03 Call #R$977F. The player dies if no object is left.
  $90CD,$02 Pass #N$06 to the routine that names this object.
  $90CF,$03 Jump to #R$A16C to print the object's name.

c $90D2 Action: You Are Dead
@ $90D2 label=YouAreDead
D $90D2 Kills the player and ends the game.
. Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($00,$01,$01)($) for action #N($00), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $90D2,$01 Clear the character whose turn it is.
  $90D3,$03 Update *#R$B6EA.
  $90D6,$03 Point at the message "You are dead" (#R$AFF1).
  $90D9,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $90DC,$03 Call #R$83F5 to print the score.
@ $90DF label=YouAreDead_WaitForKey
N $90DF This is the same key wait as #R$84C2, but it ends with a jump to #R$6C27 to restart the game.
  $90DF,$03 Read from the keyboard port.
  $90E2,$02,b$01 Keep bits 0-4, one for each key in the half-row. XOR A then IN A,(#N$FE) reads every half-row at once, and a pressed key reads as a reset bit.
  $90E4,$04 Loop back to #R$90DF until any key has been pressed.
  $90E8,$03 Jump back to #R$6C27 to restart the game.

c $90EB Action: Inventory
@ $90EB label=Action_Inventory
D $90EB Lists what the player is carrying.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
  $90EB,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the inventory is not listed.
  $90EE,$03 Point at the message "You are carrying[0x15]" (#R$ADF6).
  $90F1,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $90F4,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
N $90F7 Count the objects being held.
  $90F7,$03 Call #R$9D97 to count the visible objects.
  $90FA,$01 See whether nothing is held.
  $90FB,$03 Point at the message "{6} nothing" (#R$B33B).
  $90FE,$03 Jump to #R$72DD to print it if nothing is held.
  $9101,$03 Read the character whose turn it is.
  $9104,$04 Use the record of the character whose turn it is.
  $9108,$03 Read this character's location.
  $910B,$03 Jump to #R$9FAF to list what is being carried.

c $910E Action: Open Or Close
@ $910E label=ActionOpenOrClose
D $910E Opens or closes the current object.
. Reached through the action lists of #OBJECT($01,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($05,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($08,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0A,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($13,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($1E,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($20,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($21,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($25,$01,$01)($) for action #N($10), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $910E,$03 Call #R$A1F9 to read the object's lock flag.
  $9111,$03 Jump to #R$A16C to say so if #R$A1F9 found the object locked (#N$80) or already open (#N$85).
N $9114 If the message is held back, the object is not opened or closed.
  $9114,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the object is not opened or closed.
  $9117,$04 Mark the object open.
  $911B,$03 Read the copy count from the object record.
  $911E,$02 Return unless this object has one copy.
  $9120,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
N $9123 Count the objects being held.
  $9123,$03 Call #R$9D97 to count the visible objects.
  $9126,$02 Return if #R$9D97 counted nothing inside the current object.
  $9128,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $912B,$03 Call #R$A050 to list what is here.
  $912E,$01 Return if #R$A050 set the carry flag.
  $912F,$03 Read the location from the object record.
  $9132,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $9135,$03 Jump to #R$9FAF to list what is here.

c $9138 Action: Close
@ $9138 label=Action_Close
D $9138 Closes the current object (clears bit 5 of its attributes). An object that is not open is named with message #N$05.
. Reached through the action lists of #OBJECT($01,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($05,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($06,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($08,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0A,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0B,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($11,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($13,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($20,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($21,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($25,$01,$01)($) for action #N($0C), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $9138,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $913C,$03 Call #R$A5CA to test whether the object is open.
  $913F,$03 Jump to #R$A16C if the object is not open.
N $9142 If the message is held back, the object stays open.
  $9142,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the object stays open.
  $9145,$04 Close the object.
  $9149,$01 Return.

c $914A Action: Test The Shared Flag Bits
@ $914A label=Action_TestTheSharedFlagBits
D $914A Stops the verb when bits 4-6 of the current object's flag byte overlap the same bits of the
. character's flag byte. On the player's turn bit 4 of the object's flag byte is cleared first.
  $914A,$04 Read *#R$B708.
N $914E Test whose turn it is.
  $914E,$06 Jump to #R$915E if *#R$B6EA is not zero.
  $9154,$06 Jump to #R$915E if bit 4 of the object's second flag is clear.
  $915A,$04 Clear the object's second flag so bit 4 is off.
@ $915E label=Action_TestTheSharedFlagBits_CompareTheFlags
  $915E,$03 Read the object's flag byte.
  $9161,$06 Keep bits 4-6 of the object's flag byte and read the character at #R$B70C.
  $9167,$04 Return when those bits do not overlap the character's flag byte.
  $916B,$05 Drop the return address and clear the message-suppressed flag.
  $9170,$01 Return.

c $9171 Action: Attack
@ $9171 label=Action_Attack
D $9171 Attacks the current object, using strength and the combat byte.
. Reached through the action lists of #OBJECT($3D,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($3F,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($45,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($49,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($4A,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($4B,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($4C,$01,$01)($) for action #N($0F), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
  $9171,$03 Call #R$914A to test the shared flag bits.
  $9174,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $9177,$07 Jump to #R$9188 with the token #TOKEN($026B) if no object was named.
  $917E,$04 Read *#R$B70A.
  $9182,$03 Read the name token from the object record.
  $9185,$03 Read the name token's high byte from the object record.
@ $9188 label=Action_Attack_StoreTheWeapon
  $9188,$03 Store the weapon-name token at #R$B6FC.
  $918B,$04 Read *#R$B70C.
  $918F,$03 Read the strength from the object record.
  $9192,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $9195,$03 Jump to #R$91AF if the second object is #N$FF.
  $9198,$04 Read *#R$B70A.
  $919C,$03 Read the copy count of the object being attacked.
  $919F,$07 Jump back to #R$72DD unless that object has one copy.
  $91A6,$03 Read the attacked object's strength.
  $91A9,$01 Add the attacker's strength onto the attacked object's strength.
  $91AA,$02 Jump to #R$91AE if the sum did not overflow.
  $91AC,$02 Cap the sum at #N$FF.
@ $91AE label=Action_Attack_KeepTheRollLimit
  $91AE,$01 Keep the sum in #REGb as the roll limit.
@ $91AF label=Action_Attack_TakeThatLimitBack
  $91AF,$01 Take that limit back into #REGa.
  $91B0,$03 Call #R$9213 to roll #N$0A against the attacker's limit.
  $91B3,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
N $91B4 If the message is held back, the attack is not resolved.
  $91B4,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the attack is not resolved.
  $91B7,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $91BB,$03 Read the combat byte from the object record.
  $91BE,$03 Call #R$9213 to roll #N$0A against the attacker's limit.
  $91C1,$07 Jump back to #R$72DD if this roll is not below the first.
  $91C8,$01 Keep this roll in #REGc.
  $91C9,$09 Add #N$10 to this roll (capped at #N$FF) and jump to #R$91FE if the first roll is still higher.
  $91D2,$01 Take the first roll back into #REGa.
  $91D3,$01 Take this roll off the first.
  $91D4,$01 Double the difference.
  $91D5,$01 Keep the doubled difference in #REGe.
  $91D6,$02 The message-table offset's high byte is zero.
  $91D8,$06 Index the attack-message table at #R$9226.
  $91DE,$03 Read the low byte of the message address.
  $91E1,$03 Read the high byte of the message address.
  $91E4,$01 Shift the doubled difference back down.
  $91E5,$01 Shift it down again, undoing the double.
  $91E6,$01 Keep that difference in #REGb.
  $91E7,$01 Complement #REGa.
  $91E8,$03 Add the current object's strength.
  $91EB,$02 Leave the strength unchanged if the damage would take it below zero.
  $91ED,$03 Store the reduced strength.
@ $91F0 label=Action_Attack_TakeTheDifferenceBack
  $91F0,$01 Take the difference back into #REGa.
  $91F1,$01 Shift the difference down once more.
  $91F2,$01 Complement #REGa.
  $91F3,$03 Add the current object's combat byte.
  $91F6,$02 Leave the combat byte unchanged if the damage would take it below zero.
  $91F8,$03 Store the reduced combat byte.
@ $91FB label=Action_Attack_PrintTheMessage
  $91FB,$03 Jump to #R$72DD to print the message at #REGhl.
@ $91FE label=Action_Attack_PointAtTheCleaveMessage
  $91FE,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$AE3A.
  $9201,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $9204,$04 Mark the object broken.
  $9208,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $920B,$03 Call #R$977F. The player dies if no object is left.
  $920E,$02 Pass #N$06 to the routine that names this object.
  $9210,$03 Jump to #R$A16C to print the object's name.

c $9213 Action: Roll Against Ten
@ $9213 label=Action_RollAgainstTen
D $9213 Rolls #N$0A against the caller's limit and returns the hit or the miss for the attack.
  $9213,$02 Save #REGbc and keep the roll limit in #REGb.
  $9215,$02 Load #N$0A as the range of the roll.
  $9217,$03 Call #R$9CA8 to scale the roll.
  $921A,$01 Copy the result into #REGc.
  $921B,$01 Add the limit in #REGb to the scaled roll.
  $921C,$02 Return (through #R$9224) if the sum did not overflow.
  $921E,$05 Clear #REGa, and jump to #R$9224 if bit 7 of the scaled roll in #REGc is set.
  $9223,$01 Step a zero result down by one.
@ $9224 label=Action_RollAgainstTen_Attack_RestoreTheRoll
  $9224,$01 Restore #REGbc from the stack.
  $9225,$01 Return.
W $9226,$20
  $9246,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $924A,$03 Read the copy count from the object record.
  $924D,$01 Take one off the copy count.
  $924E,$01 Return.
N $924F Reached through the action lists of #OBJECT($13,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($1D,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($1F,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($21,$01,$01)($) for action #N($11), and of #OBJECT($25,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($29,$01,$01)($) for actions #N$0E and #N($11), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $924F,$03 Call #R$8D25 to stop the verb when the object is fixed or a fluid.
  $9252,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $9255,$06 Jump back to #R$72CE if the second object is where this object is.
  $925B,$04 Read *#R$B70A.
N $925F Test the current action.
  $925F,$07 Jump to #R$926C if *#R$B6E7 is #N$12 (put on).
  $9266,$06 Jump to #R$9297 if the object is not open.
@ $926C label=Action_RollAgainstTen_Attack_ReadTheVolume
  $926C,$03 Read the volume from #REGiy.
  $926F,$05 Jump to #R$927E if the volume just read is below the volume of the object at #R$B708.
  $9274,$04 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $9278,$03 Call #R$9CE8 to total the volume inside this object.
  $927B,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $927C,$02 Subtract that volume from the value saved on the stack.
@ $927E label=Action_RollAgainstTen_Attack_PrintTooFull
  $927E,$06 Point #REGhl at #R$AE1A and jump back to #R$72DD if the result went below zero.
  $9284,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD if the result is zero.
N $9287 If the message is held back, the hit is not applied.
  $9287,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the hit is not applied.
  $928A,$03 Read the location from #REGiy.
  $928D,$03 Update the object's location.
  $9290,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $9293,$03 Update where this object is.
  $9296,$01 Return.
@ $9297 label=Action_RollAgainstTen_Attack_NameTheSecondObject
  $9297,$02 Pass #N$05 to the routine that names the second object.
  $9299,$03 Jump to #R$A164 to name the second object.
N $929C If the message is held back, the holder's flag is not cleared.
N $929C Reached through the action lists of #OBJECT($14,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($15,$01,$01)($) for action #N($21), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $929C,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the holder's flag is not cleared.
  $929F,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $92A3,$03 Read where this object is from the object record.
  $92A6,$04 Jump to #R$92B5 if this object is not inside another object.
N $92AA Find the object's record.
  $92AA,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $92AD,$04 Mark the object not full.
  $92B1,$02 The strength added here is #N$01.
  $92B3,$02 Jump to #R$92BA to add #N$01 to the character's strength.

c $92B5 Action: Add To The Player's Strength
@ $92B5 label=Action_AddToThePlayersStrength
D $92B5 Adds #N$0A to the player's strength. At #N$80 the player reads that foul gluttony has
. killed you.
. Reached through the action lists of #OBJECT($00,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($22,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($26,$01,$01)($) for action #N($1B), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
N $92B5 If the message is held back, the player's strength is not increased.
  $92B5,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the player's strength is not increased.
  $92B8,$02 The strength added for a hit is #N$0A.
@ $92BA label=Action_AddToThePlayersStrength_AddToTheStrength
  $92BA,$04 Read *#R$B70C.
  $92BE,$07 Jump to #R$92DC if the character's strength plus #REGa is not below #N$80.
  $92C5,$03 Update the character's strength.
  $92C8,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $92CC,$04 Mark where this object is as unset.
  $92D0,$03 Load the copy count into #REGb.
@ $92D3 label=Action_AddToThePlayersStrength_Loop
  $92D3,$04 Clear the object's location.
  $92D7,$02 Step to the next byte of this object.
  $92D9,$02 Loop back to #R$92D3 until every copy's location is clear.
  $92DB,$01 Return.
@ $92DC label=Action_AddToThePlayersStrength_PrintTheDeath
  $92DC,$03 Point at the message "[0x0c] foul gluttony has{5} killed you[0x15]" (#R$B146).
N $92DF Print the message at #REGhl.
  $92DF,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $92E2,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $92E5,$03 Jump to #R$977F to skip the rest of attack.
  $92E8,$02 Pass code #N$83 on to the naming routine.
  $92EA,$03 Jump to #R$A164 to save the address and #R$B020.

c $92ED Action: Use The Other Object
@ $92ED label=Action_UseTheOtherObject
D $92ED Uses the second object on the current one. A fluid, a broken object, or a combat byte of
. #N$83 will not work.
. Reached through the action lists of #OBJECT($01,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($05,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($07,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0A,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0C,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0E,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($13,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($19,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($1A,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($1E,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($21,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($25,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($29,$01,$01)($) for action #N($0B), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $92ED,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $92F1,$07 Jump to #R$9F76 if the object is a fluid.
  $92F8,$07 Jump to #R$9399 if the object is broken.
  $92FF,$07 Jump to #R$9F76 if the object's combat byte is zero.
  $9306,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $9307,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $930A,$03 Jump to #R$932C if there is no second object (#N$FF).
  $930D,$04 Read *#R$B70A.
  $9311,$03 Read the strength from #REGiy.
  $9314,$04 Jump to #R$9F76 if the object's strength is zero.
  $9318,$06 Read *#R$B70A.
  $931E,$02 Look up action #N$0B.
  $9320,$03 Call #R$9B81 to find an action record.
  $9323,$06 Jump to #R$9F76 if #R$9B81 found no action record (#N$FF).
  $9329,$03 Read the strength from #REGiy.
@ $932C label=Action_UseTheOtherObject_Attack_HoldTheMessage
N $932C If the message is held back, the second object is not used.
  $932C,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the second object is not used.
  $932F,$02 Scale the roll by #N$15.
  $9331,$03 Call #R$9CA8 to scale the roll.
  $9334,$01 Add #REGb to the roll.
  $9335,$04 Read *#R$B70C.
  $9339,$03 Add the strength of the object at #R$B70C.
  $933C,$02 Jump to #R$9340 if the sum did not overflow.
  $933E,$02 Cap the sum at #N$FF.
@ $9340 label=Action_UseTheOtherObject_Attack_TestTheCombatByte
  $9340,$05 Jump to #R$9360 if the total is below the object's combat byte.
  $9345,$04 Mark the object broken.
  $9349,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $934C,$03 Call #R$A18C to clear the tie on the object.
  $934F,$04 Halve the strength.
  $9353,$03 Read the second flag from the object record.
  $9356,$05 Call #R$9D50 if the object's second flag is below #N$02.
  $935B,$02 Name this object under code #N$83.
  $935D,$03 Call #R$A16C to print "is" with this object's name.
@ $9360 label=Action_UseTheOtherObject_Attack_ReadSecondObject
  $9360,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $9363,$03 Return if no object was named.
  $9366,$04 Read *#R$B70A.
  $936A,$05 Return if the object is broken.
  $936F,$03 Read the combat byte from #REGiy.
  $9372,$02 Scale the roll by #N$15.
  $9374,$03 Call #R$9CA8 to scale the roll.
  $9377,$01 Add the combat byte in #REGb to the roll.
  $9378,$02 Jump to #R$937C if the sum did not overflow.
  $937A,$02 Cap the sum at #N$FF.
@ $937C label=Action_UseTheOtherObject_Attack_ReturnIfWeaker
  $937C,$04 Return if the roll is below the object's combat byte.
  $9380,$04 Mark the object broken.
  $9384,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $9387,$03 Call #R$A18C to clear the tie on the object.
  $938A,$03 Read the strength from #REGiy.
  $938D,$02 Halve the strength.
  $938F,$03 Update the object's strength.
  $9392,$03 Call #R$9D50 to find where the object is.
  $9395,$04 Use the second-object pointer as the object record.
@ $9399 label=Action_UseTheOtherObject_Attack_PrintTheObjectName
  $9399,$02 Pass code #N$83 on to the naming routine.
  $939B,$03 Jump to #R$A16C to print the object's name.

c $939E Action: Give
@ $939E label=Action_Give
D $939E Gives the current object to the second character.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
  $939E,$04 Read *#R$B70A.
N $93A2 Test whether the object is being carried.
  $93A2,$03 Call #R$9C78 to test whether the object is carried.
  $93A5,$03 Point at the message "You are not carrying it[0x15]" (#R$ADF1).
  $93A8,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD if #R$9C78 found the object not carried, to print "You are not carrying it".
  $93AB,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $93AE,$03 Call #R$9CED to total the mass inside the object.
  $93B1,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $93B5,$03 Add the object's mass onto the second object.
  $93B8,$05 Read the mass from #REGiy.
  $93BD,$07 Point #REGhl at #R$AE0C and jump to #R$72DD if the total in #REGb is more than the mass of the object at #REGiy.
  $93C4,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the object is not given.
  $93C7,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $93CA,$03 Update where this object is.
  $93CD,$03 Read the location from #REGiy.
  $93D0,$03 Copy the second object's location.
  $93D3,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $93D4,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $93D7,$03 Jump to #R$9BDD to move what is inside the current object to the other character's location.

c $93DA Action: Examine
@ $93DA label=Action_Examine
D $93DA Examines the current object and prints what is special about it.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
  $93DA,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the object is not examined.
  $93DD,$03 Read the current object.
  $93E0,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $93E3,$03 Read byte #N$0E of this object.
  $93E6,$03 Read byte #N$0F of this object.
  $93E9,$05 Jump to #R$72DD when the pointer is not zero.
  $93EE,$03 Point at the message "You see[0x16]" (#R$B000).
  $93F1,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $93F4,$07 Call #R$9EC7 to name the object.
  $93FB,$02 Use the character '.'.
  $93FD,$03 Call #R$858B to print one character.
  $9400,$03 Call #R$8583 to print a newline.
  $9403,$01 Return.
N $9404 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($13,$01,$01)($) for action #N($22), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $9404,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $9408,$03 Call #R$A5CA to test whether the object is open.
  $940B,$03 Jump to #R$A16C if the object is not open.
  $940E,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
N $9411 Count the objects being held.
  $9411,$03 Call #R$9D97 to count the visible objects.
  $9414,$04 Jump to #R$9423 if #R$9D97 counted nothing inside the current object.
N $9418 If the message is held back, the object is not examined.
  $9418,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the object is not examined.
  $941B,$03 Call #R$9D50 to find where the object is.
  $941E,$04 Mark the object not full.
  $9422,$01 Return.
@ $9423 label=Action_Examine_PrintTheName
  $9423,$02 Pass code #N$02 on to the naming routine.
  $9425,$03 Jump to #R$A16C to print the object's name.
N $9428 Reached through the action lists of #OBJECT($09,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($2A,$01,$01)($) for actions #N$0E and #N($11), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $9428,$03 Call #R$8D25 to stop the verb when the object is fixed or a fluid.
N $942B If the message is held back, the special description is not printed.
  $942B,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the special description is not printed.
  $942E,$03 Read *#R$B6F6.
  $9431,$04 Read *#R$B70A.
  $9435,$03 Load the copy count into #REGb.
@ $9438 label=Action_Examine_Loop
  $9438,$05 Jump to #R$9444 if the script location is the object's location.
  $943D,$02 Step to the next byte of this object.
  $943F,$02 Loop back to #R$9438 until every copy's location has been checked.
  $9441,$03 Jump to #R$72CE to say "i cannot do that".
@ $9444 label=Action_Examine_ReadTheCopyLocation
  $9444,$03 Read byte +#N$11 from the object record.
  $9447,$03 Jump to #R$944B if this was not the last copy.
  $944A,$01 Take zero for this object's location.
@ $944B label=Action_Examine_PrintSweptAway
  $944B,$04 Point at the message "and it get(s|d|ing|es) swept away".
  $944F,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $9452,$05 Read *#R$B708.
  $9457,$03 Set this object's location to the next copy's location (or zero).
  $945A,$04 Mark where this object is as unset.
  $945E,$04 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $9462,$03 Call #R$9BDD to move what is inside the current object to that location.
N $9465 Test this flag.
  $9465,$07 Jump to #R$90D2 if *#R$C12B is zero.
  $946C,$01 Return.

c $946D Action: Lock
@ $946D label=Action_Lock
D $946D Locks the current object with the second object as the key. An object that is already
. locked or is open is refused. #R$9488 is set up to SET bit 0.
  $946D,$03 Call #R$A1F9 to read the object's lock flag.
  $9470,$03 Jump to #R$A16C if the object is already locked or is open.
  $9473,$02 Use #N($C6), the SET 0 operation.
@ $9475 label=Action_Lock_TestTheKey
N $9475 #R$948D joins here with RES 0 to unlock the object.
  $9475,$03 Plant the operation at byte #N$948B of #R$9488.
  $9478,$04 Read *#R$B70A.
  $947C,$09 Take #N($83), and jump to #R$A164 if the key is broken.
N $9485 If the message is held back, the object is not locked or unlocked.
  $9485,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the object is not locked or unlocked.
  $9488,$04 Lock the object (SET 0), or unlock it when #R$948D has planted RES 0.
  $948C,$01 Return.

c $948D Action: Unlock
@ $948D label=Action_Unlock
D $948D Unlocks the current object. An object that is not locked, or is open, is refused;
. otherwise #R$9488 is set up to RES bit 0 and the key is tested at #R$9475.
  $948D,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $9491,$09 Take zero, and jump to #R$A16C if the object is not locked.
  $949A,$03 Call #R$A204 to test whether the object is open.
  $949D,$03 Jump to #R$A16C if the object is open.
  $94A0,$02 Use #N($86), the RES 0 operation.
  $94A2,$02 Jump back to #R$9475 to plant it and unlock the object.
N $94A4 Refuse the verb when the object is not carried.
N $94A4 Reached through the action lists of #OBJECT($01,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($05,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($08,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0A,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0B,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0C,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($0D,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($11,$01,$01)($) for action #N($2C), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $94A4,$03 Call #R$8C9B to refuse the verb when the object is not carried.
  $94A7,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $94AA,$03 Call #R$9F28 to find the exit through the object in #REGa.
  $94AD,$05 Jump to #R$9F76 if no exit runs through this object.
  $94B2,$04 Read *#R$B70A.
  $94B6,$09 Take #N($05), and jump to #R$A164 if the object is not open.
N $94BF If the message is held back, the locked object is not named.
  $94BF,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the locked object is not named.
  $94C2,$03 Read this exit's destination.
  $94C5,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $94C9,$04 Mark where this object is as unset.
  $94CD,$03 Update the object's location.
  $94D0,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $94D3,$03 Jump to #R$9BDD to move what is inside the current object to this exit's destination.

c $94D6 Turn: Run The Action Handler
@ $94D6 label=Turn_RunTheActionHandler
D $94D6 Runs the action handler for the current object and the current action, and stores the
. result in the message-suppressed flag.
  $94D6,$06 Save #REGix and #REGhl and call #R$9B44 to accept the named object.
  $94DC,$04 Take zero and jump to #R$9508 if #R$9B44 returned with the zero flag set.
  $94E0,$03 Read *#R$B6E7.
  $94E3,$04 Point #REGix at the action: "".
N $94E7 Find this id in the table at #REGix.
  $94E7,$03 Call #R$9DBD to look this id up in the table.
  $94EA,$06 Take #N($01), and jump to #R$9508 if #R$9DBD found the action in the table.
  $94F0,$03 Call #R$A1D0 to match the action against the five verb bytes.
  $94F3,$04 Take #N($01), and jump to #R$9508 if #R$A1D0 found the action in the verb list.
  $94F7,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
N $94FA Find the object's record.
  $94FA,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $94FD,$03 Read *#R$B6E7.
  $9500,$03 Call #R$9B81 to find an action record.
  $9503,$04 Take #N($01), and jump to #R$9508 if #R$9B81 found the action record.
  $9507,$01 Take zero.
@ $9508 label=Turn_RunTheActionHandler_Examine_RecordTheResult
  $9508,$03 Record in the message-suppressed flag whether this action ran.
  $950B,$03 Restore #REGhl and #REGix from the stack.
  $950E,$01 Return.

c $950F Turn: Run The Handler If Present
@ $950F label=Turn_RunTheHandlerIfPresent
D $950F Runs the handler when one is present. In the dark, with nothing named, the player sees
. "i see nothing here".
  $950F,$07 Save #REGhl, #REGix and #REGbc, and call #R$9B44 to accept the named object.
  $9516,$03 Jump to #R$95DA when #R$9B44 returns with the zero flag set.
N $9519 Test whether the location is lit.
  $9519,$03 Call #R$95ED to test whether the location is lit.
  $951C,$02 Jump to #R$9539 if the location is lit.
N $951E Test this flag.
  $951E,$06 Jump to #R$9531 if *#R$B711 is not zero.
N $9524 Test whether the object is being carried.
  $9524,$03 Call #R$9C78 to test whether the object is carried.
  $9527,$02 Jump to #R$9531 if the object is not being carried.
  $9529,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $952C,$03 Call #R$9C7B to test whether the character whose turn it is is carrying it.
  $952F,$02 Jump to #R$9539 if the character whose turn it is is carrying the object.
@ $9531 label=Turn_RunTheHandlerIfPresent_Examine_PrintNothing
  $9531,$03 Point at the message "i see nothing here" (#R$AFC4).
  $9534,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $9537,$02 Jump to #R$959B to test the message gate.
@ $9539 label=Turn_RunTheHandlerIfPresent_Examine_TestNounFlag
N $9539 Test the noun-print flag.
  $9539,$08 Jump to #R$95CC if *#R$B6FE is #N$01.
N $9541 Test the current object.
  $9541,$08 Jump to #R$95CC if *#R$B6E8 is #N$FF.
N $9549 Find the object's record.
  $9549,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $954C,$04 Update *#R$B708.
  $9550,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $9553,$03 Call #R$9728 to report who is carrying the object.
  $9556,$02 Jump to #R$959B unless #R$9728 returned zero.
N $9558 Test the second object.
  $9558,$07 Jump to #R$957A if *#R$B6E9 is #N$FF.
N $955F Test the second-noun flag.
  $955F,$07 Jump to #R$95CC if *#R$B6FF is #N$01.
  $9566,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
N $9569 Find the object's record.
  $9569,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $956C,$04 Update *#R$B70A.
  $9570,$03 Call #R$9728 to report who is carrying the object.
  $9573,$02 Jump to #R$959B if #R$9728 reported that another character is carrying the object.
  $9575,$03 Call #R$A1D0 to match the action against the five verb bytes.
  $9578,$02 Jump to #R$957E if #R$A1D0 found the action in the verb list.
@ $957A label=Turn_RunTheHandlerIfPresent_Examine_ReadTheObject
  $957A,$04 Read *#R$B708.
@ $957E label=Turn_RunTheHandlerIfPresent_Examine_ReadTheAction
  $957E,$03 Read *#R$B6E7.
  $9581,$03 Call #R$9B81 to find an action record.
  $9584,$02 Jump to #R$95CC if #R$9B81 did not find the action record.
@ $9586 label=Turn_RunTheHandlerIfPresent_Examine_CallHandler
  $9586,$06 Read the handler address from this action-list entry.
  $958C,$03 Call #R$9B6C to call the handler stored for this action.
  $958F,$0C Jump back to #R$9586 if the next action-list byte is zero.
@ $959B label=Check_Lit
  $959B,$07 If #R$B6FA is not #N$01 then jump to #R$95C7.
N $95A2 This check is a little unnecessary as the first thing the routine at #R$95ED does, is to check exactly this...
  $95A2,$07 If #R$B6EA is not #N$00 (i.e. "#TEXTTOKEN($C11B+$08,$01)") then skip the "is location lit" check, instead jump to #R$95AF.
N $95A9 #R$95ED uses the carry flag to indicate if it is light or dark.
  $95A9,$06 If the location is dark then call #R$72DD to tell the user #R$AE1F("it is dark").
@ $95AF label=Check_Action
  $95AF,$03 Read the current action.
  $95B2,$01 Keep the current action in #REGb.
  $95B3,$0A Call #R$95DF with #REGix=#R$B708 and #REGa=#R$B6E8.
  $95BD,$0A Call #R$95DF with #REGix=#R$B70A and #REGa=#R$B6E9.
N $95C7 Performs housekeeping and returns.
@ $95C7 label=Check_Return
  $95C7,$04 Restore #REGbc, #REGix and #REGhl from the stack.
  $95CB,$01 Return.
N $95CC Checks if the action given is valid.
@ $95CC label=Check_ValidAction
  $95CC,$0A Call #R$9DBD with #R$C730, to match the key value of #R$B6E7.
  $95D6,$04 Jump to #R$9586 if #R$9DBD found the action, rather than the #N$FF terminator.
@ $95DA label=Turn_RunTheHandlerIfPresent_Examine_CannotDoThat
  $95DA,$05 Else, call #R$72CE and jump to #R$95C7.

c $95DF Turn: Wake An Animal
@ $95DF label=Turn_WakeAnAnimal
D $95DF Returns at once when this character is not an animal or is broken. Otherwise the
. special object is used.
  $95DF,$05 Return if the object is not an animal.
  $95E4,$05 Return if the object is broken.
  $95E9,$03 Call #R$9AA0 to look the id up as a special object.
  $95EC,$01 Return.

c $95ED Condition: Is The Location Lit?
@ $95ED label=LocationLit
D $95ED Reports whether the current location is lit.
N $95ED We're only interested if the currently processed character is the player. Return if not.
  $95ED,$05 Return if #R$B6EA is not zero.
N $95F2 Begin...
  $95F2,$07 Save #REGix and #REGbc, and point #REGix at the player.
N $95F5 Check if the player object is inside "something".
  $95F9,$03 Call #R$9E7A to test whether the container is lit.
  $95FC,$03 If the player is inside of another object then jump to #R$9608.
N $95FF Check the location data to see if this location is light or dark.
  $95FF,$03 Call #R$9D37 to point #REGix at this location.
  $9602,$06 If the location is marked as being "LIGHT" then jump to #R$9624 to return.
N $9608 The "#TEXTTOKEN($C305+$08,$01)" is a light source, can it be used?
@ $9608 label=LocationLit_Sword
  $9608,$0B Save #REGiy, point it at #OBJECT($0E,$01,$01)($), and call #R$9E34.
  $9613,$04 Jump to #R$9620 if #R$9E34 did not find that object here.
  $9617,$03 Read that object's attributes.
  $961A,$02 XOR #EVAL($F7,$02,$08).
  $961C,$04 Jump to #R$9628 if that object gives light, is not broken and is full.
N $9620 The below handle the two responses for "light" and "dark" using the carry flag to signify darkness when set, and light unset.
@ $9620 label=LocationLit_IsDark
  $9620,$03 Point at the message "it is dark" (#R$AE1F).
  $9623,$01 Set carry: this location is dark.
@ $9624 label=LocationLit_Return
  $9624,$03 Restore #REGbc and #REGix from the stack.
  $9627,$01 Return.
@ $9628 label=LocationLit_IsLight
  $9628,$01 Clear carry: this location is lit.
  $9629,$02 Jump back to #R$9624 to return.

c $962B Turn: You See
@ $962B label=YouSeeEntry
D $962B Prints "you see" and describes the place the player is in.
  $962B,$03 Point at the message "You see[0x16]" (#R$B000).
  $962E,$02 Jump to #R$964D to print the location text.

c $9630 Turn: Describe This Location
@ $9630 label=YouSeePreposition
E $9630 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D0DD }
. LIST#
D $9630 Describes this location: the preposition, "You are in", and the location text.
  $9630,$04 Call #R$9BB1 to find this location's record.
M $9634,$05 Fetch the location attribute, keep only bits 1-3 which signify the location preposition ("IN", "ON", "AT", etc).
  $9637,$02,b$01
  $9639,$03 Create an offset for the preposition word look-up.
  $963C,$04 Add the offset to #R$BA80.
  $9640,$03 Fetch the referenced text bytes which display the preposition word.
  $9643,$06 Write the text tokens to #R$AFFC(#N$AFFD) which updates the copy for "You are XXXX[0x16]".
  $9649,$04 Point at the message "You are in[0x16]".
@ $964D label=YouSee_Wrapper
  $964D,$08 Save #REGix, #REGiy and #REGbc and call #R$965B to print the location text.
  $9655,$05 Restore #REGbc, #REGiy and #REGix from the stack.
  $965A,$01 Return.

c $965B Turn: Print The Location Text
@ $965B label=YouSeeStart
D $965B Prints the location text, draws the picture, waits for a key, and lists the exits and
. what is here.
N $965B From here starts the "intro" to a location;
. #LIST
. { Print the "You see[0x16]" text }
. { Handle printing the location data message for this location (if one exists) }
. { Handles initiating the location graphics/ drawing (if it exists) }
. { Handles waiting for a keypress (if something was drawn) }
. { Prints available exits }
. LIST#
  $965B,$01 Store #REGa in #REGb temporarily.
  $965C,$03 Call #R$9BB1 to set #REGix to point to the appropriate #R$BA8A(location data) for the current location.
  $965F,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $9662,$06 Load the #R$BA8A(location data) offset for the current location description.
  $9668,$01 Use byte 9 of this object again.
  $9669,$01 Combine this flag with the low byte.
  $966A,$03 Call #R$9686 to print the location description.
  $966D,$04 Call #R$7F78 with the location id.
  $9671,$07 Test if #R$7F77 contains #N($FF), call #R$969A if it does not (i.e. wait for a keypress only if something has been drawn).
  $9678,$03 Call #R$8583 to print a newline.
  $967B,$04 Call #R$A0C8 with the location id.
@ $967F label=YouSee_Exits
  $967F,$04 Call #R$A138 with the location id.
  $9683,$03 Jump to #R$9F94 to describe what is here.

c $9686 Turn: Print Or Describe
@ $9686 label=YouSeePrint
E $9686 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D1C6 }
. LIST#
D $9686 Prints the location description when one is present. Otherwise it prints the location
. name.
  $9686,$03 Jump to #R$72DD to print the location description if its pointer in #REGhl is not zero (the caller sets the zero flag).
@ $9689 label=YouSeePrint_PrintTheLocationName
  $9689,$03 The location name starts #N$02 bytes into the record.
  $968C,$08 Point #REGiy at the name, #N$02 bytes into the record.
  $9694,$03 Call #R$9ED6 to choose which noun byte to print.
  $9697,$02 Restore #REGiy from the stack.
  $9699,$01 Return.

c $969A Turn: Wait For A Key
@ $969A label=Turn_WaitForAKey
D $969A Waits until a key is pressed, then sets the border to white.
N $969A This is the same key wait as #R$84C2, but it sets the border to white before returning.
  $969A,$03 Read from the keyboard port.
  $969D,$02,b$01 Keep bits 0-4, one for each key in the half-row. XOR A then IN A,(#N$FE) reads every half-row at once, and a pressed key reads as a reset bit.
  $969F,$04 Loop back to #R$969A until any key has been pressed.
  $96A3,$04 Set the border to white.
  $96A7,$01 Return.

c $96A8 Turn: Describe And List
@ $96A8 label=YouSeeController
D $96A8 Describes the location, prints its name, and lists the exits.
  $96A8,$03 Call #R$9BB1 to locate location.
  $96AB,$03 Call #R$9689 to print the location name.
  $96AE,$03 Call #R$8583 to print a newline.
  $96B1,$02 Jump back to #R$967F to display exits.

c $96B3 Turn: Advance The Other Characters
@ $96B3 label=TurnAdvanceTheOtherCharacters
D $96B3 Gives every other character a turn, running their scripts.
N $96B3 Save the registers used while the other characters take a turn.
  $96B3,$0A Save #REGhl, #REGix, #REGiy, #REGbc and #REGde, then call #R$A9D6 to run the location #N$25 event.
  $96BD,$03 Call #R$980E to walk the special-object records.
  $96C0,$01 Clear the flag at #R$B6F0.
  $96C1,$03 Update *#R$B6F0.
  $96C4,$01 The other characters start with messages held back.
  $96C5,$03 Store that in the message-suppressed flag.
  $96C8,$03 Update *#R$B6FA.
  $96CB,$04 Point #REGiy at the handler table at #R$CA84.
@ $96CF label=TurnAdvanceTheOtherCharacters_Loop
  $96CF,$03 Read the id from this handler record.
  $96D2,$04 Jump to #R$971B if this handler id is #N$FF.
  $96D6,$03 Read the countdown from this handler record.
  $96D9,$04 Jump to #R$9713 if the countdown is zero.
  $96DD,$08 Step the countdown down and jump to #R$96FE if it has not reached zero.
  $96E5,$03 Read *#R$B6F0.
  $96E8,$07 Jump to #R$96FE if the other-characters gate is #N$01.
  $96EF,$01 Count on from the other-characters gate.
  $96F0,$03 Update *#R$B6F0.
  $96F3,$03 Read the low byte of the first handler.
  $96F6,$03 Read the high byte of the first handler.
N $96F9 Run the handler for this action.
  $96F9,$03 Call #R$9B6C to call the handler stored for this action.
  $96FC,$02 Jump to #R$9713 to update the day flag.
@ $96FE label=TurnAdvanceTheOtherCharacters_TestTheSecondFlag
  $96FE,$03 Read the second flag from this handler record.
  $9701,$04 Jump to #R$9713 if the second flag is zero.
  $9705,$05 Jump to #R$9713 if the second flag is below the countdown.
  $970A,$03 Read the low byte of the second handler.
  $970D,$03 Read the high byte of the second handler.
N $9710 Run the handler for this action.
  $9710,$03 Call #R$9B6C to call the handler stored for this action.
@ $9713 label=TurnAdvanceTheOtherCharacters_StepToTheNext
  $9713,$05 Step the pointer on by #N$07 bytes.
  $9718,$03 Jump back to #R$96CF to try the next object.
@ $971B label=TurnAdvanceTheOtherCharacters_SetTheDayAndReturn
  $971B,$05 Write #N$01 (day) to *#R$B702.
  $9720,$07 Restore #REGde, #REGbc, #REGiy, #REGix and #REGhl from the stack.
  $9727,$01 Return.

c $9728 Report Who Is Carrying An Object
@ $9728 label=Turn_ReportWhoIsCarryingAnObject
D $9728 On the player's turn, if the object in #REGa is held by a visible animal, it prints
. "<animal> is carrying <object>" and returns with the zero flag clear; otherwise it returns
. with the zero flag set.
  $9728,$03 Return if the object id is #N$FF.
  $972B,$06 Save #REGix, #REGiy and #REGbc, and keep the object id in #REGb.
N $9731 Test whose turn it is.
  $9731,$07 Jump to #R$973B if *#R$B6EA is #N$00 (the player).
  $9738,$01 Set the zero flag.
  $9739,$02 Jump to #R$9776 to return with the zero flag set.
@ $973B label=Turn_ReportWhoIsCarryingAnObject_RestoreTheId
  $973B,$01 Take the object id back into #REGa.
N $973C Find the object's record.
  $973C,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $973F,$03 Read where this object is from the object record.
  $9742,$04 Jump to #R$9776 if this object is not inside another object.
  $9746,$01 Take the object id back into #REGa.
  $9747,$07 Point #REGiy at this object and call #R$9C7B to test whether the character whose turn it is is carrying it.
  $974E,$02 Jump to #R$9776 if the character is carrying it.
N $9750 Find the object's record.
  $9750,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $9753,$06 Jump to #R$9776 if the object is not an animal.
  $9759,$07 Jump to #R$9776 if the player is not visible.
  $9760,$03 Read the name token from #REGiy.
  $9763,$03 Read the name token's high byte from #REGiy.
  $9766,$04 Read the name token from the object record.
  $976A,$03 Read the name token's high byte from the object record.
  $976D,$04 Point at the message "[0x04] is carrying[0x04][0x15]".
  $9771,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $9774,$02 Clear the zero flag.
@ $9776 label=Turn_ReportWhoIsCarryingAnObject_Return
  $9776,$05 Restore #REGbc, #REGiy and #REGix from the stack.
  $977B,$01 Return.

c $977C Turn: The Player Has Died
@ $977C label=Turn_ThePlayerHasDied
D $977C The player has died. The current object is marked broken, its copies are cleared, the
. tie is cut, and the character slots are walked.
N $977C Test the current object.
  $977C,$07 Jump to #R$90D2 if *#R$B6E8 is zero.
  $9783,$06 Save #REGbc, #REGiy and #REGix, and keep the object id in #REGc.
N $9789 Find the object's record.
  $9789,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $978C,$04 Mark the object broken.
  $9790,$03 Call #R$9D53 to find where the object is.
  $9793,$01 Use the current object again.
  $9794,$03 Call #R$9A85 to find the special-object record.
  $9797,$05 Jump to #R$97A0 if this object has no special-object record.
  $979C,$04 Clear the special-object id.
@ $97A0 label=Turn_ThePlayerHasDied_ClearTheTieOnTheObject
  $97A0,$03 Call #R$A18C to clear the tie on the object.
  $97A3,$01 Use the current object again.
  $97A4,$03 Call #R$7F60 to walk the character slots.
  $97A7,$05 Restore #REGix, #REGiy and #REGbc from the stack.
  $97AC,$01 Return.

c $97AD Turn: Reset To The Player
@ $97AD label=Turn_ResetToThePlayer
D $97AD Resets the turn to the player, clears the talk flag, and points the current character at
. the player's object.
  $97AD,$01 Clear the character, the scratch flag and the talk flag.
  $97AE,$03 Update *#R$B6EA.
  $97B1,$03 Store the result at #R$B6F1.
  $97B4,$03 Update *#R$B6F9.
  $97B7,$06 Point *#R$B70C at "#TEXTTOKEN($C11B+$08,$01)".
  $97BD,$02 Roll a random number below #N$04.
N $97BF Roll a random number.
  $97BF,$03 Call #R$9C9F to roll a number from the limit in #REGa.
  $97C2,$01 Add one, making the roll #N$01-#N$04.
  $97C3,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $97C4,$04 Point #REGiy at #R$C808.
  $97C8,$03 Each entry is #N$06 bytes.
@ $97CB label=Turn_ResetToThePlayer_StepToTheNextExtraExit
  $97CB,$02 Step #REGiy on to the next #N$06-byte entry.
  $97CD,$02 Loop back to #R$97CB until the roll in #REGb runs out.
  $97CF,$04 Plant this extra-exit entry in the LD IY at #R$A7CF.
  $97D3,$03 Read the low byte of this extra exit's direction token.
  $97D6,$03 Read the high byte of this extra exit's direction token.
  $97D9,$02 Count #N$03 passes.
@ $97DB label=Turn_ResetToThePlayer_ClearTheBytes
  $97DB,$02 Write zero into the byte #REGhl points at.
  $97DD,$01 Step to the next byte.
  $97DE,$02 Loop back to #R$97DB until #N$03 runs out.
  $97E0,$02 Roll a random number below #N$03.
N $97E2 Roll a random number.
  $97E2,$03 Call #R$9C9F to roll a number from the limit in #REGa.
  $97E5,$01 Keep #N$03.
  $97E6,$02 The character-table offset's high byte is zero.
  $97E8,$02 Double the roll and keep the overflow.
  $97EA,$02 Double the roll and keep the overflow.
  $97EC,$04 Index the riddle table at #R$C7FC.
  $97F0,$03 Update *#R$B6EE.
  $97F3,$01 Return.

c $97F4 Turn: Finish The Sentence
@ $97F4 label=Turn_FinishTheSentence
D $97F4 Finishes the sentence with the message at #REGhl, a full stop, and a newline.
N $97F4 Print the message at #REGhl.
  $97F4,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
N $97F7 Print a full stop.
  $97F7,$05 Print a full stop at the end of the sentence.
  $97FC,$03 Jump to #R$8583 to print a newline.

c $97FF Turn: Gate The Message
@ $97FF label=Turn_GateTheMessage
D $97FF Gates the message. The caller continues only when the message gate and the
. message-suppressed flag are both set.
  $97FF,$05 Read *#R$B6FA.
  $9804,$05 Jump to #R$9809 if the message gate and the message-suppressed flag are both set.
@ $9809 label=Turn_GateTheMessage_Return
  $9809,$01 Restore #REGbc from the stack.
  $980A,$01 Return.
  $980B,$01 Try count for this turn. #R$9815 clears it, #R$99AA adds one, and #R$9885 stops at #N$06.
  $980C,$01 Workspace byte. #R$9B12 writes whether the location is dark.
  $980D,$01 Result of #R$9F82, written at #R$99EF and read at #R$9A4B.
  $980E,$03 Call #R$9B02, the empty action, before the special objects are walked.
  $9811,$04 Point #REGiy at the special-object records.
@ $9815 label=Turn_GateTheMessage_ClearTheTryCount
  $9815,$04 Clear the try count.
  $9819,$03 Read the special-object id.
  $981C,$05 Jump to #R$9909 at the end of the special-object records.
  $9821,$05 Jump to #R$9901 if this special-object id is zero.
  $9826,$03 Update *#R$B6EA.
  $9829,$03 Call #R$9F82 to get object first location.
  $982C,$04 Update *#R$B70C.
  $9830,$03 Update *#R$B6F6.
  $9833,$01 Clear the time-of-day flag at #R$B702.
  $9834,$03 Update *#R$B702.
  $9837,$03 Read the special-object id.
  $983A,$06 Save #REGiy and point it at #OBJECT($00,$01,$01)($).
  $9840,$03 Call #R$9E25 to swap the objects and test visibility again.
  $9843,$04 Jump to #R$9868 if #R$9E25 did not find the object visible.
N $9847 Test the flag.
  $9847,$07 Jump to #R$9868 if *#R$980C is #N$02.
  $984E,$05 Write #N$01 (day) to *#R$B702.
N $9853 Test the flag.
  $9853,$07 Jump to #R$9868 if *#R$980C is not #N$01.
  $985A,$01 Count on from the turn phase.
  $985B,$03 Store the result at #R$980C.
N $985E Say "you{5} hear a noise".
  $985E,$06 Point #REGhl at the message and call #R$72DD.
  $9864,$01 Clear the time-of-day flag at #R$B702.
  $9865,$03 Update *#R$B702.
@ $9868 label=Turn_GateTheMessage_TestIfInside
  $9868,$08 Jump to #R$9B16 if this object is inside another object.
  $9870,$04 Read *#R$B70C.
  $9874,$03 Call #R$7F10 to keep the character slot.
  $9877,$04 Take zero, and jump to #R$987C unless #R$7F10 found this character's slot.
  $987B,$01 Count on from zero.
@ $987C label=Turn_GateTheMessage_StoreTheSlotFlag
  $987C,$03 Store the slot flag at #R$B6F4.
@ $987F label=Turn_GateTheMessage_ReadTheScriptPointer
  $987F,$03 Read the low byte of the script pointer.
  $9882,$03 Read the high byte of the script pointer.
@ $9885 label=Turn_GateTheMessage_TestTheTryCount
N $9885 Test the flag.
  $9885,$07 Jump to #R$9901 if the try count is #N$06.
  $988C,$01 Read the next script byte.
  $988D,$03 The next field is #N$04 bytes on.
  $9890,$03 Point #REGix at this script byte.
  $9893,$06 Jump to #R$98CB if the low nibble of the script byte is not below #N$05.
N $9899 Test the flag.
  $9899,$07 Jump to #R$98BF if *#R$B6F4 is not #N$01.
  $98A0,$04 Jump to #R$98BF if bit 6 of the script byte is set.
  $98A4,$01 Clear the flag at #R$B6F4.
  $98A5,$03 Store the result at #R$B6F4.
  $98A8,$01 Set #REGa to #N$01 for #R$7F1A.
  $98A9,$03 Call #R$7F1A to save the clause and act.
  $98AC,$02 Jump to #R$98BF if #R$7F1A returned with the zero flag set.
N $98AE Update the message gate.
  $98AE,$08 Hold messages back: set the message gate and the message-suppressed flag.
  $98B6,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$9901, where the message routine should return.
  $98B9,$06 Jump to #R$99CE to test the noun-print flag.
@ $98BF label=Turn_GateTheMessage_TestTheEntryKind
  $98BF,$08 Jump to #R$9974 if the low nibble of the script code is #N$04.
  $98C7,$02 Jump to #R$9928 if the low nibble of the script code is below #N$04.
  $98C9,$02 Jump to #R$9901 to step on to the next special object.
@ $98CB label=Turn_GateTheMessage_TakeThePointer
  $98CB,$04 Jump to #R$98DD if the low nibble of the script code is not #N$0E.
  $98CF,$03 Read the next byte of this script.
  $98D2,$03 Store it as the low byte of the script pointer.
  $98D5,$03 Read the byte after that.
  $98D8,$03 Store it as the high byte of the script pointer.
  $98DB,$02 Jump back to #R$987F to read the script pointer.
@ $98DD label=Turn_GateTheMessage_TakeTheCount
  $98DD,$04 Jump to #R$98EC if the low nibble of the script code is not #N$0C.
  $98E1,$03 Read the next script byte into #REGb.
  $98E4,$03 Read the special-object id.
  $98E7,$03 Call #R$9AA0 to look the id up as a special object.
  $98EA,$02 Jump back to #R$987F to read the script pointer.
@ $98EC label=Turn_GateTheMessage_PickAtRandom
  $98EC,$04 Jump to #R$98F5 if this script code is not #N$0F.
  $98F0,$03 Call #R$9A59 to pick a random script entry.
  $98F3,$02 Jump back to #R$987F to read the script pointer.
@ $98F5 label=Turn_GateTheMessage_TestForNoCode
  $98F5,$04 Jump to #R$98FC if this script code is not zero.
  $98F9,$01 Step the script pointer in #REGhl on by #N$04 bytes.
  $98FA,$02 Jump back to #R$9885 to try the next script entry.
@ $98FC label=Turn_GateTheMessage_KeepTheSmaller
  $98FC,$01 Clear #REGe before the locations are compared.
  $98FD,$01 Clear #REGe.
  $98FE,$03 Call #R$9A68 to keep the smaller of the location and the roll.
@ $9901 label=Turn_GateTheMessage_StepThePointerOnBySevenBytes
  $9901,$05 Step the pointer on by #N$07 bytes.
  $9906,$03 Jump back to #R$9815 to try the next special object.
@ $9909 label=Turn_GateTheMessage_ClearTheCharacterWhoseTurn
  $9909,$01 Clear the character whose turn it is.
  $990A,$03 Update *#R$B6EA.
  $990D,$01 Count on to #N$01 for the day flag.
  $990E,$03 Update *#R$B702.
  $9911,$06 Point *#R$B70C at "#TEXTTOKEN($C11B+$08,$01)".
  $9917,$01 Return.

c $9918 Turn: Step The Script Pointer
@ $9918 label=Turn_StepTheScriptPointer
D $9918 Stores a script pointer on the special-object record. Bit 4 of the script byte skips
. two bytes first.
  $9918,$07 Step #REGhl on, and jump to #R$9921 if bit 4 of this script byte is clear.
  $991F,$02 Skip two bytes of the pointer.
@ $9921 label=Turn_StepTheScriptPointer_StoreThePointer
  $9921,$03 Store the low byte of the script pointer on the special-object record.
  $9924,$03 Store the high byte of the script pointer on the special-object record.
  $9927,$01 Return.
@ $9928 label=Turn_StepTheScriptPointer_RunTheScriptEntry
  $9928,$03 Call #R$9918 to step the script pointer past this entry and store it on the special-object record.
  $992B,$06 Jump to #R$994A if bit 0 of this script byte is set.
  $9931,$03 Read the action id from this script.
  $9934,$03 Update *#R$B6E7.
  $9937,$03 Read the object id from this script.
  $993A,$03 Update *#R$B6E8.
  $993D,$03 Read the second object id from this script.
  $9940,$03 Update *#R$B6E9.
  $9943,$03 Call #R$99C6 to build the action sentence from the saved match.
  $9946,$02 Jump to #R$99AA if #R$99C6 returned zero.
  $9948,$02 Jump to #R$9967 to skip updating the message gate.
@ $994A label=Turn_StepTheScriptPointer_ReadTheHandlerAddress
  $994A,$03 Read the low byte of the handler address.
  $994D,$03 Read the high byte of the handler address.
  $9950,$01 Clear A so the message gate and the message-suppressed flag are both cleared.
  $9951,$03 Clear the message gate so the handler may speak.
  $9954,$03 Clear the message-suppressed flag before the handler runs.
N $9957 Run the handler for this action.
  $9957,$03 Call #R$9B6C to call the handler stored for this action.
N $995A Test the message-suppressed flag.
  $995A,$07 Jump to #R$99AA unless the message-suppressed flag is #N$01.
  $9961,$03 Update *#R$B6FA.
N $9964 Run the handler for this action.
  $9964,$03 Call #R$9B6C to call the handler stored for this action.
@ $9967 label=Turn_StepTheScriptPointer_TestAndClear
  $9967,$07 Jump back to #R$9901 if bit 5 of this script byte is clear.
  $996E,$04 Clear this script byte.
  $9972,$02 Jump back to #R$9901 to clear *#R$980B.
@ $9974 label=Turn_StepTheScriptPointer_StepTwoBytes
  $9974,$03 Step the script pointer by #N$02 bytes.
  $9977,$03 Call #R$9918 to step the script pointer past this entry and store it on the special-object record.
  $997A,$03 Read the next byte of this script.
  $997D,$04 Jump to #R$9994 if that script byte is #N$FF.
  $9981,$03 Update *#R$B6E7.
N $9984 Update the current object.
  $9984,$08 Write #N$FF to *#R$B6E8 and *#R$B6E9.
  $998C,$03 Call #R$99C6 to build the action sentence from the saved match.
  $998F,$02 Jump to #R$99AA if #R$99C6 returned zero.
  $9991,$03 Jump back to #R$9901 to clear *#R$980B.
@ $9994 label=Turn_StepTheScriptPointer_FollowThePointer
  $9994,$07 Jump back to #R$9901 if bit 4 of this script byte is clear.
  $999B,$03 Read the low byte of the pointer in this script.
  $999E,$03 Read the high byte of the pointer in this script.
  $99A1,$03 Store the low byte of that pointer on the special-object record.
  $99A4,$03 Store the high byte of that pointer on the special-object record.
  $99A7,$03 Jump back to #R$9901 to clear *#R$980B.
@ $99AA label=Turn_StepTheScriptPointer_CountAnotherTry
  $99AA,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$980B.
  $99AD,$01 Count one more try.
  $99AE,$07 Jump back to #R$987F if bit 4 of this script byte is clear.
  $99B5,$02 Step the script pointer on by #REGde.
  $99B7,$03 Read the high byte of the word at this script byte.
  $99BA,$03 Read this script byte into #REGl.
  $99BD,$03 Store the low byte of that word on the special-object record.
  $99C0,$03 Store the high byte of that word on the special-object record.
  $99C3,$03 Branch #R$9885 on this test.

c $99C6 Turn: Apply The Saved Match
@ $99C6 label=Turn_ApplyTheSavedMatch
D $99C6 Builds the action sentence through #R$7AF5 and returns when that routine sets the zero flag.
  $99C6,$05 Save the script pointer and call #R$7AF5 to build the action sentence.
  $99CB,$03 Jump to #R$9A56 if #R$7AF5 returned with the zero flag set.
@ $99CE label=Turn_ApplyTheSavedMatch_TestTheNounPrintFlag
N $99CE Test the noun-print flag.
  $99CE,$07 Jump to #R$9A32 if *#R$B6FE is #N$01.
N $99D5 Test the current action.
  $99D5,$07 Jump to #R$99E5 if *#R$B6E7 is not #N$1E.
  $99DC,$03 Read *#R$B6F6.
  $99DF,$03 Point #REGhl at *#R$C12B.
  $99E2,$03 Jump to #R$9A32 if the script location is not the current location.
@ $99E5 label=Turn_ApplyTheSavedMatch_TestForNoObject
N $99E5 Test the current object.
  $99E5,$07 Jump to #R$9A2B if *#R$B6E8 is #N$FF.
  $99EC,$03 Call #R$9F82 to get object first location.
  $99EF,$07 Jump to #R$9A2B unless #R$9F82 returned #N$FF.
  $99F6,$04 Read *#R$B6F5.
  $99FA,$04 Jump to #R$9A2B if the script location (*#R$B6F6) is where the player starts (*#R$B6F5).
  $99FE,$03 Load the copy count into #REGb.
@ $9A01 label=Turn_ApplyTheSavedMatch_TestWhetherTheObjectIsHere
  $9A01,$05 Jump to #R$9A0C if the current object is the object's location.
  $9A06,$02 Step to the next byte of this object.
  $9A08,$02 Loop back to #R$9A01 until every copy's location has been checked.
  $9A0A,$02 Jump to #R$9A2B to describe the action.
@ $9A0C label=Turn_ApplyTheSavedMatch_ReadTheCurrentCharacter
  $9A0C,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $9A0F,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $9A10,$05 Write #N$FF to *#R$B6EA.
  $9A15,$05 Write #N$01 (day) to *#R$B702.
  $9A1A,$05 Call #R$712B to print the sentence for the current action.
  $9A1F,$03 Clear the time-of-day flag at #R$B702.
  $9A22,$03 Update *#R$B702.
  $9A25,$01 Use the character whose turn it is again.
  $9A26,$03 Update *#R$B6EA.
  $9A29,$02 Jump to #R$9A32 to run the handler, as the action has already been described.
@ $9A2B label=Turn_ApplyTheSavedMatch_PrintTheSentence
  $9A2B,$07 Call #R$712B to print the sentence for the current action.
@ $9A32 label=Turn_ApplyTheSavedMatch_RunTheHandler
  $9A32,$03 Call #R$950F to run the handler when one is present.
  $9A35,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $9A38,$03 Point #REGhl at the script location.
  $9A3B,$03 Point #REGde at #R$B017.
  $9A3E,$03 Call #R$9ACD to print a line about another character here.
N $9A41 Test the noun-print flag.
  $9A41,$07 Jump to #R$9A54 if *#R$B6FE is #N$01.
  $9A48,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $9A4B,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$980D.
  $9A4E,$03 Point #REGde at #R$B01C.
  $9A51,$03 Call #R$9ACD to print a line about another character here.
@ $9A54 label=Turn_ApplyTheSavedMatch_ClearTheZeroFlag
  $9A54,$02 Clear the zero flag.
@ $9A56 label=Turn_ApplyTheSavedMatch_RestoreTheSavedMatch
  $9A56,$02 Restore #REGix from the stack.
  $9A58,$01 Return.

c $9A59 Turn: Pick A Script Entry
@ $9A59 label=Turn_PickAScriptEntry
D $9A59 Takes the smaller of the script limit and the special-object limit, rolls below that,
. and stores the script pointer from the chosen entry.
  $9A59,$03 Read the limit from this script byte.
  $9A5C,$05 Jump to #R$9A64 if that limit is below the special-object limit.
  $9A61,$03 Use the special-object limit instead.
@ $9A64 label=Turn_PickAScriptEntry_RollBelowTheLimit
N $9A64 Roll a random number.
  $9A64,$03 Call #R$9C9F to roll a number from the limit in #REGa.
  $9A67,$01 Keep the roll in #REGe.
  $9A68,$03 Read the special-object limit.
  $9A6B,$03 Jump to #R$9A6F if the special-object limit is not below the roll.
  $9A6E,$01 Keep the special-object limit in #REGe.
@ $9A6F label=Turn_PickAScriptEntry_Store
  $9A6F,$03 Read the low byte of the table pointer.
  $9A72,$03 Read the high byte of the table pointer.
  $9A75,$02 The record offset's high byte is zero.
  $9A77,$01 Add the roll in #REGde.
  $9A78,$01 Add the roll in #REGde again.
  $9A79,$01 Add the roll in #REGde a third time. Each entry is three bytes.
  $9A7A,$01 Step to the next byte.
  $9A7B,$01 Read the low byte of the chosen script pointer.
  $9A7C,$01 Step to the next byte.
  $9A7D,$01 Read the high byte of the chosen script pointer.
  $9A7E,$06 Store the chosen script pointer in bytes 2 and 3 of the special-object record.
  $9A84,$01 Return.

c $9A85 Table: Find A Special Object
@ $9A85 label=TableFindASpecialObject
D $9A85 Finds a special object by its index.
R $9A85 A The index to search for
  $9A85,$03 Keep the id being sought.
  $9A88,$03 Point #REGhl at the special-object records.
  $9A8B,$03 Each special-object entry is #N$07 bytes.
@ $9A8E label=TableFindASpecialObject_Loop
  $9A8E,$04 If the current pointer matches our key jump to #R$9A9A.
  $9A92,$04 Jump to #R$9A9A at the end of the special-object records.
  $9A96,$01 Increase the pointer address by #N($0007,$04,$04) (using #REGde).
  $9A97,$03 Jump back to #R$9A8E for the next special-object record.
@ $9A9A label=TableFindASpecialObject_PointAtTheRecord
  $9A9A,$05 Restore #REGbc and #REGde from the stack, and copy the special-object record from #REGhl into #REGiy.
  $9A9F,$01 Return.

c $9AA0 Turn: Use A Special Object
@ $9AA0 label=Turn_UseASpecialObject
D $9AA0 Uses a special object. It scans the seven-byte records until the id is #N$FF and looks
. that id up in the table.
  $9AA0,$07 Call #R$9A85 to find the special-object record.
  $9AA7,$04 Jump to #R$9AC8 if the termination character was reached.
  $9AAB,$03 Read the low byte of the table pointer on this special-object record.
  $9AAE,$03 Read the high byte of that table pointer.
  $9AB1,$03 Point #REGix at the table named by this special entry.
  $9AB4,$01 Use the result again.
  $9AB5,$03 Call #R$9DBD to look this id up in the table.
  $9AB8,$04 Jump to #R$9AC8 if the termination character was reached.
  $9ABC,$0C Store the address #R$9DBD found into bytes 2 and 3 of this special-object record.
@ $9AC8 label=Turn_UseASpecialObject_RestoreTheSpecialObject
  $9AC8,$04 Restore #REGix and #REGiy from the stack.
  $9ACC,$01 Return.

c $9ACD Turn: Report A Character
@ $9ACD label=TurnReportACharacter
D $9ACD Prints a line about another character in this place.
  $9ACD,$03 Return if the object id is #N$FF.
  $9AD0,$01 See whether the object id is zero.
  $9AD1,$01 Return if the result is zero.
  $9AD2,$07 Return if the turn phase is #N$02.
  $9AD9,$01 Use the result again.
  $9ADA,$03 Call #R$9F82 to get object first location.
  $9ADD,$03 Return if the byte at #REGhl equals this object's location.
  $9AE0,$03 Read *#R$B6F5.
  $9AE3,$02 Return unless *#R$B6F5 matches #REGc.
  $9AE5,$05 Return if the flag at #R$9BDC is zero.
  $9AEA,$05 Write #N$01 (day) to *#R$B702.
  $9AEF,$03 Point #REGhl at the address in #REGde and take #REGb into #REGa.
N $9AF2 Find the object's record.
  $9AF2,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $9AF5,$06 Step the pointer on by #N$08 bytes.
  $9AFB,$06 Restore #REGde from the stack, save the name pointer, and call #R$72DD to print the message.
N $9AFE Print the message at #REGhl.
  $9B01,$01 Return.

c $9B02 Action: None
@ $9B02 label=Action_None
D $9B02 The empty action. Records where the player is and does nothing else.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
  $9B02,$05 Call #R$9F82 for #OBJECT($00,$01,$01)($).
  $9B07,$03 Write #REGa to #R$B6F5.
  $9B0A,$03 Call #R$95ED to test whether the location is lit.
  $9B0D,$02 Start from zero.
  $9B0F,$02 If it is light jump to #R$9B12.
  $9B11,$01 Make it #N$01 when the location is dark.
@ $9B12 label=Action_None_StoreTheDarkFlag
  $9B12,$03 Write #REGa to #R$980C.
  $9B15,$01 Return.

c $9B16 Action: Climb
@ $9B16 label=ActionClimb
D $9B16 Climbs the current object.
  $9B16,$05 Write #N$FF to *#R$B6E9.
  $9B1B,$03 Read where this object is from the object record.
  $9B1E,$03 Update *#R$B6E8.
N $9B21 Find the object's record.
  $9B21,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $9B24,$07 Jump back to #R$9870 if the object is broken.
  $9B2B,$07 Jump back to #R$9870 if the object is an animal.
  $9B32,$07 Jump back to #R$9901 if the object is not open.
  $9B39,$05 Write #N$37 (climb out) to *#R$B6E7.
  $9B3E,$03 Call #R$99C6 to build the action sentence from the saved match.
  $9B41,$03 Jump back to #R$9901 to clear *#R$980B.

c $9B44 Turn: Accept The Named Object
@ $9B44 label=Turn_AcceptTheNamedObject
D $9B44 Accepts the named object as the current one. A zero object counts as one; otherwise the
. noun flags must agree.
  $9B44,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $9B47,$03 Jump to #R$9B4C if the current object is not #N$FF.
  $9B4A,$01 Make #REGa #N$01 (no object counts as one).
  $9B4B,$01 Return.
@ $9B4C label=Turn_AcceptTheNamedObject_TestTheNounFlag
N $9B4C Test the noun-print flag.
  $9B4C,$06 Jump to #R$9B5F if *#R$B6FE is not zero.
  $9B52,$03 Point #REGhl at the current object.
  $9B55,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $9B58,$02 Return if the character whose turn it is is the current object.
  $9B5A,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $9B5D,$02 Return if the second object is the current object.
@ $9B5F label=Turn_AcceptTheNamedObject_TestTheSecondNoun
  $9B5F,$03 Read *#R$B6FF.
  $9B62,$02 Return if the second-noun flag is not zero.
  $9B64,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $9B67,$03 Point #REGhl at the character whose turn it is.
  $9B6A,$01 Compare the second object with the character whose turn it is.
  $9B6B,$01 Return.

c $9B6C Action: Call The Handler
@ $9B6C label=TriggerAction
D $9B6C Calls the handler address stored for an action.
R $9B6C HL Pointer to the action code
N $9B6C Save the registers and call the handler when its address is not zero.
  $9B6C,$0C Save #REGix, #REGiy, #REGde, #REGbc and the handler address, and call #R$9B80 when that address is not zero.
  $9B78,$07 Restore #REGhl, #REGbc, #REGde, #REGiy and #REGix from the stack.
  $9B7F,$01 Return.

c $9B80 Table: Jump To The Record
@ $9B80 label=TriggerAction_Jump
D $9B80 Jumps to the action handler the caller addressed.
  $9B80,$01 Jump to the action handler the caller addressed.

c $9B81 Table: Find An Action Record
@ $9B81 label=TableFindAnActionRecord
E $9B81 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D1DC }
. LIST#
D $9B81 Finds an action handler. The copy count plus #N$10 is where the action list starts.
  $9B81,$05 Save #REGde, keep the action id in #REGd, and read the copy count.
  $9B86,$02 Add #N$10 to the copy count. The action list starts that far into the object.
  $9B88,$01 Keep the copy count plus #N$10.
  $9B89,$01 Use the result again.
  $9B8A,$02 The action-record offset's high byte is zero.
  $9B8C,$02 Point #REGix at the action list, that far into the object.
N $9B8E Find this id in the table at #REGix.
  $9B8E,$03 Call #R$9DBD to look this id up in the table.
  $9B91,$01 Restore #REGde from the stack.
  $9B92,$01 Return.

c $9B93 Table: Next Record
@ $9B93 label=Table_StepThroughThreeByteRecords
E $9B93 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D206 }
. LIST#
D $9B93 Steps to the next record in a table.
R $9B93 IX Pointer to the record data
R $9B93 O:A The record ID
R $9B93 O:IY The record data
  $9B93,$01 Switch register sets before the index is stepped.
  $9B94,$05 Move onto the next record #REGix=#REGix+#N($0003,$04,$04).
  $9B99,$03 Read byte 2 of this record.
  $9B9C,$03 Read byte 1 of this record.
  $9B9F,$06 Read byte 0 of this record.
  $9BA5,$02 Set flag if this is the termination character (#N$FF).
  $9BA7,$01 Exchange registers.
  $9BA8,$01 Return.

c $9BA9 Table: Find The Record
@ $9BA9 label=Table_FindTheRecord
E $9BA9 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D21C }
. LIST#
D $9BA9 Finds the record whose id matches #REGa.
R $9BA9 IX Pointer to 3 byte table data
R $9BA9 O:IX Pointer moved to the next record
R $9BA9 O:IY Contents of the record
  $9BA9,$02 Save #REGbc and keep the id being sought in #REGb.
  $9BAB,$03 Call #R$9B93 to step to the next record.
  $9BAE,$03 Return the record id.

c $9BB1 Locate Location
@ $9BB1 label=LocateLocation
E $9BB1 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D224 }
. LIST#
D $9BB1 Finds a location record from its id.
R $9BB1 A Location ID
R $9BB1 O:IX Pointer to #R$BA8A(location data)
  $9BB1,$04 If the location ID is less than #N$50 jump to #R$9BB7 to process it.
  $9BB5,$01 Reset #REGa to #N$00.
  $9BB6,$01 Return.
N $9BB7 Work out the location now #REGa is validated as a legitimate location ID.
@ $9BB7 label=LocateLocation_Action
  $9BB7,$04 Point at the location table.
  $9BBB,$04 Store offset in the #REGhl register pair.
N $9BBF Calculate Location address, some examples would be;
. #TABLE(default,centre,centre,centre,centre)
. { =h Location ID | =h Sum | =h Location Table Entry | =h Location Data }
. { #LET(id=$05) #N({id},$02,$03,$01,$01)($) | (#N({id},$02,$03,$01,$01)($)*#N$02) + #N($B9E0,$02,$03,$01,$01)($) | #R(#EVAL($B9E0+{id}*$02)) | #R(#EVAL(#PEEK(#EVAL($B9E0+{id}*$02)) + #EVAL(#PEEK(#EVAL($B9E1+{id}*$02))*$100))) }
. { #LET(id=$20) #N({id},$02,$03,$01,$01)($) | (#N({id},$02,$03,$01,$01)($)*#N$02) + #N($B9E0,$02,$03,$01,$01)($) | #R(#EVAL($B9E0+{id}*$02)) | #R(#EVAL(#PEEK(#EVAL($B9E0+{id}*$02)) + #EVAL(#PEEK(#EVAL($B9E1+{id}*$02))*$100))) }
. { #LET(id=$33) #N({id},$02,$03,$01,$01)($) | (#N({id},$02,$03,$01,$01)($)*#N$02) + #N($B9E0,$02,$03,$01,$01)($) | #R(#EVAL($B9E0+{id}*$02)) | #R(#EVAL(#PEEK(#EVAL($B9E0+{id}*$02)) + #EVAL(#PEEK(#EVAL($B9E1+{id}*$02))*$100))) }
. TABLE#
  $9BBF,$02 Multiply the offset by #N$02 and add the table base.
  $9BC1,$01 Read the low byte of the token.
  $9BC2,$01 Step to the next letter.
  $9BC3,$01 Read the high byte of the token.
  $9BC4,$05 Copy #REGde into #REGix, restore #REGhl and #REGde.
  $9BC9,$01 Return.

c $9BCA Locate Object
@ $9BCA label=LocateObject
E $9BCA View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D237 }
. LIST#
D $9BCA Finds an object record from its id.
N $9BCA Locates an object from a given object ID.
R $9BCA A Object ID
R $9BCA O:IX The object data address from #R$C11B
  $9BCA,$04 Use the object table as this object.
  $9BCE,$03 Call #R$9DBD to locate the object.
  $9BD1,$04 Read the object's address from the index entry.
  $9BD5,$03 Read the high byte of the object's address.
  $9BD8,$01 Swap #REGhl with the address on the stack.
  $9BD9,$02 Restore #REGix from the stack.
  $9BDB,$01 Return.

c $9BDC Object: Move Contents
@ $9BDC label=Object_MoveContents
D $9BDC Moves everything inside the object in #REGa (recursively) to the location in #REGb; if the player is among them, the player moves too. The byte here is set while the player has not been found among those contents.
B $9BDC,$01
  $9BDD,$03 Point #REGhl at the flag that says the player was not found.
  $9BE0,$02 Set the flag.
  $9BE2,$03 Call #R$9C17 to move the contents.
  $9BE5,$03 Return if the player was not among the contents (the flag at #R$9BDC is still set).
  $9BE8,$01 Save #REGhl.
N $9BE9 Test this flag.
  $9BE9,$06 Jump to #R$9C13 if *#R$C12B is zero.
  $9BEF,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $9BF2,$04 Read *#R$B70C.
  $9BF6,$07 Point *#R$B70C at "#TEXTTOKEN($C11B+$08,$01)".
  $9BFD,$04 Clear *#R$B6EA.
  $9C01,$04 Store #REGb at #R$8D9B.
  $9C05,$03 Call #R$8E12 to move the character, unless the message is held back.
  $9C08,$03 Call #R$9B02, the empty action.
  $9C0B,$04 Update *#R$B70C.
  $9C0F,$04 Update *#R$B6EA.
@ $9C13 label=Object_MoveContents_ClearAndReturn
  $9C13,$03 Restore #REGhl from the stack and clear the byte it points at.
  $9C16,$01 Return.

c $9C17 Object: Move Nested Contents
@ $9C17 label=Object_MoveContents_Recurse
E $9C17 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D249 }
. LIST#
D $9C17 Moves every object held by the id in #REGa to the location in #REGb, then does the same for what those objects hold. Meeting the player (id #N$00) clears the flag at #R$9BDC.
  $9C17,$08 Start the object search one slot before #R$C063.
@ $9C1F label=Object_MoveContents_Recurse_Loop
  $9C1F,$03 Call #R$9BA9 to find the record.
  $9C22,$02 Jump to #R$9C3C if #R$9BA9 reached the end of the index.
  $9C24,$05 Jump back to #R$9C1F if where this object is is not the id in #REGa.
  $9C29,$03 Set this object's location to the location in #REGb.
@ $9C2C label=Object_MoveContents_Recurse_Player
  $9C2C,$04 Save the id being sought and read the id from the index entry.
  $9C30,$03 Jump to #R$9C36 if this object's id is not zero.
  $9C33,$03 Clear the flag at #R$9BDC: the player was among the contents.
@ $9C36 label=Object_MoveContents_Recurse_MoveTheContents
  $9C36,$03 Call #R$9C17 to move the contents.
  $9C39,$03 Jump back to #R$9C1F to find the record.
@ $9C3C label=Object_MoveContents_Recurse_Done
  $9C3C,$04 Restore #REGix and #REGiy from the stack.
  $9C40,$01 Return.

c $9C41 Table: Find An Exit
@ $9C41 label=TableFindAnExit
D $9C41 Finds an exit out of the current location.
  $9C41,$06 Save #REGix, #REGiy and #REGbc, and keep the location id in #REGb.
  $9C47,$03 Call #R$9BB1 to locate location.
  $9C4A,$03 Read the location's volume.
  $9C4D,$01 Copy the result into #REGc.
  $9C4E,$04 Point #REGix at the slot before the object table.
@ $9C52 label=TableFindAnExit_Loop
  $9C52,$03 Call #R$9B93 to step to the next index entry.
  $9C55,$02 Jump to #R$9C6D at the end of the object index.
  $9C57,$07 Jump back to #R$9C52 unless this object has one copy.
N $9C5E This object has one copy. Compare its location with the one being sought.
  $9C5E,$06 Jump back to #R$9C52 if the object's location is not the location being sought.
  $9C64,$06 Jump to #R$9C74 if the object's volume is above the location's volume.
  $9C6A,$01 Copy the result into #REGc.
  $9C6B,$02 Jump back to #R$9C52 for the next index entry.
@ $9C6D label=TableFindAnExit_ReturnTheCount
  $9C6D,$07 Return what #REGc was holding.
@ $9C74 label=TableFindAnExit_ReturnAZeroCount
  $9C74,$02 Take a count of zero.
  $9C76,$02 Jump back to #R$9C6D to return the count in #REGc.

c $9C78 Condition: Is The Object Carried
@ $9C78 label=ConditionIsTheObjectCarried
E $9C78 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D26C(Sherlock #N$D26C) }
. { #SHERLOCK$D278(Sherlock #N$D278) }
. LIST#
D $9C78 Tests whether the object is being carried.
  $9C78,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $9C7B,$03 Point #REGhl at the character whose turn it is.
  $9C7E,$02 Compare the object id with #N$FF.
  $9C80,$01 If the object matches, return with carry set: it is carried.
  $9C81,$01 Return if no object was named.
  $9C82,$05 Call #R$9C8A to see whether the object's location is this character.
  $9C87,$02 Restore #REGix from the stack.
  $9C89,$01 Return.

c $9C8A Condition: Test The Object Is Held
@ $9C8A label=Condition_TestTheObjectIsHeld
E $9C8A View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D288 }
. LIST#
D $9C8A Returns with carry set when the character whose turn it is holds the object, either directly or inside
. something the character holds. Otherwise returns with carry clear.
N $9C8A Find the object's record.
  $9C8A,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $9C8D,$04 Read where this object is from the object record.
  $9C91,$04 Jump to #R$9C9C if this object is not inside another object.
  $9C95,$05 Jump back to #R$9C8A if where this object is is not the character whose turn it is.
  $9C9A,$01 Return with carry set: this character is carrying it.
  $9C9B,$01 Return.
@ $9C9C label=Condition_TestTheObjectIsHeld_NotInside
  $9C9C,$02 See whether where this object is is zero.
  $9C9E,$01 Return.

c $9C9F Random Number
@ $9C9F label=GetRandomNumber
E $9C9F View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D2A4 }
. LIST#
D $9C9F Returns a random number from the seed taken at the start of the
. game.
R $9C9F A Random seed?
R $9C9F O:A Random number
  $9C9F,$03 Call #R$9CA8 to scale the roll.
  $9CA2,$03 Return if bit 7 of the scaled roll is clear.
  $9CA5,$02 Negate the accumulator.
  $9CA7,$01 Return.

c $9CA8 Random: Scale The Roll
@ $9CA8 label=CalculateRandom
E $9CA8 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D2AD }
. LIST#
D $9CA8 Scales the random roll by the limit passed in #REGb, advances the random seed at
. #R$B712, and returns the scaled roll in #REGa.
  $9CA8,$04 Keep the limit passed in.
  $9CAC,$02 #REGa*=#N$02.
  $9CAE,$02 Jump to #R$9CB2 if there's no carry over.
  $9CB0,$02 Else, set #REGa=#N$FF.
@ $9CB2 label=CalculateRandom_KeepTheDoubledLimit
  $9CB2,$01 Keep the doubled limit in #REGb.
@ $9CB3 label=CalculateRandom_Loop
  $9CB3,$07 Increase the LSB of the #R$B712 by one.
  $9CBA,$02 Jump to #R$9CBF if the low byte of the random seed did not roll over, skipping the high byte.
  $9CBC,$03 Increase the MSB of the #R$B712 by one.
@ $9CBF label=CalculateRandom_UseTheRandomCounterAsTheRecord
  $9CBF,$04 Use the random counter as the record being walked.
  $9CC3,$03 Read the random seed.
  $9CC6,$03 Add the carry onto the random seed.
  $9CC9,$02 Step on through the random counter.
  $9CCB,$03 Fold byte 1 of this record into it.
  $9CCE,$05 Compare #R$B70E against #REGa.
  $9CD3,$03 If the result of the comparison was zero, jump back to #R$9CB3 to try again.
  $9CD6,$03 Write #REGa to #R$B70E.
@ $9CD9 label=CalculateRandom_HalveTheSeed
  $9CD9,$03 Jump to #R$9CE3 if the random seed is below the doubled limit in #REGb.
  $9CDC,$02 Jump to #R$9CE3 if the random seed equals it.
  $9CDE,$02 Halve the random seed.
  $9CE0,$03 Jump back to #R$9CD9 to repeat this search.
@ $9CE3 label=CalculateRandom_TakeTheResultOffTheRandomSeed
  $9CE3,$01 Take the result off the random seed.
  $9CE4,$03 Restore #REGbc and #REGix from the stack.
  $9CE7,$01 Return.

c $9CE8 Condition: Total The Volume Inside
@ $9CE8 label=Condition_TotalTheVolumeInside
E $9CE8 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D2ED }
. LIST#
D $9CE8 Returns in #REGa the total volume of the objects inside object #REGa, or #N$FF on overflow.
  $9CE8,$03 Set #REGb to add volumes.
  $9CEB,$02 Jump to #R$9CF0 to add them up.

c $9CED Condition: Total The Mass Inside
@ $9CED label=Condition_TotalTheMassInside
E $9CED View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D2F2 }
. LIST#
D $9CED Returns in #REGa the total mass of the objects inside object #REGa, including what they hold, or #N$FF on overflow.
  $9CED,$03 Set #REGb to zero to add masses.
@ $9CF0 label=Condition_TotalTheMassInside_StartTheTotal
  $9CF0,$06 Start the total at zero.
  $9CF6,$03 Call #R$9D00 to add up the objects inside.
  $9CF9,$01 Copy the total into #REGa.
  $9CFA,$05 Restore #REGiy, #REGix and #REGbc from the stack.
  $9CFF,$01 Return.

c $9D00 Condition: Add Up The Objects Inside
@ $9D00 label=Condition_AddUpTheObjectsInside
E $9D00 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D305 }
. LIST#
D $9D00 Adds the volume or the mass of each object inside the id in #REGa. Volume covers
. those objects only; mass also adds what they hold. An overflow returns #N$FF.
  $9D00,$06 Point #REGix at the object table.
@ $9D06 label=Condition_AddUpTheObjectsInside_Loop
  $9D06,$03 Call #R$9BA9 to find the record.
  $9D09,$02 Jump to #R$9D2F at the end of the object index.
  $9D0B,$05 Jump back to #R$9D06 if where this object is is not the id in #REGa.
  $9D10,$06 Jump to #R$9D1F if #REGb is zero (add masses instead of volumes).
  $9D16,$03 Add the object's volume onto the total.
  $9D19,$03 Jump to #R$9D32 if the total overflowed.
  $9D1C,$01 Copy the result into #REGc.
  $9D1D,$02 Jump to #R$9D2C to find the next record.
@ $9D1F label=Condition_AddUpTheObjectsInside_AddTheMass
  $9D1F,$03 Add the object's mass onto the total.
  $9D22,$03 Jump to #R$9D32 if the total overflowed.
  $9D25,$01 Copy the result into #REGc.
  $9D26,$03 Read the id from the index entry.
  $9D29,$03 Call #R$9D00 to add the mass of what this object holds.
@ $9D2C label=Condition_AddUpTheObjectsInside_FindTheRecord
  $9D2C,$03 Jump back to #R$9D06 to find the record.
@ $9D2F label=Condition_AddUpTheObjectsInside_RestoreAndReturn
  $9D2F,$02 Restore #REGix from the stack.
  $9D31,$01 Return.
@ $9D32 label=Condition_AddUpTheObjectsInside_MarkTheTotalAsFull
  $9D32,$03 Pop the saved id and mark the total as full (#N$FF).
  $9D35,$02 Jump back to #R$9D2F to return.

c $9D37 Get Object Location In IX
@ $9D37 label=GetObjectLocationInIX
E $9D37 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D33C }
. LIST#
D $9D37 Points #REGix at the location of the object.
R $9D37 O:IX The object location
  $9D37,$08 Fetch the location ID for the object held by #R$B70C.
  $9D3F,$03 Call #R$9BB1 to locate location.
  $9D42,$01 Restore #REGaf from the stack.
  $9D43,$01 Return.

c $9D44 Condition: Suppress The Message
@ $9D44 label=ConditionSuppressTheMessage
D $9D44 Suppresses a message when this character should stay quiet.
  $9D44,$06 If #R$B6FA is #N$01 then return.
  $9D4A,$01 A message is being held back, so the flag becomes non-zero.
  $9D4B,$03 Store that in the message-suppressed flag and return past the caller.
  $9D4E,$01 Restore #REGbc from the stack.
  $9D4F,$01 Return.

c $9D50 Condition: Where The Object Is
@ $9D50 label=Condition_WhereTheObjectIs
D $9D50 Works out where the object is. A fluid that is no longer contained evaporates, and
. "evaporate(s|d|ing|es)" is printed.
  $9D50,$03 Read the current object.
  $9D53,$08 Call #R$9BCA to locate the object.
  $9D5B,$03 Read where this object is.
  $9D5E,$04 Point #REGix at the slot before the object table.
@ $9D62 label=Condition_WhereTheObjectIs_Loop
  $9D62,$03 Call #R$9BA9 to find the record.
  $9D65,$02 Jump to #R$9D91 if #R$9BA9 reached the end of the index.
  $9D67,$05 Jump back to #R$9D62 if where this object is is not the id in #REGa.
  $9D6C,$06 Jump to #R$9D8B if the object is not a fluid.
  $9D72,$05 Clear the object's location.
  $9D77,$04 Mark where this object is as unset.
  $9D7B,$04 Mark the object not visible.
  $9D7F,$03 Call #R$9EC7 to print the object's name.
  $9D82,$03 Point at the message "evaporate(s|d|ing|es)[0x15]" (#R$B142).
  $9D85,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $9D88,$03 Jump back to #R$9D62 to find the record.
@ $9D8B label=Condition_WhereTheObjectIs_UpdateWhereThisObject
  $9D8B,$03 Update where this object is.
  $9D8E,$03 Jump back to #R$9D62 to find the record.
@ $9D91 label=Condition_WhereTheObjectIs_RestoreAndReturn
  $9D91,$05 Restore #REGhl, #REGix and #REGiy from the stack.
  $9D96,$01 Return.

c $9D97 Condition: Count The Visible Objects
@ $9D97 label=ObjectCount
E $9D97 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D4CC }
. LIST#
D $9D97 Counts the visible objects at the location or container in #REGa. A character
. id counts what that character holds. An object is visible when bit 7 of its
. attribute byte is set.
R $9D97 I:A Location or container id (the character id counts objects held)
R $9D97 O:A The number of objects
  $9D97,$07 Initialise the object count.
  $9DB7,$05 Restore #REGbc, #REGiy and #REGix from the stack.
  $9DBC,$01 Return.
  $9D9E,$04 Use the slot before the object table as this object.
@ $9DA2 label=ObjectCount_Loop
  $9DA2,$03 Call #R$9BA9 to fetch the next record in #REGiy.
  $9DA5,$02 Jump to #R$9DB6 at the end of the object index.
  $9DA7,$05 Jump back to #R$9DA2 without counting this object if its location is not the id in #REGa.
  $9DAC,$06 Jump back to #R$9DA2 without counting this object if it is not visible.
  $9DB2,$01 Count one more object.
  $9DB3,$03 Jump back to #R$9DA2 to find the record.
@ $9DB6 label=ObjectCount_End
  $9DB6,$01 Store the object count in #REGa.

c $9DBD Index ID Table
@ $9DBD label=IndexIdTable
E $9DBD View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D34E }
. LIST#
D $9DBD Looks an id up in a table of id-and-address pairs.
N $9DBD Searches through what is essentially key value data to find a key match.
R $9DBD A Key ID
R $9DBD IX Address of table data
R $9DBD O:IX The address in the table which matches the key ID
  $9DBD,$01 Exchange registers.
  $9DBE,$04 Point #REGhl at the table in #REGix and keep the sought id in #REGb.
  $9DC2,$02 Each index entry is #N$03 bytes.
  $9DC4,$02 The index stride's high byte is zero.
N $9DC6 Begin searching the table.
@ $9DC6 label=IndexIdTable_Loop
  $9DC6,$01 Grab the next ID.
  $9DC7,$03 Jump to #R$9DD2 if this entry's id is the one sought.
  $9DCA,$04 Jump to #R$9DD2 at the end of the index.
  $9DCE,$01 Step #N$03 bytes to the next id.
  $9DCF,$03 Jump back to #R$9DC6 to begin searching the table.
N $9DD2 Handle the output.
@ $9DD2 label=IndexIdTable_Found
  $9DD2,$05 Set flags (checking if the key ID was not found).
  $9DD7,$01 Exchange registers.
  $9DD8,$01 Return.

c $9DD9 Condition: Same Place As The Player
@ $9DD9 label=ConditionSamePlaceAsThePlayer
E $9DD9 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D36A }
. LIST#
D $9DD9 Tests whether an object is in the same place as the player.
  $9DD9,$08 Read *#R$B70C.
  $9DE1,$03 Read the location from #REGiy.
  $9DE4,$03 Read *#R$B710.
  $9DE7,$01 Keep the kind filter (#N$00 not a live animal, #N$01 a live animal, #N$02 anything).
@ $9DE8 label=ConditionSamePlaceAsThePlayer_Loop
  $9DE8,$03 Call #R$9B93 to step to the next index entry.
  $9DEB,$02 Jump to #R$9E1D at the end of the index.
  $9DED,$05 Jump to #R$9E01 if the kind filter is #N$02 (any object).
  $9DF2,$03 Read the attribute flags from #REGiy.
  $9DF5,$02 Keep bits 3, 6.
  $9DF7,$06 Take zero unless the object is an animal that is not broken.
  $9DFD,$01 Count on from zero.
@ $9DFE label=ConditionSamePlaceAsThePlayer_TestTheKind
  $9DFE,$03 Jump back to #R$9DE8 unless this object's kind (#N$01 for a live animal, else #N$00) matches the filter.
@ $9E01 label=ConditionSamePlaceAsThePlayer_PointAtTheName
  $9E01,$03 Start at the object's name, #N$08 bytes into the record.
  $9E04,$04 Add #N($0008,$04,$04) onto the current object record.
  $9E08,$03 Call #R$71F3 to match clause pairs.
  $9E0B,$04 Jump back to #R$9DE8 if #R$71F3 did not match the name.
N $9E0F Test this flag.
  $9E0F,$06 Jump to #R$9E1D if *#R$B70F is not zero.
  $9E15,$03 Read the id from the index entry.
  $9E18,$03 Call #R$9E34 to test whether the object is here.
  $9E1B,$02 Jump back to #R$9DE8 if #R$9E34 did not find the object here.
@ $9E1D label=ConditionSamePlaceAsThePlayer_ReturnTheId
  $9E1D,$03 Read the id from the index entry.
  $9E20,$04 Restore #REGiy, #REGde and #REGbc from the stack.
  $9E24,$01 Return.

c $9E25 Condition: Swap And Retest
@ $9E25 label=Condition_SwapAndRetest
D $9E25 Swaps the two object pointers and tests again whether the object is visible.
  $9E25,$03 Call #R$9E2B to swap the two object pointers.
  $9E28,$03 Call #R$9E40 to test a visible object.
  $9E2B,$08 Swap #REGix with #REGiy.
  $9E33,$01 Return.

c $9E34 Condition: The Object Is Here
@ $9E34 label=ConditionTheObjectIsHere
D $9E34 Tests whether the object is in the current location.
  $9E34,$06 Save #REGix and use the record of the character whose turn it is.
  $9E3A,$03 Call #R$9E40 to test a visible object.
  $9E3D,$02 Restore #REGix from the stack.
  $9E3F,$01 Return.

c $9E40 Condition: A Visible Object
@ $9E40 label=Condition_AVisibleObject
E $9E40 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$C821 }
. LIST#
D $9E40 Returns whether the object is visible and in the location just tested.
  $9E40,$05 Return if the object is not "visible".
  $9E45,$06 Save #REGiy, #REGix and #REGbc, and keep #REGa in #REGb.
  $9E4B,$03 Read the object's location.
  $9E4E,$05 Call #R$9E7A to test whether the container is lit.
  $9E53,$01 Compare it with the result.
  $9E54,$01 Keep the result in #REGb.
  $9E55,$04 Jump to #R$9E72 if #R$9E7A's result matches the flag kept in #REGb.
  $9E59,$03 Call #R$9E7A to test whether the container is lit.
  $9E5C,$01 Compare it with the result.
  $9E5D,$02 Jump to #R$9E6F unless it matches #REGb.
  $9E5F,$03 Jump to #R$9E72 unless the compared byte was #N$FF.
  $9E62,$03 Load the copy count into #REGb.
  $9E65,$01 Use the object's location again.
@ $9E66 label=Condition_AVisibleObject_Loop
  $9E66,$05 Jump to #R$9E72 if this copy's location matches.
  $9E6B,$02 Step to the next byte of this object.
  $9E6D,$02 Loop back to #R$9E66 until every copy's location has been checked.
@ $9E6F label=Condition_AVisibleObject_ReturnNotVisible
  $9E6F,$01 Set the zero flag.
  $9E70,$02 Jump to #R$9E74 to return with the zero flag set.
@ $9E72 label=Condition_AVisibleObject_ReturnVisible
  $9E72,$02 Clear the zero flag.
@ $9E74 label=Condition_AVisibleObject_Return
  $9E74,$05 Restore #REGbc, #REGix and #REGiy from the stack.
  $9E79,$01 Return.

c $9E7A Condition: Is The Container Lit?
@ $9E7A label=ContainerLit
E $9E7A View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$C86E }
. LIST#
D $9E7A Tests whether the container the object is inside is lit.
R $9E7A IX Object Data Address e.g. #R$C11B
R $9E7A O:A Either #N$FF if this object is not inside a container, or the object ID of the container object
R $9E7A O:IX Object Data Address of either the input value, or the address of the container object
  $9E7A,$09 If the object is not inside of anything then jump to #R$9E92.
@ $9E7C label=ContainerLit_Fetch
  $9E83,$01 Switch to the shadow register for #REGaf.
  $9E84,$03 Read where this object is.
  $9E87,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $9E8A,$03 Fetch the objects attributes.
  $9E8D,$04,b$02,$02 Jump back to #R$9E7C if the container is open or broken.
  $9E91,$01 Switch back to the "normal" #REGaf register.
@ $9E92 label=ContainerLit_Return
  $9E92,$02 Restore #REGix from the stack.
  $9E94,$01 Return.

c $9E95 Exits: Point At The Exit List
@ $9E95 label=ExitsPointAtTheExitList
D $9E95 Points #REGix at the exit list (record + #N$07) of the location holding the object at #R$B70C.
  $9E95,$04 Call #R$9D37 to point #REGix at the location of the object at #R$B70C.
  $9E99,$05 Add #N($0007,$04,$04) to the object location.
  $9E9E,$01 Restore #REGde from the stack.
  $9E9F,$01 Return.

c $9EA0 Exits: Find The Exit To A Named Place
@ $9EA0 label=ExitsFindTheExitToANamedPlace
E $9EA0 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D39B }
. LIST#
D $9EA0 Walks the current location's exits, finds each destination and matches its name against the clause.
  $9EA0,$06 Set #REGde to #N($0002,$04,$04), the offset of a location's name.
@ $9EA6 label=ExitsFindTheExitToANamedPlace_Loop
  $9EA6,$03 Call #R$9B93 to step to the next exit.
  $9EA9,$02 Jump to #R$9EC3 at the end of the exit list.
  $9EAB,$03 Read this exit's destination.
  $9EAE,$05 Call #R$9BB1 to find this location's record.
N $9EB0 Find the location's record.
  $9EB3,$08 Point #REGiy at this location's name.
  $9EBB,$03 Call #R$71F3 to match clause pairs.
  $9EBE,$02 Jump back to #R$9EA6 to try the next exit unless the name matched.
  $9EC0,$03 Read the destination's location id to return it.
@ $9EC3 label=ExitsFindTheExitToANamedPlace_RestoreAndReturn
  $9EC3,$03 Restore #REGde and #REGiy from the stack.
  $9EC6,$01 Return.

c $9EC7 Message: Name An Object
@ $9EC7 label=MessageNameAnObject
E $9EC7 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D3D5 }
. LIST#
D $9EC7 Prints the name of an object.
  $9EC7,$08 Step the pointer on by #N$08 bytes.
  $9ECF,$03 Call #R$9ED6 to choose which noun byte to print.
  $9ED2,$03 Restore #REGde and #REGiy from the stack.
  $9ED5,$01 Return.

c $9ED6 Message: Choose The Noun Byte
@ $9ED6 label=Message_ChooseTheNounByte
E $9ED6 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D3E8(Sherlock #N$D3E8) }
. { #SHERLOCK$D405(Sherlock #N$D405) }
. LIST#
D $9ED6 Chooses which noun byte to print. A set #R$B703 prints the class token; otherwise the
. object's name tokens are printed.
  $9ED6,$09 If #R$B703 is not zero then jump to #R$9EFA.
  $9EDF,$03 Read the high byte of the first name word.
  $9EE2,$03 Read the low byte of the first name word.
  $9EE5,$03 Call #R$743F to turn the class token into a word.
  $9EE8,$03 Read the low byte of the second name word.
  $9EEB,$03 Read the high byte of the second name word.
  $9EEE,$03 Call #R$74C1 to print a dictionary token.
  $9EF1,$03 Read the low byte of the third name word.
  $9EF4,$03 Read the high byte of the third name word.
  $9EF7,$03 Call #R$74C1 to print a dictionary token.
@ $9EFA label=Message_ChooseTheNounByte_PrintTheFirstWord
  $9EFA,$03 Read the low byte of the first name word.
  $9EFD,$03 Read the high byte of the first name word.
  $9F00,$05 If #REGde is not zero call #R$7478.
  $9F05,$02 Restore #REGde and #REGaf from the stack.
  $9F07,$01 Return.

c $9F08 Table: Find An Exit In A Direction
@ $9F08 label=TableFindAnExitInADirection
E $9F08 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D448 }
. LIST#
D $9F08 Finds the exit that leaves in a given direction.
  $9F08,$04 Keep the direction.
  $9F0C,$03 Call #R$9E95 to point #REGix at the exit list.
@ $9F0F label=TableFindAnExitInADirection_Loop
  $9F0F,$03 Call #R$9B93 to step to the next exit.
  $9F12,$02 Jump to #R$9F21 at the end of the exit list.
  $9F14,$03 Read this exit's destination.
  $9F17,$03 Jump back to #R$9F0F if byte 2 of this record is zero.
  $9F1A,$03 Read this exit's direction.
  $9F1D,$04 Jump back to #R$9F0F unless this exit's direction is the one in #REGb.
@ $9F21 label=TableFindAnExitInADirection_RestoreAndReturn
  $9F21,$03 Restore #REGiy and #REGbc from the stack.
  $9F24,$01 Return.

c $9F25 Exits: Find The Exit Through An Object
@ $9F25 label=ExitsFindTheExitThroughAnObject
E $9F25 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D46C }
. LIST#
D $9F25 Searches the exit list for an exit whose via byte (offset #N$01) matches the object id in #REGa (#R$9F28 entry point). Returns with #REGix at the exit, or with #REGa=#N$FF and the zero flag set at the end of the list.
  $9F25,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $9F28,$03 Search on the via byte (offset #N$01).
  $9F2B,$02 Jump to #R$9F30 to search on that offset.

c $9F2D Exits: Find The Exit To A Location
@ $9F2D label=ExitsFindTheExitToALocation
E $9F2D View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D471 }
. LIST#
D $9F2D As #R$9F25, but matches the destination byte (offset #N$02).
  $9F2D,$03 Search on the destination byte (offset #N$02).
@ $9F30 label=ExitsFindTheExitToALocation_PatchTheOffset
  $9F30,$03 Patch the offset of the LD A,(IX+n) at #R$9F40.
  $9F33,$05 Restore #REGa from the stack, save #REGbc and #REGiy, and keep the id being sought in #REGb.
  $9F38,$03 Call #R$9E95 to point #REGix at the exit list.
@ $9F3B label=ExitsFindTheExitToALocation_Loop
  $9F3B,$03 Call #R$9B93 to step to the next exit.
  $9F3E,$02 Jump to #R$9F46 at the end of the exit list.
  $9F40,$03 Read the patched byte of this exit.
  $9F43,$03 Jump back to #R$9F3B unless it matches the id in #REGb.
@ $9F46 label=ExitsFindTheExitToALocation_RestoreAndReturn
  $9F46,$03 Restore #REGiy and #REGbc from the stack.
  $9F49,$01 Return.

c $9F4A Action: Hand An Object Over
@ $9F4A label=ActionHandAnObjectOver
D $9F4A Moves an object from one character to another.
  $9F4A,$04 Read the current object's record pointer.
  $9F4E,$04 Read the second object's record pointer.
  $9F52,$04 Store the second object's record as the current one.
  $9F56,$04 Store the saved record as the second object's.
  $9F5A,$04 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $9F5E,$01 Take the second object.
  $9F5F,$03 Update *#R$B6E8.
  $9F62,$01 Take the current object.
  $9F63,$03 Update *#R$B6E9.
  $9F66,$03 Call #R$9B6C to call the handler stored for this action.
  $9F69,$04 Update *#R$B6E8.
  $9F6D,$04 Update *#R$B708.
  $9F71,$04 Update *#R$B70A.
  $9F75,$01 Return.

c $9F76 Response: That Will Not Work
@ $9F76 label=ResponseThatWillNotWork
D $9F76 Prints that the command will not work.
  $9F76,$03 Read the message gate.
  $9F79,$01 Step the message gate down by one.
  $9F7A,$03 Jump to #R$712B if #REGa is now zero.
  $9F7D,$04 Clear the message-suppressed flag.
  $9F81,$01 Return.

c $9F82 Get Object First Location
@ $9F82 label=ObjectFirstLocation
E $9F82 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D495 }
. LIST#
D $9F82 Reads the first location an object appears in.
R $9F82 A Object ID
R $9F82 O:A #N$FF if the object does not appear exactly once, otherwise the location where it first appears.
  $9F82,$03 Return if this is the terminator character (#N$FF).
  $9F85,$03 Call #R$9BCA to set #REGix to the object data address.
  $9F88,$05 Does the object appear in the game once?
  $9F8D,$02 The not-found id is #N$FF.
  $9F8F,$01 Return if not.
  $9F90,$03 Read the object's location.
  $9F93,$01 Return.

c $9F94 Turn: Describe What Is Here
@ $9F94 label=TurnDescribeWhatIsHere
E $9F94 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D4A7 }
. LIST#
D $9F94 Describes the place and what is in it.
  $9F94,$07 Point at the message "You see :[0x14]".
  $9F9B,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $9F9E,$02 Use #N$FF as the end marker while describing what is here.
  $9FA0,$04 Read the record of the character whose turn it is.
  $9FA4,$03 Read this character's location.
  $9FA7,$03 Call #R$9FAF to list the objects here.
  $9FAA,$04 Restore #REGbc, #REGaf and #REGiy from the stack.
  $9FAE,$01 Return.

c $9FAF Message: List With A Separator
@ $9FAF label=Message_ListWithASeparator
D $9FAF Lists the objects with a separator. When the count is zero the player sees "nothing".
  $9FAF,$06 Start the separator count at zero.
  $9FB5,$02 Four bytes of the separator list are considered.
  $9FB7,$03 Call #R$9FC7 to walk the objects here with the saved separator.
  $9FBA,$01 See whether the separator count in #REGc is zero.
  $9FBB,$01 Compare the separator count with zero.
  $9FBC,$03 Point at the message " {6} nothing" (#R$B33B).
  $9FBF,$03 Print " nothing" if no objects were listed.
  $9FC2,$04 Restore #REGbc, #REGde and #REGiy from the stack.
  $9FC6,$01 Return.

c $9FC7 Message: Remember The Separator
@ $9FC7 label=Message_RememberTheSeparator
D $9FC7 Remembers the separator at #R$869F and walks the objects here, skipping any with no
. copies left.
  $9FC7,$02 Save #REGhl and keep the id in #REGl.
  $9FC9,$03 Read the space count.
  $9FCC,$01 Keep the space count.
  $9FCD,$04 Store #REGd in the space count.
  $9FD1,$01 Take the id back into #REGa.
  $9FD2,$01 Swap the id and the saved separator with #REGhl on the stack.
  $9FD3,$06 Point #REGix at the slot before the object table.
@ $9FD9 label=Message_RememberTheSeparator_Loop
  $9FD9,$03 Call #R$9BA9 to find the record.
  $9FDC,$02 Jump to #R$A046 when the object search returns with the zero flag set.
  $9FDE,$05 Jump back to #R$9FD9 unless this object is inside the one in #REGa.
  $9FE3,$02 Save #REGa, then increment it (the zero flag is set if it was #N$FF).
  $9FE5,$03 Read the copy count from the object record.
  $9FE8,$02 Jump to #R$9FEF unless #REGa was #N$FF.
  $9FEA,$05 Jump to #R$A03D unless this object has one copy.
@ $9FEF label=Message_RememberTheSeparator_TestTheLocation
  $9FEF,$03 Read the location from #REGiy.
  $9FF2,$03 Jump to #R$9FFE if the object is at the location in #REGb.
  $9FF5,$03 Jump to #R$A03D if the copy count has run out.
  $9FF8,$03 Read byte +#N$11 from #REGiy.
  $9FFB,$03 Jump to #R$A03D if byte #N$11 of this record is not the object's location.
@ $9FFE label=Message_RememberTheSeparator_TestWhoseTurn
  $9FFE,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $A001,$05 Jump to #R$A00B if the character whose turn it is is not this object's id.
  $A006,$05 Jump to #R$A03D if the separator in #REGd is #N$04.
@ $A00B label=Message_RememberTheSeparator_TestTheObjectHere
  $A00B,$03 Read the id from the index entry.
  $A00E,$03 Call #R$9E34 to test whether the object is here.
  $A011,$02 Jump to #R$A03D if #R$9E34 did not find the object here.
  $A013,$01 Count one more object.
  $A014,$01 Take zero for the two flags below.
  $A015,$03 Clear the capitalise-the-next-letter flag before the object's name.
  $A018,$03 Update *#R$B703.
  $A01B,$03 Call #R$9EC7 to print the object's name.
  $A01E,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $A021,$05 Jump to #R$A041 if the character whose turn it is is this object's id.
  $A026,$02 Use the character '.'.
  $A028,$03 Call #R$858B to print one character.
  $A02B,$03 Read the id from the index entry.
  $A02E,$03 Call #R$A050 to list what is here.
  $A031,$02 Jump to #R$A03D if #R$A050 returned with carry set.
  $A033,$03 Read the id from the index entry.
  $A036,$02 Save #REGde and step the separator on.
  $A038,$01 Step the separator on once more.
  $A039,$04 Call #R$9FC7 to remember the separator and walk the objects, then restore #REGde.
@ $A03D label=Message_RememberTheSeparator_FindTheRecord
  $A03D,$04 Jump back to #R$9FD9 to find the record.
@ $A041 label=Message_RememberTheSeparator_PrintANewline
  $A041,$03 Call #R$8583 to print a newline.
  $A044,$02 Jump back to #R$A03D to find the record.
@ $A046 label=Message_RememberTheSeparator_RestoreTheSeparator
  $A046,$03 Restore #REGix from the stack and swap #REGhl with the address on the stack.
  $A049,$04 Put the saved separator back at #R$869F.
  $A04D,$01 Take the id back into #REGa.
  $A04E,$01 Restore #REGhl from the stack.
  $A04F,$01 Return.

c $A050 Table: List What Is Here
@ $A050 label=TableListWhatIsHere
D $A050 Lists the objects in the current place.
  $A050,$05 Keep the object id.
N $A055 Find the object's record.
  $A055,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $A058,$03 Read the attribute flags from the object record.
  $A05B,$02 Keep bits 3, 5.
  $A05D,$02 Jump to #R$A097 when the object is neither broken nor open.
  $A05F,$01 Use the result again.
N $A060 Count the visible objects.
  $A060,$03 Call #R$9D97 to count the visible objects.
  $A063,$02 Compare it with zero.
  $A065,$02 Jump to #R$A097 if #R$9D97 counted nothing inside the current object.
  $A067,$06 Jump to #R$A074 if the object is not an animal.
  $A06D,$01 Use the result again.
  $A06E,$04 Point #REGhl at "[0x13] carrying[0x14]".
  $A072,$02 Jump to #R$A08E to print "carrying".
@ $A074 label=TableListWhatIsHere_UseTheToken
  $A074,$03 Use the token #TOKEN($039B).
  $A077,$01 Drop one from the hold count.
  $A078,$02 Jump to #R$A07D if the count was #N$01.
  $A07A,$03 Use the token #TOKEN($0065).
@ $A07D label=TableListWhatIsHere_ReadTheNameTokenFromTheObject
  $A07D,$04 Read the name token from the object record.
  $A081,$03 Read the name token's high byte from the object record.
  $A084,$03 Keep the low nibble of byte 9 of this object.
  $A087,$04 Call #R$A09D to list the exits for this location.
  $A091,$01 Clear carry and return through #R$A092.
@ $A092 label=TableListWhatIsHere_RestoreAndReturn
  $A092,$04 Restore #REGde, #REGbc and #REGix from the stack.
  $A096,$01 Return.
  $A08B,$03 Point at the message "[0x01] there[0x01][0x14]" (#R$AFE0).
@ $A08E label=TableListWhatIsHere_PrintCarryingOrThere
  $A08E,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
@ $A097 label=TableListWhatIsHere_FinishTheList
  $A097,$03 Call #R$8583 to print a newline.
  $A09A,$01 Set carry and return: the list of what is here is finished.
  $A09B,$02 Jump back to #R$A092.

c $A09D Message: Exits For This Location
@ $A09D label=Message_ExitsForThisLocation
D $A09D Prints the exit messages for this location from the list at #R$AFCA, indexed by the
. object's second flag.
  $A09D,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$AFCA.
  $A0A0,$03 Read the second flag from the object record.
  $A0A3,$05 Shift the object's second flag and keep the middle bits.
  $A0A8,$02 The message offset's high byte is zero.
  $A0AA,$01 Add the exit index to the message list.
  $A0AB,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD to print this exit's message.

c $A0AE Exits: Point At The List
@ $A0AE label=ExitsPointAtTheList
E $A0AE View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D5EF }
. LIST#
D $A0AE Finds the current location and points #REGix at its exit list, seven
. bytes into the record. #REGbc is #N($03), the length of one exit.
N $A0AE Find the location's record.
  $A0AE,$03 Call #R$9BB1 to locate location.
  $A0B1,$05 Step #REGix on by #N$07 bytes, to the exit list.
  $A0B6,$03 One exit is #N$03 bytes.
  $A0B9,$01 Return.

c $A0BA Exits: The Direction Word
@ $A0BA label=ExitsTheDirectionWord
E $A0BA View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D5FB }
. LIST#
D $A0BA Looks a direction id up at #R$A20E plus twice the id. The words run
. from #TOKEN($0480) to #TOKEN($01C4). Bit 7 of the id is ignored.
  $A0BA,$03 Point at the token-address table at #N$A20E.
  $A0BD,$03 Clear bit 7 of the token id.
  $A0C0,$02 The direction-word offset's high byte is zero.
  $A0C2,$01 Add the token id.
  $A0C3,$01 Add the token id again. Each entry in #N$A20E is one word.
  $A0C4,$01 Read the low byte of #N$A20E.
  $A0C5,$01 Step to the high byte of the direction token.
  $A0C6,$01 Read the high byte of the direction token at #R$A20E plus twice the direction id.
  $A0C7,$01 Return.

c $A0C8 Exits: To The North There Is
@ $A0C8 label=MessageListTheObjects
E $A0C8 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D60B }
. LIST#
D $A0C8 Walks the location's exit triples. For each exit guarded by a visible
. object it prints "to the <direction> there is". Direction #N$09 is printed
. as #TOKEN($07B5) and direction #N$0A as #TOKEN($082B).
N $A0C8 Point at this location's exits.
  $A0C8,$09 Point at this location's exits.
  $A0D1,$06 Step #REGix on by #REGbc to the next exit and point #REGiy at it.
@ $A0D7 label=MessageListTheObjects_Loop
  $A0D7,$06 Jump to #R$A113 if this exit goes via no object.
  $A0DD,$03 Read the object this exit goes via.
  $A0E0,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $A0E3,$06 Jump to #R$A113 if the object is not visible.
  $A0E9,$05 Step the pointer on by #N$08 bytes.
  $A0EE,$05 Save the name pointer and read this exit's direction.
  $A0F3,$03 Call #R$A0BA to describe the direction word.
  $A0F6,$04 Jump to #R$A104 if the direction is below #N$09.
  $A0FA,$05 Use the token #TOKEN($07B5) when the direction is #N$09.
  $A0FF,$03 Use the token #TOKEN($082B).
  $A102,$02 Jump to #R$A10A to print a dictionary token.
@ $A104 label=MessageListTheObjects_PrintTheLeadIn
  $A104,$03 Point at the message "to the[0x16]" (#R$AE17).
  $A107,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
@ $A10A label=MessageListTheObjects_PrintADictionaryToken
  $A10A,$03 Call #R$74C1 to print a dictionary token.
  $A10D,$03 Point at the message "there is[0x00][0x14]" (#R$B013).
  $A110,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
@ $A113 label=MessageListTheObjects_StepToTheNextExit
  $A113,$0A Step to the next exit and jump back to #R$A0D7 unless the direction is #N$FF.
  $A11D,$06 Restore #REGix, #REGiy, #REGde and #REGbc from the stack.
  $A123,$01 Return.

c $A124 Message: A Conjunction
@ $A124 label=MessageAConjunction
E $A124 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D671 }
. LIST#
D $A124 Prints "and" between listed objects.
  $A124,$08 Return if this direction is #N$FF.
  $A12C,$06 Jump back to #R$A124 if this exit goes via an object.
  $A132,$05 Jump back to #R$A124 if this direction is zero.
  $A137,$01 Return.

c $A138 Display Exits
@ $A138 label=DisplayExits
E $A138 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #SHERLOCK$D68C }
. LIST#
D $A138 Prints the exits out of the current place.
N $A138 Save the registers used while the exits are listed.
  $A138,$09 Save #REGix, #REGiy, #REGde and #REGbc, then call #R$A0AE to list the exits.
  $A141,$03 Call #R$A124 to print a conjunction.
  $A144,$02 Jump to #R$A15D if there are no exits.
  $A146,$03 Point at the message "visible exits are:[0x16]" (#R$B020).
  $A149,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
@ $A14C label=DisplayExits_Loop
  $A14C,$03 Read this exit's direction.
  $A14F,$03 Call #R$A0BA to describe the direction word.
  $A152,$03 Call #R$74C1 to print a dictionary token.
  $A155,$03 Call #R$A124 to print a conjunction.
  $A158,$02 Jump back to #R$A14C while #R$A124 reports another name follows.
  $A15A,$03 Call #R$8583 to print a newline.
@ $A15D label=DisplayExits_RestoreAndReturn
N $A15D Restore the registers saved for the exit list.
  $A15D,$06 Restore #REGbc, #REGde, #REGiy and #REGix from the stack.
  $A163,$01 Return.

c $A164 Message: Name From The Other Record
@ $A164 label=Message_NameFromTheOtherRecord
D $A164 Reads the name token from the other record and joins the name printer.
  $A164,$03 Read the name token from #REGiy.
  $A167,$03 Read the name token's high byte from #REGiy.
  $A16A,$02 Jump to #R$A172 to save the address and #R$B020.

c $A16C Message: Name From This Record
@ $A16C label=Message_NameFromThisRecord
D $A16C Reads the name token from this object's record and prints "is" with that name.
  $A16C,$03 Read the name token from the object record.
  $A16F,$03 Read the name token's high byte from the object record.
@ $A172 label=Message_NameFromThisRecord_ChooseTheNameTable
  $A172,$05 Point #REGhl at the name table used when bit 7 is clear.
  $A177,$04 Jump to #R$A17E if bit 7 of #REGa is clear.
  $A17B,$03 Point #REGhl at the name table used when bit 7 is set.
@ $A17E label=Message_NameFromThisRecord_DescribeTheDirection
  $A17E,$03 Call #R$A0BD to describe the direction word.
  $A181,$06 Point at the message "[0x04] is[0x01][0x15]".
  $A187,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $A18A,$01 Restore #REGde from the stack.
  $A18B,$01 Return.

c $A18C Action: Clear The Tie
@ $A18C label=Action_ClearTheTie
D $A18C Clears the tie on the object. An animal's name bytes are overwritten with #TOKEN($0192), and the
. object record is returned.
  $A18C,$05 Call #R$9BCA to locate the object.
N $A18E Find the object's record.
  $A191,$04 Clear byte #N$0C of this object.
  $A195,$04 Clear byte #N$0D of this object.
  $A199,$09 Keep #TOKEN($00CD) and jump to #R$A1A5 if the object is not an animal.
  $A1A2,$03 Use the token #TOKEN($0192).
@ $A1A5 label=Action_ClearTheTie_StoreTheTie
  $A1A5,$03 Update byte #N$0A of this object.
  $A1A8,$03 Update byte #N$0B of this object.
  $A1AB,$02 Restore #REGix from the stack.
  $A1AD,$01 Return.
  $A1AE,$08 Start the object search one slot before #R$C063.
@ $A1B6 label=Action_ClearTheTie_FindTheRecord
  $A1B6,$03 Call #R$9BA9 to find the record.
  $A1B9,$02 Jump to #R$A1C0 if the index has ended.
  $A1BB,$05 Jump back to #R$A1B6 if where this object is is not the id in #REGa.
@ $A1C0 label=Action_ClearTheTie_ReadTheIdFromTheIndexEntry
  $A1C0,$03 Read the id from the index entry.
  $A1C3,$04 Restore #REGix and #REGiy from the stack.
  $A1C7,$01 Return.

c $A1C8 Condition: Animal And Not Broken
@ $A1C8 label=Condition_AnimalAndNotBroken
D $A1C8 Returns with the zero flag set when the object is an animal (bit 6) and not broken (bit 3).
  $A1C8,$03 Read the attribute flags from the object record.
  $A1CB,$02 Keep the animal bit and the broken bit.
  $A1CD,$02 Compare with #N$40: animal set, broken clear.
  $A1CF,$01 Return.

c $A1D0 Action: Match A Verb In The Table
@ $A1D0 label=Action_MatchAVerbInTheTable
D $A1D0 Matches the current action against the five verb bytes at #R$A20B and returns when one
. of them matches.
  $A1D0,$04 Count #N$05 passes.
  $A1D4,$03 Point #REGhl at table: Verb List.
  $A1D7,$03 Read *#R$B6E7.
@ $A1DA label=Action_MatchAVerbInTheTable_TestTheVerb
  $A1DA,$03 Jump to #R$A1E0 if the current action is the verb byte.
  $A1DD,$01 Step to the next byte in display exits.
  $A1DE,$02 Loop back to #R$A1DA until #N$05 runs out.
@ $A1E0 label=Action_MatchAVerbInTheTable_ReturnFromTheVerbTable
  $A1E0,$02 Restore #REGbc and #REGhl from the stack.
  $A1E2,$01 Return.

c $A1E3 Response: You Say
@ $A1E3 label=Response_YouSay
D $A1E3 Prints "You say ", then the message and a full stop, and arms the capitalise-the-next-letter flag.
  $A1E3,$04 Point at the message "You say ".
  $A1E7,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $A1EA,$06 Set the capitalise-the-next-letter flag before the spoken message.
  $A1F0,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $A1F3,$03 Point at the message ".[0x14]" (#R$B00F).
  $A1F6,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD to print the full stop.

c $A1F9 Action: Read The Object's Lock Flag
@ $A1F9 label=Action_ReadTheObjectLockFlag
D $A1F9 Reads the current object's lock flag. A locked object returns #N$80; otherwise its attributes
. are tested.
  $A1F9,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $A1FD,$07 Take #N($80), and return if the object is locked.
  $A204,$04 Test whether the object is open.
  $A208,$02 Return #N($85), the message code for an open object.
  $A20A,$01 Return.

b $A20B Table: Verb List
D $A20B Five action ids. #R$A1D4 compares the current action with these bytes, and #R$A0BA reads #R$A20E as the direction-word table.
. Read by #R$A1D0.
  $A20B,$03 #N$0E drop, #N$11 put in, #N$12 put on.
  $A20E,$02 #N$14 take out of, #N$2C throw. #R$A0BA adds twice the direction id to this address.

b $A210 Table: Direction Words
D $A210 #TOKEN($0480) through #TOKEN($01C4). #R$A0BA reads the direction token at #R$A20E plus twice the direction id, so direction #N$01 is the first word here.
W $A210,$02 #WORDPTR
L $A210,$02,$0A

b $A224 Table: Names For A Clear Bit 7
D $A224 Eight words. #R$A174 points here when bit 7 of the name byte is clear, and #R$A0BD picks one word.
W $A224,$02 #WORDPTR
W $A226,$02 Empty.
W $A228,$02 #WORDPTR
W $A22A,$02 Empty.
W $A22C,$02 #WORDPTR
W $A22E,$02 #WORDPTR
W $A230,$02 #WORDPTR
W $A232,$02 Empty.

b $A234 Table: Names For A Set Bit 7
D $A234 Eight words. #R$A17B points here when bit 7 of the name byte is set.
W $A234,$02 #WORDPTR
W $A236,$02 Empty.
W $A238,$02 #WORDPTR
W $A23A,$02 #WORDPTR
W $A23C,$02 #WORDPTR
W $A23E,$02 #WORDPTR
W $A240,$02 #WORDPTR
W $A242,$02 Empty.

c $A244 Action: Drink Water
@ $A244 label=Action_DrinkWater
D $A244 Holds the message back when the player drinks #OBJECT($17,$01,$01)($). Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($17,$01,$01)($) for action #N($21), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A244,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, it returns past this handler.
  $A247,$01 Return.

c $A248 Action: Tie
@ $A248 label=ActionTie
D $A248 Ties the current object to the second object.
. Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($12,$01,$01)($) for action #N($2E), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
N $A248 Test the current object.
  $A248,$07 Jump to #R$A2AE if *#R$B6E8 is #N$12.
N $A24F Test the second object.
  $A24F,$08 Jump to #R$9F76 if *#R$B6E9 is not #N$12.
  $A257,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $A25B,$07 Jump back to #R$9F76 if the object is a fluid.
N $A262 Count the objects being held.
  $A262,$03 Call #R$9D97 to count the visible objects.
  $A265,$08 Point #REGhl at the message, and jump back to #R$72DD if #R$9D97 counted any visible objects inside it.
  $A26D,$06 Jump to #R$A27A if the object is broken.
  $A273,$07 Jump back to #R$9F76 if the object is an animal.
@ $A27A label=ActionTie_StartTheTie
N $A27A If the message is held back, the object is not tied.
  $A27A,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the object is not tied.
  $A27D,$04 Read *#R$B70A.
N $A281 Test whether the object is being carried.
  $A281,$03 Call #R$9C78 to test whether the object is carried.
  $A284,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $A287,$03 Update where this object is.
  $A28A,$02 Jump to #R$A2A2 if #R$9C78 found the object carried (carry set).
  $A28C,$01 Clear the message gate before the object is picked up.
  $A28D,$03 Update *#R$B6FA.
  $A290,$03 Call #R$8D33 to pick the object up.
  $A293,$03 Read the message-suppressed flag left by the pick-up.
  $A296,$02 Compare the message-suppressed flag with zero.
N $A298 Update the message gate.
  $A298,$08 Hold later messages back.
  $A2A0,$02 Jump to #R$A2A9 if the message-suppressed flag is zero.
@ $A2A2 label=ActionTie_ReadTheCurrentCharacter
  $A2A2,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $A2A5,$03 Update where this object is.
  $A2A8,$01 Return.
@ $A2A9 label=ActionTie_MarkWhereThisObjectIsAsUnset
  $A2A9,$04 Mark where this object is as unset.
  $A2AD,$01 Return.
@ $A2AE label=ActionTie_PointAtTheTieAction
  $A2AE,$03 Point #REGhl at the tie action.
  $A2B1,$03 Jump to #R$9F4A to hand an object over.

c $A2B4 Action: Untie
@ $A2B4 label=ActionUntie
D $A2B4 Unties the current object.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
  $A2B4,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $A2B8,$03 Read where this object is from the object record.
  $A2BB,$02 Compare where this object is with #N$12.
  $A2BD,$03 Point #REGhl at "[0x07] is not tied".
  $A2C0,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD if where this object is is not #N$12.
N $A2C3 If the message is held back, the object stays tied.
  $A2C3,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the object stays tied.
  $A2C6,$06 Copy where #OBJECT($12,$01,$01)($) is into where this object is.
  $A2CC,$01 Return.
N $A2CD Find the exit through the current object.
N $A2CD Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($2A,$01,$01)($) for action #N($32), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A2CD,$03 Call #R$9F25 to find the exit through the current object.
  $A2D0,$03 Read this exit's destination.
  $A2D3,$04 Jump to #R$A2DB if this exit's destination is not zero.
N $A2D7 If the message is held back, the routine returns without untying.
  $A2D7,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the routine returns without untying.
  $A2DA,$01 Return.
@ $A2DB label=ActionUntie_OpenAndMove
  $A2DB,$03 Read this exit's direction.
  $A2DE,$03 Read *#R$B6E7.
  $A2E1,$04 Update *#R$B6E7.
  $A2E5,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $A2E9,$04 Mark the object open.
  $A2ED,$07 Write #N$FF to *#R$B6E8.
  $A2F4,$03 Call #R$8DAB to carry on with the move.
  $A2F7,$06 Restore the object from the stack and close it.
  $A2FD,$04 Update *#R$B6E7.
  $A301,$01 Return.

c $A302 Action: Burn
@ $A302 label=ActionBurn
D $A302 Burns the current object.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
N $A302 Test whose turn it is.
  $A302,$08 Jump to #R$9F76 if *#R$B6EA is not #N$3C.
N $A30A If the message is held back, the object is not burned.
  $A30A,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the object is not burned.
  $A30D,$03 Jump back to #R$977C to test the current object.
N $A310 If the message is held back, the river message is not printed.
N $A310 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($09,$01,$01)($) for action #N($32), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A310,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the river message is not printed.
  $A313,$03 Point #REGhl at "as soon as you touch the river you fall asleep and gently float away".
@ $A316 label=ActionBurn_Loop
N $A316 Print the message at #REGhl.
  $A316,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $A319,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $A31C,$02 See whether the character whose turn it is is zero.
  $A31E,$03 Point #REGhl at "Time passes.." in #R$ADA3.
  $A321,$03 Call #R$72DD if it is the player's turn.
  $A324,$04 Jump back to #R$977F to try the next object.
N $A328 If the message is held back, the sleep message is not printed.
N $A328 Reached through the action lists of #OBJECT($16,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($18,$01,$01)($) for action #N($21), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A328,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the sleep message is not printed.
  $A32B,$03 Point #REGhl at "You fall asleep".
  $A32E,$02 Jump back to #R$A316 to print "You fall asleep".
N $A330 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($0B,$01,$01)($) for action #N($26), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A330,$02 Use object #N$02.
  $A332,$02 Jump to #R$A33A to test whether that object is the second object.
N $A334 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($08,$01,$01)($) for actions #N$25 and #N($26), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A334,$02 Use object #N$0F.
  $A336,$02 Jump to #R$A33A to test whether that object is the second object.
N $A338 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($01,$01,$01)($) for actions #N$25 and #N($26), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A338,$02 Use object #N$04.
@ $A33A label=ActionBurn_TestTheSecondObject
  $A33A,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $A33D,$03 Jump to #R$A34B unless the second object is the one in #REGb.
N $A340 Test the current action.
  $A340,$08 Jump to #R$946D to lock the object if *#R$B6E7 is #N$25.
  $A348,$03 Jump to #R$948D to unlock the object.
@ $A34B label=ActionBurn_TestTheAction
  $A34B,$04 Jump to #R$A358 if the current action is #N$02.
  $A34F,$04 Jump to #R$A358 if the current action is #N$04.
  $A353,$05 Jump back to #R$72CE if the current action is not #N$0F.
@ $A358 label=ActionBurn_PrintDoesNotFit
N $A358 Say "[0x09] does not fit this lock".
N $A358 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($05,$01,$01)($) for actions #N$25 and #N($26), and of #OBJECT($1E,$01,$01)($) for action #N($26), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A358,$06 Point #REGhl at the message and call #R$72DD.
N $A35E Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($0B,$01,$01)($) for action #N($00), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A35E,$03 Call #R$97FF to let the message through or hold it back.
  $A361,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $A365,$03 Jump back to #R$9117 to open the object.
N $A368 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($06,$01,$01)($) for action #N($10), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A368,$04 Read *#R$B70C.
  $A36C,$03 Read the location from the object record.
  $A36F,$05 Jump back to #R$9F76 if the object's location is not #N$0F.
  $A374,$03 Jump to #R$910E to open or close.
N $A377 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($07,$01,$01)($) for action #N($00), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A377,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $A37B,$05 Return if the object is not broken.
  $A380,$04 Mark the object open.
  $A384,$06 Store the object's location in the spider web script.
N $A38A Say "some spiders start mend(s|d|ing|es) the broken web".
  $A38A,$06 Point #REGhl at the message and call #R$72DD.
N $A390 Wear the ring: the character turns invisible and its strength is quartered.
N $A390 If the message is held back, the ring is not put on.
N $A390 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($10,$01,$01)($) for action #N($28), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A390,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the ring is not put on.
  $A393,$04 Read the character.
  $A397,$04 Point #REGix at the ring.
  $A39B,$04 Make the character invisible.
  $A39F,$04 Halve the strength.
  $A3A3,$04 Halve the strength.
  $A3A7,$04 Hide the ring.
  $A3AB,$06 Move the ring onto the character.
  $A3B1,$02 Roll a random number below #N$08.
N $A3B3 Roll a random number.
  $A3B3,$03 Call #R$9C9F to roll a number from the limit in #REGa.
  $A3B6,$02 Add #N$02 to the roll.
  $A3B8,$03 Store the roll at #R$CAAF.
  $A3BB,$01 Return.

c $A3BC Action: Remove The Ring
@ $A3BC label=Action_RemoveTheRing
D $A3BC Takes the ring off. If the ring is not being worn (it is visible), prints "You are not wearing" and its name; otherwise the ring and the character become visible again, the strength is multiplied by four, and #R$CAAF is cleared.
. Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($10,$01,$01)($) for action #N($16), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A3BC,$04 Read the character.
  $A3C0,$04 Point #REGiy at the ring.
  $A3C4,$04 Test whether the ring is visible.
  $A3C8,$03 Point #REGhl at "You are not wearing[0x07]".
  $A3CB,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD if the ring is not being worn.
N $A3CE If the message is held back, the ring is not taken off.
  $A3CE,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the ring is not taken off.
  $A3D1,$04 Show the ring again.
  $A3D5,$04 Make the character visible again.
  $A3D9,$04 Double the strength and keep the overflow.
  $A3DD,$04 Double the strength and keep the overflow.
  $A3E1,$01 Clear the byte at #N$CAAF.
  $A3E2,$03 Store the result at #R$CAAF.
  $A3E5,$01 Return.

c $A3E6 Action: Capture
@ $A3E6 label=ActionCapture
D $A3E6 Captures the current creature.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
  $A3E6,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $A3EA,$03 Read the second flag from the object record.
  $A3ED,$08 Keep bits 4-6 of the flags, save #REGix, and point #REGix at the object in *#R$B70C.
  $A3F5,$03 Test these bits of the object's second flag.
  $A3F8,$05 Restore the object record from the stack and jump to #R$9F76 if those flag bits were set.
  $A3FD,$07 Jump back to #R$9F76 if the object is not an animal.
  $A404,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $A407,$06 Use location #N$1F and jump to #R$A413 if this character is #N$40.
  $A40D,$04 Jump to #R$A413 if the character whose turn it is is #N$42.
  $A411,$02 Use location #N$0D for any other character.
@ $A413 label=ActionCapture_TestTheLocation
  $A413,$03 Read *#R$B6F6.
  $A416,$04 Jump back to #R$9F76 if the script location is the location in #REGb.
N $A41A If the message is held back, the creature is not captured.
  $A41A,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the creature is not captured.
  $A41D,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $A421,$03 Update the object's location.
  $A424,$04 Mark where this object is as unset.
  $A428,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $A42B,$03 Call #R$9BDD to move what is inside the current object to the capture location.
  $A42E,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $A431,$03 Return if the current object is not zero.
  $A434,$03 Update *#R$B6EA.
  $A437,$06 Point *#R$B70C at "#TEXTTOKEN($C11B+$08,$01)".
N $A43D Test whether the location is lit.
  $A43D,$03 Call #R$95ED to test whether the location is lit.
  $A440,$01 Return if the location is dark (carry set).
  $A441,$01 Take the capture location into #REGa.
  $A442,$03 Point #REGhl at "You are in".
  $A445,$03 Jump to #R$964D to print the location text.

c $A448 Action: Goblin Capture
@ $A448 label=ActionGoblinCapture
D $A448 Runs for the goblins #OBJECT($3D,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($45,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($49,$01,$01)($)
. to #OBJECT($4C,$01,$01)($). Their action lists reach here through #R$9586 and #R$9B6C, called from
. #R$950F. #REGb holds the character from #R$B6EA when reached through #R$9A0C, and #N$00 when reached
. through #R$7B3A. The player's location is compared with it before the goblin speaks.
. Reached through the action lists of #OBJECT($3D,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($45,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($49,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($4A,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($4B,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($4C,$01,$01)($) for action #N($00), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A448,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $A44C,$05 Return if the object is not broken.
  $A451,$04 Mark the object not broken.
  $A455,$03 Each capture entry is #N$06 bytes.
@ $A458 nowarn
  $A458,$04 Point #REGiy at the capture table.
  $A45C,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
@ $A45F label=ActionGoblinCapture_Loop
  $A45F,$05 Jump to #R$A468 if this capture entry is the current object.
  $A464,$02 Step #REGiy on by one capture entry.
  $A466,$02 Jump back to #R$A45F to try the next object.
@ $A468 label=ActionGoblinCapture_StepToTheNextByteInCapture
  $A468,$04 Step to the next byte in capture.
  $A46C,$01 Read the low byte of the pointer in this capture entry.
  $A46D,$01 Step to the next byte in capture.
  $A46E,$01 Read the high byte of the pointer in this capture entry.
  $A46F,$02 Store the current object id at the address taken from this entry.
  $A471,$04 Store the next table byte as the object's location.
  $A475,$01 Step to the next byte in capture.
  $A476,$04 Store the next table byte as name byte #N$0A of the object.
  $A47A,$01 Step to the next byte in capture.
  $A47B,$04 Store the next table byte as name byte #N$0B of the object.
N $A47F Say "[0x07] fall(s|d|ing|es) down a hole and vanish(s|d|ing|es)".
  $A47F,$06 Point #REGhl at the message and call #R$72DD.
  $A485,$05 Return if the player's location is not the #REGb passed in by the caller of #R$950F.
  $A48A,$03 Use the token #TOKEN($005E).
N $A48D Print the token in #REGde.
  $A48D,$03 Call #R$74C1 to print a dictionary token.
  $A490,$03 Use the token #TOKEN($02E2).
N $A493 Print the token in #REGde.
  $A493,$03 Call #R$74C1 to print a dictionary token.
N $A496 Print the message at #REGhl.
  $A496,$06 Point #REGhl at the message and call #R$72DD.

b $A49C Goblin Capture Table
@ $A49C label=ActionGoblinCapture_CaptureTable
D $A49C Six entries of #N$06 bytes. Read by #R$A45F when a goblin captures an object. Each is a goblin id, the address that stores this goblin's id, the location the object is moved to, and name bytes #N$0A and #N$0B.
  $A49C,$06 #OBJECT($3D,$01,$01)($) stores this goblin's id at #R$CB18 and moves the object to #LOCATION($0F).
  $A4A2,$06 #OBJECT($45,$01,$01)($) stores this goblin's id at #R$CB1F and moves the object to #LOCATION($3A).
  $A4A8,$06 #OBJECT($49,$01,$01)($) stores this goblin's id at #R$CB2D and moves the object to #LOCATION($12).
  $A4AE,$06 #OBJECT($4A,$01,$01)($) stores this goblin's id at #R$CB34 and moves the object to #LOCATION($38).
  $A4B4,$06 #OBJECT($4B,$01,$01)($) stores this goblin's id at #R$CB26 and moves the object to #LOCATION($40).
  $A4BA,$06 #OBJECT($4C,$01,$01)($) stores this goblin's id at #R$CB3B and moves the object to #LOCATION($10).

c $A4C0 Action: Open The Goblins Door
@ $A4C0 label=Action_OpenTheGoblinsDoor
D $A4C0 Opens #OBJECT($11,$01,$01)($) when the character in *#R$B70C is in #LOCATION($10). The action list stores this address at #N($C357), and the copy of the objects stores it at #N$F63C.
. Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($11,$01,$01)($) for action #N($10), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A4C0,$04 Point #REGix at the character in *#R$B70C.
  $A4C4,$03 Read the location from the object record.
  $A4C7,$05 Jump back to #R$9F76 if the object's location is not #N$10.
  $A4CC,$03 Call #R$910E to open or close the object.
  $A4CF,$03 Call #R$97FF to let the message through or hold it back.
  $A4D2,$06 Copy the goblins door id at #R$CA99 into the flag at #R$CA9A.
  $A4D8,$01 Return.

c $A4D9 Event: Close The Goblins Door
@ $A4D9 label=Event_CloseTheGoblinsDoor
D $A4D9 Closes the object at #R$C34B. The goblins door record at #R$CA9B calls this.
  $A4D9,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$C34B.
  $A4DC,$02 Close that object.
  $A4DE,$01 Return.
N $A4DF If the message is held back, the goblins' door is not closed.
  $A4DF,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the goblins' door is not closed.
  $A4E2,$02 Roll a random number below #N$0A.
N $A4E4 Roll a random number.
  $A4E4,$03 Call #R$9C9F to roll a number from the limit in #REGa.
  $A4E7,$03 Point #REGhl at "what do you expect me to do with this ?".
  $A4EA,$05 Jump back to #R$A1E3 if the roll is not below #N$08.
  $A4EF,$03 Point #REGhl at "thank you.".
  $A4F2,$03 Jump back to #R$A1E3 to print "thank you.".

c $A4F5 Event: What's This
@ $A4F5 label=Event_WhatsThis
D $A4F5 Prints "what's this ?" through #R$A1E3. A character script calls this from #R$C8C9.
N $A4F5 If the message is held back, "what's this ?" is not printed.
  $A4F5,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, "what's this ?" is not printed.
  $A4F8,$03 Point #REGhl at "what's this ?".
  $A4FB,$03 Jump back to #R$A1E3 to print "what's this ?".

c $A4FE Event: A Short Reply
@ $A4FE label=Event_AShortReply
D $A4FE Prints one of "you are doing a great job", "hurry up" or "hello". A character script
. calls this from #R$C8D7.
N $A4FE If the message is held back, the short reply is not printed.
  $A4FE,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the short reply is not printed.
  $A501,$02 Roll a random number below #N$02.
N $A503 Roll a random number.
  $A503,$03 Call #R$9C9F to roll a number from the limit in #REGa.
  $A506,$03 Point #REGhl at "you are do(s|d|ing|es) a great job.".
  $A509,$05 Jump back to #R$A1E3 when #REGa is zero.
  $A50E,$03 Point #REGhl at "hurry up.".
  $A511,$05 Jump back to #R$A1E3 if the roll is #N$01.
  $A516,$03 Point #REGhl at "hello.".
  $A519,$03 Jump back to #R$A1E3 to print "hello.".
N $A51C If the message is held back, Thrain's key is not mentioned.
  $A51C,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, Thrain's key is not mentioned.
  $A51F,$03 Point #REGhl at "this was Thrains key.".
  $A522,$03 Jump back to #R$A1E3 to print "this was thrains key.".
  $A525,$03 Read *#R$B6F6.
  $A528,$05 Return if the script location is not where the player starts.
N $A52D If the message is held back, the hello is not printed.
  $A52D,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the hello is not printed.
  $A530,$03 Point #REGhl at "hello.".
  $A533,$03 Jump back to #R$A1E3 to print "hello.".
N $A536 Test whose turn it is.
  $A536,$08 Jump to #R$9F76 if *#R$B6EA is not #N$40.
  $A53E,$03 Jump to #R$910E to open or close.

c $A541 Action: Climb Out
@ $A541 label=ActionClimbOut
D $A541 Climbs out of the container the character is in.
. Reached through the action table at #R$C730, dispatched by #R$950F.
  $A541,$04 Read the record of the character whose turn it is.
  $A545,$03 Read the current object.
  $A548,$06 Jump to #R$9F76 if the character is not inside the current object.
  $A54E,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $A551,$03 Call #R$A5CA to test whether the object is open.
  $A554,$03 Jump back to #R$A16C if the object is not open.
  $A557,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the character does not climb out.
  $A55A,$04 Mark where this object is as unset.
  $A55E,$01 Return.

c $A55F Event: Containers And Scripts
@ $A55F label=EventContainersAndScripts
D $A55F Runs container checks and the character scripts for this turn.
. Reached through the action lists of #OBJECT($13,$01,$01)($), #OBJECT($25,$01,$01)($) and #OBJECT($29,$01,$01)($) for action #N($36), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A55F,$04 Read *#R$B70C.
  $A563,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $A566,$06 Jump back to #R$9F76 if the current object is where this object is.
  $A56C,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $A570,$03 Call #R$9E34 to test whether the object is here.
  $A573,$03 Jump back to #R$9F76 if #R$9E34 did not find the object here.
  $A576,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
@ $A579 label=EventContainersAndScripts_Loop
N $A579 Find the object's record.
  $A579,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $A57C,$03 Read where this object is from the object record.
  $A57F,$04 Jump to #R$A596 if this object is not inside another object.
  $A583,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $A586,$03 Compare the character whose turn it is with where this object is.
  $A589,$03 Read where this object is from the object record.
  $A58C,$02 Jump back to #R$A579 if the character whose turn it is is not where this object is.
  $A58E,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $A592,$04 Mark where this object is as unset.
@ $A596 label=EventContainersAndScripts_TotalTheVolume
  $A596,$04 Read *#R$B70C.
  $A59A,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $A59D,$03 Call #R$9CE8 to total the volume inside the character.
  $A5A0,$03 Add the character's volume onto the total from #R$9CE8.
  $A5A3,$02 Jump to #R$A5A7 if the sum overflowed, keeping the wrapped total.
  $A5A5,$02 There was no carry, so take #N$FF as the total.
@ $A5A7 label=EventContainersAndScripts_TestTheVolume
  $A5A7,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $A5A8,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $A5AC,$03 Call #R$A5CA to test whether the object is open.
  $A5AF,$03 Jump to #R$A16C if the object is not open.
  $A5B2,$03 Read the volume from the object record.
  $A5B5,$04 Jump to #R$A5C0 if this object's volume byte is #N$FF.
  $A5B9,$01 Subtract #REGb from the object's volume.
  $A5BA,$03 Point #REGhl at "You are too big".
  $A5BD,$03 Jump to #R$72DD to print it if the object's volume is not below #REGb.
@ $A5C0 label=EventContainersAndScripts_LetTheMessageThrough
N $A5C0 If the message is held back, the container checks are skipped.
  $A5C0,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the container checks are skipped.
  $A5C3,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $A5C6,$03 Update where this object is.
  $A5C9,$01 Return.

c $A5CA Event: Test The Open Flag
@ $A5CA label=Event_TestTheOpenFlag
D $A5CA Tests whether the object is open and returns #N$05. The zero flag is set when the object is not
. open. The bytes after that return belong to later events.
  $A5CA,$04 Test whether the object is open.
  $A5CE,$02 Take #N$05. The open test is left in the flags.
  $A5D0,$01 Return.
N $A5D1 If the message is held back, the container test is skipped.
  $A5D1,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the container test is skipped.
  $A5D4,$03 Point #REGhl at the location byte at #R$C452.
  $A5D7,$05 Return if the current location is not that object's location.
  $A5DC,$03 Point at the message "the vicious warg run around you and howls." (#R$B085).
  $A5DF,$03 Jump back to #R$97F4 to print "the vicious warg run around you and howls.".
N $A5E2 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($0C,$01,$01)($) for action #N($00), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A5E2,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $A5E5,$03 Return if the current object is not #N$13.
  $A5E8,$03 Call #R$97FF to let the message through or hold it back.
  $A5EB,$04 Point #REGix at #OBJECT($13,$01,$01)($).
  $A5EF,$03 Read the object's location.
  $A5F2,$03 Return if "#TEXTTOKEN($C3EE+$08,$01)" is not at location #R$BDC1(#N$21) - "#TEXTTOKEN($BDC1+$02,$01)".
  $A5F5,$05 Write #N$02 to *#R$CA85.
  $A5FA,$01 Return.

c $A5FB Event: The Barrel
@ $A5FB label=Event_TheBarrel
D $A5FB Runs for the barrel. When the player is inside it, the player is thrown onto the bank of
. the long lake. The barrel record at #R$CA86 calls this.
  $A5FB,$01 Clear the flag at #R$B6F0.
  $A5FC,$03 Update *#R$B6F0.
  $A5FF,$03 Read where the player is.
  $A602,$02 Is the player inside object #N($13), i.e. using the "#TEXTTOKEN($C3EE+$08,$01)"?
  $A604,$03 Point at the message "you are thrown onto the bank of the long lake" (#R$B32C).
  $A607,$03 Call #R$72DD to print it if the player is inside the barrel.
  $A60A,$05 Write #N$22 to *#R$C3FE.
  $A60F,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $A610,$02 Use the barrel, object #N$13.
  $A612,$03 Call #R$9BDD to move what is inside the barrel to location #N$22.
  $A615,$04 Point #REGix at #OBJECT($13,$01,$01)($).
  $A619,$04 Update the location of the object to location #R$BDAD(#N$20) - "#TEXTTOKEN($BDAD+$02,$01)".
  $A61D,$04 Close the object.
  $A621,$04 Mark the object full.
  $A625,$01 Clear the time-of-day flag, then look up object #N$13.
  $A626,$03 Update *#R$B702.
  $A629,$02 Look up where object #N$13 is.
  $A62B,$03 Call #R$9D53 to find where the object is.
  $A62E,$05 Write #N$01 (day) to *#R$B702.
  $A633,$04 Point #REGiy at #OBJECT($14,$01,$01)($).
  $A637,$04 Set the object's location to #N$20.
  $A63B,$04 Set where this object is to #N$13.
  $A63F,$01 Return.
  $A640,$03 Read *#R$B6F6.
  $A643,$05 Return if the script location is not where the player starts.
  $A648,$06 Return if the player is visible.
N $A64E If the message is held back, the thief question is not printed.
  $A64E,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the thief question is not printed.
  $A651,$03 Point at the message "where's the thief ?[0x16]" (#R$B0CB).
  $A654,$03 Jump back to #R$A1E3 to print "where's the thief ?".
N $A657 If the message is held back, the barrel does not throw the player out.
  $A657,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the barrel does not throw the player out.
  $A65A,$02 Roll a random number below #N$08.
N $A65C Roll a random number.
  $A65C,$03 Call #R$9C9F to roll a number from the limit in #REGa.
  $A65F,$03 Return if the roll is not below #N$05.
  $A662,$08 Jump back to #R$97F4 if the roll is not below #N$03.
  $A66A,$06 Point #REGhl at #R$B0D5 and jump back to #R$A1E3 if the roll is #N$03 (never taken: a roll of #N$03 has already jumped at #R$A667).
  $A670,$08 Jump back to #R$97F4 when the roll is zero.
  $A678,$03 Point at the message "hurry up." (#R$B05B).
  $A67B,$03 Jump back to #R$A1E3 to print "hurry up.".
N $A67E Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($0C,$01,$01)($) for actions #N$0C and #N($10), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A67E,$04 Read *#R$B70C.
  $A682,$03 Read the location from the object record.
  $A685,$08 Jump back to #R$72DD if the object's location is not #N$20.
N $A68D Test the current action.
  $A68D,$08 Jump to #R$9138 if *#R$B6E7 is #N$0C (close).
  $A695,$03 Jump to #R$910E to open or close.
N $A698 Test the flag.
  $A698,$07 Jump to #R$A6A6 if *#R$C12B is #N$27.
  $A69F,$04 Jump to #R$A6A6 if the current location is #N$2C.
  $A6A3,$03 Return if the current location is not #N$29.
@ $A6A6 label=Event_TheBarrel_LetTheMessageThrough
N $A6A6 If the message is held back, the location is not changed.
  $A6A6,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the location is not changed.
  $A6A9,$03 Read the current location.
  $A6AC,$03 Point #REGhl at the location byte at #R$C143.
  $A6AF,$02 Return if the current location is that object's location.
  $A6B1,$01 Store it at #R$C143.
  $A6B2,$05 Write #N$01 (day) to *#R$B702.
  $A6B7,$03 Point #REGhl at "#TEXTTOKEN(#PC)".
  $A6BA,$04 Point at the message "[0x00] enter(s|d|ing|es).[0x14]".
@ $A6BE label=Event_TheBarrel_PrintMessage
N $A6BE Print the message at #REGhl.
  $A6BE,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $A6C1,$01 Return.
  $A6C2,$08 Return if the current location is not the script location.
N $A6CA If the message is held back, the player's attributes are left alone.
  $A6CA,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the player's attributes are left alone.
  $A6CD,$03 Read the player's attributes.
  $A6D0,$02 Keep only bit 7.
  $A6D2,$03 Point #REGhl at "the dragon say(s|d|ing|es)".
  $A6D5,$02 Jump to #R$A6DA if the player is not visible.
  $A6D7,$03 Point #REGhl at "the dragon say(s|d|ing|es)".
@ $A6DA label=Event_TheBarrel_DragonSays
  $A6DA,$02 Jump back to #R$A6BE to print "the dragon say(s|d|ing|es)".
  $A6DC,$06 Return if that object's location is #N$29.
  $A6E2,$03 Read the current location.
N $A6E5 Find the location's record.
  $A6E5,$03 Call #R$9BB1 to locate location.
  $A6E8,$05 Return if bit 7 of the location attribute is clear.
N $A6ED If the message is held back, day is not announced.
  $A6ED,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, day is not announced.
  $A6F0,$05 Write #N$01 (day) to *#R$B702.
  $A6F5,$03 Point #REGhl at "in the distance you see the shape of a monstrous dragon flying after you".
  $A6F8,$02 Take the roll limit #N$64.
N $A6FA Roll a random number.
  $A6FA,$03 Call #R$9C9F to roll a number from the limit in #REGa.
  $A6FD,$04 Jump to #R$A6BE if the roll is below #N$50.
N $A701 Print the message at #REGhl.
  $A701,$06 Point #REGhl at the message and call #R$72DD.
  $A707,$03 Jump to #R$90D2 to end the game.
N $A70A Find the exit through the current object.
  $A70A,$03 Call #R$9F25 to find the exit through the current object.
  $A70D,$03 Return if no matching exit was found.
  $A710,$01 Return zero; the exit is cleared below.
  $A711,$04 Clear this exit's direction.
  $A715,$04 Clear the object this exit goes via.
  $A719,$04 Clear this exit's destination.
  $A71D,$01 Return.
N $A71E If the message is held back, the barrel event stops.
N $A71E Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($0D,$01,$01)($) for action #N($1C), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A71E,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the barrel event stops.
  $A721,$04 Read *#R$B70C.
  $A725,$04 Test the object's attributes.
  $A729,$03 Point #REGhl at "You see nothing special here".
  $A72C,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD if the object is visible.
  $A72F,$06 Copy the magic-door id at #N$CAA7 into the next byte.
N $A735 Say "The magic door warn(s|d|ing|es) of elves approach(s|d|ing|es)".
  $A735,$06 Point #REGhl at the message and call #R$72DD.
N $A73B Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($3F,$01,$01)($) for action #N($00), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A73B,$06 Return if that object is not broken.
  $A741,$03 Call #R$97FF to let the message through or hold it back.
  $A744,$04 Point #REGix at #OBJECT($02,$01,$01)($).
  $A748,$04 Mark the object broken.
  $A74C,$02 Clear the tie on object #N$02.
  $A74E,$03 Call #R$A18C to clear the tie on the object.
  $A751,$02 Pass object id #N$02.
  $A753,$04 Point #REGiy at #OBJECT($00,$01,$01)($).
  $A757,$03 Call #R$9E25 to swap the objects and test visibility again.
  $A75A,$01 Return if the object is not visible.
N $A75B Say "the small curious key shatter(s|d|ing|es)".
  $A75B,$06 Point #REGhl at the message and call #R$72DD.
N $A761 Test whose turn it is.
N $A761 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($1B,$01,$01)($) for actions #N$0C and #N($10), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A761,$07 Jump to #R$A776 if *#R$B6EA is not #N$00 (the player).
  $A768,$03 Read where the player is.
  $A76B,$03 Point #REGhl at "you cannot reach[0x07]".
  $A76E,$05 Jump back to #R$72DD if where the player is is #N$FF.
  $A773,$03 Jump to #R$9F76 to say that will not work.
@ $A776 label=Event_TheBarrel_TestForClose
N $A776 Test the current action.
  $A776,$08 Jump to #R$9138 if *#R$B6E7 is #N$0C (close).
  $A77E,$05 Jump back to #R$910E if the current action is #N$10.
  $A783,$01 Return.
N $A784 Test whose turn it is.
N $A784 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($1B,$01,$01)($) for actions #N($0B), #N$18 and #N($1E), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A784,$08 Jump to #R$A797 if *#R$B6EA is not #N$00 (the player).
  $A78C,$03 Read where the player is.
  $A78F,$02 Compare where the player is with #N$FF.
  $A791,$03 Point #REGhl at "you cannot reach[0x07]".
  $A794,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD if where the player is is #N$FF.
@ $A797 label=Event_TheBarrel_TestTheAction
N $A797 Test the current action.
  $A797,$08 Jump to #R$8F3B if *#R$B6E7 is #N$1E.
  $A79F,$05 Jump back to #R$92ED if the current action is #N$0B.
  $A7A4,$05 Jump back to #R$8EEC if the current action is #N$18.
  $A7A9,$01 Return.

c $A7AA Event: The Deep Bog
@ $A7AA label=Event_TheDeepBog
D $A7AA Prints that you are sinking into the bog, and the bog can be fatal. The deep bog record
. at #R$CAA0 calls this.
N $A7AA Test the flag.
  $A7AA,$07 Jump to #R$A7B5 if *#R$C12B is not #N$1D.
  $A7B1,$01 Clear the byte at #R$CAA1.
  $A7B2,$03 Store the result at #R$CAA1.
@ $A7B5 label=Event_TheDeepBog_PrintTheSinking
N $A7B5 Say "You are slowly sink(s|d|ing|es) into the bog".
  $A7B5,$06 Point #REGhl at the message and call #R$72DD.
  $A7BB,$06 Return if the deep bog script is not zero.
  $A7C1,$03 Jump to #R$90D2 to end the game.
N $A7C4 Test whose turn it is.
N $A7C4 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($03,$01,$01)($) for action #N($1C), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A7C4,$08 Jump to #R$93DA if *#R$B6EA is not #N$41.
N $A7CC If the message is held back, the sinking message is not printed.
  $A7CC,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the sinking message is not printed.
  $A7CF,$04 Point #REGiy at the extra-exit entry (#R$97CF writes this address into #N$A7D1).
  $A7D3,$03 Read the low byte of this extra exit's direction token.
  $A7D6,$03 Read the high byte of this extra exit's direction token.
N $A7D9 Test the flag.
  $A7D9,$07 Jump to #R$A7EF if *#R$B6F1 is not #N$00.
  $A7E0,$02 Count #N$03 passes.
@ $A7E2 label=Event_TheDeepBog_ReadTheExtraByte
  $A7E2,$03 Read the next byte of this extra exit.
  $A7E5,$02 Store it at #R$B153 and step on.
  $A7E7,$02 Step to the next byte of this extra exit.
  $A7E9,$02 Loop back to #R$A7E2 until #N$03 runs out.
  $A7EB,$04 Read the extra-exit pointer saved at #N$A7D1.
@ $A7EF label=Event_TheDeepBog_ReadTheDestination
  $A7EF,$03 Read the destination from this extra exit.
N $A7F2 Find the location's record.
  $A7F2,$03 Call #R$9BB1 to locate location.
  $A7F5,$04 Step #REGix on to the location's name.
  $A7F9,$05 Save the location's name and read the location from this extra exit.
N $A7FE Find the location's record.
  $A7FE,$03 Call #R$9BB1 to locate location.
  $A801,$04 Step #REGix on to the location's name.
  $A805,$05 Save that name and read the direction from this extra exit.
  $A80A,$03 Call #R$A0BA to describe the direction word.
  $A80D,$04 Point #REGhl at "{5} go[0x01] from[0x00] to{5} get to[0x00]".
  $A811,$03 Jump back to #R$A1E3 to print the way from here to there.
N $A814 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($12,$01,$01)($) for action #N($2B), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A814,$03 Read *#R$B6E9.
  $A817,$03 Call #R$9F28 to find the exit through the object in #REGa.
  $A81A,$05 Jump back to #R$9F76 if no exit runs through this object.
N $A81F If the message is held back, the fatal bog is not described.
  $A81F,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the fatal bog is not described.
N $A822 Say "it sail(s|d|ing|es) across and.".
  $A822,$06 Point #REGhl at the message and call #R$72DD.
  $A828,$08 Jump to #R$A84C if the location of #OBJECT($29,$01,$01)($) is not this exit's destination.
  $A830,$03 Call #R$A86E to roll a random half.
  $A833,$02 Jump to #R$A842 if the roll was below half.
  $A835,$03 Point #REGhl at "fall(s|d|ing|es) just short of the other side".
  $A838,$03 Call #R$A86E to roll a random half.
  $A83B,$02 Jump to #R$A86B if the roll was below half.
  $A83D,$03 Point #REGhl at "[0x0b][0x08] but slide(s|d|ing|es) out again".
  $A840,$02 Jump to #R$A86B to print "but slide(s|d|ing|es) out again".
@ $A842 label=Event_TheDeepBog_LandTheRopeInTheBoat
  $A842,$05 Put the rope (#N$12) into the boat's container at #R$C61C.
  $A847,$03 Point #REGhl at "lands in the boat".
  $A84A,$02 Jump to #R$A86B to print "lands in the boat".
@ $A84C label=Event_TheDeepBog_PrintTheShortFall
  $A84C,$03 Point #REGhl at "fall(s|d|ing|es) just short of the other side".
  $A84F,$03 Call #R$A86E to roll a random half.
  $A852,$02 Jump to #R$A86B if #R$A86E rolled below #N$32.
  $A854,$03 Read this exit's destination.
  $A857,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $A85B,$03 Update the object's location.
  $A85E,$04 Mark where this object is as unset.
  $A862,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $A865,$03 Call #R$9BDD to move what is inside the current object to the location in #REGb.
  $A868,$03 Point #REGhl at "lands on the other side".
@ $A86B label=Event_TheDeepBog_PrintTheMessage
  $A86B,$03 Jump to #R$72DD to print the message at #REGhl.

c $A86E Event: A Random Half
@ $A86E label=Event_ARandomHalf
D $A86E Rolls a random half. Below #N$32 it returns at once; otherwise the boat may glide across
. the river.
  $A86E,$02 Take the roll limit #N$64.
N $A870 Roll a random number.
  $A870,$03 Call #R$9C9F to roll a number from the limit in #REGa.
  $A873,$02 Compare the roll with #N$32.
  $A875,$01 Return.
N $A876 If the message is held back, the boat does not sail.
N $A876 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($12,$01,$01)($) for action #N($31), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A876,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the boat does not sail.
  $A879,$06 Return if where that object is is not #N$12.
  $A87F,$03 Point #REGhl at "the boat glides across the river and lands on this side".
@ $A882 label=Event_ARandomHalf_PrintMessage
N $A882 Print the message at #REGhl.
  $A882,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $A885,$09 Keep #N($42), and jump to #R$A890 if the boat's location is not already #N$42.
  $A88E,$02 Take location #N$43 for the boat.
@ $A890 label=Event_ARandomHalf_MoveTheBoat
  $A890,$03 Store the boat's new location at #R$C62B.
  $A893,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $A894,$05 Write #N$FF to *#R$C61C.
  $A899,$02 Take the id of #OBJECT($29,$01,$01)($).
  $A89B,$03 Jump to #R$9BDD to move what is inside object #N$29 to the boat's new location.
N $A89E Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($29,$01,$01)($) for action #N($00), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $A89E,$03 Call #R$97FF to let the message through or hold it back.
  $A8A1,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $A8A4,$02 Return if it is not the player's turn.
  $A8A6,$03 Point #REGhl at "with a lurch the boat glides across the river and[0x02] ngfar".
  $A8A9,$02 Jump back to #R$A882 to print "with a lurch the boat glides across the river".
  $A8AB,$06 Return if the slot flag at #R$B6F4 is not #N$01.
  $A8B1,$01 Clear the flag at #R$B6F4.
  $A8B2,$03 Store the result at #R$B6F4.
  $A8B5,$01 Set #REGa to #N$01 for #R$7F1A.
  $A8B6,$03 Call #R$7F1A to save the clause and act.
  $A8B9,$01 Return if #R$7F1A returned with the zero flag set.
  $A8BA,$01 Take zero.
  $A8BB,$03 Clear the message-suppressed flag.
  $A8BE,$03 Read *#R$B6E6.
  $A8C1,$03 Store the result at #R$C9E3.
  $A8C4,$04 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $A8C8,$04 Store #REGbc at #N$C9E4.
  $A8CC,$05 Write #N$42 to #N$C9E2.
  $A8D1,$01 Return.
  $A8D2,$08 Return if the location of #OBJECT($44,$01,$01)($) is not where the player starts.
  $A8DA,$06 Return if the player is not visible.
N $A8E0 If the message is held back, the glide is not started.
  $A8E0,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the glide is not started.
  $A8E3,$03 Read *#R$B6EE.
  $A8E6,$02 Step on through the token held for the printer.
  $A8E8,$01 Read the low byte of the token held for the printer.
  $A8E9,$01 Step to the next byte of the token held for the printer.
  $A8EA,$01 Read the high byte of the token held for the printer.
  $A8EB,$05 Call #R$A1E3 to print "You say " and the message.
  $A8F0,$05 Write #N$01 to *#R$B6F9.
  $A8F5,$01 Return.
N $A8F6 If the message is held back, the random half is not taken.
  $A8F6,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the random half is not taken.
  $A8F9,$01 Clear the talk flag at #R$B6F9.
  $A8FA,$03 Update *#R$B6F9.
  $A8FD,$03 Call #R$7F1A to save the clause and act.
  $A900,$02 Jump to #R$A915 when #R$7F1A returns with the zero flag set.
  $A902,$03 Search #N$18 bytes of the clause slot for the riddle answer.
  $A905,$04 Read *#R$B6EE.
  $A909,$01 Read the first byte of the riddle answer.
@ $A90A label=Event_ARandomHalf_SearchForTheRiddleAnswer
  $A90A,$02 Search from #REGhl for this byte of the riddle answer.
  $A90C,$02 Jump to #R$A915 if the byte was not found.
  $A90E,$06 Jump back to #R$A90A unless the next byte of the riddle answer matches.
  $A914,$01 Return.
@ $A915 label=Event_ARandomHalf_HoldTheMessage
N $A915 If the message is held back, day is not set.
  $A915,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, day is not set.
  $A918,$05 Set #R$B702 to #N$01 (daytime).
  $A91D,$03 Point at the message "someone strangle(s|d|ing|es) you from behind" (#R$B1DB).
  $A920,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $A923,$03 Jump back to #R$90D2 to end the game.
  $A926,$08 Return if that object's location is not where the player starts.
N $A92E If the message is held back, Gollum's question is not printed.
  $A92E,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, Gollum's question is not printed.
  $A931,$03 Point #REGhl at "what has it got in its pocket(s|d|ing|es) ?".
  $A934,$02 Roll a random number below #N$08.
N $A936 Roll a random number.
  $A936,$03 Call #R$9C9F to roll a number from the limit in #REGa.
  $A939,$03 Jump back to #R$A1E3 unless #R$9C9F negated the roll (carry clear).
  $A93C,$0C Jump back to #R$A1E3 if #OBJECT($10,$01,$01)($) is with #N$44.
  $A948,$03 Point #REGhl at "my birthday present".
  $A94B,$03 Jump back to #R$A1E3 to print "my birthday present".
  $A94E,$03 Read *#R$B6F6.
  $A951,$05 Return if the script location is not where the player starts.
N $A956 If the message is held back, the action code is not stored.
  $A956,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the action code is not stored.
  $A959,$05 Write #N$1B to *#R$B6E7.
  $A95E,$05 Write #N$00 to *#R$B6E8.
  $A963,$05 Write #N$FF to *#R$B6E9.
N $A968 Say that will not work.
  $A968,$03 Call #R$9F76 to answer with that will not work.
  $A96B,$03 Call #R$92B5 to add #N$0A to the player's strength.
  $A96E,$03 Jump to #R$90D2 to end the game.

c $A971 Event: Day Dawns On The Trolls
@ $A971 label=Event_DayDawnsOnTheTrolls
D $A971 Turns the two trolls to stone, announces day, and repaints Trolls Clearing. A character
. script in #R$C913 calls this through the handler address at #N$CA58.
  $A971,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the trolls are not turned to stone.
  $A974,$05 Call #R$977F using object #R$C63F(#N$47) - "#TEXTTOKEN($C63F+$08,$01)".
  $A979,$05 Call #R$977F using object #R$C651(#N$48) - "#TEXTTOKEN($C651+$08,$01)".
  $A97E,$03 Point #REGhl at the attributes of #OBJECT($47,$01,$01)($).
  $A981,$02 Mark that object not visible.
  $A983,$03 Point #REGhl at the attributes of #OBJECT($48,$01,$01)($).
  $A986,$02 Mark that object not visible.
  $A988,$03 Point at the message "in a clearing with two stone trolls." (#R$B262).
  $A98B,$03 Write #REGhl to #R$BAC4(the empty location description) for #R$BABC(#N$05) - "#TEXTTOKEN($BABC+$02,$01)".
  $A98E,$03 Point #REGhl at the record for location #N$05 at #R$BABC.
  $A991,$02 Clear bit 6 of the attributes for #LOCATION($05) - "#LOCATIONNAME($05)".
  $A993,$05 Call #R$9D53 using object #R$C63F(#N$47) - "#TEXTTOKEN($C63F+$08,$01)".
  $A998,$05 Call #R$9D53 using object #R$C651(#N$48) - "#TEXTTOKEN($C651+$08,$01)".
  $A99D,$03 Point at the message "day dawn(s|d|ing|es)" (#R$B30B).
  $A9A0,$05 Set #R$B702 to #N$01 (daytime).
  $A9A5,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $A9A8,$04 Point #REGix at the location graphics table.
  $A9AC,$05 Load the graphics data for #R$E142 "#LOCATIONNAME$05".
  $A9B1,$03 Read the low byte of the graphics address for #LOCATION($05) (#R$E142).
  $A9B4,$03 Read the high byte of the graphics address for #LOCATION($05) (#R$E142).
  $A9B7,$02 Change the paper colour to #INK$05.
  $A9B9,$01 Increment the graphics data pointer by one.
  $A9BA,$02 Change the colour to #COLOUR$28.
  $A9BC,$01 Return.

c $A9BD Event: Gollum's Reply
@ $A9BD label=Event_GollumsReply
D $A9BD When the player started in Trolls Clearing, prints Gollum's reply. A character script
. in #R$C913 calls this through the handler address at #N$CA3C.
  $A9BD,$03 Read *#R$B6F5.
  $A9C0,$03 Return if where the player starts is not #N$05.
N $A9C3 If the message is held back, Gollum's reply is not printed.
  $A9C3,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, Gollum's reply is not printed.
  $A9C6,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $A9C9,$07 Jump to #R$A9D3 if the character whose turn it is is #N$47.
  $A9D0,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$B24C.
@ $A9D3 label=Event_GollumsReply_PrintGollumReply
  $A9D3,$03 Jump back to #R$A1E3 to print Gollum's reply.

c $A9D6 Event: Location Twenty-Five
@ $A9D6 label=Event_LocationTwentyFive
D $A9D6 Runs when that object's location is #N$25. It prints the message at #R$B3D9 and joins #R$90DF.
  $A9D6,$06 Return if where that object is is not #N$25.
  $A9DC,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$B3D9.
  $A9DF,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $A9E2,$03 Jump back to #R$90DF to wait for a key and restart the game.

c $A9E5 Event: Give The Lunch
@ $A9E5 label=Event_GiveTheLunch
D $A9E5 When the script location matches where the player started, gives the lunch (object
. #N$26). A character script in #R$C913 calls this through the handler address at #N$C9A1.
  $A9E5,$03 Read *#R$B6F6.
  $A9E8,$05 Return if the script location is not where the player starts.
  $A9ED,$04 Point #REGix at #OBJECT($26,$01,$01)($).
  $A9F1,$03 Read the location from the object record.
  $A9F4,$04 Jump to #R$A9FE if the object's location is zero.
  $A9F8,$06 Return unless this object is with #N$41.
@ $A9FE label=Event_GiveTheLunch_HoldTheMessage
N $A9FE If the message is held back, the lunch is not given.
  $A9FE,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the lunch is not given.
  $AA01,$03 Read *#R$B6F6.
  $AA04,$03 Update the object's location.
  $AA07,$04 Set where this object is to #N$41.
  $AA0B,$03 Make #N$26 the current object and #N$00 (the player) the second object.
  $AA0E,$04 Update *#R$B6E8.
  $AA12,$05 Write #N$1D (give) to *#R$B6E7.
  $AA17,$04 Update *#R$B708.
  $AA1B,$06 Point *#R$B70A at "#TEXTTOKEN($C11B+$08,$01)".
N $AA21 Say that will not work.
  $AA21,$03 Call #R$9F76 to answer with that will not work.
  $AA24,$03 Jump to #R$939E to give.
N $AA27 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($13,$01,$01)($) for action #N($38), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $AA27,$04 Read *#R$B708.
  $AA2B,$03 Read the current object's location.
  $AA2E,$01 Copy the result into #REGb.
  $AA2F,$03 Call #R$9F2D to find an exit whose destination is that location.
  $AA32,$02 Compare the exit search with #N$FF.
  $AA34,$03 Point #REGhl at "You cannot jump onto[0x07] from here".
  $AA37,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD if no exit leads there.
  $AA3A,$03 Read this exit's direction.
  $AA3D,$05 Jump to #R$B301 if this direction is not down (#N$0A). #R$B301 is inside the message text, which looks like a bug in the original.
N $AA42 If the message is held back, the lunch is not handed over.
  $AA42,$03 Call #R$9D44. If the message is held back, the lunch is not handed over.
  $AA45,$03 Read *#R$B6E8.
  $AA48,$04 Read *#R$B70C.
  $AA4C,$03 Update where this object is.
  $AA4F,$03 Call #R$9BDD to move what is inside the current object to that location.
  $AA52,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $AA55,$03 Return if it is not the player's turn.
  $AA58,$01 Use the object's location again.
  $AA59,$03 Jump back to #R$9630 to locate location.

c $AA5C Event: The Spider
@ $AA5C label=Event_TheSpider
D $AA5C Clears bits on the spider and stores the token #TOKEN($0623). The spider record at
. #R$CA8D calls this.
  $AA5C,$04 Point #REGix at #OBJECT($07,$01,$01)($).
  $AA60,$04 Mark the spider as not broken (clear bit 3).
  $AA64,$04 Close the object.
  $AA68,$04 Double the strength and keep the overflow.
  $AA6C,$03 Store the token #TOKEN($0623).
  $AA6F,$04 Store #REGde at #R$C20F.
  $AA73,$01 Return.

c $AA74 Event: The Hole Vanishes
@ $AA74 label=Event_TheHoleVanishes
D $AA74 Makes #OBJECT($0B,$01,$01)($) invisible, then at location #N$2A prints that the hole vanishes. The hole record at #R$CAC5 calls this.
  $AA74,$06 Return if #OBJECT($0B,$01,$01)($) is open.
  $AA7A,$06 Store the object's location in the hole script.
  $AA80,$0B Make #OBJECT($0B,$01,$01)($) invisible, and return if the current location is not #N$2A.
@ $AA8B label=Event_TheHoleVanishes_PrintTheVanishing
  $AA8B,$03 Point at the message "the hole vanish(s|d|ing|es)[0x15]" (#R$B2A4).
  $AA8E,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD to print "the hole vanish(s|d|ing|es)".

c $AA91 Event: A Hole Appears
@ $AA91 label=Event_AHoleAppears
D $AA91 Makes #OBJECT($0B,$01,$01)($) visible, then at location #N$2A prints that a hole appears. The hole record at #R$CAC8 calls this.
  $AA91,$0B Make #OBJECT($0B,$01,$01)($) visible, and return if the current location is not #N$2A.
  $AA9C,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$B277.
  $AA9F,$03 Jump back to #R$72DD to print "there is a loud crack and a hole appears".

c $AAA2 Event: Close The Hole
@ $AAA2 label=Event_CloseTheHole
D $AAA2 Writes the hole script and then says the hole vanishes. An object record at #N$C2CB
. calls this.
. Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($0B,$01,$01)($) for action #N($00), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $AAA2,$03 Call #R$97FF to let the message through or hold it back.
  $AAA5,$05 Write #N$06 to *#R$CAC4.
  $AAAA,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$C2BC.
  $AAAD,$02 Mark that object locked.
  $AAAF,$02 Mark that object not visible.
  $AAB1,$02 Jump back to #R$AA8B to print "the hole vanish(s|d|ing|es)".

c $AAB3 Event: Open The Magic Door
@ $AAB3 label=Event_OpenTheMagicDoor
D $AAB3 Opens the magic door and an elf sweeps past. The magic door record at #R$CAAC calls
. this.
  $AAB3,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$C388.
  $AAB6,$02 Mark that object open.
  $AAB8,$03 Point at the message "the magic door open(s|d|ing|es).[0x14]" (#R$B037).
  $AABB,$03 Call #R$AAC7 to print the lake or the river.
  $AABE,$03 Point #REGhl at "an elf sweeps past".
  $AAC1,$03 Call #R$AAC7 to print the lake or the river.
  $AAC4,$03 Jump to #R$AAE0 to handle the ring.

c $AAC7 Event: The Lake Or The River
@ $AAC7 label=Event_TheLakeOrTheRiver
D $AAC7 Prints the lake or the river message when the current location is #N$1E or #N$1C. Any
. other location returns at once.
N $AAC7 Test the flag.
  $AAC7,$08 Jump to #R$72DD if *#R$C12B is #N$1E.
  $AACF,$05 Jump back to #R$72DD if the current location is #N$1C.
  $AAD4,$01 Return.

c $AAD5 Event: Close The Magic Door
@ $AAD5 label=Event_CloseTheMagicDoor
D $AAD5 Closes the magic door. The magic door record at #R$CAA9 calls this.
  $AAD5,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$C388.
  $AAD8,$02 Close that object.
  $AADA,$03 Point at the message "the magic door close(s|d|ing|es).[0x14]" (#R$B03F).
  $AADD,$03 Jump to #R$AAC7 to print it at #LOCATION($1E) or #LOCATION($1C).

c $AAE0 Event: The Ring
@ $AAE0 label=Event_TheRing
D $AAE0 Handles the ring (object #N$10) when it is visible. The ring record at #R$CAB0 calls
. this.
  $AAE0,$04 Point #REGix at #OBJECT($10,$01,$01)($).
  $AAE4,$03 Read where this object is from the object record.
  $AAE7,$03 Return if this object is not inside anything.
N $AAEA Find the object's record.
  $AAEA,$03 Call #R$9BCA to locate object.
  $AAED,$04 Update *#R$B70C.
  $AAF1,$07 Jump back to #R$A3BC if the character is invisible (wearing the ring).
  $AAF8,$01 Return.
N $AAF9 Reached through the action list of #OBJECT($14,$01,$01)($) for action #N($00), dispatched by #R$9B6C.
  $AAF9,$03 Read *#R$B6EA.
  $AAFC,$03 Return if it is not the player's turn.
  $AAFF,$05 Write #N$01 to *#R$B700.
  $AB04,$06 Copy the byte at #R$CAB5 into the next byte.
  $AB0A,$01 Return.

c $AB0B Event: Clear The Flag
@ $AB0B label=Event_ClearTheFlag
D $AB0B Clears #R$B700, the flag that makes S type an F as well. The record at #R$CAB5 calls
. this.
  $AB0B,$01 Clear the flag at #R$B700.
  $AB0C,$03 Update *#R$B700.
  $AB0F,$01 Return.

c $AB10 Event: The Web Smothers You
@ $AB10 label=Event_TheWebSmothersYou
D $AB10 At location #N($1A), prints that the spider web is smothering you. The web record at
. #R$CA94 calls this.
  $AB10,$06 Return if the current location is not #N$1A.
  $AB16,$03 Point at the message "the spider web is slowly smothering you." (#R$B0FD).
  $AB19,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $AB1C,$03 Jump back to #R$90D2 to end the game.

c $AB1F Event: Drops From Above
@ $AB1F label=Event_DropsFromAbove
D $AB1F Prints the forest warning and can sting the player. The forest record at #R$CAC1 calls
. this.
  $AB1F,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$B311.
  $AB22,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $AB25,$09 Return if the current location is the one saved at #R$B6F3.
  $AB2E,$01 Read a byte from the pointer into #REGb.
  $AB2F,$05 Take #N($02), and jump to #R$AB36 unless *#R$B6F3 is #N$02.
  $AB34,$02 Take #N$03.
@ $AB36 label=Event_DropsFromAbove_ReturnIfThisIsTheLocation
  $AB36,$02 Return if the current location is the one in #REGa.
  $AB38,$02 Jump to #R$AB4A to print "some thing drop(s|d|ing|es) from above and sting(s|d|ing|es)".

c $AB3A Event: Stung From Above
@ $AB3A label=Event_StungFromAbove
D $AB3A In the forest, prints that something drops from above and stings. The forest record at
. #R$CABE calls this.
N $AB3A Test the flag.
  $AB3A,$07 Jump to #R$AB44 if *#R$C12B is #N$02.
  $AB41,$03 Return if the current location is not #N$03.
@ $AB44 label=Event_StungFromAbove_PointAtThePaleEyesMessage
  $AB44,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$B311.
  $AB47,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
@ $AB4A label=Event_StungFromAbove_PrintAndEnd
  $AB4A,$03 Point #REGhl at #R$B31F.
  $AB4D,$03 Call #R$72DD to print message.
  $AB50,$03 Jump back to #R$90D2 to end the game.

