b $B6DA Flags: Game State
D $B6DA Variables read and written by the turn loop, the Inglish matcher and the message printer. A saved game starts at #R$B6EB. The word at the start is read by #R$6CEC, #R$6E97, #R$77B9 and #R$7873.
W $B6DA,$02 Start of the unknown word. #R$6DAD reads it while quoting the unknown word.
W $B6DC,$02 Pointer into the token buffer. #R$6D8D stores #R$709C here, and the matcher saves and restores it.
  $B6DE,$01 High byte of the class being matched being matched. Compared with #N$C0 and #N$A0.
  $B6DF,$01 Low byte kept with the class being matched at #R$B6DE.
  $B6E0,$06 Six bytes copied out of the match by #R$7C86.
  $B6E6,$01 Scratch byte beside the match.
@ $B6E7 label=CurrentAction
  $B6E7,$01 Current action id. The verb routines store the verb here before it runs.
@ $B6E8 label=CurrentObject
  $B6E8,$01 Current object id. The noun the command names.
  $B6E9,$01 Second object id, used by give, attack and message code #N$09.
@ $B6EA label=CurrentCharacter
  $B6EA,$01 Character whose turn it is. #N$00 is the player. Stored in the saved game.
  $B6EB,$03 First three bytes of the saved game. #R$6C14 copies them to #R$5F00 and #R$6C52 copies them back.
W $B6EE,$02 Word address held for the printer.
  $B6F0,$01 Cleared at the start of #R$96B3, before the other characters run.
  $B6F1,$01 Scratch byte beside #R$B6F0.
  $B6F2,$01 Printer on. #R$8B22 returns at once when this is zero. #TOKEN($04FB) at #R$82A5 sets it and #TOKEN($0479) at #R$82AF clears it.
  $B6F3,$01 A location id, copied from #R$8D9B by #R$C7DD. #R$AB25 compares it with the current location.
  $B6F4,$01 Slot flag. #R$987C stores #N$01 when the character slot was found.
  $B6F5,$01 Where the player starts. #R$9B02 writes it.
  $B6F6,$01 Location the script at #R$A6C2 compares with the player's location.
@ $B6F7 label=PercentageComplete
W $B6F7,$02 Percentage complete. #R$83EF prints it when the game is over.
  $B6F9,$01 Talk flag. #R$9034 treats zero as a character who still has something to say.
  $B6FA,$01 Message gate. #R$72DD skips its setup when this is not zero. Set to #N$01 when a game starts.
  $B6FB,$01 Message suppressed. Non-zero when #R$9D44 holds a message back and returns past its caller. #R$72DD writes #N$00 here when a message is printed. #R$7D10 keeps running the action while this is zero.
W $B6FC,$02 Weapon-name token. #R$9188 stores #TOKEN($026B) or the named object's name, and message code #N$03 at #R$738D returns it.
  $B6FE,$01 Tested by #R$7CD1 and by message code #N$09.
  $B6FF,$01 Tested by message code #N$09 at #R$73C7.
  $B700,$01 When this is not zero, #R$858B watches for S and types an F as well. Cleared when a game starts.
@ $B701 label=WaitCountdown
  $B701,$01 #N$01=action countdown, #N$00=countdown finished.
@ $B702 label=NightDay
  $B702,$01 #N$00=night, #N$01=day.
  $B703,$01 Which noun the printer is naming. The message codes write #N$00 or #N$01.
  $B704,$01 Capitalise the next letter. A carriage return at #R$86CC sets it, a full stop at #R$8744 bumps it, and #R$873E clears it once a letter has been printed in upper case. #R$72C9 clears it after a printed carriage return.
  $B705,$01 Set by #R$6D13 before a line is read, and cleared by #R$75F6.
  $B706,$01 #N$FF selects the replay path in #R$6CEC. The matcher also stores a class byte here.
  $B707,$01 Picture switch. #R$6C7C stores bit 3 of port #N($7F), read while N is held. #R$7F78 draws nothing when this is zero.
  $B708,$02 Pointer to the current object's record (#R$B6E8).
W $B70A,$02 Pointer to the second object's record (#R$B6E9).
@ $B70C label=Pointer_CurrentCharacter
W $B70C,$02 Pointer to the character whose turn it is. A new game leaves it at #R$C11B, the player.
@ $B70E label=RandomSeed
  $B70E,$01 Random seed, taken from the refresh register at #R$6CA7.
  $B70F,$01 Byte beside the random seed.
  $B710,$01 Kind filter read by #R$9DD9: #N$00 not a live animal, #N$01 a live animal, #N$02 anything. #R$7D2E stores the low two bits of the clause and #R$7E0C stores #N$02.
  $B711,$01 Byte beside #R$B710.
@ $B712 label=RandomCounter
W $B712,$02 Random counter.
W $B714,$02 Delay. #R$6DD6 starts it at #N($0BB8,$04) and #R$7285 stores the next wait here.
  $B716,$01 How many words will be accepted. #R$6CEC writes #N$11 and #R$6D18 writes #N$09.
W $B717,$02 Scratch word beside the limit on how many words are accepted.
  $B719,$01 Matcher phase. #R$82C4 sets #N($02), and #R$76F2 compares it with #N$02.
  $B71A,$01 Cleared when a game starts. #R$7574 takes another path when it is not zero.
  $B71B,$01 Conjunction count. #R$6D42 raises it, and #R$72D3 opens the message gate when it is set.
  $B71C,$01 Scratch byte. #R$7C06 clears it before a phrase is resolved.
  $B71D,$01 Scratch byte read at #R$7C5E while a phrase is resolved.
  $B71E,$01 Packed action scores. #R$70F3 writes this byte, and the matcher reads it.
  $B71F,$18 Verb endings read by #R$7519 in #R$74C1. Four bytes each: "s", "es", "ies", a backspace and "ies", "d" and "ing". Copying one onto a stem turns "take" into "Thorin takes".
  $B737,$01 How many character slots #R$7EBA walks.
  $B738,$C8 Buffer cleared for #N$C8 bytes at the start of a game by #R$6C9F.

u $B800 Unused
D $B800 Zero bytes between the buffer at #R$B738 and the parser slots at #R$B9B0. Only #R$834B writes here: it puts #N$FF at #R$B800 when all eight slots are in use.
  $B800,$01B0

b $B9B0 Parser Slots
D $B9B0 Three slots under the location table. #R$7642 points #REGiy at #R$B9B0, #R$764C reads #R$B9B8 and #R$7665 reads #R$B9C2.
. Read by #R$75FA.
  $B9B0,$08 First slot.
  $B9B8,$0A Second slot.
  $B9C2,$06 Third slot.

b $B9C8 Parser Workspace
D $B9C8 #R$7585 points #REGiy here while a clause is matched. The twenty-four bytes end at the location table.
. Read by #R$7574, #R$75FA, #R$7960 and #R$7D74.
  $B9C8,$18

w $B9E0 Location Table
@ $B9E0 label=LocationTable
D $B9E0 Read by #R$75FA and #R$9BB1.
  $B9E0,$02 #LOCATION(#EVAL((#PC-$B9E0)/$02)) - "#LOCATIONNAME(#EVAL((#PC-$B9E0)/$02))".
L $B9E0,$02,$50

b $BA80 Location Prepositions
D $BA80 Read by #R$9630 when a location's preposition is written into the message.
N $BA80 These are written directly to #R$AFFC(#N$AFFD) which updates the copy for: "You are XXXX[0x16]".
